window.EOM={"curriculum": {"modules": [{"id": "m1", "number": "01", "title": "The manager’s toolkit", "subtitle": "Foundations, principles & responsibility", "description": "Start with what managers do, then learn how they make responsible decisions.", "color": "#c9f36b"}, {"id": "m2", "number": "02", "title": "Turn intent into a plan", "subtitle": "Planning & strategy", "description": "Choose the destination, compare routes, then commit resources.", "color": "#9ebcff"}, {"id": "m3", "number": "03", "title": "Build the organization", "subtitle": "Organizing & departmentation", "description": "Give the plan a structure: tasks, people, authority and relationships.", "color": "#d8b5fa"}, {"id": "m4", "number": "04", "title": "Find & grow your people", "subtitle": "Talent acquisition & management", "description": "Fill the structure with the right people and develop their capabilities.", "color": "#ffbf91"}, {"id": "m5", "number": "05", "title": "Lead, connect & correct", "subtitle": "Leadership, motivation & control", "description": "Help people act, communicate and keep results aligned with the plan.", "color": "#80ddcf"}, {"id": "sdl1", "number": "S1", "title": "Understand quality", "subtitle": "Quality control foundations", "description": "Connect customer needs to design, conformance, performance and assurance.", "color": "#ffd773"}, {"id": "sdl2", "number": "S2", "title": "Make quality a culture", "subtitle": "Total quality management", "description": "Learn the tools and improvement philosophies in the supplied SDL notes.", "color": "#ffd773"}, {"id": "sdl3", "number": "S3", "title": "Solve quality problems", "subtitle": "Tools & implementation", "description": "Choose the right tool for organizing ideas, priorities and actions.", "color": "#ffd773"}, {"id": "sdl4", "number": "S4", "title": "Make quality systematic", "subtitle": "ISO, safety & environment", "description": "Study the standards and versions described in your PDF.", "color": "#ffd773"}], "lessons": [{"id": "business", "module": "m1", "start": 1, "end": 15, "title": "Business, industry & the course map", "idea": "Management begins with an activity that creates value. A business is an individual organization; an industry groups organizations with similar economic activities.", "points": ["The introductory pages preserve the course outcomes, syllabus, assessment and assignment information.", "Primary industries obtain natural resources; secondary industries transform them; tertiary industries provide services.", "Quaternary activities emphasize knowledge and information. Use the examples and classification tree in the source to distinguish the categories.", "Efficiency concerns the resources used; effectiveness concerns whether the intended result is achieved. Read the opening case with both questions in mind."], "memory": "A chain: obtain → transform → serve → know. This is a recall aid, not an additional classification.", "question": "How do business and industry differ?", "answer": "A business is an individual organization pursuing its activity; an industry is the larger group of businesses with related economic activities.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "functions", "module": "m1", "start": 16, "end": 26, "title": "Levels, skills & five functions", "idea": "A manager coordinates people and resources. The five functions give the whole course its sequence.", "points": ["Levels: top, middle and lower management. Technical, human and conceptual skills have different relative importance at different levels.", "Top managers need a broad conceptual view; lower managers need more technical expertise. Human skills matter throughout.", "Planning selects objectives and actions. Organizing establishes roles and relationships. Staffing fills positions.", "Leading influences people to contribute. Controlling measures performance and corrects deviations."], "memory": "POSLC: Plan → Organize → Staff → Lead → Control. Imagine launching and running one organization.", "question": "Which skill matters most for a newly appointed CEO, and why?", "answer": "Conceptual skill: the CEO must understand the enterprise as a whole, connect its parts and set its direction. Human and technical skills still contribute.", "trap": "A function describes the work of managing; a Mintzberg role describes a pattern of managerial behavior.", "minutes": 25}, {"id": "systems", "module": "m1", "start": 27, "end": 30, "title": "The organization as an open system", "idea": "Departments affect one another. An organization takes inputs from its environment, transforms them and returns outputs.", "points": ["Inputs include people, capital, information and resources.", "Managerial functions coordinate the transformation process.", "Outputs and feedback reconnect the organization with its environment.", "Sub-systems must cooperate; optimizing one department alone can harm the whole."], "memory": "Input → transformation → output → feedback. Draw the environment around the whole loop.", "question": "Why is the systems approach useful?", "answer": "It shows the interdependence of organizational parts and the connection between inputs, transformation, outputs, feedback and the external environment.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "fayol", "module": "m1", "start": 31, "end": 52, "title": "Fayol’s 14 principles + 5S", "idea": "These principles help diagnose a management situation. Match each principle to a concrete clue in the case.", "points": ["Work and authority: division of labor; authority and responsibility; unity of command; unity of direction.", "Fair treatment and organization: equity; order; discipline; initiative; fair remuneration.", "Continuity and coordination: stability of tenure; scalar chain; subordination of individual interest to general interest; esprit de corps; centralization and decentralization.", "Unity of command means one boss for an employee. Unity of direction means one head and one plan for activities with a common objective.", "The order discussion includes 5S: sort, set in order, shine, standardize and sustain. Check the source table for the Japanese terms and its additional safety column.", "Centralization concerns where decision authority is concentrated. Its degree depends on the situation."], "memory": "Chunk the list into 4 + 5 + 5: work/authority, fair treatment, then continuity/coordination. Reconstruct each group before checking the source.", "question": "Explain unity of command versus unity of direction.", "answer": "Unity of command: one employee receives orders from one superior. Unity of direction: activities sharing one objective follow one plan under one head.", "trap": "Do not confuse equal treatment with identical treatment regardless of relevant circumstances. Equity emphasizes fairness.", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "roles", "module": "m1", "start": 53, "end": 68, "title": "Mintzberg’s ten managerial roles", "idea": "Ask whether the manager is working through people, handling information or making a decision.", "points": ["Interpersonal: figurehead performs ceremonial duties; leader guides and motivates; liaison maintains contacts.", "Informational: monitor collects information; disseminator shares it internally; spokesperson represents the organization externally.", "Decisional: entrepreneur initiates change; disturbance handler handles unexpected problems; resource allocator assigns resources; negotiator bargains.", "For a case, write both the role category and the specific role. The overview diagram is on page 55."], "memory": "People: F–L–L. Information: M–D–S. Decisions: E–D–R–N. Remember “collect, share inside, speak outside” for the information roles.", "question": "How do disseminator and spokesperson differ?", "answer": "Both communicate information. A disseminator sends it within the organization; a spokesperson communicates to outsiders.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "csr", "module": "m1", "start": 69, "end": 76, "title": "Environment & corporate social responsibility", "idea": "Organizations interact with society. CSR goes beyond the firm’s economic and legal obligations.", "points": ["Review the external-environment diagram on page 69; image content is retained in the source reader.", "The classical view emphasizes economic performance; the socioeconomic view extends responsibility to society.", "CSR drivers in the notes: employees and shareholders; brand equity; ethical marketing and social awareness; environmental consciousness; energy conservation and global warming; responsibility towards government.", "Connect a CSR action to the stakeholder or social issue it addresses."], "memory": "People, brand, ethics, environment, energy, government: six reasons a firm feels responsible.", "question": "What distinguishes CSR from simply obeying the law?", "answer": "CSR includes intentions and responsibilities beyond legal and economic obligations, directed toward social welfare and stakeholder concerns.", "trap": "", "minutes": 19}, {"id": "ethics", "module": "m1", "start": 77, "end": 87, "title": "Three ethical lenses", "idea": "The same decision can be examined through consequences, rights and fairness.", "points": ["Utilitarian theory evaluates consequences: greatest good for the greatest number.", "Rights theory protects basic rights such as conscience, speech and due process.", "Justice theory emphasizes fairness, equity and impartiality.", "Institutionalizing ethics means embedding ethical expectations in organizational practices. Read the source examples and measures, not just the three definitions."], "memory": "U–R–J: Useful consequences, Rights respected, Justice applied.", "question": "Which theory fits a decision based on impartial treatment?", "answer": "The theory of justice, because it asks decision makers to act with fairness, equity and impartiality.", "trap": "A favorable consequence alone does not establish that rights or fairness have been respected.", "minutes": 25}, {"id": "trust", "module": "m1", "start": 88, "end": 91, "title": "Trust makes change possible", "idea": "Change management helps people adapt when goals, processes or technologies change.", "points": ["Change requires strategies for implementing and controlling the transition.", "Trust includes integrity, loyalty, caring and keeping promises.", "The notes extend trust beyond individual relationships to an organizational culture that lasts when leaders change."], "memory": "People change; a culture of trust should remain.", "question": "Why should trust extend beyond a single leader?", "answer": "Leaders may leave, while the organization continues. Trust embedded in organizational culture supports ongoing cooperation and change.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "international", "module": "m1", "start": 92, "end": 103, "title": "Culture & international management", "idea": "The notes compare management practices across cultural settings. Treat these as the course’s comparative descriptions.", "points": ["Read the cultural dimensions and country examples in the source before using the comparison tables.", "Japan: consensus, group harmony, long-term employment and extensive internal development feature in the notes.", "USA: individual achievement, direct communication, faster individual decisions and performance emphasis feature in the comparison.", "Korea: hierarchy, loyalty, seniority and respect for authority feature in the comparison.", "The notes also discuss German, Austrian, Australian, Italian and British management. Compare the same basis across countries rather than writing disconnected lists."], "memory": "Compare using the same columns: decisions, employment, communication, relationships, leadership, motivation, conflict, development, change, work-life, evaluation, CSR.", "question": "Give three matched bases for comparing Japanese and American practices.", "answer": "Compare decision-making, employment practices and performance evaluation: the notes contrast consensus/long-term/group emphasis with more individual/performance/mobile employment emphasis.", "trap": "These are the PDF’s generalizations, not claims that every company or person in a country behaves identically.", "minutes": 27}, {"id": "theoryz", "module": "m1", "start": 104, "end": 108, "title": "Theory Z: a blended approach", "idea": "Ouchi’s Theory Z combines selected organizational practices rather than simply copying one country’s approach.", "points": ["Long-term employment; consensual participative decisions; individual responsibility.", "Slow evaluation and promotion; informal control with explicit formal measures.", "Moderately specialized careers and holistic concern including family.", "Use the Japanese-type and American-type organization lists to understand the comparison."], "memory": "Long employment, shared decisions, individual responsibility; slow promotion, blended controls, moderate careers, whole-person concern.", "question": "What is the responsibility pattern in Theory Z?", "answer": "Decision-making is consensual and participative, while responsibility is individual.", "trap": "Theory Z is separate from McGregor’s Theory X and Theory Y.", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "plans", "module": "m2", "start": 1, "end": 18, "title": "Planning & eight types of plans", "idea": "A plan connects a desired future result to action. Different types of plans answer different questions.", "points": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "memory": "Why exist? → What result? → Which route? → Decision guide → Steps → Must/must not → Coordinated activities → Numbers.", "question": "How do a policy, procedure and rule differ?", "answer": "Policy guides judgment; procedure specifies a sequence of actions; rule prescribes an action or non-action without discretion.", "trap": "A budget can express quantities such as labor hours, not only money.", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "plantypes", "module": "m2", "start": 19, "end": 25, "title": "Classify a plan on several dimensions", "idea": "One plan can have more than one label because each classification asks a different question.", "points": ["Content: strategic, tactical and operational.", "Time: long-term versus short-term; specificity: directional versus specific.", "Frequency: single-use versus standing plans.", "Strategic plans concern broad enterprise direction; operational or tactical plans translate direction into implementation as described in the notes."], "memory": "Content, time, precision, reuse: four questions to classify the same plan.", "question": "How does a standing plan differ from a single-use plan?", "answer": "A standing plan guides recurring activities; a single-use plan is prepared for a particular, nonrecurring situation.", "trap": "", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "planningsteps", "module": "m2", "start": 26, "end": 26, "title": "The eight-step planning process", "idea": "Planning is a sequence from recognizing an opportunity to budgeting the selected course of action.", "points": ["1 Be aware of opportunities; 2 set objectives; 3 consider planning premises; 4 identify alternatives.", "5 Compare alternatives against goals; 6 choose an alternative; 7 formulate supporting plans; 8 numberize the plans through budgets.", "In an application answer, give each step an action in the same business case."], "memory": "Opportunity → Objective → Premises → Alternatives → Compare → Choose → Support → Budget.", "question": "What follows choosing an alternative?", "answer": "Formulate supporting plans, then express the plans numerically through budgets.", "trap": "", "minutes": 5}, {"id": "objectives", "module": "m2", "start": 27, "end": 31, "title": "Objectives: hierarchy & multiplicity", "idea": "Objectives give planning a target. They can exist at different levels and may need to be balanced.", "points": ["Enterprise objectives connect to departmental and individual objectives.", "Top-down and bottom-up approaches relate objectives across the hierarchy.", "Organizations usually pursue multiple objectives.", "Quantitative objectives specify measurable amounts; qualitative objectives describe qualities or directions."], "memory": "Enterprise → department → person. Ask “what evidence would show achievement?”", "question": "Why is an objective hierarchy useful?", "answer": "It connects individual and departmental contributions to enterprise objectives and supports coordination.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "mbo", "module": "m2", "start": 32, "end": 45, "title": "Management by objectives", "idea": "MBO links agreed objectives with action, review and performance appraisal.", "points": ["Superior and subordinate work with connected objectives and expected results.", "Objectives should be clear and verifiable; review the source table of vague versus verifiable objectives.", "The source process links inputs, agreement, actions and final review/appraisal.", "Benefits include clarity, participation and commitment; weaknesses include poor guidance, difficulty setting objectives, short-term emphasis and inflexibility as discussed in the notes."], "memory": "Agree → act → review. The objective is the reference point throughout.", "question": "Why is a verifiable objective stronger than “improve performance”?", "answer": "It supplies an observable target against which achievement can be assessed; the vague phrase gives no clear basis for review.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "strategy", "module": "m2", "start": 46, "end": 52, "title": "Strategic planning & critical questions", "idea": "Before choosing a competitive move, understand the business, its environment and its direction.", "points": ["Study the strategic-planning process diagram and corporate, business and functional levels.", "Critical question analysis asks what the business is, where it is going, what environment it faces and what can achieve objectives.", "Use the worked example to connect questions to a strategy instead of treating them as isolated definitions."], "memory": "Who are we? Where are we going? What surrounds us? What can we do?", "question": "Why does strategy need external analysis?", "answer": "External conditions influence opportunities, threats and the feasibility of achieving the organization’s objectives.", "trap": "", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "blueocean", "module": "m2", "start": 53, "end": 66, "title": "Blue Ocean & the four actions", "idea": "Blue Ocean strategy seeks new demand and value rather than relying only on existing competitive boundaries.", "points": ["The notes emphasize value innovation and creating new market space.", "Four actions: eliminate, reduce, raise and create. Apply them to competitive factors.", "Advantages concern new demand and differentiated value; challenges include risk, resource investment and execution.", "Work through the examples, competitive-factor analysis and four-actions diagrams in the source."], "memory": "ERRC: Eliminate the unnecessary, Reduce excess, Raise value, Create something missing.", "question": "Name the four actions in the framework.", "answer": "Eliminate, reduce, raise and create.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "swot", "module": "m2", "start": 67, "end": 74, "title": "SWOT: sort the evidence", "idea": "The key is to classify by origin first and favorability second.", "points": ["Internal + helpful = strength. Internal + harmful = weakness.", "External + helpful = opportunity. External + harmful = threat.", "Use the actual case statements. A competitor’s action is external; the firm’s own capability is internal.", "Practice the exercise before checking the source classification."], "memory": "Inside = S/W. Outside = O/T. Then ask: helpful or harmful?", "question": "Is an emerging market opportunity or strength?", "answer": "Opportunity: it is a favorable condition outside the firm. A strength would be an internal capability to exploit it.", "trap": "", "minutes": 19}, {"id": "tows", "module": "m2", "start": 75, "end": 84, "title": "TOWS: turn diagnosis into action", "idea": "SWOT classifies facts; TOWS combines them into strategic responses.", "points": ["SO: use strengths to exploit opportunities.", "ST: use strengths to counter threats.", "WO: address weaknesses by using opportunities.", "WT: minimize weaknesses and threats; the notes connect this to defensive or retrenchment approaches.", "Use the worked examples and the dynamic TOWS discussion to see that conditions can change."], "memory": "S can exploit O or resist T; W needs help from O or protection from T.", "question": "How is TOWS different from just listing SWOT?", "answer": "TOWS combines internal and external factors to formulate SO, ST, WO and WT strategies.", "trap": "", "minutes": 23}, {"id": "bcg", "module": "m2", "start": 85, "end": 101, "title": "BCG portfolio matrix", "idea": "Two axes classify a business: relative market share and market growth.", "points": ["High share + high growth = star; low share + high growth = question mark.", "High share + low growth = cash cow; low share + low growth = dog.", "Use the category to justify investment, cash generation or a selective/defensive response; read the source’s strategies and applications.", "The product life cycle and portfolio steps provide context.", "Limitations: two dimensions simplify a complex business, and interdependencies and other factors may be missed."], "memory": "Stars grow and lead. Cows lead in mature markets. Question marks grow without leadership. Dogs have neither.", "question": "How do a star and a cash cow differ?", "answer": "Both have high relative market share. A star operates in a high-growth market; a cash cow operates in a low-growth market.", "trap": "A shrinking market alone does not establish low relative market share. If a case omits an axis, state your assumption.", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "porter", "module": "m2", "start": 102, "end": 108, "title": "Growth & Porter’s generic strategies", "idea": "Competitive advantage and market scope are separate choices.", "points": ["Read the growth-strategy slide before the generic-strategy framework.", "Cost leadership pursues a low-cost position; differentiation offers something distinctive valued by buyers.", "Focus concentrates on a narrower segment and may use cost or differentiation.", "The notes warn about being stuck in the middle when a firm fails to achieve a coherent advantage."], "memory": "Two questions: cheaper or distinctive? Broad market or narrow segment?", "question": "What makes a strategy a focus strategy?", "answer": "Its restricted market scope: it concentrates on a specific segment rather than the entire market.", "trap": "An inexpensive product does not automatically prove broad cost leadership; inspect the target segment.", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "organizing", "module": "m3", "start": 1, "end": 7, "title": "From plan to organizational structure", "idea": "Organizing establishes intentional roles so that people can contribute toward common objectives.", "points": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line."], "memory": "Activities → groups → authority → relationships.", "question": "What problem does organizing solve?", "answer": "It clarifies tasks, roles, authority and relationships so coordinated work can achieve organizational objectives.", "trap": "", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "formal", "module": "m3", "start": 8, "end": 12, "title": "Formal & informal organization", "idea": "A formal structure is deliberately designed. Informal relationships develop through people’s interactions.", "points": ["Formal structure defines roles, reporting and authority.", "Informal organization emerges through shared interests and relationships, illustrated by clubs and social groups.", "The two coexist; an informal connection is not automatically an official reporting line.", "Use the comparison table and overlaid organization diagrams."], "memory": "Formal = designed. Informal = develops.", "question": "Does an informal group replace the reporting hierarchy?", "answer": "No. It develops alongside the formal structure through personal and social relationships.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "span", "module": "m3", "start": 13, "end": 16, "title": "Tall, flat & span of control", "idea": "Span of control is the number of people one manager supervises effectively.", "points": ["A narrow span tends to create more management levels; a wide span tends to create fewer levels.", "Factors include subordinate training, delegation, planning, rate of change, communication techniques, activity, organization type and level.", "Wide spans can reduce bureaucracy but overload managers; narrow spans allow closer contact but add cost and levels.", "Read the source comparison for the full advantages, limitations and influencing factors."], "memory": "Wide → fewer layers. Narrow → more layers. Capacity decides what is workable.", "question": "Why can training permit a wider span?", "answer": "Trained subordinates need less continuous supervision, allowing the manager to supervise more people effectively.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "urwick", "module": "m3", "start": 17, "end": 28, "title": "Urwick’s ten organizing principles", "idea": "A useful organization aligns goals, keeps costs sensible and makes authority clear.", "points": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "memory": "Goals, efficiency, expertise, span, chain; one boss, delegation, parity, flexibility, definition.", "question": "Why must authority and responsibility be balanced?", "answer": "A person needs sufficient authority to fulfill assigned responsibility; responsibility should correspond to the authority delegated.", "trap": "Delegating work does not allow a superior to escape responsibility.", "minutes": 27}, {"id": "departmentation", "module": "m3", "start": 29, "end": 38, "title": "Departmentation: numbers, time & function", "idea": "Departmentation groups activities into manageable units. Choose the grouping that fits the work.", "points": ["Numbers: group people by headcount; the source discusses conditions and limitations.", "Time: organize by shifts to cover different working periods.", "Function: group by specialized functions such as production, marketing and finance.", "Functional grouping builds specialization but can create coordination difficulties between departments.", "For an exam answer: name the grouping, draw a labeled structure, then give merits and limitations."], "memory": "How many? When? Which expertise? These identify numbers, time and function.", "question": "Which structure groups specialists by their work function?", "answer": "Functional departmentation.", "trap": "", "minutes": 23}, {"id": "departmentation2", "module": "m3", "start": 39, "end": 43, "title": "Geography, process, customer & product", "idea": "The same organization can be grouped around locations, work stages, customers or outputs.", "points": ["Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "memory": "Where? How produced? For whom? Which output?", "question": "Which grouping fits a nationwide field sales network?", "answer": "Geographic/territorial departmentation is a defensible choice because work is distributed across regions and needs local coordination.", "trap": "A case may justify a combination of approaches. Explain which level uses which grouping.", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "matrix", "module": "m3", "start": 44, "end": 46, "title": "Matrix structure & identification practice", "idea": "Matrix organization combines two dimensions, commonly function and project/product.", "points": ["Specialists retain functional connections while contributing to projects.", "The structure permits expertise to be shared across projects but requires coordination of authority.", "Study the diagram before trying the final identification exercise.", "Distinguish a matrix’s intersecting dimensions from a simple product hierarchy."], "memory": "Two dimensions cross: expertise × project.", "question": "Why can matrix structures create authority difficulties?", "answer": "People operate across functional and project relationships, which can create competing demands unless authority and coordination are clear.", "trap": "", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "hrm", "module": "m4", "start": 1, "end": 9, "title": "HRM, HRD & their integration", "idea": "HRM manages people and employment arrangements; HRD develops people’s capabilities.", "points": ["HRM includes policies, administration and managing human resources; HRD emphasizes employee development.", "Use the source comparison for scope, objectives, functions and emphasis.", "Integration supports holistic development, organizational performance and aligned people practices.", "The notes distinguish managerial and operational HRM functions."], "memory": "Manage the employment system; develop the person. They support each other.", "question": "Give one reason to integrate HRM and HRD.", "answer": "People-management policies and development activities can reinforce each other, aligning employee growth with organizational needs.", "trap": "", "minutes": 21}, {"id": "jobanalysis", "module": "m4", "start": 10, "end": 13, "title": "Job analysis → description + specification", "idea": "Analyze the job before looking for the person.", "points": ["Job analysis identifies duties, skills and knowledge required.", "Job description describes the job: work, duties and responsibilities.", "Job specification describes the person requirements: qualifications, knowledge, skills and other requirements.", "When asked to draft both, use two clearly separate headings for the same position."], "memory": "Description = the job. Specification = the person.", "question": "Which document lists the qualifications a candidate needs?", "answer": "Job specification; the job description states the work and responsibilities.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "recruitment", "module": "m4", "start": 14, "end": 18, "title": "Recruitment, selection & the ten-step process", "idea": "Recruitment attracts candidates; selection chooses among them; placement assigns the chosen person.", "points": ["1 Hiring needs; 2 job analysis/description; 3 sourcing; 4 screening/shortlisting; 5 interviews.", "6 Assessments/tests; 7 background checks; 8 decision; 9 offer/negotiation; 10 onboarding.", "Each step should follow logically: understand the role, attract candidates, assess suitability and bring the chosen person into the organization."], "memory": "Need → job → source → screen → interview → assess → check → decide → offer → onboard.", "question": "What is the main difference between recruitment and selection?", "answer": "Recruitment searches for and attracts prospective employees; selection evaluates and chooses suitable candidates.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "sources", "module": "m4", "start": 19, "end": 21, "title": "Recruitment approaches, challenges & sources", "idea": "Source selection depends on the role and the organization’s talent needs.", "points": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "memory": "Internal = already connected to the workforce; external = attracting from outside. Follow the classifications in your source slide.", "question": "Why might promotion be useful for an urgent team-leader vacancy?", "answer": "The organization already knows the employee’s performance and experience, reducing familiarization needs and supporting internal progression.", "trap": "Employee recommendations are listed under external sources in this PDF; avoid silently changing its classification.", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "selection", "module": "m4", "start": 22, "end": 31, "title": "Selection: six stages & assessment methods", "idea": "Selection gathers different kinds of evidence about suitability.", "points": ["Initial/preliminary interview → application form → reference checks → employment tests → interview → selection decision.", "Read the test classifications and human-characteristic measures on pages 27–29.", "The interview slide covers several formats; use the format’s purpose and features to distinguish them.", "A decision should integrate evidence rather than rely on a single test or impression."], "memory": "Interview briefly → application → references → tests → interview deeply → decide.", "question": "List the six selection stages in source order.", "answer": "Preliminary interview, application form, reference checks, employment tests, interview and selection decision.", "trap": "The separate ten-step recruitment process places some activities differently. Use the sequence named by the question.", "minutes": 23}, {"id": "training", "module": "m4", "start": 32, "end": 40, "title": "Training & development: process and trends", "idea": "Training supports capability. Its value depends on identifying a need and checking whether learning helps.", "points": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "memory": "Need → design → deliver → evaluate → follow up. Short learning units = microlearning.", "question": "Why is evaluation followed by follow-up?", "answer": "Evaluation checks the training’s results; follow-up supports transfer and continued improvement after delivery.", "trap": "", "minutes": 21}, {"id": "trainingmethods", "module": "m4", "start": 41, "end": 44, "title": "Training methods, induction & sensitivity", "idea": "Choose a method that fits how the skill should be learned.", "points": ["On the job: apprenticeship programs, coaching, internship training and job rotation.", "Off the job: conferences, films, case study, computer modelling, vestibule training and programmed instruction.", "Read the source’s induction and orientation definitions and comparison.", "Sensitivity training develops awareness of interpersonal behavior and its effect on others."], "memory": "On = learning in work. Off = learning away from normal work. Use the source labels for induction/orientation.", "question": "Name two on-the-job and two off-the-job methods.", "answer": "On the job: coaching and job rotation. Off the job: case studies and vestibule training.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "staffing", "module": "m4", "start": 45, "end": 46, "title": "Systems approach to staffing", "idea": "Staffing links enterprise plans to available talent and future capability.", "points": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "memory": "Plans → needs versus inventory → sources → staffing actions → develop/appraise → lead/control.", "question": "Why is manager inventory included in staffing?", "answer": "It shows existing managerial resources so the organization can compare supply with present and future requirements.", "trap": "The source diagram is image-based. Open page 46 to practice the full labeled sketch.", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "managerdevelopment", "module": "m4", "start": 47, "end": 48, "title": "Developing excellent managers", "idea": "Manager development requires organizational support, useful information and continued learning.", "points": ["Willingness to learn; accelerated management development; planning for innovation; effective measurement and rewards.", "Tailored information; research and development in tools and techniques; managerial inventories; strong intellectual leadership.", "Connect each consideration to how it improves managerial capability."], "memory": "Learn, accelerate, innovate, reward; inform, research, inventory, lead.", "question": "Why tailor information for developing managers?", "answer": "Managers need information suited to their roles and development needs to learn and make effective decisions.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "leadership", "module": "m5", "start": 1, "end": 8, "title": "Leadership: influence toward contribution", "idea": "Leadership influences people to contribute willingly and enthusiastically to group goals.", "points": ["Read the distinction between managing and leading.", "Review the ingredients of leadership and the qualities of an effective leader in the source diagrams.", "Authority alone does not explain willingness; influence connects goals with people’s motives and situation."], "memory": "Lead = influence people toward shared goals.", "question": "What distinguishes leadership from merely issuing orders?", "answer": "Leadership seeks willing and enthusiastic contribution through influence, not only formal instruction.", "trap": "", "minutes": 19}, {"id": "maslow", "module": "m5", "start": 9, "end": 14, "title": "Motivation & Maslow’s needs", "idea": "A motive energizes action; motivation is the drive to satisfy a need; a motivator is a technique; satisfaction is the resulting state.", "points": ["Maslow: physiological → safety → social → esteem → self-actualization.", "Physiological: basic living needs. Safety: security. Social: belonging.", "Esteem: respect and recognition. Self-actualization: realizing potential.", "In a case, identify the unmet need using the employee’s specific statement. Multiple unmet needs may appear."], "memory": "Body → safety → belonging → respect → becoming.", "question": "An employee feels excluded despite adequate salary and safety. Which need is evident?", "answer": "Social/belonging needs are unmet. Connect the answer to exclusion and lack of group acceptance.", "trap": "Growth, recognition and belonging are different clues. If asked for the highest unmet level, inspect all the stated evidence.", "minutes": 15}, {"id": "herzberg", "module": "m5", "start": 15, "end": 18, "title": "Herzberg: remove dissatisfaction, then motivate", "idea": "Hygiene factors and motivators act differently. Removing dissatisfaction does not automatically create strong satisfaction.", "points": ["Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "memory": "Hygiene protects the floor. Motivators raise the ceiling.", "question": "Why can good salary coexist with low motivation?", "answer": "Salary can address a hygiene issue while achievement, recognition, responsibility or meaningful work remain missing.", "trap": "Do not say that removing every hygiene problem automatically produces motivation.", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "xy", "module": "m5", "start": 19, "end": 23, "title": "McGregor’s Theory X & Theory Y", "idea": "These are contrasting managerial assumptions about workers and work.", "points": ["X assumes work is disliked, direction/control is needed and responsibility may be avoided.", "Y assumes work can be natural, people can exercise self-direction and seek responsibility under suitable conditions.", "Management practices should be linked to the assumptions and the situation.", "Use the source comparison for motivation, control and responsibility."], "memory": "X = external control. Y = your own direction, with commitment and conditions.", "question": "Which assumptions support giving a capable team greater autonomy?", "answer": "Theory Y: people can exercise self-direction and accept responsibility when committed to objectives.", "trap": "These are managerial assumptions; do not permanently label every employee as an X or Y person.", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "motivationtechniques", "module": "m5", "start": 24, "end": 27, "title": "Motivational techniques", "idea": "Techniques are practical ways of influencing effort, distinct from theories explaining motivation.", "points": ["Compare positive and negative techniques in the notes.", "Study the special motivational techniques, including money, participation and quality of working life.", "For an application answer, connect the technique to the need or behavior it is intended to address."], "memory": "Theory explains why; technique describes what the manager does.", "question": "How does a motivation theory differ from a technique?", "answer": "A theory explains motivation; a technique is an action or method used to influence it.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "authoritystyles", "module": "m5", "start": 28, "end": 40, "title": "Autocratic, democratic & free-rein styles", "idea": "The central question is where decision authority and freedom lie.", "points": ["Autocratic: leader retains decision authority and directs activity.", "Democratic/participative: members contribute to decisions.", "Free-rein/delegative: employees have substantial freedom within the arrangement.", "Learn each style’s strengths, limitations and suitable conditions from the source.", "Style choice is influenced by the leader, subordinates and situation; justify the fit to the case."], "memory": "Leader decides → group participates → team has freedom.", "question": "Why might a creative, capable team benefit from participation?", "answer": "It allows members’ expertise and ideas to influence decisions and supports involvement; justify it using the team’s stated needs.", "trap": "No style is automatically best for every team and situation.", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "likert", "module": "m5", "start": 41, "end": 46, "title": "Likert’s four systems", "idea": "Likert’s systems form a progression in trust and participation.", "points": ["1 Exploitative-authoritative: low trust, centralized decisions and coercive direction.", "2 Benevolent-authoritative: paternal confidence and rewards, while control stays largely at the top.", "3 Consultative: managers seek input but retain important decisions.", "4 Participative: substantial trust, group participation and shared involvement.", "Identify systems from evidence about trust, motivation, communication and decisions."], "memory": "E–B–C–P: Exploit → Benevolent → Consult → Participate. Increasing trust is the spine.", "question": "What separates consultative from participative management?", "answer": "Consultative management seeks input while retaining major decisions; participative management involves groups more fully in decision-making.", "trap": "", "minutes": 15}, {"id": "grid", "module": "m5", "start": 47, "end": 56, "title": "The managerial grid", "idea": "Two concerns vary independently: people and production.", "points": ["1,1 impoverished: low concern for both.", "1,9 country club: high people, low production.", "9,1 task/authority-compliance: high production, low people.", "5,5 middle-of-the-road: moderate concern for both; 9,9 team: high concern for both.", "Practice placing each style on the axes and identifying it from cases."], "memory": "First coordinate = production. Second = people. 9,9 is high on both.", "question": "What does 1,9 represent?", "answer": "Country-club management: low concern for production and high concern for people.", "trap": "Read the axis labels before assigning coordinates.", "minutes": 23}, {"id": "continuum", "module": "m5", "start": 57, "end": 58, "title": "Leadership as a continuum", "idea": "Leadership behavior can vary between manager-centered authority and greater subordinate freedom.", "points": ["The continuum connects the manager’s use of authority with the area of freedom for subordinates.", "Read the exact stages on the source diagram.", "Explain a shift in style by saying how participation and decision freedom change."], "memory": "As subordinate freedom expands, unilateral manager control contracts.", "question": "What changes as we move along the leadership continuum?", "answer": "The balance between the manager’s authority and the subordinates’ freedom in decision-making.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "groups", "module": "m5", "start": 59, "end": 67, "title": "Committees, groups & four development stages", "idea": "A committee has an assigned matter; groups involve interdependent people working toward common goals.", "points": ["Forming: getting acquainted. Storming: conflict and clarification of objectives. Norming: agreed rules. Performing: task execution.", "Read committee functions/formality, reasons for use and disadvantages.", "Reasons include group judgment, representation and coordination; costs and delays are among the limitations.", "Groups can influence behavior, attitudes, values, motivation and relationships."], "memory": "Meet → clash → agree → do. The notes use four stages.", "question": "What distinguishes norming from performing?", "answer": "Norming establishes agreed behavior and rules; performing focuses on carrying out the task.", "trap": "Do not add an extra development stage to a question asking for the four stages in these notes.", "minutes": 21}, {"id": "teams", "module": "m5", "start": 68, "end": 72, "title": "Teams & team building", "idea": "Teams need a meaningful purpose and coordinated contributions.", "points": ["Read the team definition and the distinctions from groups and committees.", "The source discusses recommendations, decision-making and operational roles.", "Team building requires a worthwhile purpose, suitable skills, rules of behavior and commitment.", "Study the team types in the final slide of this range."], "memory": "Purpose + people + rules + coordinated action.", "question": "Why do rules of group behavior matter in a team?", "answer": "They clarify how members work together and support coordinated effort toward the team’s purpose.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "communication", "module": "m5", "start": 73, "end": 87, "title": "Communication: process & nine types", "idea": "Communication links people and integrates managerial functions.", "points": ["Sender forms a thought and encodes a message; it is transmitted, received, decoded and understood; feedback closes the loop. Noise can interfere.", "Formal and informal; downward and upward; horizontal/crosswise and diagonal; written, oral and non-verbal.", "Each classification describes a different aspect: official status, direction or medium.", "Compare advantages and limitations of written and oral communication using the source."], "memory": "Think → encode → transmit → receive → decode → understand → feedback; noise can interrupt.", "question": "How does diagonal communication differ from horizontal?", "answer": "Horizontal communication links similar levels; diagonal communication links different levels without a direct reporting relationship.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "barriers", "module": "m5", "start": 88, "end": 92, "title": "Communication barriers & remedies", "idea": "A message can be sent without being understood. Diagnose the obstacle and match a remedy.", "points": ["Source barriers include lack of planning, unclear assumptions, semantic distortion, poor expression, loss/retention issues and poor listening.", "Also review perception, attitudes, status/power, information overload and other barriers listed in the pages.", "Remedies include clarity, audience awareness, consultation, suitable language and follow-up/feedback.", "Read listening guidance and the separate written-communication checklist."], "memory": "Barrier → mechanism → remedy. Explain how the remedy fixes this barrier.", "question": "How can feedback help with unclear understanding?", "answer": "It reveals how the receiver interpreted the message so the sender can clarify or correct misunderstanding.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "controlprocess", "module": "m5", "start": 93, "end": 99, "title": "Controlling: the eight-step loop", "idea": "Control compares what happened with what was planned, then corrects deviations.", "points": ["Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "memory": "Set → perform → measure → compare → identify → analyze → plan correction → implement.", "question": "Why analyze causes before implementing corrections?", "answer": "The same observed deviation can have different causes; correction should address the actual cause rather than only the symptom.", "trap": "", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "controltypes", "module": "m5", "start": 100, "end": 101, "title": "Before, during & after: three controls", "idea": "The timing of a control helps identify it in a case.", "points": ["Feedforward anticipates problems before they arise.", "Concurrent control monitors ongoing transformation of inputs into outputs.", "Feedback control uses results and reactions after the output.", "Quote the time/action clue and explain why it fits the selected control."], "memory": "Before = feedforward. During = concurrent. After = feedback.", "question": "Monitoring a process while it runs is which type?", "answer": "Concurrent control: it gives immediate information about ongoing operations.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "budgets", "module": "m5", "start": 102, "end": 110, "title": "Exam-included self-study: budgetary control", "idea": "These source pages are explicitly marked as included for the end-semester exam.", "points": ["Budgetary control compares actual results with budgeted results and supports corrective action.", "A budget is a numerical plan; categories include financial, operating and non-monetary budgets.", "Read the cash, capital and balance-sheet-related financial budgets and the operating budget categories.", "Non-monetary budgets can state labor, space or output quantities.", "Dangers include over-budgeting, overriding enterprise goals and inflexibility; read all source explanations."], "memory": "Budget = plan in numbers. Budgetary control = compare reality with those numbers.", "question": "Can a budget be non-financial?", "answer": "Yes. The notes include non-monetary budgets expressed in quantities such as labor or other resources.", "trap": "", "minutes": 21}, {"id": "flexiblebudget", "module": "m5", "start": 111, "end": 114, "title": "Variable & zero-based budgets", "idea": "Different budgeting approaches handle changing activity and the justification of expenditure.", "points": ["Variable/flexible budgets vary with output or another activity measure.", "A fixed budget does not adjust in the same way.", "Zero-based budgeting requires expenditure to be examined and justified rather than simply carrying forward the past.", "Study the source’s explanations and cost behavior diagrams."], "memory": "Flexible asks “what if volume changes?” Zero-based asks “why spend this at all?”", "question": "Why is a variable budget useful when output changes?", "answer": "It adjusts budgeted amounts to the activity level, making comparison more meaningful.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "nonbudget", "module": "m5", "start": 115, "end": 120, "title": "Non-budgetary controls & project networks", "idea": "Not all useful control information is a budget.", "points": ["Statistical data; special reports and analysis; operational audit; other devices listed on the source overview.", "Operational audit independently appraises operations and their effectiveness.", "PERT and CPM support project planning and control; Gantt charts show time relationships.", "Review the time-event network material and source diagrams rather than substituting a financial budget."], "memory": "Data tells, reports explain, audit appraises, networks schedule.", "question": "How does a Gantt chart support control?", "answer": "It shows activities or events against time, helping managers compare progress with the schedule.", "trap": "", "minutes": 15}, {"id": "overall", "module": "m5", "start": 121, "end": 136, "title": "Overall control: summaries, profit & ROI", "idea": "Overall control assesses the enterprise or a major unit rather than only isolated operations.", "points": ["Budget summaries consolidate individual budgets and show overall performance.", "Profit-and-loss control compares revenue and expenses and gives divisional managers responsibility for results.", "Read its limitations, including accounting effort and overhead allocation issues.", "ROI relates profit to investment; the source diagram links return to its influencing factors.", "Study ROI advantages and limitations, including the information and valuation difficulties in the notes."], "memory": "Whole-enterprise lens: combined budgets → profit/loss → return on invested resources.", "question": "What does ROI add beyond simply knowing profit?", "answer": "It relates profit to the investment used to produce it, supporting comparison of how effectively invested resources are used.", "trap": "", "minutes": 28}, {"id": "preventive", "module": "m5", "start": 137, "end": 143, "title": "Direct & preventive control", "idea": "Direct control investigates deviations; preventive control improves managers so fewer avoidable deviations arise.", "points": ["Direct control traces causes and responsibility for unsatisfactory results.", "Read the seven causes/assumptions discussion on negative deviations.", "Preventive control develops knowledge and application of managerial principles, functions and techniques.", "Study the three assumptions and the advantages: psychological benefit, accuracy, self-control and lighter managerial burden."], "memory": "Direct: trace the error. Preventive: improve the capability that avoids it.", "question": "How is preventive control connected to manager development?", "answer": "Improved managerial knowledge and capability reduce errors that would otherwise require corrective action.", "trap": "Do not collapse this managerial concept into merely another label for feedforward control.", "minutes": 17}, {"id": "quality", "module": "sdl1", "start": 1, "end": 3, "title": "Quality starts with the customer", "idea": "Quality concerns meeting requirements and needs, not simply making something expensive or durable.", "points": ["Juran emphasizes features that meet customer needs and freedom from deficiencies.", "Products include goods, software and services; customers may be external or internal.", "Concepts include fitness for purpose, conformance to requirements and grade.", "Quality of design translates customer needs into the product’s intended features and specifications."], "memory": "Need → specification. A durable fuse that fails to protect is not good quality for its purpose.", "question": "Why is durability alone an inadequate definition of quality?", "answer": "A product must serve its intended purpose and requirements; the source’s fuse example shows that lasting longer can be undesirable.", "trap": "", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "qualitydimensions", "module": "sdl1", "start": 4, "end": 5, "title": "Design, conformance & performance", "idea": "A good idea, correct manufacture and satisfactory use are different links in the quality chain.", "points": ["Design: specify the right product for the need.", "Conformance: the actual output follows the design and other requirements; process variability and inspection matter.", "Performance: how the product functions in customer use, depending on both design and conformance.", "Review the listed factors affecting conformance and the service-sector example."], "memory": "Design = intended. Conformance = made as intended. Performance = works in use.", "question": "Can perfect conformance compensate for a poor design?", "answer": "No. A product can match its design precisely yet fail to satisfy the customer if the design itself is unsuitable.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "qc", "module": "sdl1", "start": 6, "end": 7, "title": "Quality control & specifications", "idea": "QC measures actual performance, compares it with a standard and acts on deviation.", "points": ["Quality belongs across marketing, R&D, design, planning, purchasing, production, inspection and retail/service.", "Specifications can be standard, customer or company specifications.", "QC seeks acceptable output, fewer defects and losses, conformity, loyal customers and sustained value.", "Key characteristics include technological, psychological, time-oriented, contractual and ethical dimensions.", "Inspection finds defects; QC aims to regulate quality and reduce variation in future output."], "memory": "Measure → compare → correct. Inspection detects; QC feeds improvement.", "question": "Give three objectives of quality control.", "answer": "Improve customer acceptance, reduce losses from defects and ensure conformity to established standards; connect them to customer satisfaction.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "stakeholders", "module": "sdl1", "start": 8, "end": 8, "title": "Stakeholders in quality", "idea": "People affected by an organization can influence its quality decisions and their success.", "points": ["Stakeholders include investors, consumers, employees and other affected or influential groups.", "Roles include plan/policy development, support for improvement initiatives and performance monitoring.", "Quality assurance begins by considering confidence in product performance under actual conditions."], "memory": "Help plan → support improvement → monitor progress.", "question": "Why involve stakeholders early in a quality initiative?", "answer": "Their interests and support affect implementation; involvement helps identify concerns and align the initiative with relevant needs.", "trap": "", "minutes": 5}, {"id": "qa", "module": "sdl1", "start": 9, "end": 12, "title": "Quality assurance, audit & survey", "idea": "Assurance builds confidence that quality requirements will be met. Audits and surveys examine whether arrangements support that confidence.", "points": ["Read the detailed quality-assurance explanation before the audit process.", "Quality audit examines quality activities and their effectiveness systematically.", "The notes discuss audit planning, evidence, reporting and follow-up; preserve the full procedure in the source.", "Quality survey evaluates a supplier’s quality capabilities and arrangements."], "memory": "Assurance = confidence supported by evidence. Audit = examine. Survey = assess capability.", "question": "How is assurance broader than inspecting the finished product?", "answer": "It concerns confidence in the arrangements and activities that support quality, not just sorting finished output.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "productaudit", "module": "sdl1", "start": 13, "end": 15, "title": "Product audits & economic effects", "idea": "Product audits independently evaluate quality and help reveal defects missed earlier.", "points": ["Read the defect classifications, demerit weighting and example table.", "Inspection effectiveness example: 40 previously detected defects out of 50 total detected defects = 80%.", "Compare against a stable base period or market quality as described.", "Quality affects costs through fewer deficiencies and income through valued product features."], "memory": "Fewer deficiencies → lower waste. Better features → greater customer value.", "question": "In the source example, what is inspection effectiveness if inspection found 40 defects and audit found 10 more?", "answer": "40 ÷ (40 + 10) × 100 = 80%.", "trap": "Do not divide by only the additional audit defects.", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "qualityreview", "module": "sdl1", "start": 16, "end": 19, "title": "Quality foundations: summary & worked answers", "idea": "Use the unit’s own summary, keywords and SAQ answers to test whether the distinctions have become clear.", "points": ["Review quality, QC, assurance, audit and the effects on cost and income.", "Work through the supplied self-assessment answers.", "Explain the complete chain from customer need through product design, conformance, use and feedback."], "memory": "Need → design → make → check → use → learn.", "question": "How can quality simultaneously reduce costs and increase income?", "answer": "Freedom from deficiencies reduces waste, rework and user losses; useful features improve satisfaction, sales and market position.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "tqm", "module": "sdl2", "start": 1, "end": 5, "title": "TQM, objectives & Joiner’s triangle", "idea": "TQM makes quality a shared, continuing organizational commitment.", "points": ["Study the definition, principle of objectives and reasons for popularity.", "Three axioms: commitment, scientific knowledge and involvement; represented by Joiner’s triangle.", "Management commitment requires action and resources, not slogans alone.", "Scientific methods support fact-based decisions; involvement brings people, teams and suppliers into improvement.", "Benefits discussed include cost reduction, a supportive environment and broader measures of success."], "memory": "CSI: Commitment + Scientific knowledge + Involvement. All three sides must support the culture.", "question": "State and explain the three TQM axioms.", "answer": "Commitment sustains improvement; scientific knowledge supplies appropriate tools and evidence; involvement engages everyone in the change.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "basictools", "module": "sdl2", "start": 6, "end": 9, "title": "The seven basic tools in your PDF", "idea": "Choose a tool by the question you need to answer.", "points": ["Cause-and-effect/fishbone: possible causes. Flowchart: process steps. Pareto: priorities among causes.", "Run chart: change over time. Control chart: process behavior with control limits.", "Correlation chart: relationship between factors. Histogram: frequency distribution.", "The source illustrates mean, standard deviation and UCL/LCL calculations and a histogram example.", "Keep this exact seven-tool list: other textbooks may use a different list."], "memory": "Causes, steps, priorities, time, limits, relationships, distribution.", "question": "Which tool helps prioritize the largest causes of defects?", "answer": "A Pareto chart, which arranges causes by magnitude so the most significant contributors can be addressed first.", "trap": "The PDF’s list includes flowchart and run chart. Do not substitute an external “standard seven” list.", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "kaizen", "module": "sdl2", "start": 10, "end": 11, "title": "Kaizen, Kanban, circles & maintenance", "idea": "Small continuing improvements need people, flow and reliable equipment.", "points": ["Read the Kaizen introduction at the end of page 9 with the tools lesson.", "Total quality control integrates quality across organizational groups.", "Kanban supports just-in-time flow; quality circles bring small groups into improvement.", "The source calls TPM “Total Predictive Maintenance”; use its discussion and terminology for this course."], "memory": "Improve continually; pull materials; discuss locally; maintain equipment.", "question": "How do quality circles support improvement?", "answer": "Small groups use participation and shared experience to identify and solve quality-related problems.", "trap": "Retain the terminology used in the supplied PDF; do not silently replace its TPM expansion.", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "deming", "module": "sdl2", "start": 12, "end": 15, "title": "Deming’s 14 points, seven sins & PDCA", "idea": "Deming connects quality to improving the system and management’s behavior.", "points": ["14 points, grouped for recall: constancy and new philosophy; prevention rather than mass inspection; avoid price-only purchasing; continually improve.", "Train, lead, remove fear and break barriers; eliminate slogans and numerical targets/work standards.", "The PDF’s list continues with ending annual merit reviews, education/self-improvement and permanent top-management commitment.", "Read all seven deadly sins on pages 14–15; they include lack of constancy, short-term emphasis and management practices that undermine improvement.", "PDCA: plan, do, check, act. Use the cycle as a repeating learning loop."], "memory": "Direction → better system → better leadership → remove obstacles → learn and commit. PDCA: plan a change, try it, check it, act on learning.", "question": "What is the order of the PDCA cycle?", "answer": "Plan → Do → Check → Act; repeat the cycle for continued improvement.", "trap": "Use the 14-point wording in this PDF; do not merge it with Crosby’s different 14 points.", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "juran", "module": "sdl2", "start": 16, "end": 19, "title": "Juran’s trilogy & breakthrough", "idea": "Juran separates setting up quality, maintaining it and raising it to a better level.", "points": ["Quality planning identifies customers/needs, translates them into requirements and develops product/process capability.", "Quality control detects and corrects adverse change.", "Quality improvement raises performance beyond the previous level.", "Read the breakthrough sequence, managerial responsibilities and project-by-project approach."], "memory": "Plan the level → control the level → improve the level.", "question": "Why is improvement different from control?", "answer": "Control prevents or corrects deterioration from the set level; improvement establishes a higher level of quality.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "crosby", "module": "sdl2", "start": 20, "end": 23, "title": "Crosby: four absolutes & zero defects", "idea": "Crosby makes requirements and the cost of doing things wrong central to quality management.", "points": ["Quality means conformance to requirements; the system is prevention; the measurement is price of non-conformance; the performance standard is zero defects.", "Zero defects is a continuing improvement objective; the notes warn that slogans without a sound system or unrealistic short-term pressure can fail.", "Read Crosby’s separate 14-point improvement program on pages 21–22.", "Review the steps for implementing a zero-defect program on page 23."], "memory": "Conformance → prevention → zero defects → cost of non-conformance. Use these four labels to reconstruct the explanation.", "question": "What is the price of non-conformance?", "answer": "The costs of doing things wrong, such as rejects, rework and warranty costs.", "trap": "“Quality is free” does not mean improvement requires no effort; it concerns the savings from avoiding poor quality.", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "pokayoke", "module": "sdl2", "start": 24, "end": 26, "title": "Poka-yoke & concurrent engineering", "idea": "Quality improves when errors are prevented and product decisions are coordinated early.", "points": ["Poka-yoke concerns mistake-proofing: preventing mistakes or making them evident.", "Concurrent engineering coordinates product and related process considerations rather than leaving everything to a late sequence.", "Read the unit summary and keywords, then answer its SAQs without looking."], "memory": "Poka-yoke: stop the mistake. Concurrent: work together early.", "question": "How does poka-yoke support quality?", "answer": "It helps prevent errors or detect them promptly, reducing the chance that mistakes become defects.", "trap": "", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "tqmhouse", "module": "sdl3", "start": 1, "end": 5, "title": "The house of TQM: eight elements", "idea": "The PDF gives a ready-made memory picture: quality culture as a house.", "points": ["Foundation: ethics, integrity, trust.", "Bricks: training, teamwork, leadership.", "Mortar: communication. Roof: recognition.", "Read the team types, communication directions and recognition discussion within these pages.", "The components depend on each other; recognition cannot compensate for an untrustworthy foundation."], "memory": "Foundation EIT; bricks TTL; communication binds; recognition crowns.", "question": "List the eight elements of TQM.", "answer": "Ethics, integrity, trust, training, teamwork, leadership, communication and recognition.", "trap": "", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "ideatools", "module": "sdl3", "start": 6, "end": 9, "title": "Affinity, tree & interrelations diagrams", "idea": "Different diagrams make different relationships visible.", "points": ["Affinity groups related ideas or verbal information.", "Tree breaks a broad objective into more specific means/actions.", "Interrelations shows cause-and-effect links among issues and identifies drivers/effects.", "Follow the source construction steps and arrow-counting example."], "memory": "Affinity groups. Tree decomposes. Interrelations connects causes.", "question": "Which tool decomposes a broad objective into specific actions?", "answer": "A tree diagram.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "priority", "module": "sdl3", "start": 10, "end": 11, "title": "Prioritization & PDPC", "idea": "One tool chooses where to act; the other prepares for what could go wrong.", "points": ["Prioritization matrices evaluate options against criteria and ratings; the introductory explanation begins on page 9.", "PDPC maps a proposed process or plan, possible outcomes and countermeasures.", "Study the scoring tables and the lawnmower-related example.", "When explaining a tool, state its purpose, construction and interpretation."], "memory": "Prioritization = choose. PDPC = anticipate problems and responses.", "question": "What is PDPC used for?", "answer": "To anticipate possible problems or outcomes in a planned process and prepare appropriate countermeasures.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "network", "module": "sdl3", "start": 12, "end": 14, "title": "Activity networks & matrix diagrams", "idea": "Networks organize tasks through time; matrices organize relationships between sets of factors.", "points": ["Activity network/PERT shows dependencies, parallel activities and scheduling.", "Review activities, events, paths, dummy activities and construction rules in the source.", "The expected-time example uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic estimates; inspect the worked figure and SAQ.", "Matrix diagrams show presence and strength of relationships; review the restaurant example and matrix types."], "memory": "Network = sequence through time. Matrix = intersections between factor sets.", "question": "What does a matrix diagram reveal?", "answer": "Whether relationships exist between sets of factors and how strong those relationships are.", "trap": "A matrix diagram here is a quality tool, not the matrix organization structure in Module 3.", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "tqmimplementation", "module": "sdl3", "start": 15, "end": 17, "title": "Implementing TQM", "idea": "Implementation depends on the organization’s condition and its capacity for change.", "points": ["The notes discuss different organizational scenarios and readiness.", "Compare integrated-management and traditional-management approaches.", "Guidelines include commitment, a shared direction, customer identification/feedback, quality teams and organizational assessment.", "Read the full sequence rather than assuming a single universal implementation formula."], "memory": "Assess readiness → align people → act on quality → learn from results.", "question": "Why should organizational readiness be assessed before implementing TQM?", "answer": "Its history, culture and ability to change affect the approach and support required for successful implementation.", "trap": "", "minutes": 9}, {"id": "measurement", "module": "sdl3", "start": 18, "end": 21, "title": "Measure quality: cost, time & scorecards", "idea": "Measurement gives feedback about whether improvement is working.", "points": ["Cost of poor quality is introduced on page 17 and continued here: waste and non-value-adding activity consume resources.", "Improper time management leaves more effort in firefighting and less in prevention.", "Scorekeeping makes results visible and enables comparison with objectives.", "Read the cost figures, time-management comparison, scorecard and final keywords."], "memory": "Cost shows waste. Time shows where effort goes. Scorecards show progress.", "question": "Why distinguish firefighting time from prevention time?", "answer": "It reveals whether effort is consumed by recurring problems or invested in preventing them.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "iso", "module": "sdl4", "start": 1, "end": 5, "title": "ISO 9000: purpose, family & benefits", "idea": "These pages describe quality-management-system standards and their historical development.", "points": ["ISO 9000 concerns management systems and quality assurance practices, rather than certifying that every individual product is perfect.", "Read the history and the source’s original ISO 9000/9001/9002/9003/9004/8402 descriptions.", "The PDF then explains the revised ISO 9000:2000 family: vocabulary, requirements and improvement guidance.", "Benefits include confidence, consistency, market access and improved organization, as elaborated in the pages."], "memory": "A system for consistent quality. Learn the version labels along with the roles.", "question": "Does ISO 9000 simply specify the quality of a single product?", "answer": "No. The supplied unit focuses on the organization’s quality management and assurance system.", "trap": "Historical syllabus content: these notes discuss 1994/2000 versions. This app preserves them for your exam and does not present them as current regulatory guidance.", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "isorequirements", "module": "sdl4", "start": 6, "end": 9, "title": "QMS requirements & management responsibility", "idea": "A quality system connects policy, resources, product realization, measurement and improvement.", "points": ["Read the requirements in the source: management commitment, customer focus, policy, planning, responsibilities and review.", "Resource management supports competent people and appropriate infrastructure/work environment.", "Product realization covers planning, customer processes, design, purchasing and production/service control.", "Measurement, analysis and improvement include monitoring, non-conforming output and corrective/preventive action."], "memory": "Direction → resources → realization → measurement → improvement.", "question": "What should happen when a non-conforming product is detected?", "answer": "Identify and control it to prevent unintended use/delivery, address the cause and retain evidence of correction as described in the PDF.", "trap": "", "minutes": 11}, {"id": "documentation", "module": "sdl4", "start": 10, "end": 10, "title": "Documentation: say, do, show", "idea": "Documentation communicates methods, supports consistent action and provides evidence.", "points": ["Read page 9 with the preceding lesson for the quality manual and six required documented procedures in the source edition.", "Those six: document control, record control, internal audit, non-conforming product control, corrective action and preventive action.", "Other documents include specifications, quality plans and records.", "A document provides information; a record provides evidence of results or activities."], "memory": "Manual = system. Procedure = method. Record = evidence.", "question": "How does a record differ from a procedure?", "answer": "A procedure specifies how an activity is performed; a record provides evidence of results achieved or activities performed.", "trap": "", "minutes": 5}, {"id": "isoimplementation", "module": "sdl4", "start": 11, "end": 12, "title": "Implementing ISO 9000 QMS", "idea": "Implementation moves from understanding and documentation to internal correction and external assessment.", "points": ["Exposure/training → draft quality manual → internal audit and incorporation of requirements → external certification audit.", "The notes distinguish organizations starting a QMS, adapting an existing QMS and demonstrating conformity.", "Documentation also supports communication, evidence and knowledge sharing.", "Read the differences between existing process-based arrangements and arrangements needing more definition."], "memory": "Train → document → audit inside → correct → assess outside.", "question": "Why conduct an internal audit before external assessment?", "answer": "It checks existing practices against requirements and allows deficiencies to be corrected before external evaluation.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}, {"id": "osha", "module": "sdl4", "start": 13, "end": 17, "title": "Occupational safety & OSHA", "idea": "The unit connects quality management with safe and healthy work.", "points": ["Read OSHA’s purposes and the benefits to employers, employees and the public as described in the course material.", "Standard categories: specification, performance, particular/vertical and general/horizontal.", "Safety assessment uses documentation, interviews and site-condition review.", "Key components include leadership/participation/accountability, worksite analysis, hazard prevention/control and safety/health training.", "The source explains why awareness campaigns alone may not ensure safe practice."], "memory": "Lead and involve → analyze the site → prevent/control hazards → train.", "question": "Name three ways to assess a safety program in the source.", "answer": "Review documents, interview people and inspect site conditions.", "trap": "Study this as supplied course content, not as advice on current legal compliance.", "minutes": 13}, {"id": "environment", "module": "sdl4", "start": 18, "end": 19, "title": "ISO 14000 & final quality review", "idea": "Environmental management connects the organization’s activities and product life cycle to environmental effects.", "points": ["Read the ISO 14000 explanation beginning on page 17 with the preceding lesson.", "The source covers environmental policy, auditing, performance evaluation, labeling and life-cycle assessment.", "Policy requires senior-management support and commitments to improvement, pollution prevention and applicable requirements.", "The cycle involves planning, implementation/operation, checking/corrective action and management review.", "Review the summary, glossary and SAQs. The bibliography is retained as source material, not added to the syllabus."], "memory": "9000: quality system. 14000: environmental system. OSHA: worker safety and health.", "question": "How do ISO 9000 and ISO 14000 differ in focus?", "answer": "ISO 9000 focuses on quality management/customer satisfaction; ISO 14000 focuses on environmental management and the effects of organizational activities.", "trap": "", "minutes": 7}]}, "questions": [{"id": "pyq1-1A", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "Different managerial activities are listed in the table below. With reference to Mintzberg's managerial roles, mention the role\ncategory and specific role for the following activities.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["roles"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Interpersonal: figurehead performs ceremonial duties; leader guides and motivates; liaison maintains contacts.", "Informational: monitor collects information; disseminator shares it internally; spokesperson represents the organization externally.", "Decisional: entrepreneur initiates change; disturbance handler handles unexpected problems; resource allocator assigns resources; negotiator bargains.", "For a case, write both the role category and the specific role. The overview diagram is on page 55."], "tip": "Use the original question image: its activities table is embedded as an image and is absent from text extraction. For each row, give both category and specific role. Decide whether the action concerns people, information or decisions.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-1B", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Compare and contrast the managerial practices in Japan and the USA, focusing on cultural influences and\norganizational behavior.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["international"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Read the cultural dimensions and country examples in the source before using the comparison tables.", "Japan: consensus, group harmony, long-term employment and extensive internal development feature in the notes.", "USA: individual achievement, direct communication, faster individual decisions and performance emphasis feature in the comparison.", "Korea: hierarchy, loyalty, seniority and respect for authority feature in the comparison.", "The notes also discuss German, Austrian, Australian, Italian and British management. Compare the same basis across countries rather than writing disconnected lists."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-1C", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "Five years ago, Mr. Rahul Modi completed his degree in food processing. He worked for one year in a\ncompany manufacturing noodles, masala powders, and pasta. He was not happy in the company and\ndecided to have his own organic food processing unit for the same. He set the objectives and the targets\nand formulated an action plan to achieve the same. One of his objectives was to earn a 10% profit on the\namount invested in the first year. It was also decided that raw materials like fruits, vegetables, spices, etc.\nwill be purchased on three months' credit from farmers cultivating organic crops only. He also decided to\nfollow the steps required for marketing the products through his own outlets. He appointed Ms. Mamta Patel\nas a production manager who decides the exact manner in which the production activities are to be carried\nout. Ms. Mamta Patel also prepared a statement showing the number of workers that will be required in the\nfactory throughout the year. Mr. Rahul Modi informed Ms. Mamta Patel about his sales target for different\nproducts, area-wise for the forthcoming quarter. While working on the production table a penalty of 100\nrupees per day for not wearing the caps, gloves, and apron was announced.\nQuestion to be answered:\nQuoting lines from the above paragraph, outline any four types of plans discussed, and briefly explain them.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plans"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "tip": "Choose four clearly evidenced plans. The 10% profit target is an objective; buying only from organic farmers on credit expresses a purchasing policy; the production manner/procedure and staffing numbers need careful matching to the exact wording; the penalty for not wearing protective items supports a rule. Quote the relevant clause before explaining.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-2A", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "As the leader of the organization, you are inevitably responsible for the direction and development of your\norganization. With this regard, it is important to consistently design a strategic plan for your operations.\nProficiency in several tools and methodologies is necessary to accomplish this task. Using appropriate\nrepresentation and examples, discuss any two strategic tools that you will implement to facilitate the\ndevelopment of your firm.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["swot", "tows"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Internal + helpful = strength. Internal + harmful = weakness.", "External + helpful = opportunity. External + harmful = threat.", "Use the actual case statements. A competitor’s action is external; the firm’s own capability is internal.", "Practice the exercise before checking the source classification.", "SO: use strengths to exploit opportunities.", "ST: use strengths to counter threats.", "WO: address weaknesses by using opportunities.", "WT: minimize weaknesses and threats; the notes connect this to defensive or retrenchment approaches.", "Use the worked examples and the dynamic TOWS discussion to see that conditions can change."], "tip": "Pick two tools actually covered in Module 2, such as SWOT/TOWS and BCG. Draw and label each framework, explain how to interpret it and show how the firm could use it. Do not merely list tool names.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-2B", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in organizing\nwith a suitable representation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["organizing"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-2C", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "Propose a scheme of departmentation for a MNC with a field network of sales all over the country. Discuss\nits merits and limitations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-2C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["departmentation2"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-3A", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "A new team leader in the customer service department is training an employee to handle customer\nescalations independently. Following the Flow of Delegation model, analyze how the team leader would\napply each step in training the employee on handling escalated calls.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "The papers explicitly ask for delegation, but the supplied lecture slides provide principles rather than a complete named four-step Flow of Delegation model. A study scaffold is: specify expected results → assign duties → give suitable authority → establish accountability/review. For escalated calls, define the resolution target, assign the calls, set decision limits and review outcomes. Treat this as a derived scaffold, not a verbatim slide model.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-3B", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze the differences between Human Resource Management (HRM) and Human Resource\nDevelopment (HRD). How do these functions complement each other in achieving long-term organizational\nsuccess?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["hrm"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["HRM includes policies, administration and managing human resources; HRD emphasizes employee development.", "Use the source comparison for scope, objectives, functions and emphasis.", "Integration supports holistic development, organizational performance and aligned people practices.", "The notes distinguish managerial and operational HRM functions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-3C", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyze McGregor's THEORY X AND THEORY Y motivational theory that guides the Manager to develop\nstrategies to motivate his/her employees toward organizational goals. Give corporate examples for the\nsame.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["xy"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["X assumes work is disliked, direction/control is needed and responsibility may be avoided.", "Y assumes work can be natural, people can exercise self-direction and seek responsibility under suitable conditions.", "Management practices should be linked to the assumptions and the situation.", "Use the source comparison for motivation, control and responsibility."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-4A", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "Evaluate three types of recruitment methods commonly used by modern businesses. For each method,\nprovide relevant examples and assess how it aligns with organizational goals and talent needs.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-4B", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "Identify and examine three current trends in Training & Development. For each trend, analyze its potential\nimpact on employee performance and organizational growth, providing examples from recent industry\npractices.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-4C", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "TechNova Corporation is a rapidly growing software development company that has expanded into various\nsectors, including mobile applications, cloud services, and cybersecurity. The company has two main\ndepartments: Product Development and Technical Support.\nThe Product Development team consists of creative software engineers and designers who work\ncollaboratively on innovative projects. Recently, the team has expressed frustration with strict guidelines and\nconstant oversight from their manager. They believe their creativity is stifled, and they could produce better\nresults if given more freedom to explore their ideas. The team flourishes on brainstorming sessions and\nvalues input from all members.\nThe Technical Support department handles customer inquiries and troubleshooting issues. The team works\nin a fast-paced environment, where quick responses are essential to maintaining customer satisfaction. The\nmanager has been directive and emphasizes adherence to scripts and procedures, ensuring all team\nmembers follow the same protocol for handling customer issues.\nBased on the scenarios presented for each department, identify which leadership style would be most\nsuitable for each department and justify your reasoning. Consider the unique needs and dynamics of the\nteams when providing your analysis", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["authoritystyles"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Autocratic: leader retains decision authority and directs activity.", "Democratic/participative: members contribute to decisions.", "Free-rein/delegative: employees have substantial freedom within the arrangement.", "Learn each style’s strengths, limitations and suitable conditions from the source.", "Style choice is influenced by the leader, subordinates and situation; justify the fit to the case."], "tip": "Product Development needs creative participation and room to contribute; democratic/participative leadership is defensible. Technical Support requires rapid, standardized responses, supporting more directive/autocratic behavior. Free-rein may be argued for a sufficiently capable creative team, but explain why the case supports the level of freedom you choose.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-5A", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "How can 'systems approach to staffing' help the HR managers' activities of a manufacturing plant? Provide a\nlabeled sketch to illustrate its main components.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["staffing"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-5B", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "In a technology organization, various barriers can hinder effective communication among team members.\nIdentify and discuss three common barriers to effective communication in the workplace and suggest ways\nto address them.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-5B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["barriers"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Source barriers include lack of planning, unclear assumptions, semantic distortion, poor expression, loss/retention issues and poor listening.", "Also review perception, attitudes, status/power, information overload and other barriers listed in the pages.", "Remedies include clarity, audience awareness, consultation, suitable language and follow-up/feedback.", "Read listening guidance and the separate written-communication checklist."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq1-5C", "paper": "pyq1", "date": "Nov 2024 · Regular", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "TechGear is a mid-sized manufacturer specializing in high-quality consumer electronics, including\nsmartwatches and fitness trackers. The company has recently advertised the launch of a new smartwatch\nmodel that includes advanced health monitoring features. The market response has been overwhelmingly\npositive, leading to a significant increase in orders. The supply chain manager proactively orders additional\ncomponents and materials needed for production. The human resources team hires 25 temporary workers\nand schedules 3 hours of overtime for existing employees to ramp up production capacity. As the launch\ndate of the new smartwatch approaches, the marketing team at TechGear conducts extensive market\nresearch. They project that the demand will exceed the company's planned production capacity (2000 units)\ndue to high pre-orders and promotional activities. After launching the smartwatch, 850 customers provided\nfeedback through surveys and online reviews. While most feedback is positive, some customers report\nissues with battery life and software glitches, which affected the company's goal of 100 % customer\nsatisfaction. The product development team analyzed customer feedback and identified that a software\nupdate is needed to fix the glitches, and there is a need to enhance battery optimization in the next\nproduction run starting in the 1st week of March. The engineering team develops a software patch and\nbegins working on hardware changes for the next iteration of the smartwatch.\nQuestions to be answered:\na) Identify and explain the TWO types of control being practiced by quoting the lines.\nb) Identify and explain TWO critical point standards by quoting the line.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq1-5C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["controltypes", "controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Feedforward anticipates problems before they arise.", "Concurrent control monitors ongoing transformation of inputs into outputs.", "Feedback control uses results and reactions after the output.", "Quote the time/action clue and explain why it fits the selected control.", "Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Advance orders, staffing and overtime in anticipation of demand support feedforward control. Survey results followed by software/battery improvements support feedback control. For standards, distinguish numeric output or manpower measures from time targets and customer-satisfaction goals; quote two explicit targets rather than treating every number as a standard.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-1A", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "Identify and explain the managerial functions performed by a newly joined CEO of State Bank of\nIndia. Explain which of the three managerial skills is the top priority for this position.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["functions"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Levels: top, middle and lower management. Technical, human and conceptual skills have different relative importance at different levels.", "Top managers need a broad conceptual view; lower managers need more technical expertise. Human skills matter throughout.", "Planning selects objectives and actions. Organizing establishes roles and relationships. Staffing fills positions.", "Leading influences people to contribute. Controlling measures performance and corrects deviations."], "tip": "Apply all five functions to the CEO: establish goals; arrange roles; appoint/develop people; lead; monitor results. Explain why conceptual skill is relatively most important at the top level.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-1B", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "\"A business firm's intentions, beyond its legal and economic obligations\". What does this intention\nmean and what are the drivers that ensure these intentions being fulfilled?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["csr"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Review the external-environment diagram on page 69; image content is retained in the source reader.", "The classical view emphasizes economic performance; the socioeconomic view extends responsibility to society.", "CSR drivers in the notes: employees and shareholders; brand equity; ethical marketing and social awareness; environmental consciousness; energy conservation and global warming; responsibility towards government.", "Connect a CSR action to the stakeholder or social issue it addresses."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-1C", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "\"The plans made by the top-level manager will differ from the ones that lower managers make.\"\nElaborate by giving suitable examples for six types of plans.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plans"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-2A", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "Illustrate how Porter's Generic Strategy is incorporated in the cases below.\nCase#1: Maruti Suzuki, India's leading car manufacturer adopts lean manufacturing and co-locates\nsuppliers near its plants to minimize logistics expenses. Its strategic focus on cost-effective R&D\nand an extensive distribution network ensures affordable pricing without compromising quality. This\napproach has secured Maruti Suzuki over 40% of the market share, sustained profitability, and\nstrong customer loyalty in a highly price-sensitive market.\nCase#2: Patanjali Ayurved targets the niche market of health-conscious consumers seeking natural\nand Ayurvedic products. By tightly controlling its supply chain and distribution, the company kept\nprices competitive while ensuring wide availability through exclusive stores and partnerships.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["porter"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Read the growth-strategy slide before the generic-strategy framework.", "Cost leadership pursues a low-cost position; differentiation offers something distinctive valued by buyers.", "Focus concentrates on a narrower segment and may use cost or differentiation.", "The notes warn about being stuck in the middle when a firm fails to achieve a coherent advantage."], "tip": "Maruti’s broad low-cost position supports cost leadership. Patanjali’s stated niche of health-conscious Ayurvedic buyers and competitive pricing supports cost focus in this particular case. Explain both advantage and scope.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-2B", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "EcoEarth, a growing startup in sustainable packaging, recently expanded its operations to cater to\nthe increasing demand for eco-friendly products. To manage this growth, EcoEarth's founders,\nMaya and Arjun, recognized the need to refine the organization's structure. They started by\nanalyzing the core functions their team handles daily, from sourcing materials to customer support.\nThey observed that while the company had strong values and a mission, many tasks overlapped,\ncausing confusion and delays.\nMaya and Arjun decided to bring in an experienced consultant, Priya, to help clarify and streamline\nthe workflow. Priya suggested dividing tasks based on expertise and reorganizing the team's\nworkflow for better coordination. Following her advice, they formed dedicated teams for research\nand development, marketing, customer service, and logistics, each with its own supervisor. These\nsupervisors would have the autonomy to make decisions within their departments but would\ncoordinate major decisions with Maya and Arjun.\nAs the company grew, Maya and Arjun realized that, although they had distributed responsibilities,\nthere was a need to formalize decision-making processes. They worked with Priya to develop a\nhierarchy that balanced authority and responsibility across teams. This also meant setting clear\nboundaries so that every team understood its role and whom to report to, creating accountability\nand smoother communication across departments.\nIdentify and explain the steps taken by EcoEarth in organizing their operations, following the\nsequence of organizing activities. For each identified step, cite specific lines from the case to\nsupport your explanation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["organizing"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line."], "tip": "Follow the case sequence: identify daily tasks; group by expertise into departments; assign supervisors authority; formalize reporting and responsibility. Cite a short line for each organizing step.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-2C", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "In an organization that serves diverse customer groups, explain how customer departmentalization\ncan be used to tailor services and improve responsiveness. Provide an organizational structure that\nillustrates customer departmentalization and discuss its advantages and potential challenges. In an\norganization that serves diverse customer groups, explain how customer departmentalization can\nbe used to tailor services and improve responsiveness. Provide an organizational structure that\nillustrates customer departmentalization and discuss its advantages and potential challenges.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-2C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["departmentation2"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-3A", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "Draw an organizational chart for a company of your choice, showing both line and staff functions.\nThen, describe how each line and staff function contribute to the company's goals. Provide\nexamples of situations where line and staff departments would need to collaborate to solve a\nspecific organizational problem.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-3B", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "You are an executive at the management level of a reputable firm. You are assigned with a critical\ntask to :\n\"Prepare a comprehensive job description and job specification for a Graduate Engineer Trainee for\nthe position in an R&D department\".", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["jobanalysis"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Job analysis identifies duties, skills and knowledge required.", "Job description describes the job: work, duties and responsibilities.", "Job specification describes the person requirements: qualifications, knowledge, skills and other requirements.", "When asked to draft both, use two clearly separate headings for the same position."], "tip": "Use two headings for one entry-level role. Job description: title, department/reporting, purpose and duties. Job specification: education, knowledge, skills and relevant personal/physical requirements where the notes support them. Make requirements appropriate to a fresher; do not invent mandatory industry certifications.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-3C", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Innovatech Solutions, a growing tech company, is experiencing productivity and engagement\nchallenges across its departments. The CEO, M J Nair, wants to improve motivation but is unsure\nhow to approach two very different teams:\nTeam#1: Innovation Team: This group of developers, designers, and project managers tackles\ncomplex, creative projects. They perform well with flexibility and minimal supervision but have\nrecently shown frustration with unclear project goals and a lack of growth opportunities, leading to\nscattered productivity.\nTeam#2: Support Team: This team handles structured, repetitive tasks like data processing and\ncustomer support. They've shown inconsistent performance and low engagement, often expressing\ndissatisfaction with the routine nature of their work.\nQuestion to be answered: Given the situation, advise the CEO on which Motivational theory he\nneeds to apply to his two teams to meet the organizational goals and why. Justify your answer.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["xy", "herzberg"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["X assumes work is disliked, direction/control is needed and responsibility may be avoided.", "Y assumes work can be natural, people can exercise self-direction and seek responsibility under suitable conditions.", "Management practices should be linked to the assumptions and the situation.", "Use the source comparison for motivation, control and responsibility.", "Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "tip": "The prompt does not name one uniquely required theory. Theory Y supports autonomy for the innovation team, but its unclear goals require better direction. Herzberg supports growth/recognition and more satisfying work for both groups. Justify your chosen theory from evidence rather than declaring the repetitive-work team inherently lazy.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-4A", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "Describe the 10-step recruitment process for finding the right candidate for a job.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["recruitment"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["1 Hiring needs; 2 job analysis/description; 3 sourcing; 4 screening/shortlisting; 5 interviews.", "6 Assessments/tests; 7 background checks; 8 decision; 9 offer/negotiation; 10 onboarding.", "Each step should follow logically: understand the role, attract candidates, assess suitability and bring the chosen person into the organization."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-4B", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "As your organization's human resource manager, you are responsible for your employees' training\nand development. Hence, discuss the processes of training and development. Also, shed light on\ncurrent trends in training and development through suitable examples and/or representations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-4C", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain, with a neat sketch, Likert's Four Systems of Managing and examine the effect of each\nsystem on employee motivation and productivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["likert"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["1 Exploitative-authoritative: low trust, centralized decisions and coercive direction.", "2 Benevolent-authoritative: paternal confidence and rewards, while control stays largely at the top.", "3 Consultative: managers seek input but retain important decisions.", "4 Participative: substantial trust, group participation and shared involvement.", "Identify systems from evidence about trust, motivation, communication and decisions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-5A", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Using a clear diagram, describe the Systems Approach to Staffing and explain its significance in the\nrecruitment/selection process.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["staffing"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-5B", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Summarize the different stages of group development. What distinguishes a committee, group, and\nteam from one another?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-5B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["groups", "teams"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Forming: getting acquainted. Storming: conflict and clarification of objectives. Norming: agreed rules. Performing: task execution.", "Read committee functions/formality, reasons for use and disadvantages.", "Reasons include group judgment, representation and coordination; costs and delays are among the limitations.", "Groups can influence behavior, attitudes, values, motivation and relationships.", "Read the team definition and the distinctions from groups and committees.", "The source discusses recommendations, decision-making and operational roles.", "Team building requires a worthwhile purpose, suitable skills, rules of behavior and commitment.", "Study the team types in the final slide of this range."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq2-5C", "paper": "pyq2", "date": "Dec 2024 · Makeup", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "With the help of a block diagram, list the 8 steps involved in the controlling process and discuss the\nsteps with the help of a common example.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq2-5C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-1A", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "In a leading software firm in Udupi, employees are assigned roles based on their expertise,\nensuring that developers, designers, and analysts focus on specialized tasks. This structured\napproach has led to faster project turnaround and improved software quality. The company\nmaintains a professional work environment where expectations around deadlines and performance\nare clearly communicated and upheld. While high standards are enforced, leadership values long-\nterm employee retention, offering career development programs and mentorship to reduce turnover.\nThe firm also fosters a culture where employees are encouraged to take ownership of their ideas-\nthose who propose workflow improvements or innovative product features are given opportunities to\nlead their implementation.\nIdentify and analyze the Henry Fayol's management principles by citing the relevant lines\nfrom the case study to support your explanation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["fayol"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Work and authority: division of labor; authority and responsibility; unity of command; unity of direction.", "Fair treatment and organization: equity; order; discipline; initiative; fair remuneration.", "Continuity and coordination: stability of tenure; scalar chain; subordination of individual interest to general interest; esprit de corps; centralization and decentralization.", "Unity of command means one boss for an employee. Unity of direction means one head and one plan for activities with a common objective.", "The order discussion includes 5S: sort, set in order, shine, standardize and sustain. Check the source table for the Japanese terms and its additional safety column.", "Centralization concerns where decision authority is concentrated. Its degree depends on the situation."], "tip": "Specialized roles → division of labor; clear standards/deadlines → discipline; retention and development → stability of tenure; encouraging employees to own improvements → initiative. Quote evidence and explain each link.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-1B", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Critically analyze the three fundamental moral theories in normative ethics and their role in fostering\nethical decision-making within organizations.\nUsing real-world examples demonstrate how companies can institutionalize ethics through\nthese frameworks.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["ethics"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Utilitarian theory evaluates consequences: greatest good for the greatest number.", "Rights theory protects basic rights such as conscience, speech and due process.", "Justice theory emphasizes fairness, equity and impartiality.", "Institutionalizing ethics means embedding ethical expectations in organizational practices. Read the source examples and measures, not just the three definitions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-1C", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "As the Supply Manager of a leading organization, you play a critical role in ensuring efficient\ninventory management to support the company's operational and financial goals.\nTo optimize supply chain performance in the upcoming financial year, you are required to\ndevelop an 8-STEP PLANNING PROCESS for inventory management that enhances\nefficiency, cost-effectiveness, and resource utilization.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["planningsteps"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["1 Be aware of opportunities; 2 set objectives; 3 consider planning premises; 4 identify alternatives.", "5 Compare alternatives against goals; 6 choose an alternative; 7 formulate supporting plans; 8 numberize the plans through budgets.", "In an application answer, give each step an action in the same business case."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-2A", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "Categorize the following Nestlé products into the BCG matrix with a brief explanation of the matrix.\nAlso create strategies for each of these Nestlé products based on the BCG Matrix.\nNescafé: Nestlé dominates the instant coffee segment, while its ready-to-drink cold coffee is\ngaining popularity among young consumers. With the market still growing, continued investment in\nflavors, packaging, and marketing will sustain its dominance.\nNestlé A+ Curd Alternatives: The plant-based dairy market in India is expanding due to health\ntrends and lactose intolerance. Nestlé is still establishing itself in this space, facing competition from\nbrands like Epigamia and Sofit.\nMilkmaid: While Milkmaid has strong brand nostalgia, its market is shrinking due to changing\nconsumer preferences and declining home baking trends. Nestlé may reduce investments and\nfocus on seasonal and niche demand.\nMaggi: A market leader in instant noodles and seasonings, Maggi enjoys strong consumer loyalty\ndespite competition from Yippee and Wai Wai. The mature market limits growth, but it remains\nhighly profitable.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-2A-1.webp", "assets/questions/pyq3-2A-2.webp"], "pages": [1, 2], "topics": ["bcg"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["High share + high growth = star; low share + high growth = question mark.", "High share + low growth = cash cow; low share + low growth = dog.", "Use the category to justify investment, cash generation or a selective/defensive response; read the source’s strategies and applications.", "The product life cycle and portfolio steps provide context.", "Limitations: two dimensions simplify a complex business, and interdependencies and other factors may be missed."], "tip": "Nescafé: star (high share/high growth); plant-based A+ alternatives: question mark (growth but still establishing share); Maggi: cash cow (high share/mature market). Milkmaid has low/declining growth, but the case does not explicitly give relative share: dog is a possible intended answer only if low share is assumed. State that assumption; high share would instead support cash cow. Tie strategy to both axes.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-2B", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "IDENTIFY the URWICK's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief.\n(i) Vikram entrusted key project decisions to senior team leaders while retaining overall\naccountability. By assigning authority with clear responsibilities, he empowered his team to take\nownership of tasks, leading to faster decision-making and improved efficiency.\n(ii) Priya restructured the workforce by assigning tasks based on employees' expertise. This division\nof labor improved efficiency, as each team member focused on specific functions they were skilled\nat, reducing errors and increasing productivity.\n(iii) Rohan ensured that all departments aligned their project goals with the company's vision. By\nclearly defining organizational priorities, he created a unified direction that helped teams work\ntoward common objectives without conflicts.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-2B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "(i) Delegation, with authority/responsibility balance; (ii) specialization; (iii) unity of objectives. Use the exact scenario evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-2C", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "This kind of organization occurs frequently in construction (e.g., building bridges), in aerospace\n(e.g., designing and launching satellite weather), in marketing (e.g., an advertising campaign for a\nmajor new product), in the installation of an electronic data processing system, or in management\nconsulting firms where experts from different fields work together.\nIDENTIFY this departmentation structure and explain it with a neat diagram mentioning the\npros and cons.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-2C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["matrix"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Specialists retain functional connections while contributing to projects.", "The structure permits expertise to be shared across projects but requires coordination of authority.", "Study the diagram before trying the final identification exercise.", "Distinguish a matrix’s intersecting dimensions from a simple product hierarchy."], "tip": "The combination of experts from different functions around a project supports matrix departmentation. Draw functional and project relationships and discuss both pooled expertise and coordination/authority difficulties.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-3A", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze the four features of line organization with the help of a neat block diagram representing a\nstartup in FMCG product.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-3B", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "A leading company in the FMCG sector needs to fill a team leader position quickly. Instead of hiring\nexternally, they decide to promote an experienced team member.\nDiscuss why opting for an internal source of recruitment is beneficial in this scenario.\nSupport your answer with relevant reasoning.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Promotion uses a known employee’s experience, supports continuity and can motivate internal career progression. Explain why these fit the urgent team-leader vacancy; do not simply list every recruitment source.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-3C", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyze the corporate approach to employee motivation which aligns with Herzberg's Two Factor\nTheory.\nIdentify specific corporate strategies that fulfill each factor and evaluate their impact on\nemployee retention and performance.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["herzberg"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-4A", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "Describe the 10-step recruitment process for finding the right candidate for a job.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["recruitment"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["1 Hiring needs; 2 job analysis/description; 3 sourcing; 4 screening/shortlisting; 5 interviews.", "6 Assessments/tests; 7 background checks; 8 decision; 9 offer/negotiation; 10 onboarding.", "Each step should follow logically: understand the role, attract candidates, assess suitability and bring the chosen person into the organization."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-4B", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "As your organization's human resource manager, you are responsible for your employees' training\nand development. Hence, discuss the processes of training and development. Also, shed light on\ncurrent trends in training and development through suitable examples and/or representations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-4C", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain, with a neat sketch, Likert's Four Systems of Managing and examine the effect of each\nsystem on employee motivation and productivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["likert"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["1 Exploitative-authoritative: low trust, centralized decisions and coercive direction.", "2 Benevolent-authoritative: paternal confidence and rewards, while control stays largely at the top.", "3 Consultative: managers seek input but retain important decisions.", "4 Participative: substantial trust, group participation and shared involvement.", "Identify systems from evidence about trust, motivation, communication and decisions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-5A", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Using a clear diagram, describe the Systems Approach to Staffing and explain its significance in the\nrecruitment/selection process.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["staffing"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-5B", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Summarize the different stages of group development. What distinguishes a committee, group, and\nteam from one another?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-5B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["groups", "teams"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Forming: getting acquainted. Storming: conflict and clarification of objectives. Norming: agreed rules. Performing: task execution.", "Read committee functions/formality, reasons for use and disadvantages.", "Reasons include group judgment, representation and coordination; costs and delays are among the limitations.", "Groups can influence behavior, attitudes, values, motivation and relationships.", "Read the team definition and the distinctions from groups and committees.", "The source discusses recommendations, decision-making and operational roles.", "Team building requires a worthwhile purpose, suitable skills, rules of behavior and commitment.", "Study the team types in the final slide of this range."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq3-5C", "paper": "pyq3", "date": "May 2025 · Regular", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "With the help of a block diagram, list the 8 steps involved in the controlling process and discuss the\nsteps with the help of a common example.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq3-5C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-1A", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "With reference to an organization, sketch and justify how managerial functions of management and\ntheir functions work in the systems approach.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["systems"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Inputs include people, capital, information and resources.", "Managerial functions coordinate the transformation process.", "Outputs and feedback reconnect the organization with its environment.", "Sub-systems must cooperate; optimizing one department alone can harm the whole."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-1B", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Compare and contrast the managerial practices in Japan and the USA, focusing on cultural\ninfluences and organizational behaviour.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["international"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Read the cultural dimensions and country examples in the source before using the comparison tables.", "Japan: consensus, group harmony, long-term employment and extensive internal development feature in the notes.", "USA: individual achievement, direct communication, faster individual decisions and performance emphasis feature in the comparison.", "Korea: hierarchy, loyalty, seniority and respect for authority feature in the comparison.", "The notes also discuss German, Austrian, Australian, Italian and British management. Compare the same basis across countries rather than writing disconnected lists."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-1C", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "After working as a software developer for a fintech startup, Miss Cleopatra decided to start her own\ncompany specializing in cybersecurity solutions for small and medium-sized businesses. She set an\ninitial goal of acquiring 15 clients in the first quarter and achieving a 25% return on investment by\nthe end of the year. Miss Cleopatra chose to hire independent contractors with expertise in\ncybersecurity and negotiated flexible payment terms based on project completion. She appointed\nDr. Banrakas as the project manager to create a workflow, assign tasks, and monitor progress.\nCleopatra developed a marketing strategy targeting small business owners through social media\nand tech conferences, while Banrakas set quality benchmarks for project deliverables. They also\nintroduced a late submission penalty of ₹500/day for team members who missed project deadlines.\nQuestion to be answered:\nQuoting lines from the above paragraph, outline any four types of plans discussed and briefly\nexplain them.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plans"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-2A", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "As the leader of the organization, you are inevitably responsible for the direction and development\nof your organization. With this regard, it is important to consistently design a strategic plan for your\noperations. Proficiency in several tools and methodologies is necessary to accomplish this task.\nUsing appropriate representation and examples, discuss any two strategic tools that you will\nimplement to facilitate the development of your firm.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["swot", "tows"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Internal + helpful = strength. Internal + harmful = weakness.", "External + helpful = opportunity. External + harmful = threat.", "Use the actual case statements. A competitor’s action is external; the firm’s own capability is internal.", "Practice the exercise before checking the source classification.", "SO: use strengths to exploit opportunities.", "ST: use strengths to counter threats.", "WO: address weaknesses by using opportunities.", "WT: minimize weaknesses and threats; the notes connect this to defensive or retrenchment approaches.", "Use the worked examples and the dynamic TOWS discussion to see that conditions can change."], "tip": "Pick two tools actually covered in Module 2, such as SWOT/TOWS and BCG. Draw and label each framework, explain how to interpret it and show how the firm could use it. Do not merely list tool names.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-2B", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in\norganizing with a suitable representation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["organizing"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-2C", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "Propose a scheme of departmentation for an MNC with a field network of sales all over the country.\nDiscuss its merits and limitations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-2C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["departmentation2"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-3A", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze the four features of line and staff organization with the help of a neat block diagram applied\nto a large multi-specialty hospital.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-3B", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "You have been appointed as the HR manager of a company planning to recruit freshers.\nDraft a Job Description and a Job Specification for the entry-level position in your own\nrespective domains (eg. Mechanical, Aeronautical, Chemical etc)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["jobanalysis"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Job analysis identifies duties, skills and knowledge required.", "Job description describes the job: work, duties and responsibilities.", "Job specification describes the person requirements: qualifications, knowledge, skills and other requirements.", "When asked to draft both, use two clearly separate headings for the same position."], "tip": "Use two headings for one entry-level role. Job description: title, department/reporting, purpose and duties. Job specification: education, knowledge, skills and relevant personal/physical requirements where the notes support them. Make requirements appropriate to a fresher; do not invent mandatory industry certifications.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-3C", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyze the corporate approach to employee motivation which aligns with McGregor's Theory X\nAnd Theory Y.\nIdentify specific corporate strategies that fulfill each factor and evaluate their impact on\nemployee retention and performance.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["xy"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["X assumes work is disliked, direction/control is needed and responsibility may be avoided.", "Y assumes work can be natural, people can exercise self-direction and seek responsibility under suitable conditions.", "Management practices should be linked to the assumptions and the situation.", "Use the source comparison for motivation, control and responsibility."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-4A", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "As a Talent Acquisition Manager in today's competitive job market, you are facing multiple\nrecruitment challenges. Identify three major challenges and explain how you would address\neach of them to ensure an effective and inclusive hiring process.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-4B", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "Differentiate between a Preliminary Interview and a Core Interview in the recruitment process.\nExplain the purpose of each stage in the recruitment process.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["selection"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Initial/preliminary interview → application form → reference checks → employment tests → interview → selection decision.", "Read the test classifications and human-characteristic measures on pages 27–29.", "The interview slide covers several formats; use the format’s purpose and features to distinguish them.", "A decision should integrate evidence rather than rely on a single test or impression."], "tip": "Preliminary interview screens basic suitability; a core interview investigates suitability in greater depth. Tie the contrast to the initial interview and interview stages in the selection sequence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-4C", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "PSN Corporation, a leading player in the technology sector, operates across multiple regions,\nserving diverse markets. As the company was a family business, most of the employees were\nfriends and relatives. A few were old folks too. This made the top management prioritize employee\nwell-being over productivity, fostering a relaxed and friendly work environment. Over time,\nmanagers adopted a hands-off approach, avoiding difficult decisions and failing to set clear\nexpectations, leading to a further decline in performance. Employees lacked direction, motivation,\nand accountability, causing the earlier deteriorating productivity to stagnate state.\nAfter running the business with loads of financial burdens, top management finally decided to let go\nof the non-performing employees and recruit more skilled based personnel. The new managers\nwere very target-oriented, and they focused solely on developing efficient operations, building up\nproduction capacities while displaying little concern for employee well-being. This gave them better\nreturns in production but choked creativity and teamwork, leading to dissatisfaction among several\nteam members and leading to higher attrition rates. Top management was not happy to invest in\ntraining employees who would leave them in a year or so. So, they had to come up with a better\nmanagement technique. They decided to demonstrate unwavering dedication to both people and\nproduction, fostering a collaborative work environment where individual needs align with\norganizational goals. This approach results in high-performance teams and innovative solutions. It\nalso was appreciated by the employees with high aspiration as this style gave them opportunities\nand career development.\nIDENTIFY and EXPLAIN the 4 leadership styles used by CITING the lines. Shed some light on\nthe pros and cons associated with each identified style.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-4C-2.webp", "assets/questions/pyq4-4C-3.webp"], "pages": [2, 3], "topics": ["grid"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["1,1 impoverished: low concern for both.", "1,9 country club: high people, low production.", "9,1 task/authority-compliance: high production, low people.", "5,5 middle-of-the-road: moderate concern for both; 9,9 team: high concern for both.", "Practice placing each style on the axes and identifying it from cases."], "tip": "People over production → country club (1,9); hands-off with little direction or concern → impoverished (1,1); production with little concern for people → authority/task (9,1); high commitment to both → team (9,9). Use the case sequence and discuss pros/cons of each.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-5A", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Differentiate between orientation and induction programs conducted by organizations, highlighting\nthe objective and content.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-5A-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["trainingmethods"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["On the job: apprenticeship programs, coaching, internship training and job rotation.", "Off the job: conferences, films, case study, computer modelling, vestibule training and programmed instruction.", "Read the source’s induction and orientation definitions and comparison.", "Sensitivity training develops awareness of interpersonal behavior and its effect on others."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-5B", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Using well-structured block diagrams, elaborate on the concept of corporate communication and\nexplain its process in a business environment.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-5B-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["communication"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Sender forms a thought and encodes a message; it is transmitted, received, decoded and understood; feedback closes the loop. Noise can interfere.", "Formal and informal; downward and upward; horizontal/crosswise and diagonal; written, oral and non-verbal.", "Each classification describes a different aspect: official status, direction or medium.", "Compare advantages and limitations of written and oral communication using the source."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq4-5C", "paper": "pyq4", "date": "Jun 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "SmartGadgets Inc., a leading manufacturer of wireless earbuds and smart home devices, learns\nabout an impending global semiconductor shortage. To secure the company's production capacity,\nthe manager negotiates long-term contracts with multiple-chip suppliers across different regions,\nreducing dependence on a single source. Additionally, they increase the semiconductor inventory\nby 75%, ensuring buffer stock to sustain production. During production, real-time monitoring detects\nthat one of the assembly lines is producing a higher-than-usual number of defective earbuds. The\nproduction manager immediately halts the faulty line, identifies a calibration issue in the\nsemiconductor component attachment machine, and rectifies the problem before its escalation.\nIDENTIFY and EXPLAIN the TYPE of CONTROL(s) used by SmartGadgets Inc by QUOTING\nTHE LINES from the case. What is the significance of these control types?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq4-5C-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["controltypes", "controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Feedforward anticipates problems before they arise.", "Concurrent control monitors ongoing transformation of inputs into outputs.", "Feedback control uses results and reactions after the output.", "Quote the time/action clue and explain why it fits the selected control.", "Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Read the full image and classify actions by timing: anticipating shortages is feedforward; intervention during activity is concurrent if supported by a specific action; using customer outcomes is feedback. The question also asks standards: identify actual performance yardsticks rather than any descriptive number.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-1A", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "What are the principles of Henri Fayol? Explain any four of its importance in the system approach of\nmanagement.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["fayol"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Work and authority: division of labor; authority and responsibility; unity of command; unity of direction.", "Fair treatment and organization: equity; order; discipline; initiative; fair remuneration.", "Continuity and coordination: stability of tenure; scalar chain; subordination of individual interest to general interest; esprit de corps; centralization and decentralization.", "Unity of command means one boss for an employee. Unity of direction means one head and one plan for activities with a common objective.", "The order discussion includes 5S: sort, set in order, shine, standardize and sustain. Check the source table for the Japanese terms and its additional safety column.", "Centralization concerns where decision authority is concentrated. Its degree depends on the situation."], "tip": "Choose four of Fayol’s principles and explain their contribution to coordinated enterprise operation. Do not confuse this with Mintzberg’s ten roles.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-1B", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "What is Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)? Explain the role of drivers of CSR in detail.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["csr"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Review the external-environment diagram on page 69; image content is retained in the source reader.", "The classical view emphasizes economic performance; the socioeconomic view extends responsibility to society.", "CSR drivers in the notes: employees and shareholders; brand equity; ethical marketing and social awareness; environmental consciousness; energy conservation and global warming; responsibility towards government.", "Connect a CSR action to the stakeholder or social issue it addresses."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-1C", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "The Samsung group is a large Korean conglomerate that focuses on electronics and financial\nservices. In the past, many Korean companies encountered difficulties because they diversified into\nunrelated fields. In its new approach to management, Samsung tries to avoid the pitfalls of other\ncompanies. At the turn of the century, Samsung initiated a policy designed to make the company a\nleader in its field, competing against firms such as Sony of Japan. The company is driven by its\nvalues and its philosophy. Samsung realizes the importance of its people and the use of latest\ntechnologies for achieving success in the marketplace. At the same time, it is aware of the\nresponsibility to contribute to society not only in Korea, but also worldwide. Chairman Lee Kun-Hee\nlaid the groundwork for a new approach to management that considers defects a crime. Quality,\nsuperior products and excellent customer service are considered key factors for success in the very\ncompetitive environment that has been dominated by Japanese firms. With its \"digital management\"\napproach, Samsung attempts to exploit the opportunities created by the information age\ntechnologies.\nSamsung's values, philosophy and policies as well as its goal of becoming a global leader in its field\nhave attracted many Korean students. Samsung will use the talents of these young people, to\nsuccessfully compete in the global environment.\nAnalyse this case in terms of Porter's Generic strategies.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["porter"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Read the growth-strategy slide before the generic-strategy framework.", "Cost leadership pursues a low-cost position; differentiation offers something distinctive valued by buyers.", "Focus concentrates on a narrower segment and may use cost or differentiation.", "The notes warn about being stuck in the middle when a firm fails to achieve a coherent advantage."], "tip": "Superior quality, customer service, technology and distinctive products support differentiation in the case. The narrative does not demonstrate a low-cost or narrow-market position. Connect each differentiating feature to competitive advantage.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-2A", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "What are the various types of plans used in an organization? Briefly describe any four of them.\n(4)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plans"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-2B", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in\norganizing with a suitable representation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["organizing"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-2C", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "Propose a scheme of departmentation for an MNC with a field network of sales all over the country.\nDiscuss its merits and limitations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-2C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["departmentation2"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-3A", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "As the founder of a small manufacturing company producing steel bolts, you are considering\nadopting a Line Organizational structure. Apply the features of a line organization to explain how\nauthority, communication, and decision-making would function in your company to ensure\noperational efficiency and draw the organizational chart.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-3A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-3B", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the HR manager at an esteemed Nestlé (Food and beverages) with its production factory in\nBengaluru. You need to create a comprehensive job specification on 6 factors for a Graduate\nEngineer Trainee (GET) reporting to the Production & Maintenance Manager (Technical) at their\nBengaluru factory.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-3B-1.webp", "assets/questions/pyq5-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [1, 2], "topics": ["jobanalysis"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Job analysis identifies duties, skills and knowledge required.", "Job description describes the job: work, duties and responsibilities.", "Job specification describes the person requirements: qualifications, knowledge, skills and other requirements.", "When asked to draft both, use two clearly separate headings for the same position."], "tip": "The task asks for a job specification, not a full job description. Give six suitable candidate requirements for a GET, drawing on the categories in the job-analysis slides; connect them to production work and the stated reporting context.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-3C", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Lenskart, India's leading eyewear retailer, wants to understand why some of its store employees\nreport high job satisfaction while others feel disengaged despite similar working conditions. As an\nHR analyst, analyze 4 factors each for how Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory can help Lenskart, and\nexplain how addressing these factors can improve overall employee morale and productivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["herzberg"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "tip": "Separate four hygiene/context factors from four motivators/job-content factors. Explain how good conditions can coexist with disengagement if recognition, achievement, responsibility or growth are absent.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-4A", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the Chief Human Resources Officer (CHRO) for Tata Motors - one of India's largest\nautomotive manufacturers, and your task is to pick 3 each of the best internal sources and external\nsources for the next recruitment drive. You need to justify your selections to the Board of Directors.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-4B", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the founder of a 'Tech Startup' that leverages technology to develop innovative products or\nservices, often aiming for rapid growth and scalability. You would be recruiting new employees\nshortly and thus researching the Current Trends. Elaborate on 3 current trends in Training &\nDevelopment that you will be considering for your startup.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-4C", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Quote the lines from the Tata Consultancy Services (TCS) Project Scenarios to identify and explain\nthe different Leadership Styles incorporated based on use of authority.\nIn the planning phase of a new software solution, the same manager conducts brainstorming\nsessions, encourages team members to share ideas, and collectively decides on the technology\nstack and workflow.\nOnce the project enters the development stage, experienced developers are given autonomy to\nchoose coding practices and manage their schedules, with minimal supervision, trusting their\nexpertise.\nDuring a critical client deadline, the project manager takes full control, assigns tasks without\ndiscussion, and enforces strict timelines to ensure timely delivery and facilitate quick decisions\nwithout team input.\nIn the testing phase, senior QA engineers are given full freedom to choose tools, create test cases,\nand schedule their work, with minimal managerial intervention.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["authoritystyles"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Autocratic: leader retains decision authority and directs activity.", "Democratic/participative: members contribute to decisions.", "Free-rein/delegative: employees have substantial freedom within the arrangement.", "Learn each style’s strengths, limitations and suitable conditions from the source.", "Style choice is influenced by the leader, subordinates and situation; justify the fit to the case."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-5A", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "As the HR manager at PhonePe, a leading digital payments and financial services platform in India,\napply your understanding of induction (3 factors) and orientation (3 factors) to design a program for\nnewly recruited employees. Explain how your program will help newcomers quickly adapt to the\ncompany's culture, policies, and job responsibilities while enhancing their engagement and\nproductivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["trainingmethods"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["On the job: apprenticeship programs, coaching, internship training and job rotation.", "Off the job: conferences, films, case study, computer modelling, vestibule training and programmed instruction.", "Read the source’s induction and orientation definitions and comparison.", "Sensitivity training develops awareness of interpersonal behavior and its effect on others."], "tip": "Apply the source’s induction/orientation distinction and supply three factors for each. Make the introduction to the organization and familiarization with work concrete for newly recruited staff.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-5B", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Apply your understanding of the communication process to explain how information flows in an\norganization. Illustrate your answer with a neat block diagram.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-5B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["communication"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Sender forms a thought and encodes a message; it is transmitted, received, decoded and understood; feedback closes the loop. Noise can interfere.", "Formal and informal; downward and upward; horizontal/crosswise and diagonal; written, oral and non-verbal.", "Each classification describes a different aspect: official status, direction or medium.", "Compare advantages and limitations of written and oral communication using the source."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq5-5C", "paper": "pyq5", "date": "Nov 2025 · Regular", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyze how four types of standards can be applied during the managerial function - control in a\ncompany like Micromax (an Indian consumer electronics company known for affordable\nsmartphones and mobile devices) to evaluate performance and improve operational efficiency.\nExplain the impact of each standard on decision-making and organizational outcomes for Micromax.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq5-5C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Choose four standard categories from the notes and pair each with an appropriate measure for the stated manufacturer: physical quantity, technical specification, monetary value, managerial performance or time. Explain how comparison would reveal a deviation.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-1A", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain the three levels of management. Examine the interrelationship between the three levels of\nmanagement and the varying importance of managerial skills at each level. Illustrate your answer\nwith suitable corporate examples for each level and skill.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["functions"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Levels: top, middle and lower management. Technical, human and conceptual skills have different relative importance at different levels.", "Top managers need a broad conceptual view; lower managers need more technical expertise. Human skills matter throughout.", "Planning selects objectives and actions. Organizing establishes roles and relationships. Staffing fills positions.", "Leading influences people to contribute. Controlling measures performance and corrects deviations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-1B", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze three cultural dimensions that can influence management practices and employee behavior\nin a multinational company operating across diverse countries. Give one example for each\ndimension.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["international"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Read the cultural dimensions and country examples in the source before using the comparison tables.", "Japan: consensus, group harmony, long-term employment and extensive internal development feature in the notes.", "USA: individual achievement, direct communication, faster individual decisions and performance emphasis feature in the comparison.", "Korea: hierarchy, loyalty, seniority and respect for authority feature in the comparison.", "The notes also discuss German, Austrian, Australian, Italian and British management. Compare the same basis across countries rather than writing disconnected lists."], "tip": "Select three cultural dimensions actually described on Module 1 pages 93–98. For each, explain its effect on management behavior and use a clearly framed illustration based on the contrast; avoid unsupported country stereotypes.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-1C", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "Given the following corporate examples, apply your knowledge of types of plans to identify and\njustify which type each scenario represents.\ni) Zomato implements a hyper-local delivery plan focusing on tier-2 and tier-3 cities to increase its\ncustomer base.\nii) Infosys enforces a work-from-home plan to ensure employee safety and operational continuity\nduring crises.\niii) HDFC Life follows a step-by-step claims approval plan to process customer insurance claims\nefficiently.\niv) Flipkart restricts sellers from listing counterfeit products as per its strict compliance.\nv) Reliance Foundation runs the \"Digital Udaan\" plan to provide rural youth with digital literacy\ntraining.\nvi) Asian Paints allocates an annual marketing budget to support nationwide promotional\ncampaigns and product launches.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plans"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Purpose/mission/vision expresses the basic reason or direction; objectives state the ends; strategy gives the broad route.", "Policy guides decisions; procedure gives a sequence; rule prescribes an action or prohibition without discretion.", "Program combines activities and supporting arrangements; budget expresses expected results in numbers.", "Planning asks what, why, where, when, who and how, and brings direction, coordination and a basis for control."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-2A", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "Zomato a comprehensive online platform for food delivery, restaurant discovery, and dining\nexperiences across India and several international markets. Perform a SWOT analysis for Zomato\nand create two TOWS strategies to capitalize on the opportunities.\ni) Strong brand recognition and a large, loyal customer base across India.\nii) High operational costs and dependence on third-party delivery personnel.\niii) Expansion into tier-2 and tier-3 cities with growing digital penetration.\niv) Intense competition from Swiggy, Blinkit, and other hyper-local delivery startups.\nv) Wide range of services including food delivery, dining-out, and subscription-based offerings.\nvi) Limited profitability despite high revenue due to aggressive discount strategies.\nvii) Diversification into grocery delivery, cloud kitchens, and international markets.\nviii) Regulatory changes related to labor laws and food safety compliance.\nix) Advanced technology platform for real-time tracking and efficient logistics management.\nx) Vulnerability to service quality issues and customer complaints affecting brand image.\nxi) Leveraging AI and data analytics to personalize customer experience and improve efficiency.\nxii) Economic downturns reducing consumer spending on discretionary dining and deliveries.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-2A-1.webp", "assets/questions/pyq6-2A-2.webp"], "pages": [1, 2], "topics": ["swot", "tows"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Internal + helpful = strength. Internal + harmful = weakness.", "External + helpful = opportunity. External + harmful = threat.", "Use the actual case statements. A competitor’s action is external; the firm’s own capability is internal.", "Practice the exercise before checking the source classification.", "SO: use strengths to exploit opportunities.", "ST: use strengths to counter threats.", "WO: address weaknesses by using opportunities.", "WT: minimize weaknesses and threats; the notes connect this to defensive or retrenchment approaches.", "Use the worked examples and the dynamic TOWS discussion to see that conditions can change."], "tip": "Classify every listed factor by internal/external and helpful/harmful. Then build two actual TOWS strategies combining one internal and one external factor. A SWOT list without the two strategies leaves the question incomplete.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-2B", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the steps involved in organizing. Compare on four grounds between a startup and an\nestablished multinational company in terms of formal and informal structure.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-2B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["organizing", "formal"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Identify activities; organize/group activities; classify or assign authority; build relationships between authority and responsibility.", "Structure clarifies who does what and who reports to whom.", "For a case answer, identify the step and attach the supporting action or quoted line.", "Formal structure defines roles, reporting and authority.", "Informal organization emerges through shared interests and relationships, illustrated by clubs and social groups.", "The two coexist; an informal connection is not automatically an official reporting line.", "Use the comparison table and overlaid organization diagrams."], "tip": "Give the organizing sequence, then a four-basis formal/informal comparison. A startup is not necessarily wholly informal, and an MNC still has informal relationships.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-2C", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "As the HR manager of a mid-sized manufacturing company, determine an appropriate span of\ncontrol for the production managers using the four factors. Explain how you would apply these\nfactors to decide the number of direct reports for each manager to ensure effective supervision and\nminimize micromanagement.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-2C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["span"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["A narrow span tends to create more management levels; a wide span tends to create fewer levels.", "Factors include subordinate training, delegation, planning, rate of change, communication techniques, activity, organization type and level.", "Wide spans can reduce bureaucracy but overload managers; narrow spans allow closer contact but add cost and levels.", "Read the source comparison for the full advantages, limitations and influencing factors."], "tip": "Choose four factors from the span-of-control slides. Explain how each increases or reduces supervision needs. The question asks for reasoning; the notes do not supply one universally correct number of direct reports.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-3A", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "Imagine you are the founder of a large automobile firm planning to introduce a Line and Staff\nOrganizational structure to improve technical decision-making and managerial control. Demonstrate\nhow the roles of line executives and staff specialists would operate in your firm to enhance\nefficiency and specialization without creating conflicts of authority. Also draw an organizational\nchart.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-3B", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "As the HR Manager of a mid-sized IT company, apply the benefits of integrating HRM and HRD\npractices to design a strategy that improves employee productivity and retention.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["hrm"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["HRM includes policies, administration and managing human resources; HRD emphasizes employee development.", "Use the source comparison for scope, objectives, functions and emphasis.", "Integration supports holistic development, organizational performance and aligned people practices.", "The notes distinguish managerial and operational HRM functions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-3C", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Nykaa, India's leading beauty and lifestyle retailer, has approached you to suggest them on how\nthey can use the Maslow's 'Hierarchy of Needs' to motivate employees at different career stages.\nYou need to make a report, explaining Nykaa how fulfilling each 'level-of-need' influences employee\nengagement, performance, and retention within the organization.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["maslow"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Maslow: physiological → safety → social → esteem → self-actualization.", "Physiological: basic living needs. Safety: security. Social: belonging.", "Esteem: respect and recognition. Self-actualization: realizing potential.", "In a case, identify the unmet need using the employee’s specific statement. Multiple unmet needs may appear."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-4A", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "How are employment tests instrumental in the selection procedure? With suitable representations,\nlist out the measures of human psychology and human characteristics.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["selection"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Initial/preliminary interview → application form → reference checks → employment tests → interview → selection decision.", "Read the test classifications and human-characteristic measures on pages 27–29.", "The interview slide covers several formats; use the format’s purpose and features to distinguish them.", "A decision should integrate evidence rather than rely on a single test or impression."], "tip": "Reproduce the employment-test classifications and diagrams from Module 4 pages 27–29. Explain how the measures support selection against job requirements, rather than listing tests without their purpose.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-4B", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "As the human resource manager of your technical organization which primarily consults investment\nand banking sectors, you hold the essential responsibility for the training and development of your\nstaff. Occasionally, it is necessary to send your staff members for both on-the-job and off-the-job\ntraining. Therefore, list all the on-the-job and off-the-job training methods. Illustrate one on-the-job\ntraining method and one off-the-job training method suitable for the current business context, using\nrelevant examples and/or representation.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-4C", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyse Authority leadership styles and evaluate their impact on employee motivation and\nproductivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["authoritystyles"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Autocratic: leader retains decision authority and directs activity.", "Democratic/participative: members contribute to decisions.", "Free-rein/delegative: employees have substantial freedom within the arrangement.", "Learn each style’s strengths, limitations and suitable conditions from the source.", "Style choice is influenced by the leader, subordinates and situation; justify the fit to the case."], "tip": "These two prompts repeat within this paper. Retain both in a timed paper. Compare autocratic, democratic and free-rein styles using decision authority, motivation and productivity rather than treating the repetition as an extraction error.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-5A", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Company, a leading Indian infrastructure developer, is confronting a critical decline in employee\nmotivation and productivity. This trend, evidenced by rising attrition and widespread dissatisfaction,\nhas been traced to four core issues: poor working conditions, non-competitive compensation,\nlimited career growth, and a weak organizational culture. In response, management has initiated a\ncomprehensive review of its employee motivation policies to address these systemic concerns.\nIdentify the levels of Maslow's hierarchy of needs not being met at X Company.\nExplain how the application of Maslow's theory can improve employee motivation and performance.\nCritically evaluate the sufficiency of Maslow's theory in this context and consider the relevance of\nother motivational models.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["maslow", "herzberg"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Maslow: physiological → safety → social → esteem → self-actualization.", "Physiological: basic living needs. Safety: security. Social: belonging.", "Esteem: respect and recognition. Self-actualization: realizing potential.", "In a case, identify the unmet need using the employee’s specific statement. Multiple unmet needs may appear.", "Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "tip": "Map poor conditions and compensation to basic/security concerns; weak culture to belonging; limited career growth to esteem and/or self-actualization according to the detail. Propose matched actions, then discuss why individuals can have overlapping needs. Herzberg helps separate context problems from missing motivators.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-5B", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyse Authority leadership styles and evaluate their impact on employee motivation and\nproductivity.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-5B-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["authoritystyles"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Autocratic: leader retains decision authority and directs activity.", "Democratic/participative: members contribute to decisions.", "Free-rein/delegative: employees have substantial freedom within the arrangement.", "Learn each style’s strengths, limitations and suitable conditions from the source.", "Style choice is influenced by the leader, subordinates and situation; justify the fit to the case."], "tip": "These two prompts repeat within this paper. Retain both in a timed paper. Compare autocratic, democratic and free-rein styles using decision authority, motivation and productivity rather than treating the repetition as an extraction error.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq6-5C", "paper": "pyq6", "date": "Dec 2025 · Makeup", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "Illustrate the eight steps of the controlling process using a block diagram and explain each step with\na common example.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq6-5C-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["controlprocess"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Standards → actual performance → measure actual performance → compare with standards.", "Identify deviations → analyze causes → program corrective action → implement corrections.", "Standards in the notes: physical, technical, monetary, managerial and time.", "Use one consistent example across the entire diagram; distinguish a deviation from its cause."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-1A", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "1A", "marks": 3, "text": "A hospital faces a sudden shortage of critical supplies during an emergency.\ni) Assess which of Mintzberg's roles are most critical.\nii) Find out how the manager should prioritize actions.\nSuggest a step-by-step response plan using at least 3 roles.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["roles"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Interpersonal: figurehead performs ceremonial duties; leader guides and motivates; liaison maintains contacts.", "Informational: monitor collects information; disseminator shares it internally; spokesperson represents the organization externally.", "Decisional: entrepreneur initiates change; disturbance handler handles unexpected problems; resource allocator assigns resources; negotiator bargains.", "For a case, write both the role category and the specific role. The overview diagram is on page 55."], "tip": "Disturbance handler addresses the emergency; resource allocator prioritizes scarce supplies; liaison and negotiator help secure supply; monitor/disseminator support accurate information. Put the most critical roles first and justify a practical action sequence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-1B", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Critically analyze the three fundamental moral theories in normative ethics and their role in fostering\nethical decision-making within organizations. Using real-world examples demonstrate how\ncompanies can institutionalize ethics through these frameworks.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["ethics"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Utilitarian theory evaluates consequences: greatest good for the greatest number.", "Rights theory protects basic rights such as conscience, speech and due process.", "Justice theory emphasizes fairness, equity and impartiality.", "Institutionalizing ethics means embedding ethical expectations in organizational practices. Read the source examples and measures, not just the three definitions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-1C", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "1C", "marks": 4, "text": "An EdTech company is expanding globally. Classify the required plans into strategic, tactical, and\noperational plans with examples.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["plantypes"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Content: strategic, tactical and operational.", "Time: long-term versus short-term; specificity: directional versus specific.", "Frequency: single-use versus standing plans.", "Strategic plans concern broad enterprise direction; operational or tactical plans translate direction into implementation as described in the notes."], "tip": "Strategic: overall international expansion direction. Tactical: regional/departmental plans that implement it. Operational: concrete daily delivery and support activities. Label examples as applications of the case, not external facts about a named firm.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-2A", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "2A", "marks": 3, "text": "Michael Porter's Generic Model suggests that a company attempting to engage in both Cost\nLeadership and Differentiation simultaneously risks being \"Stuck in the Middle\" with no competitive\nadvantage. Contrast this with the Blue Ocean Strategy, which explicitly pivots on \"Value\nInnovation\".", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["porter", "blueocean"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Read the growth-strategy slide before the generic-strategy framework.", "Cost leadership pursues a low-cost position; differentiation offers something distinctive valued by buyers.", "Focus concentrates on a narrower segment and may use cost or differentiation.", "The notes warn about being stuck in the middle when a firm fails to achieve a coherent advantage.", "The notes emphasize value innovation and creating new market space.", "Four actions: eliminate, reduce, raise and create. Apply them to competitive factors.", "Advantages concern new demand and differentiated value; challenges include risk, resource investment and execution.", "Work through the examples, competitive-factor analysis and four-actions diagrams in the source."], "tip": "Contrast an incoherent attempt to combine generic strategies with Blue Ocean’s deliberate value innovation. Explain how eliminate/reduce and raise/create can pursue lower cost and higher buyer value together; do not claim every mixed strategy automatically succeeds.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-2B", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Identify the Urwick's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief.\ni) Neha ensured that each manager oversaw a limited number of employees to maintain effective\nsupervision. By keeping a balanced span of control, she enabled better communication, timely\ndecision-making, and efficient coordination within teams.\nii) Arjun established a clear reporting structure where each employee received instructions from only\none direct supervisor. This eliminated confusion, reduced conflicting orders, and improved\naccountability across teams.\niii) Ravi assigned project leaders the necessary decision-making authority along with their\nresponsibilities. By ensuring that authority and responsibility were balanced, he prevented delays,\nimproved efficiency, and made individuals more accountable for their work.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-2C", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "2C", "marks": 4, "text": "Design an organizational structure for a multinational company using appropriate departmentation\n(4)\nmethods.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-2C-1.webp", "assets/questions/pyq7-2C-2.webp"], "pages": [1, 2], "topics": ["departmentation", "departmentation2", "matrix"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Numbers: group people by headcount; the source discusses conditions and limitations.", "Time: organize by shifts to cover different working periods.", "Function: group by specialized functions such as production, marketing and finance.", "Functional grouping builds specialization but can create coordination difficulties between departments.", "For an exam answer: name the grouping, draw a labeled structure, then give merits and limitations.", "Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations.", "Specialists retain functional connections while contributing to projects.", "The structure permits expertise to be shared across projects but requires coordination of authority.", "Study the diagram before trying the final identification exercise.", "Distinguish a matrix’s intersecting dimensions from a simple product hierarchy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-3A", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the four steps in the process of delegation with a suitable example.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "The papers explicitly ask for delegation, but the supplied lecture slides provide principles rather than a complete named four-step Flow of Delegation model. A study scaffold is: specify expected results → assign duties → give suitable authority → establish accountability/review. For escalated calls, define the resolution target, assign the calls, set decision limits and review outcomes. Treat this as a derived scaffold, not a verbatim slide model.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-3B", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain any three benefits of integrating HRM and HRD practices with a suitable example for each.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["hrm"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["HRM includes policies, administration and managing human resources; HRD emphasizes employee development.", "Use the source comparison for scope, objectives, functions and emphasis.", "Integration supports holistic development, organizational performance and aligned people practices.", "The notes distinguish managerial and operational HRM functions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-3C", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "Omega Solutions is an IT company. The HR manager conducted an exit interview with Sameer,\nwho was resigning. Sameer said:\n\"My salary is good and the office is comfortable. But I never receive any appreciation for my work. I\nhave been doing the same tasks for three years with no growth. My manager never gives me any\nchallenging responsibilities.\"\nBased on the case above, identify the factors affecting Sameer's motivation according to Herzberg's\nTwo-Factor Theory. Explain the theory briefly.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["herzberg"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Hygiene factors concern job context: policy, supervision, conditions, interpersonal relations, pay and security as shown in the source.", "Motivators concern job content: achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement and the work itself.", "Analyze complaints about conditions separately from the absence of meaningful, satisfying work.", "Read the Maslow–Herzberg comparison to understand overlapping needs without treating the theories as identical."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-4A", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "You are a recruitment consultant hired by AB Corporation - a mid-sized manufacturing company.\nYour task is to recommend 3 internal sources and 3 external sources for their upcoming recruitment\ndrive. Justify your recommendations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-4B", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the founder of a digital marketing agency that helps small businesses grow their online\npresence. You plan to hire fresh graduates and train them to meet industry standards. Elaborate on\nany three current trends in Training & Development that you will consider for your agency.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-4C", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Metro Logistics is a courier company. The regional manager observed four branch managers:\nAK is extremely focused on delivery targets. He pushes his team to work faster and meet\ndeadlines. He rarely asks about their personal problems or work-life balance. Employee complaints\nare high, but his branch has the best on-time delivery record.\nBK is very popular among her team. She organizes team lunches, listens to personal issues, and\navoids conflicts. Her employees are happy, but her branch often misses delivery deadlines.\nCK maintains a balanced approach. He sets reasonable targets and also checks on employee well-\nbeing. His branch meets most deadlines, and employee turnover is low.\nDK took over a branch that was performing poorly. She set high targets and also started weekly\none-on-one meetings with each employee to understand their concerns. She introduced recognition\nfor good performance and team-building activities. Within six months, her branch achieved the\nhighest on-time delivery record and the lowest employee turnover in the company.\nBased on the case above:\ni) Identify the leadership style of AK, BK, CK, and DK using Blake and Mouton's Managerial Grid.\nii) Draw the Managerial Grid and label all five leadership styles.\n(2+2 = 4 marks)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-4C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["grid"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["1,1 impoverished: low concern for both.", "1,9 country club: high people, low production.", "9,1 task/authority-compliance: high production, low people.", "5,5 middle-of-the-road: moderate concern for both; 9,9 team: high concern for both.", "Practice placing each style on the axes and identifying it from cases."], "tip": "AK = 9,1; BK = 1,9; CK = 5,5; DK = 9,9. Draw both axes and also label the fifth style, 1,1, even though no manager in this case exemplifies it.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-5A", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the systems approach to staffing with a labeled sketch. Why is this approach important for\norganizations?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-5A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["staffing"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-5B", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "Explain the four stages of group development with a brief explanation of each stage using relevant\nexamples.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-5B-2.webp", "assets/questions/pyq7-5B-3.webp"], "pages": [2, 3], "topics": ["groups"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Forming: getting acquainted. Storming: conflict and clarification of objectives. Norming: agreed rules. Performing: task execution.", "Read committee functions/formality, reasons for use and disadvantages.", "Reasons include group judgment, representation and coordination; costs and delays are among the limitations.", "Groups can influence behavior, attitudes, values, motivation and relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq7-5C", "paper": "pyq7", "date": "May 2026 · Regular", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "Professor Mehta teaches a management course to 60 students. At the start of the semester, he\nasks the students sitting at the last bench, \"Can you hear me clearly? Do you understand my\naccent?\" He then tells them, \"If you understand me now, it will be fine. Otherwise, after 36 classes,\nyou can tell me my faults. I can only improve next semester.\"\nDuring the semester, the class has monthly committee meetings where students discuss issues like\nteaching pace, assignment deadlines, and classroom facilities. The professor reviews these\nfeedback points and makes adjustments immediately.\nTo avoid losing teaching days on Fridays and Saturdays due to holidays, the institute sometimes\nswaps the timetable - for example, Thursday follows Friday's schedule, and Monday follows\nSaturday's schedule. This ensures all subjects get their required number of classes.\nIdentify and explain the three types of control used by Professor Mehta and the institute by quoting\nlines from the case. What is the significance of each control type?", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq7-5C-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["controltypes"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Feedforward anticipates problems before they arise.", "Concurrent control monitors ongoing transformation of inputs into outputs.", "Feedback control uses results and reactions after the output.", "Quote the time/action clue and explain why it fits the selected control."], "tip": "End-of-course feedback used next semester is feedback control. Monthly reviews with immediate adjustment are concurrent control. Timetable changes anticipating lost classes are feedforward control. Quote a line and explain the timing and purpose of each.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-1A", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "1A", "marks": 4, "text": "Predict how does the \"Systems Approach\" view an organization.\n(4)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-1A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["systems"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Inputs include people, capital, information and resources.", "Managerial functions coordinate the transformation process.", "Outputs and feedback reconnect the organization with its environment.", "Sub-systems must cooperate; optimizing one department alone can harm the whole."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-1B", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "1B", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze the three basic moral theories in normative ethics.\n(3)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-1B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["ethics"], "module": "m1", "guide": ["Utilitarian theory evaluates consequences: greatest good for the greatest number.", "Rights theory protects basic rights such as conscience, speech and due process.", "Justice theory emphasizes fairness, equity and impartiality.", "Institutionalizing ethics means embedding ethical expectations in organizational practices. Read the source examples and measures, not just the three definitions."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-1C", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "1C", "marks": 3, "text": "Analyze Management by Objectives (MBO) as proposed by Peter Drucker. Discuss its advantages,\nlimitations, and relevance in modern organizations.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-1C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["mbo"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["Superior and subordinate work with connected objectives and expected results.", "Objectives should be clear and verifiable; review the source table of vague versus verifiable objectives.", "The source process links inputs, agreement, actions and final review/appraisal.", "Benefits include clarity, participation and commitment; weaknesses include poor guidance, difficulty setting objectives, short-term emphasis and inflexibility as discussed in the notes."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-2A", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "2A", "marks": 4, "text": "Explain the \"Dynamics of the TOWS Matrix\" in relation to a company's progression through time.\nSpecifically, analyze how a WO Strategy can eventually transition into a high-success SO Strategy.\nUse a suitable example to show a company overcame its initial weakness to illustrate this strategic\nevolution.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-2A-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["tows"], "module": "m2", "guide": ["SO: use strengths to exploit opportunities.", "ST: use strengths to counter threats.", "WO: address weaknesses by using opportunities.", "WT: minimize weaknesses and threats; the notes connect this to defensive or retrenchment approaches.", "Use the worked examples and the dynamic TOWS discussion to see that conditions can change."], "tip": "A WO strategy uses an external opportunity to address a weakness. If it successfully develops capability, that former weakness can become a strength; the firm can then use an SO strategy. Explain the change over time rather than treating TOWS as a permanent label.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-2B", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "2B", "marks": 3, "text": "Identify the URWICK's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief.\ni) Meera streamlined project workflows and eliminated redundant steps, ensuring optimal resource\nutilization. Her efforts reduced turnaround time and improved overall productivity without increasing\ncosts.\nii) Rajesh introduced a dynamic project management framework that allowed teams to quickly adapt\nto market changes. This ensured that the company remained competitive without disrupting ongoing\noperations.\niii) Kavita clearly outlined roles and reporting structures within the organization. This minimized\nconfusion, improved accountability, and enhanced coordination between different departments.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-2B-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "(i) Organizational efficiency; (ii) flexibility; (iii) functional definition. Support each with the specific action: remove redundant costs, adapt to changes, clarify roles/reporting.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-2C", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "2C", "marks": 3, "text": "A healthcare organization wants to improve patient care and operational efficiency. Suggest a\ndepartmentation strategy integrating multiple approaches.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-2C-1.webp"], "pages": [1], "topics": ["departmentation", "departmentation2"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Numbers: group people by headcount; the source discusses conditions and limitations.", "Time: organize by shifts to cover different working periods.", "Function: group by specialized functions such as production, marketing and finance.", "Functional grouping builds specialization but can create coordination difficulties between departments.", "For an exam answer: name the grouping, draw a labeled structure, then give merits and limitations.", "Territory/geography suits geographically dispersed operations and local needs; assess duplication and coordination.", "Process/equipment groups around stages or specialized equipment.", "Customer grouping supports different customer needs; product/service grouping focuses responsibility on outputs.", "Read each source organization chart and its stated advantages and limitations."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-3A", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "3A", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the founder of a small steel bolt manufacturing company. You decide to adopt a Line\norganizational structure. Explain how authority, communication, and decision-making will work in\nyour company under this structure. Draw the organizational chart.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-3A-1.webp", "assets/questions/pyq8-3A-2.webp"], "pages": [1, 2], "topics": ["urwick"], "module": "m3", "guide": ["Unity of objectives; organizational efficiency; specialization; span of management; scalar chain.", "Unity of command; delegation; parity of authority and responsibility; flexibility; functional definition.", "Delegation gives people authority to achieve expected results; superiors retain responsibility for subordinate activities.", "Functional definition makes expected results, activities and delegated authority explicit."], "tip": "Source gap: line and line-and-staff organization are asked in the papers, but receive no full standalone treatment in the supplied lecture deck. Use the hierarchy, scalar chain, unity of command and specialization principles to build your response. A line sketch shows a direct reporting chain. For line-and-staff, distinguish the operating chain from specialist advice/support and explain their collaboration. This is an interpretive framework, not an official answer key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-3B", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "3B", "marks": 3, "text": "You have been appointed as the HR manager of a company planning to recruit freshers. Draft a\nJob Description and a Job Specification for the entry-level position in your own respective\ndomains (eg. Mechanical, Aeronautical, Chemical etc).", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-3B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["jobanalysis"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Job analysis identifies duties, skills and knowledge required.", "Job description describes the job: work, duties and responsibilities.", "Job specification describes the person requirements: qualifications, knowledge, skills and other requirements.", "When asked to draft both, use two clearly separate headings for the same position."], "tip": "Use two headings for one entry-level role. Job description: title, department/reporting, purpose and duties. Job specification: education, knowledge, skills and relevant personal/physical requirements where the notes support them. Make requirements appropriate to a fresher; do not invent mandatory industry certifications.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-3C", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "3C", "marks": 4, "text": "ConnectCall Solutions is a BPO company handling customer support for multiple clients. The\ncompany has 800 employees working in night shifts. The HR director, Mr. Mehta, conducted an\ninternal survey to understand why employee turnover was high. He compiled the following real\nstatements from employees:\nEmployee A (Ravi, age 22, works for 8 months):\n\"I earn just enough to pay for my shared room and food. But last month, the company delayed\nsalaries by two weeks. I had to borrow money. Also, there's no transport facility at night. I fear\nwalking to the bus stop after the shift. I'm thinking of quitting.\"\nEmployee B (Neha, age 25, works for 2 years):\n\"I have a decent salary and the company provides cab service at night. But honestly, I feel invisible\nhere. My team leader never says 'good job.' I have won 'Employee of the Month' twice, but no one\ncelebrated it. My colleagues don't include me in their WhatsApp group. I eat lunch alone every\nday.\"\nEmployee C (Arjun, age 30, works for 5 years):\n\"I have a good salary, my own house, and a supportive team. But for the last two years, I have been\ndoing the same troubleshooting calls. I have asked for cross-training in chat support and voice\nprocess, but my request is always ignored. I feel my career has stopped growing.\"\nEmployee D (Priya, age 40, works for 10 years):\n\"I am a senior team lead. I have a high salary, a car, respect from my team, and challenging\nprojects. Last year, I completed an MBA while working. Now I want to design a new training module\nfor freshers. I have ideas to improve the entire onboarding process. But my manager says, 'Just\nfollow the existing manual.' I feel I have more to offer.\"\nEmployee E (Sunil, age 28, works for 3 years):\n\"I have no complaints about salary or safety. My team is friendly and my work is interesting. But last\nmonth, I was passed over for a promotion despite having better numbers than the person who got\npromoted. My manager said, 'Seniority matters more than performance.' I feel humiliated and\nunderappreciated.\"\nBased on the case of ConnectCall Solutions:\ni). Identify the highest level of Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs that is currently unfulfilled for each of\nthe five employees (A, B, C, D, E).\nii) For employees A and D, explain what specific action the company should take to fulfill their unmet\nneed. Justify your answer using Maslow's theory.\n(2½+1½ = 4 marks)", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-3C-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["maslow"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Maslow: physiological → safety → social → esteem → self-actualization.", "Physiological: basic living needs. Safety: security. Social: belonging.", "Esteem: respect and recognition. Self-actualization: realizing potential.", "In a case, identify the unmet need using the employee’s specific statement. Multiple unmet needs may appear."], "tip": "A: safety is the highest explicit unmet level, with physiological concerns also present. B: esteem is higher than the also-unmet belonging need. C: blocked development supports self-actualization; an esteem interpretation needs explicit justification. D: self-actualization. E: esteem. For A provide timely pay and safe transport; for D allow meaningful creative contribution to training. These are reasoned interpretations, not an official key.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-4A", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "4A", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the Chief Human Resources Officer (CHRO) for Tata Motors - one of India's largest\nautomotive manufacturers, and your task is to pick 3 each of the best internal sources and external\nsources for the next recruitment drive. You need to justify your selections to the Board of Directors.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-4A-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["sources"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Approaches in the notes include internal, external, campus, executive search, contingent, remote, diversity/inclusion, referral, social media, full-cycle and digital recruitment.", "Internal sources listed: promotion, transfer, demotion, retired employees, notice boards and job posting.", "External sources listed: press advertisements, educational institutes, placement/employment agencies, unsolicited applicants and employee recommendations.", "Review the challenges slide, including talent shortage, competition, and time/resource constraints.", "In a justification answer, explain how each source fits the stated vacancy."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-4B", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "4B", "marks": 3, "text": "You are the founder of a 'Tech Startup' that leverages technology to develop innovative products or\nservices, often aiming for rapid growth and scalability. You would be recruiting new employees\nshortly and thus researching the Current Trends. Elaborate on 3 current trends in Training &\nDevelopment that you will be considering for your startup.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-4B-2.webp"], "pages": [2], "topics": ["training"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Needs assessment → design and development → delivery → evaluation → follow-up.", "Six trends: digital learning, personalized programs, microlearning, gamification, DEI training and soft-skills development.", "The source includes Microsoft, Google, Amazon, Starbucks and Walmart examples.", "For “any three trends,” define each, connect it to a learning need and explain the expected benefit."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-4C", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "4C", "marks": 4, "text": "Quote the lines from the Reliance Retail Expansion Project Scenarios to identify and explain the\n(4)\ndifferent Leadership Styles incorporated based on Likert's 4 System.\nFor long-term strategy, Reliance forms cross-functional teams to co-create solutions for customer\nexperience. Decision-making is collaborative, and employees feel empowered to innovate.\nAs operations stabilize, senior leaders start offering incentives and recognition for meeting sales\ngoals. While decisions remain centralized, managers are allowed minor flexibility in implementing\npromotional strategies, fostering a sense of goodwill.\nAt the initial stage of launching stores in Tier-3 cities, top management imposes strict targets and\npolicies without consulting store managers. Decisions are centralized, and employees have little\nautonomy, creating pressure to meet deadlines.\nDuring the planning of a new digital retail platform, management actively seeks input from regional\nmanagers and tech teams. Suggestions are considered before final decisions, improving\nengagement and ownership among employees.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-4C-2.webp", "assets/questions/pyq8-4C-3.webp"], "pages": [2, 3], "topics": ["likert"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["1 Exploitative-authoritative: low trust, centralized decisions and coercive direction.", "2 Benevolent-authoritative: paternal confidence and rewards, while control stays largely at the top.", "3 Consultative: managers seek input but retain important decisions.", "4 Participative: substantial trust, group participation and shared involvement.", "Identify systems from evidence about trust, motivation, communication and decisions."], "tip": "In the presented order: collaborative co-creation = participative; centralized decisions with rewards and goodwill = benevolent-authoritative; strict targets without consultation = exploitative-authoritative; seeking input before leaders decide = consultative.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-5A", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "5A", "marks": 3, "text": "Apply the systems approach to staffing to explain with a neat diagram how the State Bank of India\n(SBI) can effectively meet its organizational goals and deliver quality banking services.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-5A-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["staffing"], "module": "m4", "guide": ["Enterprise plans → organization plans → number and kinds of managers required.", "Compare requirements with the manager inventory to analyze present and future needs.", "Use internal/external sources → recruitment, selection, placement, promotion or separation.", "Appraisal, career strategy, training and development connect to leading and controlling.", "The external environment surrounds the model; personnel policies and reward systems form part of the internal environment. Include feedback relationships."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-5B", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "5B", "marks": 3, "text": "As the communication manager at ICICI Bank, apply your understanding of 'barriers to effective\ncommunication' to identify three barriers involved in the workspace and suggest practical strategies\nto overcome them for smoother information flow in the organization.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-5B-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["barriers"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Source barriers include lack of planning, unclear assumptions, semantic distortion, poor expression, loss/retention issues and poor listening.", "Also review perception, attitudes, status/power, information overload and other barriers listed in the pages.", "Remedies include clarity, audience awareness, consultation, suitable language and follow-up/feedback.", "Read listening guidance and the separate written-communication checklist."], "tip": "Answer the exact command words. Define the concept, explain the relevant points and apply them to the situation. Include the requested diagram or quoted evidence.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}, {"id": "pyq8-5C", "paper": "pyq8", "date": "Jun 2026 · Makeup", "label": "5C", "marks": 4, "text": "Analyze how three types of controls can be implemented in a company like Reliance Jio to enhance\noperational efficiency and customer satisfaction. Explain with examples how each type of control\nhelps managers anticipate problems, monitor ongoing processes, and evaluate outcomes.", "images": ["assets/questions/pyq8-5C-3.webp"], "pages": [3], "topics": ["controltypes"], "module": "m5", "guide": ["Feedforward anticipates problems before they arise.", "Concurrent control monitors ongoing transformation of inputs into outputs.", "Feedback control uses results and reactions after the output.", "Quote the time/action clue and explain why it fits the selected control."], "tip": "Use one coherent service example across before/during/after: prepare capacity before demand, monitor service during operation, and use customer results after service. These illustrate the PDF’s control concepts, not claims about Jio’s actual practices.", "caution": "These are study frameworks derived from your material, not official marking schemes."}], "notes": {"business": "<h3>What is a business?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>business</strong> is an organization that tries to earn a <strong>profit</strong> by providing the <strong>goods and services</strong> its customers want.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Goods</strong> are <em>tangible</em>: you can touch and store them (a laptop, a car).</li><li><strong>Services</strong> are <em>intangible</em>: they cannot be held, touched or stored (medical care, banking).</li></ul>\n<h3>What is an industry?</h3>\n<p><strong>Industry</strong> refers to economic activities that <strong>convert resources into useful goods</strong>. It covers producing and processing goods and materials, and even breeding and raising animals. The word is also used for activities that use mechanical appliances and technical skill.</p>\n<h3>Industry vs business</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Industry</th><th>Business</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Meaning</td><td>A <strong>group</strong> of companies producing similar goods/services</td><td>An <strong>individual</strong> company doing commercial activity</td></tr><tr><td>Represents</td><td>A sector of the economy</td><td>One entity operating <em>inside</em> an industry</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Automotive industry, technology industry</td><td>Tesla, Apple</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Think of it like this:</strong> the <em>industry</em> is the stadium, a <em>business</em> is one team playing in it.</div>\n<h3>Four types of industry</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Primary</strong>: extract raw materials directly from the earth or sea (mining, fishing, farming).</li><li><strong>Secondary</strong>: process and <strong>manufacture</strong> products from raw materials (car plants, steel mills).</li><li><strong>Tertiary</strong>: provide a <strong>service</strong> (banking, transport, retail).</li><li><strong>Quaternary</strong>: use a high degree of <strong>research and technology</strong> and employ highly qualified people (biotechnology, computer programming).</li></ol>\n<h3>Why management matters for an engineer</h3>\n<p>Engineers rarely work alone. Projects need goals, teams, budgets and deadlines, which is exactly what management deals with. The course opens with a case asking you to choose which team to join: the point is that a well-<em>managed</em> team usually beats a talented but unmanaged one.</p>", "functions": "<h3>Who is a manager?</h3>\n<p>Managers are individuals in an organization who <strong>direct the activities of others</strong>. An <strong>organization</strong> is <em>\"a deliberate arrangement of people brought together to accomplish some specific purpose.\"</em></p>\n<p>A manager is responsible for <strong>planning, organizing, leading and controlling</strong> resources and activities to achieve goals, making sure the team works <strong>efficiently</strong> (no waste of resources) and <strong>effectively</strong> (reaching the goal).</p>\n<h3>Three levels of management</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Who</th><th>Typical focus (from the slides)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Top</strong></td><td>CEO, directors</td><td>Executive coaching, change management, leadership, delegation &amp; empowerment</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Middle</strong></td><td>Department heads</td><td>Problem solving, team building, talent development, performance management</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lower / first-line</strong></td><td>Supervisors, foremen</td><td>Emotional intelligence and coaching for performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Three managerial skills</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Technical skill</strong>: knowing <em>how to do the work</em> (methods, tools, processes).</li><li><strong>Human skill</strong>: working <em>with people</em>, motivating and communicating.</li><li><strong>Conceptual skill</strong>: seeing the <em>whole organization</em> and how its parts connect.</li></ul>\n<p>How they shift with level:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Top</strong>: conceptual skill matters most.</li><li><strong>Bottom</strong>: technical skill matters most.</li><li><strong>Human skill</strong> is needed roughly equally at <strong>every</strong> level.</li></ul>\n<h3>The five functions of managers (POSLC)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Planning</strong>: selecting missions and objectives and the actions to achieve them. It needs <strong>decision-making</strong>, i.e. choosing a future course of action from alternatives.</li><li><strong>Organizing</strong>: establishing an <strong>intentional structure of roles</strong> so every task needed for the goal is assigned to the people who can do it best.</li><li><strong>Staffing</strong>: <strong>filling and keeping filled</strong> the positions in the structure. This covers identifying workforce needs, taking inventory of available people, then recruiting, selecting, placing, promoting, appraising, compensating and training them.</li><li><strong>Leading</strong>: <strong>influencing people</strong> so they contribute to organizational and group goals. It is the interpersonal side of managing, so it involves <strong>motivation, leadership styles and communication</strong>. Leadership implies followership, and people follow those who help satisfy their needs.</li><li><strong>Controlling</strong>: <strong>measuring and correcting</strong> performance so that events conform to plans. It shows where deviations from standards exist and helps correct them. Planning must come before controlling, because <strong>plans are not self-achieving</strong>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> opening a café. <em>Plan</em> the menu and target. <em>Organize</em> kitchen and counter roles. <em>Staff</em> by hiring a chef and baristas. <em>Lead</em> by motivating the team on day one. <em>Control</em> by checking daily sales against the target and fixing what's off.</div>", "systems": "<h3>The organization as an open system</h3>\n<p>The <strong>systems approach</strong> sees an organization as a set of <strong>interrelated, interdependent parts (sub-systems)</strong> arranged so that the whole is <em>more than the sum of its parts</em>.</p>\n<h3>How the system works</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Inputs</strong> come from the external environment: people, capital, managerial skill, technical knowledge, energy, information, materials, finance.</li><li>The <strong>transformation process</strong> turns inputs into outputs <em>through the managerial functions</em> (plan, organize, staff, lead, control).</li><li><strong>Outputs</strong> go back into the environment as goods, services, profits, satisfaction and goal integration.</li><li><strong>Communication</strong> links every phase and connects the organization with its environment.</li><li><strong>Feedback / re-energizing</strong>: some outputs (e.g. profit, satisfied employees) become <strong>inputs again</strong>.</li></ol>\n<h3>Key ideas</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Sub-systems are related</strong>: study parts in their inter-relationships, not in isolation.</li><li><strong>Boundary</strong>: separates the organization from other systems and decides what is internal or external.</li><li><strong>Open system</strong>: it interacts with and depends on its environment, so it is vulnerable to environmental change.</li><li><strong>Dynamic</strong>: its equilibrium keeps changing, so management must keep adjusting it.</li><li><strong>Every system is part of a super-system</strong> (a firm is part of an industry, which is part of the economy).</li><li><strong>Multidisciplinary</strong>: management draws knowledge from many disciplines.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Picture it:</strong> a body. Food and air (inputs) are processed by organs working together (transformation), producing energy and activity (outputs). If one organ fails, the whole system suffers.</div>", "fayol": "<h3>What is a principle?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>principle</strong> is a <strong>fundamental truth</strong> that explains cause and effect between variables in a given situation. <strong>Management principles</strong> are statements of fundamental truth based on logic that <strong>guide managerial decisions and actions</strong>. They come from (1) observing and analysing managers' practical experience and (2) experimental studies.</p>\n<p><strong>Henri Fayol</strong>, the <em>Father of Modern Management Theory</em>, gave a general theory that applies to all levels and departments.</p>\n<h3>The 14 principles, explained</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Division of labour</strong>: divide work and allot it by expertise. Specialization brings <strong>speed, accuracy, efficiency and economy</strong>.</li><li><strong>Authority &amp; responsibility</strong>: they <strong>co-exist</strong>. Authority is the right to get work done; responsibility is the obligation to perform. <em>Authority without responsibility leads to irresponsible behaviour; responsibility without authority makes a person ineffective.</em></li><li><strong>Unity of command</strong> (<em>one boss</em>): a subordinate takes orders from and is accountable to <strong>only one superior</strong>. Dual subordination undermines authority, weakens discipline, divides loyalty and causes confusion, delay, duplication and escaping of responsibility.</li><li><strong>Unity of direction</strong> (<em>one head, one plan</em>): activities with the <strong>same objective</strong> are grouped under one manager with one plan. Unity of command is not possible without unity of direction.</li><li><strong>Equity</strong>: fairness + kindness + justice. Managers must be fair and impartial, but equity does not mean no harshness: <em>\"at times force and harshness might become necessary for the sake of equity.\"</em></li><li><strong>Order</strong>: systematic arrangement. <strong>Material order</strong> = a place for every thing; <strong>social order</strong> = the right person in the right job.</li><li><strong>Discipline</strong>: sincerity, obedience, respect for authority and rules, required of <strong>both</strong> subordinates and management. It needs good superiors at all levels, clear and fair agreements, and judicious sanctions.</li><li><strong>Initiative</strong>: eagerness to act <strong>without being asked</strong>. Encourage it with ideas schemes and monetary/non-monetary incentives; it builds trust and zeal.</li><li><strong>Fair remuneration</strong>: pay should be fair and satisfy both sides. Base it on cost of living, work assigned, the firm's finances and prevailing wage rates. Fayol also suggested benefits such as free education, medical and housing facilities.</li><li><strong>Stability of tenure</strong>: don't move employees around too often. People need time to learn a job; frequent transfers waste training effort. Stability builds team spirit and belonging.</li><li><strong>Scalar chain</strong>: the chain of superiors from top to bottom through which all communication passes. In urgent cases a <strong>gang plank</strong> (a temporary direct link between two people at the same level) can be used, which shows principles are <strong>flexible, not rigid</strong>.</li><li><strong>Subordination of individual interest to general interest</strong>: the organization's interest comes first; reconcile individual and group interests and, in conflict, the individual gives way. Needs honest employees, regular supervision and settling differences by agreement.</li><li><strong>Esprit de corps</strong>: <strong>team spirit</strong> and harmony. Don't split workers into competing groups; encourage coordination, informal relations, enthusiasm, rewards for good work and face-to-face communication.</li><li><strong>Centralization &amp; decentralization</strong>: centralization keeps decision authority at the top; decentralization shares it downwards. <em>Anything that increases the subordinate's role is decentralization; anything that decreases it is centralization.</em> The right balance depends on business size, experience of superiors and ability of subordinates; neither extreme is feasible.</li></ol>\n<h3>Unity of command vs unity of direction</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Unity of command</th><th>Unity of direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Meaning</td><td>One subordinate, one boss</td><td>One head, one plan for activities with a common objective</td></tr><tr><td>Concerned with</td><td>Functioning of <strong>personnel</strong></td><td>Functioning of <strong>departments / whole organization</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Necessary for</td><td>Fixing each subordinate's responsibility</td><td>A sound organization</td></tr><tr><td>Avoids</td><td>Conflict, confusion, chaos</td><td>Duplication of effort, waste of resources</td></tr><tr><td>Result</td><td>Better superior–subordinate relations</td><td>Smooth running of the enterprise</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>5S (linked to the principle of Order)</h3>\n<p>A Japanese workplace-organization method:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Japanese</th><th>Meaning</th><th>English 5S</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Seiri</td><td>Put things in order</td><td><strong>Sort</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Seiton</td><td>Proper arrangement</td><td><strong>Set in order</strong> (straighten)</td></tr><tr><td>Seiso</td><td>Clean</td><td><strong>Shine</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Seiketsu</td><td>Purity</td><td><strong>Standardize</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Shitsuke</td><td>Commitment</td><td><strong>Sustain</strong> (discipline)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "roles": "<h3>Mintzberg's managerial roles approach</h3>\n<p>Henry Mintzberg (McGill University) studied what managers <em>actually do</em>. He found they play <strong>ten roles</strong> (organized sets of behaviour linked to a position) in <strong>three categories</strong>.</p>\n<h3>A. Interpersonal roles (dealing with people)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Figurehead</strong>: ceremonial duties because of the position. <em>The president greets visiting dignitaries; a foreman attends a worker's wedding; the sales manager takes an important customer to lunch.</em></li><li><strong>Leader</strong>: responsible for the unit's people. Directly: hiring and training staff. Indirectly: motivating employees and reconciling their needs with organizational goals.</li><li><strong>Liaison</strong>: building contacts <strong>outside the vertical chain of command</strong> (peers, outsiders) mainly to gather information.</li></ol>\n<h3>B. Informational roles (dealing with information)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Monitor</strong>: constantly <strong>scans the environment</strong> and contacts for information, much of it informal (gossip, hearsay, speculation).</li><li><strong>Disseminator</strong>: <strong>passes information inside</strong> the organization to subordinates who would otherwise lack it.</li><li><strong>Spokesperson</strong>: <strong>sends information outside</strong> the unit: advising directors and shareholders on financial performance, assuring consumer groups about social responsibility, satisfying government officials that the law is followed.</li></ol>\n<h3>C. Decisional roles (making decisions)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Entrepreneur</strong>: the <strong>voluntary initiator of change</strong>. Looks for new ideas and starts development projects, juggling several at a time.</li><li><strong>Disturbance handler</strong>: <strong>involuntarily responds</strong> to pressures beyond control: a strike, a major customer going bankrupt, an accident delaying raw materials.</li><li><strong>Resource allocator</strong>: decides <strong>who gets what</strong>, including the manager's own time; designs the unit's structure; authorizes important decisions.</li><li><strong>Negotiator</strong>: spends considerable time in <strong>negotiations</strong>, a duty that cannot be avoided.</li></ol>\n<h3>How to identify a role in a case</h3>\n<p>Ask three questions in order:</p>\n<ol><li>Is the manager dealing mainly with <strong>people</strong>, <strong>information</strong> or a <strong>decision</strong>? That gives the category.</li><li>Is the action <strong>inside</strong> or <strong>outside</strong> the unit? (disseminator vs spokesperson)</li><li>Is change <strong>chosen</strong> or <strong>forced</strong>? (entrepreneur vs disturbance handler)</li></ol>", "csr": "<h3>The external environment</h3>\n<p>An organization is surrounded by forces it must respond to: <strong>economic, technological, social, ecological, political &amp; legal, and ethical</strong> environments.</p>\n<h3>Two views of social responsibility</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Classical / purely economic view</strong>: management's only social responsibility is to <strong>maximize profits</strong>.</li><li><strong>Socio-economic view</strong>: responsibility goes beyond profit to <strong>protecting and improving society's welfare</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Society expects managers to be responsible and ethical, yet financial scandals, scams over material quality and simple negligence show they don't always act that way.</p>\n<h3>What is CSR?</h3>\n<p><strong>Corporate social responsibility</strong> is a firm's intention, <strong>beyond its legal and economic obligations</strong>, to do the right things and act in ways good for society. It assumes the firm already obeys the law and pursues profit, and treats the business as a <strong>moral agent</strong> that must tell right from wrong.</p>\n<p>The words: <strong>Corporate</strong> = organized business; <strong>Social</strong> = everything dealing with people; <strong>Responsibility</strong> = accountability between the two.</p>\n<p>CSR is the corporate initiative to assess and take responsibility for the company's <strong>effects on the environment and social welfare</strong>, usually going <strong>beyond what regulators require</strong>. It is also called <strong>\"corporate citizenship\"</strong> and may mean accepting <strong>short-term costs</strong> with no immediate financial benefit in exchange for positive social and environmental change.</p>\n<h3>Drivers of CSR</h3>\n<ol><li>Employees and shareholders</li><li><strong>Brand equity</strong> (the value of a brand as customers perceive its quality and desirability)</li><li>Ethical marketing practices and social awareness</li><li>Environmental consciousness</li><li>Energy conservation and global warming</li><li>Responsibility towards the government</li></ol>\n<h3>Why CSR pays off</h3>\n<ul><li>Satisfied employees become competent employees, giving better service and happier customers.</li><li>Goodwill and trust take decades to build with customers and shareholders.</li><li>An ethical culture reduces unfair practices by emphasizing professionalism and transparency.</li><li>It shows up in socially responsible investment, communication, participation, fairness, adequate pay and personal development.</li></ul>", "ethics": "<h3>What is ethics?</h3>\n<p><strong>Ethics</strong> is <em>\"the discipline dealing with what is good and bad and with moral duty and obligation.\"</em> <strong>Business ethics</strong> systematically studies morals to separate actions that harm business from those that are ethically sound.</p>\n<p>Ethics has often been dismissed as a \"soft\" topic because it was not linked to disciplines like economics and finance. The <strong>integrated approach</strong> connects ethics to everyday management decisions.</p>\n<h3>Three moral theories (normative ethics)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Core question</th><th>Slide examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Utilitarian</strong></td><td>Does it produce the <strong>greatest good for the greatest number</strong>? Judge by <strong>consequences</strong>.</td><td>Recycling programme for the whole community; affordable life-saving drug; shutting a polluting unit</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rights</strong></td><td>Does it respect people's <strong>basic rights</strong> (conscience, free speech, due process)?</td><td>Not selling users' personal data; protecting journalists from censorship; paid leave to vote</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Is it <strong>fair, equitable and impartial</strong>?</td><td>Equal pay for equal work; fair pricing for small vendors; scholarships for disadvantaged students</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Quick test:</strong> <em>Consequences</em> → utilitarian. <em>Freedoms / entitlements</em> → rights. <em>Fair distribution</em> → justice.</div>\n<h3>Institutionalizing ethics</h3>\n<p>Top managers must create an environment that encourages ethical decisions, in three ways:</p>\n<ol><li>A company <strong>policy or code of ethics</strong></li><li>A formally appointed <strong>ethics committee</strong></li><li><strong>Teaching ethics</strong> in management development programmes</li></ol>", "trust": "<h3>Change management</h3>\n<p><strong>Change management</strong> is a systematic approach to handling the transition or transformation of an organization's <strong>goals, processes and technologies</strong>. Its purpose is to implement strategies for bringing about and controlling change and to <strong>help people adapt</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Why trust is the foundation</h3>\n<p>Managers face a flood of new concepts driven by global competition, customer expectations and the need to respond quickly. The often-overlooked factor is <strong>trust</strong>, which sits at the centre of <strong>communication, collaboration and willingness to change</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li>Traditionally trust means <strong>integrity, loyalty, caring and keeping promises</strong> between individuals.</li><li>It should go further: build a <strong>culture of trust</strong> across the organization that outlives any one leader, because <em>leaders come and go; the organization continues.</em></li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Why it works:</strong> people resist change when they fear hidden agendas. If they trust management, they will accept short-term discomfort for long-term benefit.</div>", "international": "<h3>Culture shapes management</h3>\n<p>A country's culture influences employee behaviour and therefore management practice. The slides use <strong>five dimensions</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Individualism vs collectivism</strong>: do people look after themselves or their group?</li><li><strong>Large vs small power distance</strong>: how much do people accept unequal power?</li><li><strong>Uncertainty tolerance vs avoidance</strong>: how comfortable are people with risk and ambiguity?</li><li><strong>Masculinity vs femininity</strong>: aggressive vs passive goal behaviour.</li><li><strong>Short-term vs long-term orientation</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>Managers must understand the cultural environment of the country they do business in.</p>\n<h3>Country snapshots</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Germany</strong>: reliance on authority, often <em>benevolent authoritarianism</em>; managers show concern but expect obedience.</li><li><strong>Austria (and Germany)</strong>: self-realization and leadership; independence and competitiveness valued; <strong>low</strong> risk tolerance.</li><li><strong>Australia</strong>: moralistic stance, political and social values, achievement and <strong>risk taking</strong>.</li><li><strong>Italy</strong>: <strong>low</strong> risk tolerance; very competitive yet like <strong>group decision-making</strong>.</li><li><strong>Britain</strong>: job security, resourcefulness, adaptability, logic; <strong>individualism</strong> highly valued.</li></ul>\n<h3>Korea vs Japan vs USA</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Korean</th><th>Japanese</th><th>American</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Decision-making</td><td>Top-down, hierarchical</td><td>Consensus-driven, participative</td><td>Top-down, fast, individual</td></tr><tr><td>Employment</td><td>Loyalty and seniority</td><td>Lifetime employment, loyalty</td><td>Performance-based, high mobility</td></tr><tr><td>Communication</td><td>Indirect, high-context</td><td>Indirect, high-context</td><td>Direct, low-context</td></tr><tr><td>Relationships</td><td>Hierarchy, respect for elders</td><td>Group harmony, team cohesion</td><td>Individualism, personal achievement</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership</td><td>Authoritative, paternalistic</td><td>Facilitative, consensus-oriented</td><td>Authoritative, results-oriented</td></tr><tr><td>Motivation</td><td>Loyalty, respect for authority</td><td>Group harmony, job security</td><td>Individual achievement, rewards</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict</td><td>Avoidance, saving face</td><td>Avoidance, harmony</td><td>Confrontational, quick resolution</td></tr><tr><td>Training</td><td>In-house, seniority-based</td><td>Extensive in-house, long-term</td><td>Formal education, continuous development</td></tr><tr><td>Innovation</td><td>Slow, cautious</td><td>Incremental, cautious</td><td>Rapid, proactive</td></tr><tr><td>Work-life</td><td>Long hours, dedication</td><td>Long hours, commitment</td><td>Work-life balance, flexible hours</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Seniority and loyalty</td><td>Group performance and seniority</td><td>Individual results</td></tr><tr><td>CSR</td><td>Emerging</td><td>High, integrated</td><td>High, stakeholder/regulation driven</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Exam tip (Japan vs USA):</strong> contrast them on 4–5 rows of this table (decisions, employment, communication, motivation, conflict) and link each to culture: collectivist &amp; long-term (Japan) vs individualist &amp; short-term (USA).</div>", "theoryz": "<h3>Japanese vs American organizations (Ouchi)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Type J (Japanese)</th><th>Type A (American)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment</td><td>Lifetime</td><td>Short-term</td></tr><tr><td>Decision-making</td><td>Collective</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>Collective</td><td>Individual</td></tr><tr><td>Evaluation &amp; promotion</td><td>Slow</td><td>Rapid</td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>Implicit (understood)</td><td>Explicit (clear, precise)</td></tr><tr><td>Career path</td><td>Non-specialized</td><td>Specialized</td></tr><tr><td>Concern for employee</td><td>Holistic, as a person</td><td>Segmented, as an employee</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Theory Z</h3>\n<p><strong>William Ouchi</strong> developed Theory Z in the 1980s. It <strong>integrates Japanese and American practices</strong>: the Japanese emphasis on <strong>collective decision-making</strong> with the American emphasis on <strong>individual responsibility</strong>. It aims to raise <strong>employee loyalty</strong> by offering long-term jobs and caring for the employee's well-being on and off the job. Ouchi argued it promotes <strong>stable employment, high productivity, and high morale and satisfaction</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Type Z organization (the blend)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Long-term</strong> employment</li><li><strong>Consensual, participative</strong> decision-making</li><li><strong>Individual</strong> responsibility</li><li><strong>Slow</strong> evaluation and promotion</li><li><strong>Implicit, informal control with explicit, formalized measures</strong></li><li><strong>Moderately specialized</strong> career path</li><li><strong>Holistic concern, including family</strong></li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory trick:</strong> Z sits between J and A. For each row, take the Japanese answer <em>except</em> responsibility (American: individual), and make control and career path a mix of both.</div>", "plans": "<h3>What is planning?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>plan</strong> is a <strong>blueprint for goal achievement</strong>: it specifies the resource allocations, schedules, tasks and other actions needed to achieve a purpose. Planning <strong>bridges the gap between where we are and where we want to be</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Why plan?</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Reduces uncertainty</strong> and helps forecast the future</li><li><strong>Provides direction</strong> and a logical framework for the business</li><li>Guides <strong>resource allocation</strong></li><li><strong>Minimizes impulsive and arbitrary decisions</strong></li><li>Gives a <strong>benchmark</strong> to measure actual performance against</li><li>Helps avoid mistakes and spot hidden opportunities</li></ul>\n<p>Planning answers the basic questions: <strong>What</strong> must be done? What are the <strong>alternative routes</strong>? <strong>When</strong> is the deadline? <strong>Where</strong> will it be done? <strong>Who</strong> is responsible? <strong>How</strong> will it be done, and <strong>how much</strong> time, energy and resources does it need?</p>\n<h3>The eight types of plans (broad → narrow)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Purpose / mission / vision</strong>: the <strong>basic function or task</strong> of the enterprise. <em>Oil company: search for, produce, refine and market petroleum. DuPont: \"Better things through chemistry.\"</em></li><li><strong>Objectives / goals</strong>: the <strong>important ends</strong> toward which activities are directed. <strong>Verifiable</strong> objectives make it possible to measure effectiveness and efficiency.</li><li><strong>Strategy</strong>: a complete plan for achieving objectives with <strong>three dimensions</strong>: setting long-term objectives, selecting a course of action, and allocating resources. Usually made by <strong>top management</strong>.</li><li><strong>Policy</strong>: <strong>general statements or understandings that guide thinking</strong> in decision-making. They define an <em>area</em> within which decisions are made, leave <strong>room for discretion</strong>, and are sometimes only <strong>implied</strong> by managers' actions. <em>Examples: hiring university-trained engineers, encouraging employee suggestions, promoting from within, competitive pricing, buying from the lowest of three qualified bidders.</em></li><li><strong>Procedure</strong>: a <strong>step-wise, chronological guide</strong> to routine activities. <em>A college admission: fill the form → collect documents → sort applications. A vacation policy is carried out by a procedure that schedules leave without disrupting work.</em></li><li><strong>Rule</strong>: a <strong>very specific statement requiring an action or non-action</strong>, with <strong>no flexibility</strong>. The <strong>simplest</strong> type of plan; guides action <strong>without a time sequence</strong>; may or may not be part of a procedure. <em>No-smoking zone; no-phone zone.</em></li><li><strong>Program</strong>: a <strong>complex</strong> of goals, policies, procedures, rules, task assignments, steps and resources needed for a course of action, <strong>usually supported by budgets</strong>.</li><li><strong>Budget</strong>: a statement of expected results <strong>in numerical terms</strong> (a \"numberized program\"): labour hours, units, money, machine hours. Types: <strong>variable/flexible</strong> (varies with output), <strong>program</strong> budgets (each department identifies goals), <strong>zero-based</strong> (a combination of variable and program budgets).</li></ol>\n<h3>Telling them apart (the classic exam trap)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Policy</th><th>Procedure</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it does</td><td>Guides <strong>thinking</strong></td><td>Guides <strong>action step by step</strong></td><td>Requires/forbids <strong>one action</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Yes</td><td>Little</td><td><strong>None</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Time sequence</td><td>No</td><td><strong>Yes (chronological)</strong></td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Trick:</strong> \"<em>Generally</em> we promote from within\" = policy. \"<em>First</em> fill this, <em>then</em> submit that\" = procedure. \"<em>Never</em> / <em>must</em>\" = rule. A <em>number</em> target = objective or budget.</div>", "plantypes": "<h3>Classifying plans by perspective</h3>\n<p>The same plan can be described in several ways. These classifications are <strong>not mutually exclusive</strong>: one plan can be strategic, long-term, directional and standing all at once.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Content: strategic, tactical, operational</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Strategic plans</strong> (upper managers) apply to the <strong>whole organization</strong>, set overall objectives and position it in its environment. Broad, less specific, five or more years.</li><li><strong>Tactical plans</strong> (lower managers) specify <strong>how</strong> overall objectives will be achieved.</li><li><strong>Operational plans</strong> are limited in scope and measured <strong>daily, weekly or monthly</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>2. Breadth: strategic vs tactical</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Strategic</th><th>Tactical / operational</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whole organization</td><td>Specific parts</td></tr><tr><td>Sets overall objectives, positions the firm</td><td>Derived from strategic objectives; give the details</td></tr><tr><td>Basis for tactical plans</td><td>Updated continuously</td></tr><tr><td>Long periods, less detail</td><td>Shorter periods</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>3. Time frame: long-term vs short-term</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Long-term</strong>: beyond five years; what the organization wants to become; high uncertainty; focus of higher levels. <em>Sales, brand awareness, public reputation, government plans.</em></li><li><strong>Short-term</strong>: up to one year; basis for day-to-day operations; answers \"<em>are we doing things right?</em>\"; must fit within long-range plans. <em>Communication, networking, daily routines, staff training.</em></li></ul>\n<p><strong>4. Specificity: specific vs directional</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Specific</strong>: clearly defined, no room for misinterpretation; <em>what, when, where, how much and by whom</em> (process focus). <em>A lesson plan.</em></li><li><strong>Directional</strong>: flexible general guidelines, <em>\"go from here to there\"</em> (outcome focus).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>5. Frequency of use: single-use vs standing</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Single-use</strong>: for a particular, unique situation. <em>A one-day sale advertisement.</em></li><li><strong>Standing</strong>: ongoing guidance for repeated actions. <em>A customer-satisfaction policy.</em></li></ul>", "planningsteps": "<h3>The eight steps in planning</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Being aware of opportunities</strong>: look at the market, competition, customer needs, and our strengths and weaknesses.</li><li><strong>Setting objectives</strong>: where we want to be, what we want to accomplish and <strong>when</strong>.</li><li><strong>Considering planning premises</strong>: in what environment (internal or external) will the plan operate?</li><li><strong>Identifying alternatives</strong>: what are the most promising ways to reach the objectives?</li><li><strong>Comparing alternatives</strong> in the light of the goals: which gives the best chance at lowest cost and highest profit?</li><li><strong>Choosing an alternative</strong>: selecting the course of action.</li><li><strong>Formulating supporting (derivative) plans</strong>: e.g. buying equipment and materials, hiring and training staff, developing a new product.</li><li><strong>Numberizing plans by budgets</strong>: sales volume and price, operating expenses, capital expenditure.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Example (opening an EV charging chain):</strong> spot rising EV sales (1) → aim for 50 stations in 2 years (2) → assume government subsidies continue (3) → options: own land, lease, partner with malls (4) → compare cost and reach (5) → choose mall partnerships (6) → plans to buy chargers, hire technicians (7) → capital and operating budgets (8).</div>", "objectives": "<h3>What are objectives?</h3>\n<p><strong>Objectives</strong> are the important ends toward which organizational and individual activities are directed.</p>\n<h3>Hierarchy of objectives</h3>\n<p>Objectives form a hierarchy from the <strong>purpose/mission</strong> at the top, through overall enterprise objectives, down to division, department and individual objectives. Two ways to set them:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Top-down</strong>: upper managers determine subordinates' objectives.</li><li><strong>Bottom-up</strong>: subordinates propose objectives that are then integrated upward.</li></ul>\n<h3>Strategies and policies</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Strategy</strong>: determining the mission and basic long-term objectives, then adopting courses of action and allocating resources to achieve them.</li><li><strong>Policies</strong>: general statements that guide managers' thinking in decision-making.</li></ul>\n<h3>Multiplicity of objectives</h3>\n<p>A business has <strong>several objectives at every level</strong> because it must satisfy many stakeholders: owners, employees, consumers, creditors, vendors and society. It sets separate objectives for each group.</p>\n<h3>Quantitative vs qualitative objectives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quantitative</th><th>Qualitative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expressed <strong>numerically</strong>, easy to measure</td><td>Not numerical, harder to measure</td></tr><tr><td>Increase annual revenue by 10%; cut production cost by 15%</td><td>Enhance customer experience; improve brand reputation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "mbo": "<h3>What is MBO?</h3>\n<p><strong>Management by objectives</strong> (also called <em>management by planning</em>) was popularized by <strong>Peter Drucker</strong> in <em>The Practice of Management</em> (1954). It is a <strong>comprehensive managerial system</strong> that integrates key managerial activities systematically, directed at achieving organizational and individual objectives effectively and efficiently. Objectives are <strong>agreed by both management and employees</strong>, set for every department, project and person, and then used to <strong>monitor performance</strong>.</p>\n<p>MBO emphasizes <strong>performance appraisal</strong>, <strong>short-term objectives and motivation</strong>, and <strong>long-term planning</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Process of MBO</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Setting preliminary objectives at the top</strong></li><li><strong>Clarifying organizational roles</strong></li><li><strong>Setting subordinates' objectives</strong> (superior and subordinate agree, considering resources)</li><li><strong>Recycling objectives</strong> (reviewing and adjusting them)</li></ol>\n<p>The detailed MBO cycle: enterprise objectives, planning premises and key result areas → superior's objectives → superior's preliminary recommendation for the subordinate → subordinate's own statement → <strong>agreement</strong> → subordinate's objectives → ongoing performance → <strong>periodic review</strong> with corrective help → final performance → <strong>final review and appraisal</strong>, which feeds new inputs into the next cycle.</p>\n<h3>Key result areas (Drucker)</h3>\n<p>Areas where performance is <strong>essential for the enterprise's success</strong>: market standing, innovation, productivity, physical and financial resources, profitability, managerial performance and development, worker performance and attitude, public responsibility (plus service and quality).</p>\n<h3>Verifiable vs non-verifiable objectives</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Non-verifiable</th><th>Verifiable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Make a reasonable profit</td><td>12% return on investment by the end of this fiscal year</td></tr><tr><td>Improve communication</td><td>Two-page monthly newsletter from July 1, max 40 work hours to prepare</td></tr><tr><td>Improve productivity</td><td>Raise output 5% by December 31 at no extra cost, same quality</td></tr><tr><td>Install a computer system</td><td>Computerized control system by Dec 31, max 500 hours, under 10% downtime in the first 3 months</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> a verifiable objective answers <em>how much, by when, at what cost/quality</em>.</div>\n<h3>Setting employee objectives</h3>\n<p>Identify key job tasks → set <strong>specific, challenging</strong> goals → let the employee <strong>participate</strong> → <strong>prioritize</strong> → build in <strong>feedback</strong> → <strong>link rewards</strong> to achievement.</p>\n<h3>Objectives of MBO</h3>\n<p>Identify problems and opportunities; turn them into clear goals; build a system to achieve them; review the organization against objectives; set objectives for each job and unit; clarify policies; set up a review system.</p>\n<h3>Benefits</h3>\n<p>Recognizes the need for planning; clear objectives and <strong>accountability</strong>; <strong>participative</strong> management; <strong>job enrichment</strong>; good feedback; efforts focused on goals; better performance at every level; <strong>motivated people</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Weaknesses and problems</h3>\n<ul><li>Failure to <strong>teach the MBO philosophy</strong>; no <strong>guidelines</strong> for goal setters</li><li><strong>Difficulty setting goals</strong>; emphasis on <strong>short-run</strong> goals; danger of <strong>inflexibility</strong></li><li>Over-desire for verifiability: forcing numbers where they don't fit</li><li>Change disrupts planning; low morale; lower-level goals take precedence; over-centralized authority limits participation; <strong>paperwork</strong></li></ul>", "strategy": "<h3>Strategic planning</h3>\n<p><strong>Strategic planning</strong> is the process of developing the strategy (direction) and action plan to achieve an organization's goals.</p>\n<h3>The strategic planning process (Koontz model)</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Inputs</strong>: people, capital, managerial and technical skills, plus the <strong>goals of stakeholders</strong> (employees, consumers, suppliers, stockholders, government, community).</li><li><strong>Enterprise profile</strong> and <strong>top management orientation</strong> (values and vision).</li><li><strong>Purpose and major objectives</strong>.</li><li><strong>External environment</strong>: present and future <strong>threats and opportunities</strong>; <strong>industry analysis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Internal environment</strong>: <strong>strengths and weaknesses</strong>.</li><li><strong>Development of alternative strategies</strong>.</li><li><strong>Evaluation and strategic choice</strong>.</li><li><strong>Consistency testing and contingency planning</strong>.</li><li><strong>Medium and short-run planning</strong>, then <strong>implementation</strong> (re-engineering structure, staffing, leadership, control).</li></ol>\n<h3>Three levels of strategy</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question it answers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Corporate</strong></td><td><em>What business are we in?</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Business (competitive)</strong></td><td><em>How do we compete?</em></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Functional</strong></td><td><em>How do we support the business-level strategy?</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Five strategy tools (related but distinct)</h3>\n<p>Critical question analysis · Blue Ocean strategy · SWOT &amp; TOWS · Business portfolio (BCG) · Porter's generic model. Managers can use any one or a combination.</p>\n<h3>Critical question analysis</h3>\n<p>Asks basic questions before choosing a strategy:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>What is our business?</strong> <em>Glass-bottle makers lost out by seeing themselves as glass-bottle makers instead of liquid-container makers, as plastic and metal replaced glass.</em></li><li><strong>Who are our customers?</strong> What do they want? How much will they buy, at what price?</li><li>Do we want to be a <strong>product leader</strong>? Develop our own new products?</li><li>For marketing: where are customers and why do they buy? How do they buy? How should we sell? <strong>What do we offer that competitors don't?</strong></li></ul>", "blueocean": "<h3>Red oceans vs blue oceans</h3>\n<p>Developed by <strong>W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne</strong>. Instead of fighting rivals in crowded, saturated markets (<strong>red oceans</strong>), firms create <strong>blue oceans</strong>: <strong>untapped, uncontested market spaces</strong> ripe for growth. It pursues <strong>differentiation and low cost at the same time</strong>, creating new demand and making <strong>competition irrelevant</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Two principles</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Value innovation</strong>: superior value through <strong>differentiation + low cost</strong>, redefining industry boundaries rather than just beating rivals.</li><li><strong>Creation of uncontested market space</strong>.</li></ol>\n<h3>Advantages</h3>\n<p>Creates new demand and new markets; unique value propositions; avoids cut-throat competition; sustained profitability.</p>\n<h3>Challenges</h3>\n<p>High risk of failure; significant resource investment; uncertainty of unexplored markets.</p>\n<h3>Indian applications from the slides</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Hindustan Unilever, Project Shakti (2001)</strong>: direct selling of hygiene and sanitation products in rural India.</li><li><strong>Airtel</strong>: mobile banking services (2010).</li><li><strong>Flipkart</strong>: focused on the Indian consumer's needs with a unique shopping experience; later grocery delivery (2015).</li><li><strong>Paytm</strong>: created the mobile payments market in India when digital payments barely existed.</li></ul>\n<h3>The four actions framework (ERRC)</h3>\n<p>Sort the industry's competitive factors into four groups:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Eliminate</strong></td><td>Which factors the industry competes on should be dropped?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduce</strong></td><td>Which should be brought <strong>below</strong> industry standard?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Raise</strong></td><td>Which should be raised <strong>above</strong> industry standard to make rivals irrelevant?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Create</strong></td><td>What new factors should be offered that the industry never offered?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>ERRC</em>: two cost-cutting moves (E, R) and two value-adding moves (R, C) together give low cost + differentiation.</div>", "swot": "<h3>What is SWOT?</h3>\n<p><strong>Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats</strong>, credited to <strong>Albert Humphrey</strong> (Stanford, 1960s–70s). A framework to organize information from a <strong>situation analysis of the internal and external environment</strong> and move from everyday problems to a fresh perspective.</p>\n<h3>The four parts</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Helpful</th><th>Harmful</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Internal</strong> (controllable)</td><td><strong>Strengths</strong></td><td><strong>Weaknesses</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>External</strong> (uncontrollable)</td><td><strong>Opportunities</strong></td><td><strong>Threats</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Strengths</strong>: internal positive attributes giving an advantage. <em>Abundant finance, well-known brand, economies of scale, lower costs, superior management, good marketing and distribution, committed employees.</em></li><li><strong>Weaknesses</strong>: internal characteristics causing disadvantage; <strong>controllable</strong>, so they should be minimized. <em>Limited finance, weak R&amp;D, narrow product line, limited distribution, higher costs, outdated technology, weak image, under-trained staff.</em></li><li><strong>Opportunities</strong>: external conditions the firm can use to grow more profitably; from market, competition, industry/government or technology. <em>Rapid market growth, complacent rivals, changing tastes, new uses for the product, economic boom, deregulation.</em></li><li><strong>Threats</strong>: external factors <strong>beyond control</strong> that endanger the business, worse when linked to weaknesses. <em>Foreign competitors, substitute products, declining product life cycle, new rival strategies, more regulation, recession.</em></li></ul>\n<h3>Worked example: a car maker launching EVs</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>S</strong>: eco-friendly, silent, simpler mechanism, low maintenance, energy saved by regenerative braking</li><li><strong>W</strong>: high price, recharge time, expensive battery replacement, lack of charging infrastructure</li><li><strong>O</strong>: huge untapped EV market, demand for green tech, rising fuel costs, lower taxes, government subsidies</li><li><strong>T</strong>: entry of competitors, rising electricity cost, hybrid vehicles</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Sorting rule:</strong> first ask <em>inside or outside the firm?</em>, then <em>helpful or harmful?</em> A competitor's action is always external.</div>", "tows": "<h3>From diagnosis to strategy</h3>\n<p><strong>TOWS</strong> links a firm's internal strengths and weaknesses with its external opportunities and threats to produce <strong>four strategy types</strong>. SWOT <em>lists</em> the factors; TOWS <em>combines</em> them into action.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Opportunities (O)</th><th>Threats (T)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Strengths (S)</strong></td><td><strong>SO: Maxi-Maxi</strong>. Use strengths to take advantage of opportunities (potentially the most successful)</td><td><strong>ST: Maxi-Mini</strong>. Use strengths to cope with or avoid threats</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Weaknesses (W)</strong></td><td><strong>WO: Mini-Maxi</strong>. Developmental strategy to overcome weaknesses by using opportunities</td><td><strong>WT: Mini-Mini</strong>. Retrenchment, liquidation or joint venture to minimize both</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Retrenchment</strong> (a WT move): reducing the diversity or size of operations, withdrawing from markets or dropping products, to cut costs and become financially stable.</p>\n<h3>Apple examples from the slides</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>SO</strong>: strong brand + design skill + rising popularity of touchscreen smartphones → the <strong>first iPhone</strong>.</li><li><strong>ST</strong>: design strength and brand loyalty used to cope with the EU push for <strong>Type-C</strong> charging; loyal customers keep buying Apple's charger.</li><li><strong>WO</strong>: smaller product portfolio (weakness) overcome to enter the growing <strong>wearables</strong> market with the <strong>Apple Watch (2015)</strong>.</li><li><strong>WT</strong>: Apple TV device weak versus Roku/Fire Stick while streaming competition grew → launched <strong>Apple TV+</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Time dynamics</h3>\n<p>Situations change, so TOWS should be redone over time (past, present, present + T1, present + T2).</p>", "bcg": "<h3>The growth–share matrix</h3>\n<p>Developed by the <strong>Boston Consulting Group</strong> in the 1970s. Each <strong>strategic business unit (SBU)</strong> is plotted on two axes:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Relative market share</strong> = SBU's sales this year ÷ leading competitor's sales this year (shows competitive strength; based on the experience curve and cost leadership)</li><li><strong>Market growth rate</strong> = industry sales this year − industry sales last year (shows market attractiveness)</li></ul>\n<h3>The four cells</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>High relative share</th><th>Low relative share</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>High growth</strong></td><td><strong>Star</strong></td><td><strong>Question mark</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Low growth</strong></td><td><strong>Cash cow</strong></td><td><strong>Dog</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Question marks</strong> (high growth, low share): most businesses start here; they absorb lots of cash; may become stars and later cash cows, or fall to dogs. Invest heavily <strong>or</strong> drop.</li><li><strong>Stars</strong> (high growth, high share): leaders; need heavy investment to keep share; generate and consume lots of cash. When growth slows they become cash cows. <strong>Keep investing.</strong></li><li><strong>Cash cows</strong> (low growth, high share): the company's foundation, often yesterday's stars, in mature markets; generate <strong>more cash than needed</strong>; milk them with minimal investment to fund stars and question marks.</li><li><strong>Dogs</strong> (low growth, low share): <strong>cash traps</strong> in declining markets; minimize them; usually <strong>divest</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why use it?</h3>\n<p>To assess product profiles, cash demands, development cycles, and <strong>resource allocation and divestment</strong> decisions.</p>\n<h3>Main steps</h3>\n<ol><li>Divide the company into <strong>SBUs</strong>.</li><li>Assess each SBU's <strong>relative market share</strong> and <strong>industry growth</strong>.</li><li><strong>Classify</strong> SBUs into the matrix.</li><li>Develop <strong>strategic objectives</strong> for each.</li></ol>\n<h3>Four strategies</h3>\n<p><strong>Build/grow</strong> (question marks to stars) · <strong>Hold/maintain</strong> (stars, strong cows) · <strong>Harvest</strong> (cows, weak dogs/question marks) · <strong>Divest</strong> (dogs, hopeless question marks).</p>\n<h3>Application: the \"one size fits all\" mistake</h3>\n<p>Giving every unit the same growth or return target means cash cows hit targets too easily and reinvest in mature businesses, dogs get money in hopeless turnarounds, and stars and question marks receive only mediocre funding, so they never become cash cows.</p>\n<h3>Benefits and limitations</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Benefits</strong>: simple; quick screening of opportunities; shows how to use cash for future growth and profit.</li><li><strong>Limitations</strong>: only <strong>two dimensions</strong>; data for share and growth are hard to get; <strong>high share ≠ always profit</strong>, low-share businesses can be profitable; ignores <strong>synergy</strong> between units; growth isn't the only sign of attractiveness; \"market\" is not clearly defined; ignores small fast-growing competitors.</li></ul>", "porter": "<h3>Growth strategies</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Direct expansion</strong>: increasing size, revenue, operations or workforce.</li><li><strong>Merger</strong>: two companies of similar size combine to form a new company.</li><li><strong>Acquisition</strong>: a larger company buys a smaller one and absorbs its operations.</li></ul>\n<h3>Porter's generic competitive strategies</h3>\n<p>Long-run profitability depends on <strong>sustainable competitive advantage</strong>, which comes in two types: <strong>low cost</strong> or <strong>differentiation</strong>. Combined with the firm's <strong>market scope</strong> (broad or narrow), they give three generic strategies:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Low cost</th><th>Differentiation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Broad target</strong></td><td><strong>Cost leadership</strong></td><td><strong>Differentiation</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Narrow target</strong></td><td><strong>Cost focus</strong></td><td><strong>Differentiation focus</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol><li><strong>Overall cost leadership</strong>: gain advantage by having costs <strong>below competitors</strong>, watching costs in R&amp;D, sales and service, so it can sell cheap and still profit. <em>Timex: simple, low-cost watches for the mass market.</em></li><li><strong>Differentiation</strong>: offer something <strong>unique</strong> in quality or service so customers pay more. <em>Rolex (handmade, rigorously tested), Porsche, Caterpillar (prompt service and spare parts).</em></li><li><strong>Focus</strong>: concentrate on a specific <strong>region, product line or buyer group</strong>, using either:<ul><li><strong>Differentiation focus</strong>: <em>Longines selling highly jewelled watches to wealthy women; Fisher-Price calculators with big bright buttons for parents of pre-schoolers.</em></li><li><strong>Cost focus</strong>: low cost within the narrow market.</li></ul></li></ol>\n<h3>Stuck in the middle</h3>\n<p>A firm that tries every generic strategy but achieves none has <strong>no competitive advantage</strong> and is out-competed by cost leaders, differentiators and focusers. It may still profit only if the industry is very attractive or rivals are also stuck.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Two questions to classify any firm:</strong> <em>Cheaper or distinctive?</em> and <em>Whole market or a niche?</em></div>", "organizing": "<h3>What is an organization?</h3>\n<p>An <strong>organization</strong> is a collection of people pursuing <strong>defined objectives</strong>. Organizing divides work among employees and aligns tasks toward the company's ultimate goal. It is the <strong>second managerial function</strong>: it coordinates employees' work, procures resources and combines the two in pursuit of goals.</p>\n<h3>Why organizations matter</h3>\n<p>They bring resources together to achieve goals, produce goods and services <strong>efficiently</strong>, <strong>facilitate innovation</strong>, use modern manufacturing and information technology, <strong>adapt to and influence</strong> a changing environment, <strong>create value</strong> for owners, customers and employees, handle challenges of diversity and ethics, and <strong>motivate and coordinate</strong> employees.</p>\n<h3>Nature and purpose of organizing</h3>\n<p>Organization implies a <strong>formalized, intentional internal structure of roles and positions</strong>. For a role to mean something to a person, it needs:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Verifiable objectives</strong> (part of planning)</li><li>A clear idea of the <strong>major duties and activities</strong></li><li>An understood <strong>area of discretion or authority</strong>, so the person knows what they can do to reach goals</li></ol>\n<h3>The four steps in organizing</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Identify the activities</strong> of the organization (all the work that must be done).</li><li><strong>Organize (group) the activities</strong> into departments and jobs.</li><li><strong>Classify the authority</strong>: decide who has authority over what.</li><li><strong>Build the relationship between authority and responsibility</strong>, so everyone knows to whom they report and for what.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Example (a college fest):</strong> list every task (stage, food, sponsorship, publicity) → group them into committees → appoint committee heads with clear powers → define who reports to the fest secretary and who answers for what.</div>\n<h3>Syllabus extras often asked in papers: line, staff and delegation</h3>\n<p><em>These appear in the chapter syllabus and in past papers but are only named, not explained, in the slides. Build answers from the organizing principles below.</em></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Line authority</strong>: the direct superior–subordinate chain of command (the scalar chain). Line managers make and carry out decisions that achieve the main goals.</li><li><strong>Staff authority</strong>: advisory or support authority. Staff specialists (legal, HR, quality) <strong>advise</strong> line managers but don't command the operating chain.</li><li><strong>Functional staff authority</strong>: a specialist is given authority over a <em>specific process or policy</em> in other departments.</li><li><strong>Line-and-staff structure</strong>: the line chain runs the operations; staff units sit beside it to advise and support. Draw it as a vertical chain with side boxes linked by dotted lines.</li><li><strong>Delegation</strong>: giving a subordinate <strong>authority</strong> to achieve expected results. Steps to use as a scaffold: (1) decide the <strong>expected results</strong>, (2) <strong>assign tasks</strong>, (3) <strong>delegate enough authority</strong>, (4) hold the person <strong>responsible/accountable</strong> and review. Link it to Urwick's principles of <strong>delegation</strong> and <strong>parity of authority and responsibility</strong>.</li></ul>", "formal": "<h3>Formal organization</h3>\n<p>The <strong>intentional structure of roles</strong> in a formally organized enterprise: well-defined jobs, each with a measure of <strong>authority and responsibility</strong>. People achieve goals by following the norms the structure lays down; each person is responsible for their own performance.</p>\n<h3>Informal organization</h3>\n<p>A <strong>network of personal and social relationships</strong> that is <em>not required</em> by the formal organization but <strong>arises spontaneously</strong> as people associate. It is built on likes, dislikes, feelings and emotions; friendship groups are an example. Nobody designs it and it follows no written rules.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Slide picture:</strong> the same org chart overlaid with a golf club, a bowling team, morning-coffee regulars and weekend adventure-sports friends. A vice president and a divisional manager may be in the same informal group despite being far apart in the formal chart.</div>\n<h3>Formal vs informal organization</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Formal</th><th>Informal</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Designed and created by top management</td><td>Comes up on its own</td></tr><tr><td>Planned</td><td>Not planned; spontaneous</td></tr><tr><td>Authority and responsibility fixed and defined</td><td>Authority based on personal acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Requires an office to function</td><td>Functions through people</td></tr><tr><td>Rigid, definite, written constitution</td><td>Flexible, no fixed written rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "span": "<h3>Span of control (span of management)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>number of subordinates who report directly</strong> to an executive or supervisor. It directly shapes the organization:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Flat structure</strong>: <strong>few levels</strong>, <strong>wide</strong> spans.</li><li><strong>Tall structure</strong>: <strong>many levels</strong>, <strong>narrow</strong> spans.</li></ul>\n<p>The <strong>optimal span</strong> must be narrow enough to keep <strong>control</strong> over subordinates and wide enough to <strong>avoid micromanaging</strong> (interfering).</p>\n<h3>Eight factors affecting the optimal span</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Subordinates' training</strong>: well-trained people need less supervision (wider span).</li><li><strong>Delegation of authority</strong>: clear delegation allows a wider span.</li><li><strong>Planning</strong>: clear plans reduce the need for instructions.</li><li><strong>Rate of change</strong>: fast change needs closer contact (narrower span).</li><li><strong>Communication techniques</strong>: good techniques allow wider spans.</li><li><strong>Kind of activity</strong>: routine work allows wider spans than complex work.</li><li><strong>Kind of organization</strong>.</li><li><strong>Organization level</strong>: higher levels usually have narrower spans.</li></ol>\n<p>The slides also note <strong>proximity of subordinates</strong>, job complexity, employee ability, similarity of jobs, managerial ability and technology.</p>\n<h3>Wide vs narrow span</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Wide span (many per manager)</th><th>Narrow span (few per manager)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pros</strong></td><td>Quick decisions, less bureaucracy, development of lower levels, easier coordination, lower cost, motivated managers</td><td>Personal contact with employees, tightly organized team, benefit of specialization</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cons</strong></td><td>Manager under pressure, technology hurdles, chaos in large teams, too many viewpoints, issues overlooked</td><td>High managerial salaries, difficult communication, more bureaucracy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>Wide → flat → fast but stretched. Narrow → tall → close but costly.</em></div>", "urwick": "<h3>Who was Urwick?</h3>\n<p><strong>Lyndall Fownes Urwick</strong> was a British management consultant who <strong>integrated earlier ideas (such as Fayol's)</strong> into a comprehensive theory of administration. He wrote <em>The Elements of Business Administration</em> (1943).</p>\n<h3>The ten principles of organizing</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Unity of objectives</strong>: the structure is effective if it helps individuals contribute to <strong>enterprise objectives</strong>. Without a common aim, departments set their own, possibly conflicting, goals.</li><li><strong>Organizational efficiency</strong>: the structure should achieve objectives with the <strong>minimum of unsought consequences or costs</strong>.</li><li><strong>Specialization</strong>: divide work among subordinates by <strong>qualifications, abilities and skills</strong>.</li><li><strong>Span of management</strong>: there is a <strong>limit</strong> to how many people one person can manage effectively; the number depends on underlying variables.</li><li><strong>Scalar chain</strong>: a chain of command flowing from top to bottom that <strong>clarifies who reports to whom</strong>.</li><li><strong>Unity of command</strong>: every subordinate answers to <strong>one boss</strong> at a time.</li><li><strong>Delegation</strong>: delegate authority <strong>as far down as possible</strong>, and enough for people to achieve the expected results.</li><li><strong>Parity of authority and responsibility</strong>: responsibility <strong>cannot be greater or less</strong> than the authority delegated. Subordinates' responsibility to superiors is <strong>absolute</strong>, and superiors <strong>cannot escape</strong> responsibility for their subordinates' activities.</li><li><strong>Flexibility</strong>: the structure must be <strong>simple</strong> and <strong>adaptable</strong> to changes in business, technology and procedures.</li><li><strong>Functional definition</strong>: every position and department must have a clear definition of <strong>results expected, activities to be undertaken and authority delegated</strong>.</li></ol>\n<h3>How to apply them to a case (most-asked topic!)</h3>\n<p>For each case clue, ask which principle it <strong>violates or supports</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Clue in the case</th><th>Principle</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Departments chasing their own conflicting targets</td><td>Unity of objectives</td></tr><tr><td>Two managers giving one employee orders</td><td>Unity of command</td></tr><tr><td>A manager with 40 direct reports struggling</td><td>Span of management</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility given but no power to act</td><td>Parity of authority &amp; responsibility / delegation</td></tr><tr><td>Nobody knows what a role is supposed to deliver</td><td>Functional definition</td></tr><tr><td>Jobs given to people without the right skills</td><td>Specialization</td></tr><tr><td>Structure can't cope with new technology</td><td>Flexibility</td></tr><tr><td>Wasteful, costly structure</td><td>Organizational efficiency</td></tr><tr><td>Unclear reporting lines</td><td>Scalar chain</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>\"Goals, Efficiency, Expertise, Span, Chain; One boss, Delegate, Parity, Flex, Define.\"</em></div>", "departmentation": "<h3>What is departmentation?</h3>\n<p>Designing a structure has two steps: first <strong>divide the whole work into jobs</strong> so no important activity is left out; then <strong>group similar (homogeneous) jobs</strong> and decide how the groups relate. This grouping is <strong>departmentation</strong>: a logical division of work into <strong>manageable units</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Eight patterns</h3>\n<ol><li>Simple numbers 2. Time 3. Enterprise function 4. Territory/geography 5. Product 6. Process/equipment 7. Customer 8. Matrix</li></ol>\n<h3>1. By simple numbers</h3>\n<p>Group people doing the <strong>same duties</strong> under one supervisor, purely by headcount (e.g. labour gangs). An age-old method falling into disuse because technology demands <strong>specialized skills</strong>, and specialist groups are more efficient than groups formed by number. Useful only at the <strong>lowest level</strong>.</p>\n<h3>2. By time</h3>\n<p>One of the <strong>oldest</strong> forms, used at lower levels: grouping activities by <strong>shifts</strong> (e.g. hospitals, production plants).</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Service available 24×7 instead of one 8-hour shift</td><td>Night-shift supervision is difficult</td></tr><tr><td>Continuous processes aren't interrupted</td><td>Fatigue: hard to change the biological clock</td></tr><tr><td>Expensive equipment used more than 8 hours a day</td><td>Coordination and communication problems between shifts</td></tr><tr><td>Students can work night shifts</td><td>Overtime pay raises costs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>3. By enterprise function</h3>\n<p>The <strong>most widely used</strong> form: group activities by <strong>function</strong> (production, sales, finance, HR). Similar activities sit in one department under one chain of command, consistent with <strong>specialization</strong>. It eases <strong>coordination</strong> because one supervisor runs one major area.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Economies of scale within departments</td><td>Slow response to environmental change</td></tr><tr><td>In-depth knowledge and skill development</td><td>Decisions pile up at the top (hierarchy overload)</td></tr><tr><td>Accomplishes functional goals</td><td>Poor horizontal coordination between departments</td></tr><tr><td>Best with one or a few products</td><td>Less innovation; restricted view of overall goals</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "departmentation2": "<h3>4. By territory / geography</h3>\n<p>Group activities by <strong>region</strong> when customers or operations are <strong>geographically dispersed</strong>. <em>Coca-Cola: a North American sector and an international sector covering the Pacific Rim, Europe, Africa and Latin America.</em></p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Places responsibility at lower levels</td><td>Needs more people with general-manager ability</td></tr><tr><td>Emphasis on local markets and problems</td><td>Hard to keep economical central services</td></tr><tr><td>Better coordination within a region</td><td>Harder for top management to control</td></tr><tr><td>Economies of local operation</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Better face-to-face communication locally</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Measurable training ground for general managers</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Exam favourite:</strong> <em>\"An MNC with a sales field network all over the country\"</em> → <strong>territorial</strong> departmentation (regions → zones → areas), with these merits and limitations.</div>\n<h3>5. By process / equipment</h3>\n<p>Group activities around a <strong>process or type of equipment</strong> (e.g. a painting department, a welding shop, a machining section). It uses specialized equipment and skills efficiently.</p>\n<h3>6. By customer</h3>\n<p>Group activities by <strong>common customers or customer types</strong> (e.g. retail vs corporate banking). The assumption is that each customer group has <strong>common problems and needs</strong> best met by specialists.</p>\n<h3>7. By product / service</h3>\n<p>Group activities around <strong>products or product lines</strong>; also useful in service businesses. <em>A food company with frozen food, dairy and produce departments.</em> It focuses responsibility on each product's results.</p>", "matrix": "<h3>8. Matrix departmentation</h3>\n<p>A matrix structure <strong>superimposes a horizontal set of divisional (project/product) reporting relationships onto a vertical functional structure</strong>. Its essence is <strong>combining functional and project/product departmentation</strong> in the same structure.</p>\n<h3>How it works</h3>\n<ul><li>Functional heads (engineering, finance, marketing) run the <strong>vertical</strong> lines.</li><li>Project managers run <strong>horizontal</strong> lines across functions.</li><li>A team member therefore has <strong>two bosses</strong>: a functional manager and a project manager.</li></ul>\n<h3>Merits and limitations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Merits</th><th>Limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pools experts from different functions on one project</td><td>Breaks <strong>unity of command</strong> (two bosses)</td></tr><tr><td>Flexible use of specialists across projects</td><td>Possible conflict and confusion over authority</td></tr><tr><td>Project focus with functional depth</td><td>Needs strong coordination and communication</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>How to spot it in a case:</strong> experts from different departments brought together for a <strong>project</strong> while still belonging to their home department → <strong>matrix</strong>.</div>\n<h3>Identification practice</h3>\n<p>The slides show four org charts to classify: <strong>functional</strong> (production / sales / finance), <strong>product</strong> (product lines), <strong>geographical</strong> (regions) and <strong>process</strong> (stages of manufacture). Read the <strong>labels on the second level</strong> of any chart to decide which basis is being used.</p>", "hrm": "<h3>HRM vs HRD</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>HRM (Human Resource Management)</strong> manages <strong>people within the organization</strong> and handles administrative tasks, policies and procedures.</li><li><strong>HRD (Human Resource Development)</strong> <strong>enhances employees' skills, knowledge and capabilities</strong> through learning and development.</li></ul>\n<p>HRM is the <strong>bigger concept</strong>: it covers planning, staffing, developing, monitoring, maintaining, managing relationships and evaluating. HRD covers the <strong>development part</strong>: training, learning, career development, talent management, performance appraisal, engagement and empowerment.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>HRM</th><th>HRD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Managing human resources</td><td>Developing employees</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>Broad (includes HRD)</td><td>Narrower, a part of HRM</td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td>HR planning, job analysis, recruitment, selection, placement, induction &amp; orientation</td><td>Training &amp; development, career development, organizational development</td></tr><tr><td>Nature</td><td>Largely administrative and maintenance</td><td>Growth and learning oriented</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Benefits of integrating HRM and HRD</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Holistic employee development</strong>: immediate support plus long-term skill growth.</li><li><strong>Strategic alignment</strong>: HR practices directly support organizational goals.</li><li><strong>Improved productivity</strong>: better skills and engagement raise efficiency.</li><li><strong>Enhanced talent retention</strong>: investing in people builds loyalty and cuts turnover.</li></ol>\n<h3>Functions of HRM</h3>\n<p>HRM functions are divided into <strong>managerial functions</strong> (planning, organizing, directing, controlling the HR work) and <strong>operative functions</strong> (procurement, development, compensation, integration and maintenance of people).</p>\n<h3>Key definitions</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Job</strong>: a group of tasks that must be performed for the organization to achieve its goals.</li><li><strong>Position</strong>: the tasks and responsibilities performed by <strong>one person</strong>; there is a position for every individual.</li><li><strong>Job analysis</strong>: the systematic process of determining the <strong>skills, duties and knowledge</strong> required for jobs.</li><li><strong>Job description</strong>: a document on the job's <strong>tasks, duties and responsibilities</strong>.</li><li><strong>Job specification</strong>: the <strong>minimum qualifications</strong> a person needs to do the job.</li></ul>", "jobanalysis": "<h3>Job analysis</h3>\n<p>The <strong>systematic process of determining the skills, duties and knowledge</strong> required to perform jobs. It answers: What skills are needed? What qualification? What abilities are desirable? What experience? What responsibilities?</p>\n<p>Job analysis produces two documents:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Job description (about the <strong>job</strong>)</th><th>Job specification (about the <strong>person</strong>)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Tasks, duties, responsibilities</strong></td><td><strong>Knowledge, skills, abilities</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Job title, department, reporting line, purpose, duties, working conditions</td><td>Education, experience, skills, personal and physical requirements</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Where job analysis is used</h3>\n<p>Human resource planning · recruitment · selection · training and development · performance appraisal · compensation and benefits · safety and health · employee and labour relations · legal considerations · job analysis for teams.</p>\n<h3>Slide examples</h3>\n<ul><li><em>Centre for Policy Research, Librarian</em>: <strong>specification</strong> = post-graduate degree in library science, five years as assistant/deputy librarian, computer knowledge, M.Phil/Ph.D preferred.</li><li><em>\"The best job in the world\", island caretaker</em>: <strong>description</strong> = clean the pool, feed the fish, collect the mail, explore and report back.</li></ul>\n<h3>Writing one in the exam (e.g. a fresher role)</h3>\n<p><strong>Job description</strong>: title · department · reports to · job purpose (one line) · 4–6 key duties · working conditions. <strong>Job specification</strong>: essential qualification · experience (fresher / 0–1 yr) · technical skills · soft skills · personal attributes.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>Description = the Duties. Specification = the Skills (of the person).</em></div>", "recruitment": "<h3>Recruitment → selection → placement</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Recruitment</strong>: searching for <strong>prospective employees</strong> who suit the job specification.</li><li><strong>Selection</strong>: choosing the <strong>right person</strong> from among the applicants.</li><li><strong>Placement</strong>: putting the selected person on the job <strong>most suited</strong> to them.</li></ol>\n<h3>What is recruitment?</h3>\n<p>The process of <strong>locating, identifying and attracting capable candidates</strong>, for current or future needs. It is a critical activity for many corporations. The <strong>recruitment process</strong> is a strategic framework to find and attract the best candidates; details vary by industry.</p>\n<h3>The 10-step recruitment process</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Identifying hiring needs</strong>: is there a vacancy or new role?</li><li><strong>Job analysis and job description</strong>: define the role.</li><li><strong>Sourcing candidates</strong>: advertise, use portals, referrals, campuses.</li><li><strong>Screening and shortlisting</strong>: filter applications.</li><li><strong>Interviews</strong>.</li><li><strong>Assessments and tests</strong>.</li><li><strong>Background checks</strong>.</li><li><strong>Decision making</strong>.</li><li><strong>Offer and negotiation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Onboarding</strong>: welcome and integrate the new hire.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory story:</strong> <em>We <strong>need</strong> someone → write the <strong>job</strong> → <strong>source</strong> people → <strong>screen</strong> them → <strong>interview</strong> → <strong>test</strong> → <strong>check</strong> backgrounds → <strong>decide</strong> → <strong>offer</strong> → <strong>onboard</strong>.</em></div>", "sources": "<h3>Types of recruitment</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Internal</strong>: hiring from within for a vacancy.</li><li><strong>External</strong>: sourcing from outside.</li><li><strong>Campus</strong>: fresh graduates directly from universities.</li><li><strong>Executive search</strong>: specialized hiring for senior/executive roles.</li><li><strong>Contingent</strong>: agencies paid <strong>only if</strong> they fill the position.</li><li><strong>Remote</strong>: hiring people to work outside a traditional office.</li><li><strong>Diversity and inclusion</strong>: strategies to build a diverse workforce.</li><li><strong>Referral</strong>: candidates recommended by current employees or contacts.</li><li><strong>Social media</strong>: LinkedIn, Twitter, Facebook.</li><li><strong>Full-cycle</strong>: covering every stage from sourcing to onboarding.</li><li><strong>Digital</strong> recruitment.</li></ol>\n<h3>Challenges in recruitment</h3>\n<p>Talent shortage · high competition · time and resource constraints · evolving job market and skills gap · diversity and inclusion · candidate experience · technology and automation · compliance and regulations · retention and turnover · market and industry dynamics.</p>\n<h3>Sources of recruitment</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Internal sources</th><th>External sources</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promotion</td><td>Press advertisements</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer</td><td>Educational institutes (campus)</td></tr><tr><td>Demotion</td><td>Placement agencies</td></tr><tr><td>Retired employees</td><td>Employment agencies (exchanges)</td></tr><tr><td>Notice boards</td><td>Unsolicited applicants</td></tr><tr><td>Job posting</td><td>Employee recommendations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Choosing between them</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Internal</strong>: faster and cheaper, motivates staff (promotion chances), candidates already know the culture; but limits fresh ideas and leaves a chain of vacancies.</li><li><strong>External</strong>: brings new skills and ideas and a wider choice; but costlier, slower and riskier, and can demotivate insiders.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Exam approach:</strong> for a senior specialized role → executive search / external; for a growing role with good insiders → promotion / job posting; for mass entry-level hiring → campus recruitment and job portals. Justify each choice from the case.</div>", "selection": "<h3>What is selection?</h3>\n<p><strong>Picking, from among the candidates, the one who best meets the position's requirements.</strong> Recruitment is <em>positive</em> (attract many); selection <em>screens out</em> until the best fit remains.</p>\n<h3>The six steps of selection</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Initial / preliminary interview</strong>: a first meeting to check <strong>basic qualifications and fit</strong>.</li><li><strong>Application form</strong>: gives factual information and becomes part of the employee's records: identity, education, experience, expected salary and benefits, community activities.</li><li><strong>Reference checks</strong>: names of former employers and two or three unrelated people who know the candidate; done properly, they reveal what interviews and tests can't.</li><li><strong>Employment tests</strong>: instruments that <strong>measure selected qualities</strong> against the job specification; a sample of behaviour used to predict future performance.</li><li><strong>Interview</strong>: the main in-depth assessment.</li><li><strong>Selection decision</strong>: the hiring manager picks the best fit after feedback; often a <strong>runner-up</strong> is kept in case the first choice declines or fails later testing/training.</li></ol>\n<h3>Types of employment tests (measures of human psychology)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Tests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aptitude</strong></td><td>Intelligence/mental, emotional quotient, skill, aptitude, psychomotor, clerical aptitude</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Achievement</strong></td><td>Job knowledge, work sample</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Situational</strong></td><td>Group discussion, in-basket</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Interest</strong></td><td>Interest tests</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personality</strong></td><td>Objective, projective, situation tests</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multi-dimensional</strong></td><td>Combined tests</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Types of interviews</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Preliminary</strong>: informal, unstructured.</li><li><strong>Core</strong>: background-information, job and probing, <strong>stress</strong>, group-discussion, formal/structured, <strong>panel</strong>, <strong>depth</strong> interviews.</li><li><strong>Decision-making</strong> interview (final).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>Meet → fill → check → test → talk → decide.</em></div>", "training": "<h3>What is training?</h3>\n<p><strong>Acquiring specific knowledge and skills for a particular job or task.</strong> It is usually <strong>short-term</strong> and aims to improve <strong>current</strong> job performance: formal courses, on-the-job training or coaching. (<em>Development</em> is longer-term growth for future roles.)</p>\n<h3>The five-stage training &amp; development process</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Needs assessment</strong>: find the gap between required and actual skills.</li><li><strong>Design and development</strong>: set objectives, content and methods.</li><li><strong>Delivery</strong>: run the training.</li><li><strong>Evaluation</strong>: did learning and performance improve?</li><li><strong>Follow-up</strong>: reinforce and support transfer to the job.</li></ol>\n<h3>Current trends</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Digital learning</strong></li><li><strong>Personalized training programmes</strong></li><li><strong>Microlearning</strong> (short, focused units)</li><li><strong>Gamification</strong></li><li><strong>Diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) training</strong></li><li><strong>Soft-skills development</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>Company examples from the slides</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Microsoft</strong>: technical training, leadership and career workshops; an annual <strong>Hackathon</strong> for collaborative ideas.</li><li><strong>Google</strong>: leadership, technical and wellness programmes; employees <strong>teach and learn from each other</strong>.</li><li><strong>Amazon</strong>: extensive on-the-job training; <strong>Career Choice</strong> pays 95% of tuition for high-demand fields.</li><li><strong>Starbucks</strong>: the <strong>Starbucks Experience</strong>: online and in-person training on customer service, coffee and drink preparation, with continuous coaching.</li><li><strong>Walmart</strong>: customer service, compliance and leadership training plus <strong>tuition assistance</strong>.</li></ul>", "trainingmethods": "<h3>On-the-job vs off-the-job methods</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>On-the-job (learn while working)</th><th>Off-the-job (learn away from the workplace)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Apprenticeship</strong> programmes</td><td><strong>Conferences</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Coaching</strong></td><td><strong>Films</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Internship</strong> training</td><td><strong>Case study</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Job rotation</strong></td><td><strong>Computer modelling</strong></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Vestibule training</strong> (a replica of the work setup)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td><strong>Programmed instruction</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Choosing a method:</strong> on-the-job suits practical skills learnt with real equipment; off-the-job suits concepts, risky equipment, or when work can't be disrupted.</p>\n<h3>Induction vs orientation</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Induction</th><th>Orientation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The <strong>formal process of introducing</strong> a new employee to the <strong>organization</strong>, its policies and culture</td><td>A <strong>structured programme</strong> to familiarize new employees with their <strong>job role, team and work environment</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Organization-wide, \"welcome to the company\"</td><td>Job-specific, \"here is your role and team\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Sensitivity training</h3>\n<p>Training that makes people <strong>more aware of their own goals and prejudices</strong>, and <strong>more sensitive to others</strong> and to the dynamics of group interaction.</p>", "staffing": "<h3>The systems approach to staffing (draw this diagram!)</h3>\n<p>Staffing is seen as a <strong>system</strong> linked to the rest of management, inside the <strong>external environment</strong> and shaped by the <strong>internal environment</strong> (personnel policies and reward system).</p>\n<p><strong>Flow of the model:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Enterprise plans</strong> → <strong>organization plans</strong></li><li>→ the <strong>number and kinds of managers required</strong></li><li>compared with the <strong>manager inventory</strong> (who we already have)</li><li>→ <strong>analysis of present and future needs for managers</strong></li><li>→ filled from <strong>external sources</strong> and <strong>internal sources</strong></li><li>→ <strong>recruitment, selection, placement, promotion, separation</strong></li><li>→ <strong>appraisal, career strategy, training and development</strong></li><li>→ <strong>leading and controlling</strong></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Dashed feedback lines</strong> connect every box back to the plans and to the internal environment.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Drawing tip:</strong> a big outer box labelled <em>External environment</em>; two stacked boxes on the left (<em>managers required</em>, <em>manager inventory</em>) meeting at <em>analysis of needs</em>; it splits into <em>external/internal sources</em>, rejoins at <em>recruitment… separation</em>, then <em>appraisal/training</em>, then <em>leading &amp; controlling</em>; a dashed line along the bottom labelled <em>Internal environment: personnel policies, reward system</em>.</div>", "managerdevelopment": "<h3>Developing excellent managers: eight considerations</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Instilling a willingness to learn</strong></li><li><strong>Accelerating management development</strong></li><li><strong>Planning for innovation</strong></li><li><strong>Measuring and rewarding management effectively</strong></li><li><strong>Tailoring information</strong> to managers' needs</li><li><strong>Expanding research and development in tools and techniques</strong></li><li><strong>Developing more managerial inventories</strong></li><li><strong>Creating strong intellectual leadership</strong></li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> start with the <em>person</em> (learn, accelerate), then the <em>system</em> (innovation, rewards, information), then the <em>organization's capability</em> (tools, inventories, intellectual leadership).</div>", "leadership": "<h3>What is leadership?</h3>\n<p><strong>Leadership</strong> is the art or process of <strong>influencing people</strong> so that they strive <strong>willingly and enthusiastically</strong> toward group goals. Ideally people develop not just willingness to work but <em>\"willingness to work with <strong>zeal</strong> and <strong>confidence</strong>.\"</em></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Zeal</strong>: ardour, earnestness and intensity in work.</li><li><strong>Confidence</strong>: comes from experience and technical ability.</li></ul>\n<p>Leadership means <strong>leading, influencing, commanding and guiding</strong> people.</p>\n<h3>Managers vs leaders</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Managers</th><th>Leaders</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Focus on <strong>things</strong></td><td>Focus on <strong>people</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Do things right</strong></td><td><strong>Do the right things</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Plan, organize, direct, control</td><td>Inspire, influence, motivate</td></tr><tr><td>Follow the rules</td><td>Build and shape entities</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Four ingredients of leadership</h3>\n<ol><li>Ability to <strong>use power effectively and responsibly</strong>.</li><li>Ability to <strong>understand that people have different motivating forces</strong> at different times and situations.</li><li>Ability to <strong>inspire</strong>.</li><li>Ability to <strong>develop a climate</strong> that arouses and responds to motivation.</li></ol>\n<h3>Qualities of an effective leader</h3>\n<p>Not arrogant · not miserly · as open as possible with subordinates · makes sure the team knows what is expected · promotes competence and inspires · sees things from others' point of view · puts the organization's needs above their own.</p>", "maslow": "<h3>Motive, motivator, satisfaction, motivation</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Motive</strong></td><td>An <strong>inner state</strong> that energizes and directs behaviour toward goals; the driving force</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Motivator</strong></td><td>The <strong>technique</strong> used to motivate people (e.g. a bonus)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Satisfaction</strong></td><td>The <strong>contentment</strong> felt when a want is satisfied</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Motivation</strong></td><td>The <strong>drive and effort</strong> to satisfy a want; the process of stimulating people to act</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Easy way:</strong> <em>motive</em> is inside you, a <em>motivator</em> is what the manager offers, <em>motivation</em> is the resulting drive, <em>satisfaction</em> is how you feel once the want is met.</div>\n<h3>Maslow's hierarchy of needs</h3>\n<p>Needs form a ladder; <strong>as each level is substantially satisfied, the next becomes dominant</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Physiological</strong>: basic survival needs.</li><li><strong>Safety</strong>: security and protection.</li><li><strong>Social</strong>: belonging, acceptance, friendship.</li><li><strong>Esteem</strong>: respect, status, recognition.</li><li><strong>Self-actualization</strong>: <em>\"the drive to become what one is capable of becoming.\"</em></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Lower-order needs</strong> (physiological, safety, social) are satisfied <strong>externally</strong>; <strong>higher-order needs</strong> (esteem, self-actualization) are satisfied <strong>internally</strong>.</p>\n<h3>What organizations do at each level</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>How organizations meet it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-actualization</td><td>Skill development, chance to be creative, promotions, full control over one's job</td></tr><tr><td>Esteem</td><td>Merit pay rises, recognition, challenging tasks, participation in decisions, advancement</td></tr><tr><td>Social</td><td>Chances to interact, be accepted and make friends: parties, picnics, trips, office sports teams</td></tr><tr><td>Safety</td><td>Safe working conditions, job security, fringe benefits (medical insurance, sick pay, pensions)</td></tr><tr><td>Physiological</td><td>Adequate salary, work breaks, safe working conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Case method:</strong> read what the employee complains about or wants, name the level, then suggest the matching organizational action. \"<em>I feel isolated from my team</em>\" → social. \"<em>Nobody notices my work</em>\" → esteem.</div>", "herzberg": "<h3>Two-factor (motivation–hygiene) theory</h3>\n<p>Also called the <strong>dual-factor theory</strong>. Herzberg argued there are <strong>two separate, mutually exclusive sets of factors</strong>: one causes <strong>job satisfaction</strong>, the other <strong>dissatisfaction</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Motivators (satisfiers)</th><th>Hygiene factors (dissatisfiers)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Performance and <strong>achievement</strong></td><td><strong>Salary</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Recognition</strong></td><td><strong>Working conditions</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Job status</td><td>Physical workspace</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Responsibility</strong></td><td>Relationship with colleagues</td></tr><tr><td>Opportunities for <strong>advancement</strong></td><td>Relationship with supervisor</td></tr><tr><td>Personal growth</td><td>Quality of supervisor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>The work itself</strong></td><td><strong>Policies and rules</strong></td></tr><tr><td><em>Improving these increases satisfaction</em></td><td><em>Improving these decreases dissatisfaction</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Motivators relate to <strong>job content</strong>; hygiene factors relate to <strong>job context</strong>. Removing hygiene problems only makes people <strong>\"not dissatisfied\"</strong>; to truly motivate you must add motivators.</p>\n<h3>The four combinations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Low motivation factors</th><th>High motivation factors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>High hygiene</strong></td><td>Few complaints but <strong>not motivated</strong></td><td><strong>Ideal</strong>: no complaints and motivated</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Low hygiene</strong></td><td><strong>Worst</strong>: not motivated and complaining</td><td>Motivated but <strong>have complaints</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Maslow vs Herzberg</h3>\n<p>Herzberg's <strong>motivators</strong> match Maslow's <strong>upper levels</strong> (esteem and self-actualization). His <strong>hygiene factors</strong> match the <strong>lower levels</strong> (physiological, safety, social/belonging).</p>", "xy": "<h3>McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y</h3>\n<p>Two contrasting sets of <strong>managerial assumptions</strong> about people. Theory X stresses <strong>supervision</strong>; Theory Y stresses <strong>rewards and recognition</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Theory X assumptions (traditional)</h3>\n<ul><li>The average employee <strong>dislikes work</strong> and avoids it where possible.</li><li>So people must be <strong>persuaded, compelled or threatened</strong> with punishment.</li><li><strong>Close supervision</strong>; managers are dictatorial.</li><li>Employees rank <strong>job security</strong> highest and have little ambition.</li><li>They <strong>dislike responsibility</strong>, <strong>resist change</strong>, and need <strong>formal direction</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Theory Y assumptions (based on research)</h3>\n<ul><li>Work can be as <strong>natural</strong> as rest or play.</li><li>People can use <strong>self-direction and self-control</strong> when committed to objectives; threats aren't the only way.</li><li>Rewarding, satisfying work brings <strong>loyalty and commitment</strong>.</li><li>The average person can <strong>accept and even seek responsibility</strong>.</li><li>People have <strong>creativity, resourcefulness and innovative potential</strong> that should be fully used.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Theory X</th><th>Theory Y</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Work</td><td>Disliked</td><td>Natural</td></tr><tr><td>Ambition</td><td>Little or none</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>Avoided</td><td>Accepted and sought</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership style</td><td><strong>Autocratic</strong></td><td><strong>Democratic</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>Constant</td><td>Little needed</td></tr><tr><td>Control</td><td>Tight</td><td>Lenient</td></tr><tr><td>Authority</td><td><strong>Centralized</strong></td><td><strong>Decentralized</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Self-motivation</td><td>Absent</td><td>Present</td></tr><tr><td>Needs focused on</td><td>Physiological and security</td><td>Social, esteem, self-actualization</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Link it up:</strong> Theory X ↔ Maslow's lower needs ↔ autocratic style. Theory Y ↔ higher needs ↔ democratic style.</div>", "motivationtechniques": "<h3>Positive techniques</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Praise</strong> workers and give due credit.</li><li>Take a <strong>sincere interest</strong> in subordinates as individuals.</li><li>Promote <strong>healthy competition</strong>.</li><li>Develop <strong>pride</strong> in the workplace.</li><li><strong>Delegate</strong> substantial responsibility.</li><li>Fix <strong>fair wages</strong> and individual or group <strong>incentives</strong>.</li><li>Set up a <strong>suggestion system</strong>.</li><li>Provide opportunities for <strong>growth and promotion</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Negative techniques</h3>\n<p><strong>Reprimanding</strong> (scolding), <strong>demotion</strong>, <strong>lay-offs</strong>, <strong>discharge</strong>. <em>The proper balance of positive and negative techniques is the mark of a skilful manager.</em></p>\n<h3>Special motivational techniques</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Money</strong>: economists and most managers rank it high; behavioural scientists rank it low.</li><li><strong>Positive reinforcement</strong>: reward desired behaviour so it is repeated.</li><li><strong>Job enrichment</strong>: build more challenge, responsibility and achievement into the job.</li><li><strong>Participation</strong>: involve people in decisions that affect them.</li></ol>", "authoritystyles": "<h3>Three theories of leadership behaviour</h3>\n<ol><li>Leadership based on the <strong>use of authority</strong></li><li><strong>Likert's</strong> four systems of managing</li><li>The <strong>managerial grid</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>Styles based on the use of authority (Kurt Lewin, 1939)</h3>\n<p><strong>Leadership style</strong> is the manner of providing direction, implementing plans and motivating people.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Autocratic leader</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Commands and expects compliance</strong>; leads by the ability to give or withhold rewards and punishment.</li><li>Tells employees what to do and how, <strong>without their advice</strong>.</li><li>Suitable when the leader <strong>has all the information</strong>, <strong>time is short</strong>, and employees are well motivated.</li><li>Should be used <strong>rarely</strong>. <em>Examples: military, manufacturing, construction.</em></li></ul>\n<p><strong>2. Democratic / participative leader</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Consults</strong> subordinates and <strong>encourages participation</strong>, but <strong>keeps the final decision</strong>.</li><li>Used when the leader has <strong>part of the information</strong> and employees have the rest.</li><li>A sign of <strong>strength, not weakness</strong>: it makes employees part of the team and improves decisions.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>3. Free-rein (delegative) leader</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Uses power <strong>very little</strong>; gives subordinates <strong>high independence</strong> to set their own goals and methods.</li><li>The leader is <strong>still responsible</strong> for the decisions.</li><li>Used when employees can <strong>analyse the situation themselves</strong> and know what to do.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Where the power sits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Autocratic</td><td><strong>With the leader</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Democratic</td><td><strong>Shared</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Free-rein</td><td><strong>With the employees</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Forces that influence which style to use</h3>\n<p>How much <strong>time</strong> is available · relationships based on <strong>respect and trust</strong> or not · <strong>who has the information</strong> · how well <strong>employees are trained</strong> and how well the leader knows the task · <strong>internal conflicts</strong> · <strong>stress levels</strong> · <strong>type of task</strong> (structured, unstructured, complicated, simple) · <strong>laws or established procedures</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Case method:</strong> creative R&amp;D team with expertise → democratic or free-rein. Emergency, unskilled staff, no time → autocratic. Always justify with the case's facts.</div>", "likert": "<h3>Likert's four systems of managing</h3>\n<p><strong>Rensis Likert</strong> and associates (University of Michigan) studied managers in <strong>200 organizations over three decades</strong> and proposed four systems, which can be read as leadership styles.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>System 1: Exploitative-authoritative</th><th>System 2: Benevolent-authoritative</th><th>System 3: Consultative</th><th>System 4: Participative</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Trust in subordinates</strong></td><td>None</td><td><strong>Patronizing</strong> (condescending)</td><td>Substantial but not complete</td><td><strong>Complete</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Motivation</strong></td><td>Fear, threats, punishment, occasional rewards</td><td>Rewards plus some fear and punishment</td><td>Rewards, occasional punishment, some participation</td><td>Participation and involvement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Communication</strong></td><td><strong>Downward</strong> only</td><td>Some upward</td><td>Up and down</td><td>Up, down and lateral; act as a group</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decisions</strong></td><td>Only at the <strong>top</strong>; imposed</td><td>Some delegation, close control</td><td>Broad policy at top, specific decisions lower down</td><td><strong>Throughout</strong> the organization</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Responsibility</strong></td><td>Upper levels only</td><td>Managerial levels, not lower</td><td>Spread widely</td><td>Widespread at all levels</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Teamwork</strong></td><td>Very little</td><td>Very little</td><td>Fair amount</td><td><strong>High</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Likert's finding:</strong> managers using <strong>System 4</strong> were the most successful leaders; their departments set and achieved goals better and were <strong>more productive</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>E–B–C–P: Exploit → Be kind (but boss) → Consult → Participate.</em> Trust and participation rise from 1 to 4.</div>", "grid": "<h3>The managerial grid (Blake &amp; Mouton)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>self-assessment tool</strong> developed by <strong>Robert Blake and Jane Mouton</strong> in the 1960s to identify a manager's style.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Horizontal axis</strong>: <strong>concern for production</strong> (1 = low, 9 = high).</li><li><strong>Vertical axis</strong>: <strong>concern for people</strong> (1 = low, 9 = high).</li><li>That gives <strong>81 possible positions</strong>. Write coordinates as <em>(production, people)</em>.</li></ul>\n<h3>The five key styles</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Grid</th><th>Name</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1,1</strong></td><td><strong>Impoverished</strong></td><td>Minimum effort on both; managers have practically abandoned the job and just pass messages along</td></tr><tr><td><strong>1,9</strong></td><td><strong>Country club</strong></td><td>High concern for people, little for production; relaxed, friendly, happy, but no coordinated effort toward goals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5,5</strong></td><td><strong>Middle of the road</strong></td><td>Medium concern for both; adequate but not outstanding morale and output; goals not set too high; benevolently autocratic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>9,1</strong></td><td><strong>Authority-compliance / autocratic task</strong></td><td>Only efficiency matters; work arranged so human elements interfere minimally; autocratic</td></tr><tr><td><strong>9,9</strong></td><td><strong>Team management</strong></td><td>Highest dedication to both; work done by committed people with a \"common stake\", trust and respect</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Trap:</strong> the <strong>first</strong> number is <strong>production</strong>, the second is <strong>people</strong>. 1,9 = low production, high people = country club.</div>\n<h3>Putting the three theories together</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Use of authority</th><th>Likert</th><th>Managerial grid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Autocratic</td><td>System 1, System 2</td><td>9,1</td></tr><tr><td>Democratic</td><td>System 3, System 4</td><td>9,9 / 5,5</td></tr><tr><td>Free-rein</td><td></td><td>1,1 / 1,9 tendencies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "continuum": "<h3>Leadership as a continuum (Tannenbaum &amp; Schmidt)</h3>\n<p>Developed by <strong>Robert Tannenbaum and W. H. Schmidt</strong>. Leadership is not a choice between just two styles (authoritarian or democratic) but a <strong>range of styles</strong> from <strong>highly boss-centred</strong> to <strong>highly subordinate-centred</strong>, varying with the <strong>degree of freedom</strong> the leader gives subordinates.</p>\n<ul><li>One end: the <strong>area of freedom for managers</strong> is large (authoritarian).</li><li>Middle: democratic.</li><li>Other end: the <strong>area of freedom for non-managers</strong> is large (free-rein).</li></ul>\n<p>The right style depends on <strong>the leader, the followers and the situation</strong>. An <strong>ellipse</strong> around the model shows the influence of the <strong>organizational environment</strong> and <strong>societal environment</strong> on style.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Picture:</strong> a slider. Slide left and the boss decides and announces; slide right and the team decides within limits.</div>", "groups": "<h3>Committees</h3>\n<p>A <strong>committee</strong> is a group of persons to whom, <strong>as a group, some matter is committed</strong>. Also called a board, commission, task force, team, self-managing team or autonomous work group; the essential nature is the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions and formality:</strong> committees can perform planning, organizing, staffing, leading and controlling. Some have authority to <strong>make recommendations</strong> to a manager; some only <strong>receive information</strong> without recommending or deciding.</p>\n<h3>Reasons for using committees</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Group deliberation and judgment</strong></li><li><strong>Dominant logic</strong></li><li><strong>Fear of too much authority in a single person</strong></li><li><strong>Representation of interested groups</strong></li><li><strong>Coordination</strong> of departments, plans and policies</li><li><strong>Transmission and sharing of information</strong></li><li><strong>Consolidation of authority</strong></li><li><strong>Motivation through participation</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>Disadvantages and misuse</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Costly</strong>; compromise at the <strong>least common denominator</strong> rather than the best decision; <strong>indecision</strong>; <strong>split responsibility</strong>; a <strong>few may impose their will</strong> on the majority.</li><li>Misuse: committees should <strong>not replace a manager</strong>, be used for research, for unimportant decisions, or for decisions beyond members' authority.</li></ul>\n<h3>Four stages of group development</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Forming</strong>: members get to know each other.</li><li><strong>Storming</strong>: they work out the objective and <strong>conflict</strong> arises.</li><li><strong>Norming</strong>: the group agrees on <strong>norms</strong> and rules of behaviour.</li><li><strong>Performing</strong>: the group gets down to the <strong>task</strong>.</li></ol>\n<h3>Groups</h3>\n<p><strong>Two or more people acting interdependently in a unified manner toward common goals.</strong> Characteristics: <strong>common goals</strong>, <strong>interaction and communication</strong>, members take <strong>roles</strong>, groups are part of a <strong>larger group</strong>, and they develop <strong>norms</strong> (expected behaviour).</p>\n<p><strong>Functions and advantages:</strong> groups are powerful in changing <strong>behaviour, attitudes and values</strong> and disciplining members; used for decision-making, negotiating and bargaining; diverse members bring different perspectives; structure affects communication; participation in setting objectives builds <strong>commitment</strong>; they give <strong>social satisfaction</strong>, belonging and support; and they promote communication.</p>", "teams": "<h3>What is a team?</h3>\n<p><em>\"A small number of people with <strong>complementary skills</strong> who are committed to a <strong>common purpose</strong>, set of performance goals and approach for which they hold themselves <strong>mutually accountable</strong>.\"</em></p>\n<p>Some teams recommend, some decide, some run operations. Some solve problems (e.g. <strong>quality circles</strong>); others do <strong>cross-functional</strong> work (design, marketing, finance, manufacturing, personnel).</p>\n<h3>Group vs team vs committee</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Committee</th><th>Group</th><th>Team</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Essence</td><td>A <strong>matter is committed</strong> to it</td><td>People acting <strong>interdependently</strong> toward common goals</td><td><strong>Complementary skills</strong>, <strong>mutual accountability</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Accountability</td><td>Collective, can be split</td><td>Individual</td><td><strong>Mutual</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Team building</h3>\n<ul><li>Members must believe the purpose is <strong>worthwhile, meaningful and urgent</strong>.</li><li>Select members for the <strong>skills needed</strong>: technical, problem-solving and decision-making, and human-relations skills.</li><li>Set <strong>rules of behaviour</strong>: regular attendance, confidentiality, fact-based discussion, everyone contributing.</li><li>Identify <strong>goals and tasks early</strong>.</li><li>Encourage one another with <strong>recognition, positive feedback and rewards</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Types of teams</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Self-managing team</strong>: members with the various skills needed to carry out a relatively <strong>complete task</strong>.</li><li><strong>Virtual team</strong>: members are <strong>not in the same location</strong>, may not report to the team manager, and may not even work for the same organization.</li></ul>", "communication": "<h3>Why communication matters</h3>\n<p>Communication is <strong>the means by which people are linked together</strong> in an organization to achieve common goals; without it, coordination and change are impossible.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Internally</strong>, it <strong>integrates</strong> the managerial functions of planning, organizing, staffing, leading and controlling.</li><li><strong>Externally</strong>, it links the enterprise to its environment: customers' needs, suppliers, stockholders' claims, government regulations and community concerns.</li></ul>\n<h3>The communication process</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Sender</strong> has a <strong>thought</strong>.</li><li><strong>Encodes</strong> it into symbols both sides understand.</li><li><strong>Transmits</strong> it through a channel (oral or written: memo, computer, phone, TV).</li><li><strong>Receiver</strong> <strong>receives</strong> and <strong>decodes</strong> it; how well depends on knowledge, experience, trust and relationship with the sender.</li><li><strong>Understanding</strong>: effective only if the receiver understands it <strong>exactly as intended</strong>.</li><li><strong>Feedback</strong>: the final step confirming correct interpretation (verbal or non-verbal).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>Noise</strong> can disrupt any stage: physical, physiological, technical, organizational, cultural, psychological and <strong>semantic</strong> (language/words).</p>\n<h3>Nine types of communication</h3>\n<p><strong>By formality</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Formal</strong>: the officially created flow between positions. <em>Memos, intranet, meetings, conferences, one-on-ones, bulletin boards, letters, presentations, notice boards, emails from leaders.</em></li><li><strong>Informal (grapevine)</strong>: outside the official system. Patterns: <strong>single strand</strong> (each tells the next in sequence), <strong>cluster</strong> (people pass it to their cluster or keep it), <strong>probability chain</strong> (each randomly tells another).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>By direction</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Downward</strong>: higher to lower levels; common in authoritarian settings; instructions, speeches, letters, policy statements. Time-consuming; information is often lost or distorted on the way down.</li><li><strong>Upward</strong>: subordinates to superiors; often <strong>filtered</strong> (bad news held back); typical of participative settings; needs a climate where people feel free to speak.</li><li><strong>Crosswise / horizontal</strong>: same or similar level, different areas (managers of HR, marketing, sales).</li><li><strong>Diagonal</strong>: different levels with <strong>no direct reporting relationship</strong>, used to speed things up (a floor manager and the sales team).</li></ol>\n<p><strong>By medium</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Written</strong>: records, references, legal defence, carefully planned, reaches large audiences, uniform policy; but mountains of paper, poor writing, no immediate feedback.</li><li><strong>Oral</strong>: face-to-face or presentations; <strong>speedy interchange and immediate feedback</strong>; questions can be asked.</li><li><strong>Non-verbal</strong>: facial expressions and gestures that reinforce or <strong>contradict</strong> the words.</li></ol>\n<h3>General vs managerial communication</h3>\n<p>General communication is everyday exchange of information; <strong>managerial communication</strong> is purposeful communication that integrates the managerial functions and links the organization to its environment to achieve goals.</p>", "barriers": "<h3>Barriers to effective communication</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Lack of planning</strong></li><li><strong>Unclarified assumptions</strong></li><li><strong>Semantic distortion</strong>, deliberate or accidental (<em>\"We sell for less\": less than what?</em>)</li><li><strong>Poorly expressed messages</strong></li><li><strong>Loss by transmission</strong> and <strong>poor retention</strong></li><li><strong>Poor listening and premature evaluation</strong></li><li><strong>Distrust, threat and fear</strong></li><li><strong>Insufficient time to adjust to change</strong></li><li><strong>Information overload</strong></li></ol>\n<p>Others: <strong>selective perception</strong>, <strong>attitude influence</strong>, <strong>differences in status and power</strong>, <strong>too many organizational levels</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Towards effective communication</h3>\n<ul><li>Senders must be <strong>clear in their own minds</strong> about what they want to say.</li><li>Encode and decode with <strong>familiar symbols</strong>.</li><li>Don't plan communication in a vacuum; consider the <strong>receivers' needs</strong>.</li><li>Tone of voice, choice of words and <strong>congruence between what is said and how</strong> affect the reaction.</li><li>Communication is complete only when the message is <strong>understood</strong>.</li><li>It is more than transmitting information; it also deals with <strong>emotions</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Listening (\"listening is the key to understanding\")</h3>\n<p>Stop talking · put the talker at ease · show you want to listen · remove distractions · empathize · be patient · hold your temper · go easy on arguments · ask questions.</p>\n<h3>Improving written communication</h3>\n<p>Simple, short, familiar words · personal pronouns (I, you) · illustrations and examples · short sentences and paragraphs · <strong>active verbs</strong> (\"the manager plans\") · no unnecessary words.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Exam approach:</strong> name the barrier, quote the case line that shows it, then give the matching remedy.</div>", "controlprocess": "<h3>What is controlling?</h3>\n<p>Controlling is <strong>verifying whether everything happens in conformity with the plan</strong>, instructions and principles, to <strong>point out weaknesses and errors, rectify them and prevent recurrence</strong>. It applies to <strong>things, people and actions</strong>.</p>\n<p>It is the <strong>measurement and correction of performance</strong> to ensure enterprise objectives and plans are achieved. Planning and controlling are closely linked, and controlling is the job of <strong>every manager</strong>, from president to supervisor.</p>\n<h3>Establishing standards</h3>\n<p>Plans are the yardsticks for controls, but managers can't watch everything, so they set <strong>standards</strong>: <strong>criteria of performance</strong> at selected points where measurement gives signals about progress. A standard is a <strong>unit of measurement that serves as a reference point for evaluating results</strong>. Goals, objectives, quotas and targets also act as standards. <em>Examples: sales quotas, budgets, job deadlines, market share, profit margins.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Types of standards:</strong> <strong>physical</strong>, <strong>technical</strong>, <strong>monetary</strong>, <strong>managerial</strong> and <strong>time</strong> standards.</p>\n<h3>The eight steps in controlling</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Establish standards</strong></li><li><strong>Actual performance</strong> happens</li><li><strong>Measure</strong> actual performance</li><li><strong>Compare</strong> actual performance with standards</li><li><strong>Identify deviations</strong></li><li><strong>Analyse the causes</strong> of deviations</li><li><strong>Program corrective action</strong></li><li><strong>Implement</strong> corrections (and loop back)</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Worked example (a phone factory):</strong> standard = 1,000 units/day → actual production runs → count 920 units → compare with 1,000 → deviation of 80 → cause: a machine breakdown → plan: maintenance schedule and a backup line → implement, then monitor again.</div>", "controltypes": "<h3>Three types of control (by timing)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>When</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Feedforward</strong></td><td><strong>Before</strong></td><td>Anticipates problems <strong>before they arise</strong> (on inputs)</td><td>Hiring extra staff and stocking up before a festival rush</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Concurrent</strong></td><td><strong>During</strong></td><td>Immediate feedback on how efficiently <strong>inputs are becoming outputs</strong></td><td>A supervisor checking quality on the line in real time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Feedback</strong></td><td><strong>After</strong></td><td>Information on <strong>customers' reactions</strong> to goods and services</td><td>Customer satisfaction surveys after a launch</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Exam approach:</strong> quote the case line, then point to its timing word: <em>in anticipation / before</em> → feedforward; <em>while / during</em> → concurrent; <em>after / survey / results</em> → feedback.</div>", "budgets": "<p><em>This and the following control lessons are <strong>self-study material explicitly included for the end-semester exam</strong>.</em></p>\n<h3>Management control techniques</h3>\n<p>Three categories: <strong>budgetary controls</strong>, <strong>non-budgetary controls</strong> and <strong>network (time-event) techniques</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Budgetary control</h3>\n<p>Finding out what is being done and <strong>comparing actual results with the budget</strong>, to approve achievements or remedy differences. Budget types listed: fixed, flexible, master, sales, production, materials, labour and capital expenditure budgets.</p>\n<p>A <strong>budget</strong> is a <strong>single-use plan</strong> detailing the resources, usually financial, needed for a programme, project or activity; managers review the reports and act.</p>\n<h3>Three families of budgets</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Types</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Financial</strong></td><td>Where cash will come from and how it will be used</td><td><strong>Cash</strong> budget (all cash in/out by month, week or day), <strong>capital expenditure</strong> budget (plant, machinery, land), <strong>balance-sheet</strong> budget (forecast assets and liabilities)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Operating</strong></td><td>Planned operations in money terms</td><td><strong>Sales/revenue</strong> budget, <strong>expense</strong> budget, <strong>profit</strong> budget (revenue − expenses)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-monetary</strong></td><td>Planned operations in non-financial terms</td><td><strong>Labour</strong> budget (hours), <strong>space</strong> budget (square feet), <strong>production</strong> budget (units)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Dangers in budgeting</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Over-budgeting</strong>: spelling out minor expenses, taking away managers' freedom.</li><li><strong>Overriding enterprise goals</strong>: budget targets become more important than company objectives.</li><li><strong>Hiding inefficiency</strong>.</li><li><strong>Causing inflexibility</strong>, the greatest danger: decisions become rigid.</li></ul>", "flexiblebudget": "<h3>Variable (flexible) budgets</h3>\n<p>Because inflexibility is dangerous, attention has turned to <strong>variable budgets</strong>, designed to <strong>vary with the volume of sales or another measure of output</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li>Some costs don't vary with volume (especially in the short run); others range from fully to slightly variable.</li><li>A fixed budget works fine with good plans and forecasts, but a variable budget <strong>forces study of the factors that turn workload into labour and expense needs</strong>.</li><li>Typical layout: expense items (material, labour, overheads, engineering, R&amp;D, sales and distribution) in rows; columns for sales volumes (e.g. 20, 40, 60, 80, 100 thousand) showing how each cost changes; then total cost, profit and profit-to-sales %.</li></ul>\n<h3>Zero-based budgeting (ZBB)</h3>\n<p>Similar in purpose to good variable budgeting. Divide programmes into <strong>packages</strong> (goals, activities, resources) and calculate each package's cost <strong>from zero</strong> every budget period, instead of just adjusting last period's figures.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Contrast:</strong> <em>traditional</em> = last year + changes; <em>variable</em> = changes with output; <em>zero-based</em> = justify everything from scratch.</div>", "nonbudget": "<h3>Non-budgetary control devices</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Statistical data</strong>: show <strong>trends</strong> so managers can extrapolate; present as averages to remove seasonal or accounting-period effects; easiest to understand as <strong>charts</strong>, and should allow comparison with a standard.</li><li><strong>Special reports and analysis</strong>: for particular problem areas where routine reports are inadequate; money spent on targeted analysis can be better value than elaborate routine systems.</li><li><strong>Operational audit</strong>: regular, independent appraisal by <strong>internal auditors</strong> of accounting, financial and other operations, at best comparing actual results with plans.</li><li><strong>Personal observation</strong>: also called <strong>\"managing by walking around.\"</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>Time-event network analysis</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>PERT</strong> (Program Evaluation and Review Technique): a <strong>probabilistic</strong> model that allows for uncertainty using <strong>three time estimates</strong>: optimistic, pessimistic and most likely.</li><li><strong>CPM</strong> (Critical Path Method): a <strong>deterministic</strong> model that does not account for uncertainty in activity times.</li><li>Both are used for <strong>planning and controlling projects</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gantt chart</strong>: lists activities on the left against a time scale on top; each activity is a <strong>bar</strong> whose position and length show its start, duration and end, so progress can be seen at a glance.</li></ul>", "overall": "<h3>Why overall control?</h3>\n<p>Most controls cover a <strong>part</strong> of the enterprise (policies, selection, training). <strong>Overall controls</strong> measure <strong>total accomplishment</strong>. Three reasons:</p>\n<ol><li>Overall planning needs matching overall control.</li><li><strong>Decentralization</strong> (especially product or territorial divisions) creates semi-independent units that need overall control to avoid chaos.</li><li>Overall controls are <strong>generally financial</strong> and measure a manager's <strong>total</strong> effort.</li></ol>\n<h3>Three overall control devices</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Budget summaries and reports</strong>: a résumé of all individual budgets showing sales, costs, profits, capital use and ROI together, so top management sees how the <strong>whole company</strong> is meeting objectives. The budgets must portray plans accurately; attention should go to <strong>important variations</strong>, not minor ones. Very useful under decentralized authority.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Profit and loss (P&amp;L) control</strong>: uses the <strong>income statement</strong> for divisional and departmental control. If the whole firm exists to make profit, <strong>each part should contribute</strong>, so its expected profit becomes its standard. Each division details revenue and expenses (with a share of overhead) and calculates profit or loss. Works best in <strong>product and territorial divisions</strong> where managers have wide authority; rarely applied to central staff and service departments.</p>\n<ul><li><em>Limitations:</em> high cost of accounting and intra-company transfers; departments may compete unhelpfully; may be inadequate for overall performance; duplicated records.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>3. Return on investment (ROI)</strong>: the ratio of <strong>earnings to capital invested</strong>. Profit is seen not as an absolute but as a <strong>% return on capital employed</strong>; the aim is to optimize returns on capital. ROI can improve through a higher profit-to-sales ratio <strong>or</strong> faster capital turnover.</p>\n<ul><li><em>Advantages:</em> locates weaknesses (e.g. rising inventory lowers the return); focuses attention on the core objective; effective with decentralized authority; allows comparison within the company and with other firms.</li><li><em>Limitations:</em> data and allocation problems; excessive preoccupation with financial factors; may overlook social and technical developments; can make capital investment for new ventures inflexible; doesn't say what a <em>reasonable</em> return is.</li></ul>", "preventive": "<h3>Two ways to act on unsatisfactory results</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Direct control</strong>: trace the cause back to the <strong>persons responsible</strong> and get them to correct their practices.</li><li><strong>Preventive control</strong>: <strong>develop better managers</strong> who apply management concepts and principles skilfully and think in systems terms, preventing poor results in the first place.</li></ul>\n<h3>Direct control</h3>\n<p>Standards for quantity, quality, time and cost are compared with plans; a <strong>negative deviation</strong> shows performance below standard and calls for direct control.</p>\n<p><strong>Seven common causes of negative deviations</strong> (assumptions behind direct control): (1) that the responsible person will correct things at the right time, (2) uncertainty, (3) lack of knowledge, experience or judgment, (4) that performance can be measured, (5) that personal responsibility exists, (6) that the time spent is warranted, (7) that mistakes can be discovered in time.</p>\n<h3>Preventive control</h3>\n<p><strong>Principle:</strong> <em>\"the higher the quality of managers and their subordinates, the less will be the need for direct control.\"</em> It needs a wide understanding of managerial principles, functions, techniques and philosophy.</p>\n<p><strong>Three assumptions:</strong> qualified, experienced managers make minimum errors; management fundamentals can be used to measure performance; the application of those fundamentals can be evaluated.</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Psychological</strong>: managers know what is expected and see the link between performance and measurement.</li><li><strong>Higher accuracy</strong> in assigning personal responsibility.</li><li><strong>Encourages self-control</strong>: managers correct themselves voluntarily.</li><li><strong>Lightens the managerial burden</strong>: preventing problems takes less effort than fixing them.</li></ol>", "quality": "<h3>Why quality matters</h3>\n<p>Open international markets mean several firms compete for every sale. Price matters, but <strong>quality is often the major issue</strong>, and poor quality is expensive for both producer and customer. Quality applies to <strong>services</strong> too (banking, health care, education), and is often most visible there.</p>\n<h3>Definitions to remember</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Feigenbaum</strong>: <em>\"The total composite product and service characteristics of engineering, manufacturing, marketing and maintenance through which the product and service in use meet the expectation of the customer.\"</em></li><li><strong>Juran</strong>: (i) quality consists of <strong>product features which meet customers' needs</strong> and thereby provide satisfaction; (ii) quality consists of <strong>freedom from deficiencies</strong>.</li><li>Other definitions from the unit: <strong>fitness for purpose or use</strong>; the totality of features that bear on the ability to satisfy <strong>stated or implied needs</strong>; aimed at <strong>present and future</strong> consumer needs; <strong>conformance to requirements</strong>; <strong>affordable excellence</strong>.</li><li><strong>Customer's view</strong>: fitness for use. <strong>Manufacturer's view</strong>: conformance to the specifications that lead to customer satisfaction.</li></ul>\n<h3>Key terms</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Product</strong>: the output of any process: goods, software and services.</li><li><strong>Product feature</strong>: a property a product has that is meant to meet customer needs.</li><li><strong>Customer</strong>: anyone <strong>impacted</strong> by the product or service (external or internal).</li><li><strong>Grade</strong>: a degree of satisfaction, sometimes called \"quality of design\"; products that meet needs to a superior degree are <em>higher grade</em>.</li><li><strong>Degree of performance</strong>: how a product scores against competitors of the same grade.</li><li><strong>Degree of excellence</strong>: value from functions, timely availability, aesthetics, performance and cost.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><em>\"Customer is king\"</em>: the producer must observe quality in <strong>design, conformance and performance</strong>.</div>", "qualitydimensions": "<h3>1. Quality of design</h3>\n<p>Before producing, find out <strong>what users demand</strong> (surveys, market trends). These needs are quantified into <strong>specifications</strong>. Quality of design is about the <strong>tightness of specifications</strong> (a part with ±0.001 mm tolerance is a better design than one with ±0.01 mm) and whether the product performs consistently over its life.</p>\n<p>Factors controlling quality of design:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>(a) Type of customer in the market</strong>: tastes and needs vary by location and income. <em>A 10-seater three-wheeler suits rural/semi-urban shared travel; a small auto suits city commuters.</em></li><li><strong>(b) Profit consideration</strong> and <strong>(c) the environment</strong> in which the product will be used: <em>a bus body fails on hilly village tracks where a jeep works; lab appliances may fail in real factory conditions.</em></li><li><strong>(d) Special requirements</strong>: greater strength, fatigue resistance or interchangeability need <strong>tighter tolerances</strong>; electronics are designed for <strong>quick part replacement</strong>.</li><li><strong>Services</strong>: design is judged by <strong>convenience and flexibility</strong>, e.g. medical aid at home, personalized insurance.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Quality of conformance</h3>\n<p>How well the <strong>actual product matches the design</strong> and specifications. It depends on factors such as the process, operators, materials and inspection, and must <strong>control process variability</strong> (seen and unseen causes), often with statistical techniques. An <strong>inspection programme</strong> with customer feedback keeps defectives minimal. In services, conformance means each service unit meets the standard and the provider <strong>responds to feedback</strong> (an airline passenger judges everything from booking to luggage).</p>\n<h3>3. Quality of performance</h3>\n<p><strong>How well the product performs in the customer's use.</strong> It depends on <strong>both</strong> design and conformance: a great design badly made performs poorly, and perfect conformance can't rescue a wrong design. It shows up in the customer's experience and in <strong>sales</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Chain to remember:</strong> Design (right product) → Conformance (made right) → Performance (works right for the user).</div>\n<h3>Achieving and maintaining quality: everybody's business</h3>\n<p><strong>Marketing</strong> (finds needs), <strong>R&amp;D/design</strong> (sets specifications), <strong>planning</strong>, <strong>purchasing</strong> (buys materials of specified quality), <strong>production</strong> (trained operators make it to design), <strong>inspection</strong> (reduces defects and variability), <strong>retail houses</strong> (make it accessible and service it).</p>", "qc": "<h3>What is quality control?</h3>\n<p><strong>Quality control</strong> is <em>the process through which we measure actual quality performance, compare it with a standard and take corrective action if there is a deviation</em>: a systematic procedure for meeting quality goals.</p>\n<p><strong>Feigenbaum's Total Quality Control</strong>: <em>\"an effective system for integrating the quality development, quality maintenance and quality improvement efforts of various groups in an organization to enable production at the most economical level and to achieve customer satisfaction.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>Why QC is needed:</strong> processes are subject to <strong>variation</strong> from materials, tools, machines, labour, working conditions and operator skill.</p>\n<h3>Objectives of QC</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Improve income</strong> by making the product more acceptable to customers (QC looks at the quality of <strong>future</strong> production).</li><li><strong>Reduce losses due to defects</strong>: less sorting, rework, shutdowns, returns and scrap.</li><li><strong>Judge conformity</strong> of the process to standards and act on deviations.</li><li><strong>Build loyal customers</strong> in the long run, leading to \"profit escalation\".</li><li><strong>Associate value with the product</strong> as customer needs change, through feedback and process modification.</li></ol>\n<p>The ultimate aim: customer satisfaction, goodwill, confidence and the maker's reputation. QC assumes <strong>key quality characteristics can be defined and measured</strong> (length of a part, % impurity); it can reduce defects to a few parts per million. Key characteristics can be <strong>technological, psychological, time-oriented, contractual and ethical</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Inspection vs quality control</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Inspection</th><th>Quality control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Finds and sorts</strong> defectives already made</td><td><strong>Regulates</strong> quality and reduces variation in <strong>future</strong> output</td></tr><tr><td>After the fact</td><td>Preventive, systematic</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Quality policy</strong>: the overall intention and direction of an organization about quality, formally expressed by top management.</p>", "stakeholders": "<h3>Stakeholders and their expectations</h3>\n<p><strong>Stakeholders</strong> are people <strong>directly or indirectly affected</strong> by an organization's operations, or who can <strong>influence</strong> its decisions: investors, consumers, employees, surveyors, consumer panels, management–labour negotiators.</p>\n<p>Relations with stakeholders are set out in <strong>codes of practice</strong>, which usually begin by promising to act with <strong>integrity, fairly and reasonably</strong>, and to accept <strong>social responsibility</strong> to the community. These codes create an obligation to behave in certain ways toward each group.</p>\n<p>Stakeholders can take part in developing plans and policies, support improvement initiatives and monitor performance.</p>", "qa": "<h3>Quality assurance</h3>\n<p><strong>Quality assurance</strong> is <em>the activity of providing the evidence needed to establish confidence that the quality function is being performed.</em> It gives the customer <strong>proof that the product is fit for use</strong>, and recognizes early warnings of trouble.</p>\n<p>The type of evidence varies: vegetables in a village market are judged by direct inspection; packaged goods need <strong>lab testing</strong>; medicines and cosmetics carry <strong>expiry dates</strong>; long-life products state favourable conditions.</p>\n<p>A manager can assure quality if they can answer: Is the product <strong>safe</strong>? Are we <strong>complying with the law</strong>? Is it <strong>performing within the warranty</strong> period? Is it <strong>marketable</strong>?</p>\n<p><strong>Three forms of quality assurance:</strong> (a) quality audit, (b) quality survey, (c) product audit.</p>\n<h3>Quality audit</h3>\n<p>An <strong>independent review</strong> comparing some aspect of quality performance with a standard. It may examine field complaints and corrective action, tool and gauge control, design changes, whether manufacturing specs match design specs, and inspection procedures.</p>\n<p><strong>Types of audit</strong></p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Audit</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Adequacy (management) audit</strong></td><td>Does the documented system (quality manual, procedures) meet the standard's requirements?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Compliance audit</strong></td><td>Is the documented system actually <strong>followed</strong> by the workforce?</td></tr><tr><td><strong>External audit</strong></td><td>Done by a customer/contract partner (adequacy and/or compliance)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Extrinsic audit</strong></td><td>External audit by an <strong>independent, often accredited third party</strong> using a standard such as ISO 9000</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Internal audit</strong></td><td>By the company's <strong>own staff</strong>, to assure management the system meets its objectives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A good audit report is <strong>balanced</strong>: it reports what works as well as deficiencies. Audits can't show market standing, user feedback or cost-reduction opportunities. Tips: prioritize activities, define the subject matter with a checklist, switch resources once an area improves, and use reference standards (a Quality Assurance Manual).</p>\n<h3>Quality survey</h3>\n<p>A broad evaluation of an organization's (often a supplier's) <strong>quality capabilities and arrangements</strong>.</p>", "productaudit": "<h3>Product audit</h3>\n<p>An <strong>independent evaluation of product quality</strong> to determine <strong>fitness for use and conformance to specification</strong>, done <strong>after inspection</strong>. Purposes:</p>\n<ol><li>Estimate the quality level <strong>delivered to customers</strong>.</li><li>Evaluate the <strong>effectiveness of inspection</strong> decisions.</li><li>Provide information to improve outgoing quality and inspection.</li><li>Give <strong>extra assurance</strong> beyond routine inspection.</li></ol>\n<p>It may be done by a customer, internally as \"final inspection\", or externally. Its main task is <strong>sampling</strong> for defects (sample size by statistics for mass production; many characteristics for large units like cars).</p>\n<h3>Stages for product auditing (Juran)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Merits / demerits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>After acceptance by inspectors</td><td>Most economical, but misses packing, shipping, storage and use</td></tr><tr><td>After packing, before shipment</td><td>Needs unpacking and repacking, but checks original packing</td></tr><tr><td>On receipt by dealers</td><td>Hard to administer at many locations, but shows shipping and storage effects</td></tr><tr><td>On receipt by users</td><td>Even harder, but adds dealer handling and shipment to user</td></tr><tr><td>Performance in service</td><td><strong>Ideal</strong> but hardest; simplify with sampling</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Scoring and inspection effectiveness</h3>\n<p>Defects are weighted by seriousness: <strong>A critical (100)</strong>, <strong>B major (50)</strong>, <strong>C minor (10)</strong>, <strong>D incidental (1)</strong>; demerits are summed into a score.</p>\n<p><strong>Inspection effectiveness</strong> = defects found by inspection ÷ (inspection defects + extra defects found by the audit) × 100.</p>\n<ul><li><em>Example:</em> inspection found 40, audit found 10 more → 40/50 × 100 = <strong>80%</strong>.</li><li><em>Practice:</em> inspection 18, audit 2 more → 18/20 × 100 = <strong>90%</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Results are compared with a stable <strong>base period</strong> (excluding abnormal events) or with <strong>market quality</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Effects of quality</h3>\n<p>Quality affects <strong>costs</strong> (fewer deficiencies mean less waste, rework and complaints) and <strong>income</strong> (features customers value increase sales).</p>", "qualityreview": "<h3>Unit 1 in one page</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality</strong> = total composite characteristics through which a product in use meets customer expectations.</li><li>Good <strong>performance</strong> needs good <strong>design</strong> and good <strong>conformance</strong>.</li><li><strong>QC</strong> = measure → compare with standard → correct deviations.</li><li><strong>Quality assurance</strong> = assuring quality, reliability and service through scientific techniques; tools: <strong>quality audit, quality survey, product audit</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Key words</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality engineering</strong>: the branch of engineering dealing with principles and practice of quality assurance and control.</li><li><strong>Specification</strong>: the document prescribing requirements the product must conform to.</li><li><strong>Testing</strong>: determining an item's capability by subjecting it to physical, chemical, environmental or operating conditions.</li><li><strong>Service</strong>: work performed for someone else (a haircut, a security service).</li></ul>\n<h3>Model 3-mark answers</h3>\n<ul><li><em>Define QC and its objectives:</em> definition + any 3 objectives (income, fewer defects, conformity, loyal customers, value).</li><li><em>Inspection vs QC:</em> sorting after the fact vs preventive regulation of future quality.</li><li><em>Quality of design:</em> specifications from customer needs + one factor with an example.</li></ul>", "tqm": "<h3>What is TQM?</h3>\n<p><strong>Total quality management</strong> is the <strong>total involvement of everyone</strong> in the organization in achieving quality, through a <strong>participative culture</strong>. TQM rose in the early 1980s; <strong>Deming</strong> introduced statistical measures (1939–45) and showed operators could remove only <strong>15%</strong> of variation causes, with management accountable for <strong>85%</strong>. The Japanese took him seriously; the West later followed.</p>\n<p><strong>The key idea: the customer chain.</strong> Everyone, every department, is a <strong>customer of and supplier to</strong> someone else. The external customer is just the last link, so <strong>quality becomes everyone's routine duty</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Deming's definition:</strong> <em>\"A culture in which there is total commitment to quality, and an attitude expressed by everybody's involvement in continuous improvement of products and services through innovative scientific methods.\"</em></p>\n<h3>Principle of objectives</h3>\n<p>Global competition demands four abilities: give customers what they want <strong>on demand at lowest cost</strong>; deliver <strong>high quality and reliability</strong>; <strong>keep pace with change</strong>; and <strong>stay a step ahead</strong> of future needs. TQM's objectives: monitor performance and satisfaction; identify needed improvements at the customer interface; deliver better products at lowest cost; agree on customer requirements; tailor output to demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Two unique features:</strong> (1) <strong>continuous improvement and innovation</strong> (fewer errors and waste, faster response, better productivity); (2) <strong>quality methods applied to the whole organization</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Why TQM is popular</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Customer satisfaction and market share</strong>.</li><li><strong>Cost reduction</strong>: less energy, materials and inspection stoppages; prompt delivery, higher productivity, less repair and rework.</li><li><strong>Conducive environment</strong>: <strong>fear is eliminated</strong>, so people admit mistakes, feel respected and work for the organization's interest.</li><li><strong>New definition of success</strong>: not just profit and share but <strong>morale, efficiency, fewer complaints, better industrial relations and a happier workplace</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Three axioms of TQM: Joiner's triangle</h3>\n<p>An equilateral triangle of three equally important cornerstones:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Commitment</strong>: to never-ending improvement and innovation, led by <strong>senior management</strong>, backed by action and resources, <strong>not just slogans</strong> (slogans alone cause frustration). Ways to motivate: proper conditions, education and training, communication, modern leadership, good materials and tools, job satisfaction and incentives.</li><li><strong>Scientific knowledge</strong>: of proper tools and techniques for <strong>technical change</strong>; fact-based decisions.</li><li><strong>Involvement</strong>: everyone as <strong>one team</strong> for <strong>social change</strong>, including suppliers.</li></ol>", "basictools": "<h3>The seven basic quality tools (as listed in the unit)</h3>\n<p>More than 40 quality tools exist; these seven are the most commonly used with TQM. They rely on <strong>numerical data</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Cause-and-effect diagram</strong> (<strong>Ishikawa</strong> or <strong>fishbone</strong>): identifies and displays <strong>possible causes</strong> of a problem. Major causes are the big bones; contributing causes are small bones.</li><li><strong>Flowchart</strong>: a pictorial representation of <strong>all steps of a process</strong>. Symbols: <strong>rectangle</strong> = activity, <strong>diamond</strong> = decision, <strong>rounded rectangle</strong> = start/end (terminal), plus a document symbol. Helps uncover trouble spots.</li><li><strong>Pareto diagram</strong>: ranks causes by contribution so the <strong>vital few</strong> are tackled first. <em>Rejected castings: blowholes 50%, hard spots 30%, scabs 10%, shifts 7%, cold shuts 4% → fix blowholes first.</em></li><li><strong>Run chart</strong>: monitors a process over <strong>time</strong> to see if the long-range average is changing; the simplest tool.</li><li><strong>Control chart</strong>: a run chart with statistically set <strong>upper and lower control limits</strong> around the average. The process is <strong>in control</strong> when no points fall outside the limits.<ul><li>Grand mean X̄ = (X₁ + X₂ + … + Xₙ) / n</li><li>s = √[Σ(Xᵢ − X̄)² / n]</li><li><strong>UCL = X̄ + 3s</strong>, <strong>LCL = X̄ − 3s</strong></li></ul></li><li><strong>Correlation chart (scatter)</strong>: shows the relationship between two factors: <strong>positive, none or negative</strong>.</li><li><strong>Histogram</strong>: a bar graph of a <strong>frequency distribution</strong>. <em>Example: 20 spelling scores from 35 to 50; range 15; 5 classes of width 3 (35–38, 38–41 …), with frequencies 1, 2, 4, 5, 8.</em></li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>Causes, Steps, Priorities, Time, Limits, Relationship, Distribution.</em> \"<strong>C</strong>an <strong>F</strong>rank <strong>P</strong>lay <strong>R</strong>ugby <strong>C</strong>ompetitively, <strong>C</strong>oach <strong>H</strong>arry?\" (Cause-effect, Flowchart, Pareto, Run, Control, Correlation, Histogram).</div>", "kaizen": "<h3>Kaizen</h3>\n<p>A Japanese word meaning <strong>gradual, continuous improvement in small matters</strong>, in every process, involving everyone. It starts from recognizing that every company has problems and can improve as long as perfection is lacking.</p>\n<ul><li>Creates a culture where everyone can <strong>freely admit problems</strong>.</li><li><strong>Urgency</strong> about never-ending improvement; repetitiveness is seen as an obstacle.</li><li><strong>Process over output</strong>: mass inspection of output adds no value.</li></ul>\n<h3>Kaizen vs innovation</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Innovation</th><th>Kaizen</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short-term, <strong>dramatic</strong> improvement</td><td><strong>Slow, step-by-step</strong> improvement</td></tr><tr><td>Large investment, little effort to maintain</td><td>Minimal investment, effort to maintain</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Result</strong>-oriented</td><td><strong>Process</strong>-oriented</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Four kaizen practices</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Total quality control (TQC)</strong> splits quality costs into three groups, arguing that investing in prevention cuts the others:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cost</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Failure: internal</strong></td><td>Defects found <strong>before</strong> shipping</td><td>Scrap, rework, failure analysis, 100% sorting</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Failure: external</strong></td><td>Defects found <strong>after</strong> shipping</td><td>Warranty charges, complaint adjustment, allowances</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Appraisal</strong></td><td>Checking conformance</td><td>Incoming inspection, final inspection and test, product audits, calibration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prevention</strong></td><td>Keeping failure and appraisal low</td><td>Quality planning, process control, audits, training, supplier evaluation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>2. Kanban (just-in-time)</strong>: cards that pull production. A <strong>withdrawal kanban</strong> shows how much the next process takes; a <strong>production-ordering kanban</strong> tells the previous process how much to make. Benefits: parts arrive <strong>only when needed</strong> (less waste, space, inventory), fewer non-value movements, better layout.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Quality circles</strong>: <strong>informal, voluntary</strong> groups (up to about <strong>10 members</strong> plus their supervisor as leader) that meet to <strong>solve problems</strong>; they also aim at members' educational development.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Total Predictive Maintenance (TPM)</strong> <em>(the unit's term)</em>: aims at zero breakdowns, gradually higher equipment effectiveness, better profits and a satisfying workplace. It uses existing resources to minimize waste through simple daily checks, tuning, adjustment, trouble-shooting and repair, avoiding new capital spending and breakdowns.</p>", "deming": "<h3>Deming's 14 points for transformation of management</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Create constancy of purpose</strong> toward improving products and services.</li><li><strong>Adopt the new philosophy</strong>; old methods no longer work.</li><li><strong>Cease dependence on mass inspection</strong>: build quality in, do it right the first time.</li><li><strong>End awarding business on price alone</strong>; prefer a single, long-term, trusted supplier per item.</li><li><strong>Constantly improve</strong> the system of production and service (reduce variability).</li><li><strong>Institute training on the job</strong>.</li><li><strong>Institute leadership</strong>: help people and machines do better; teach and counsel rather than judge.</li><li><strong>Drive out fear</strong>; encourage two-way communication.</li><li><strong>Break down barriers</strong> between departments and individuals.</li><li><strong>Eliminate slogans</strong> and poster campaigns; workers can't fix system causes.</li><li><strong>Eliminate numerical goals and work standards</strong>; replace them with competent leadership (easy targets breed complacency, unrealistic ones breed manipulation).</li><li><strong>End annual reviews / merit rating</strong>; they destroy teamwork and focus on the short term.</li><li><strong>Institute a rigorous programme of education and self-improvement</strong>.</li><li><strong>Top management's permanent commitment</strong> to put everyone to work on the transformation.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory groups:</strong> <em>Direction</em> (1–2) → <em>Better system</em> (3–5) → <em>Better people</em> (6–7) → <em>Remove obstacles</em> (8–12) → <em>Learn and commit</em> (13–14).</div>\n<h3>The seven deadly sins</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Lack of constancy of purpose</strong></li><li><strong>Emphasis on short-term profits</strong></li><li><strong>Performance appraisal / merit rating</strong> (builds fear, rivalry and politics; destroys teamwork and long-term planning)</li><li><strong>Job-hopping</strong> (mobility of management, constant changes of leaders and direction)</li><li><strong>Use of visible figures only</strong> (cutting research and training to show profit; ignoring unknown figures like lost customers)</li><li><strong>Excessive medical costs</strong> (unsafe products, processes and workplaces, job stress)</li><li><strong>Excessive costs of liability</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>The PDCA (Deming) cycle</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Plan</strong></td><td>Decide objectives, needed changes, performance measures, responsibilities, resources and contingencies</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Do</strong></td><td>Implement, usually small-scale, with everyone involved; training, customer survey, data collection, project teams</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Check</strong></td><td>Measure and observe the effects, analyse results against expectations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Act</strong></td><td>Take corrective action; <strong>standardize</strong> and document improvements; then plan again</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout\">PDCA is a <strong>loop</strong>, not a line: each Act feeds the next Plan.</div>", "juran": "<h3>Juran's contributions</h3>\n<p><strong>Joseph Juran</strong> stressed: managing quality through planning, control and improvement; the <strong>cost of quality</strong>; the <strong>Pareto principle</strong>; and the <strong>project-by-project approach</strong>. He placed more quality responsibility on <strong>middle management and quality professionals</strong>, with top management providing guidance and policy. He said about <strong>80%</strong> of quality problems are the fault of <strong>poor management</strong>, not poor workmanship.</p>\n<h3>The quality trilogy</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Quality planning</strong>: identify <strong>customers and their needs</strong>; translate needs into a language everyone understands and develop the product; <strong>optimize</strong> the product and process; transfer the proven process to operations.</li><li><strong>Quality control</strong>: <strong>detect and correct adverse changes</strong> from the set quality level (audits, control limits).</li><li><strong>Quality improvement</strong>: raise performance to an <strong>unprecedented level</strong> (breakthrough).</li></ol>\n<h3>Breakthrough</h3>\n<p>A <strong>breakthrough</strong> is a <strong>beneficial change from the status quo</strong>, needed to overcome <strong>chronic</strong> problems (as opposed to firefighting <strong>sporadic</strong> ones). Juran's universal sequence:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Proof of the need</strong>: convince management, ideally in <strong>money</strong> terms (waste = money lost).</li><li><strong>Project identification</strong>: turn chronic problems into projects, prioritized by <strong>Pareto analysis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Organization for improvement</strong>: upper-manager guidance team; project-by-project improvement; analysis of each project.</li><li><strong>Diagnostic journey</strong>: find causes by experiment and data. Errors are <strong>inadvertent</strong> (unintentional), <strong>technical</strong> (lack of knowledge) or <strong>wilful</strong> (intentional).</li><li><strong>Remedial action</strong> (e.g. clear accountability, help workers spot defects, preventive maintenance, sampling instead of 100% inspection, Pareto to separate problems).</li><li><strong>Overcoming resistance to change</strong>: e.g. let affected people <strong>participate</strong> in planning and executing the change.</li></ol>", "crosby": "<h3>Philip Crosby: \"quality is free\"</h3>\n<p>Crosby first popularized quality for top management, claiming <strong>all quality improvements pay for themselves</strong>. His main contributions: the <strong>zero-defect concept</strong> and a <strong>14-point improvement programme</strong>.</p>\n<h3>Two meanings of zero defects</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Defect-free product</strong>: valuable as a <strong>long-range</strong> goal implying never-ending improvement; counter-productive as a <strong>short-range</strong> target.</li><li><strong>Zero defects as a slogan</strong>: a banner to motivate; works only if the drive is well conceived. Pressuring workers with a slogan <strong>fails</strong> however clever it is.</li></ul>\n<h3>The four absolutes of quality</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Absolute</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Definition</strong></td><td>Quality means <strong>conformance to requirements, not elegance</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>System</strong></td><td>The system for causing quality is <strong>prevention</strong>, not appraisal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Performance standard</strong></td><td>The only standard is <strong>zero defects</strong>: do it right the first time, on time, every time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Measurement</strong></td><td>Quality is measured by the <strong>price of non-conformance</strong> (the cost of doing things wrong)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Crosby's 14 points</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Management commitment</strong></li><li><strong>Quality improvement team</strong></li><li><strong>Measurement</strong></li><li><strong>Cost of quality</strong></li><li><strong>Quality awareness</strong></li><li><strong>Corrective action</strong></li><li><strong>Zero-defects planning</strong></li><li><strong>Employee education</strong> (the <strong>6 Cs</strong>: comprehension, commitment, competence, communication, correction, continuance)</li><li><strong>Zero Defects Day</strong></li><li><strong>Goal setting</strong></li><li><strong>Error-cause removal</strong></li><li><strong>Recognition</strong></li><li><strong>Quality council</strong></li><li><strong>Do it all over again</strong></li></ol>\n<h3>Steps in implementing a zero-defect programme</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Personal challenge</strong>: employees voluntarily sign <strong>pledge cards</strong> for error-free work.</li><li><strong>Identification of error causes</strong>: trace defect sources and fix them; employees report potential sources.</li><li><strong>Inspection</strong>: train workers on quality points to monitor using a <strong>ZD breakdown sheet</strong> (operation sequence + critical factors).</li><li><strong>Motivation</strong>: create a quality mindset by <strong>attracting attention</strong> (slogans such as \"Up with quality, down with rejects\", letters home, news articles) and <strong>generating interest</strong> (displays, workmanship awards, quality education).</li></ol>", "pokayoke": "<h3>Poka-yoke (mistake-proofing)</h3>\n<p>Pioneered by <strong>Shigeo Shingo</strong> of Japan to produce <strong>zero-defect</strong> products. Originally \"fool-proofing\", later <strong>\"mistake-proofing\"</strong>, meaning <em>to avoid inadvertent errors</em>. A poka-yoke device <strong>either prevents a mistake or makes it obvious at a glance</strong>; in production it detects the error and <strong>stops the line</strong> until the cause is removed, so the same error doesn't repeat.</p>\n<p><strong>Example from the unit (pens):</strong> put pen bodies in one dish and springs in another; count springs out for each pen. If a spring is left in the dish after assembly, a spring was missed, and it is fixed immediately at almost no cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Benefits:</strong> higher productivity, lower error rates, better morale (an alternative to demanding more worker diligence). <strong>Limitation:</strong> needs a certain level of quality already built into the system; on a line failing at many points it would stop production constantly.</p>\n<h3>Concurrent engineering</h3>\n<p>Doing product design, process design and related activities <strong>in parallel (in tandem)</strong> instead of one after another. Benefits: <strong>shorter development time</strong>, longer product life, higher sales and revenue, more customers. Organizations progress from separate methodologies to coupling <strong>project management and TQM</strong>, and finally integrating all of them with concurrent engineering. It needs <strong>superior project control</strong>, since the main risks are market delays and costly rework from poor planning.</p>\n<h3>Unit 2 key words</h3>\n<p><strong>Kaizen</strong>: continuous improvement by removing non-value-adding activity. <strong>Kanban</strong>: an instruction card to produce. <strong>Pareto principle</strong>: most problems come from a few causes. <strong>PDCA</strong>: plan-do-check-act cycle. <strong>TQC</strong>: integrating quality development, maintenance and improvement across groups for economical production and customer satisfaction.</p>", "tqmhouse": "<h3>The eight elements of TQM: the TQM house</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part of the house</th><th>Elements</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Foundation</strong></td><td><strong>Ethics, integrity, trust</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bricks</strong></td><td><strong>Training, teamwork, leadership</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Binding mortar</strong></td><td><strong>Communication</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Roof</strong></td><td><strong>Recognition</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Foundation</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Ethics</strong>: the discipline of good and bad; <strong>organizational ethics</strong> (a business code of ethics) and <strong>individual ethics</strong> (personal right and wrong).</li><li><strong>Integrity</strong>: honesty, morals, values, fairness and keeping to facts; its opposite is <strong>duplicity</strong>, in which TQM cannot work.</li><li><strong>Trust</strong>: a by-product of integrity and ethics; enables full participation, <strong>empowerment</strong>, decisions at the right level, risk-taking for improvement, and customer satisfaction.</li></ul>\n<h3>Bricks</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Training</strong>: interpersonal and team skills, problem solving, decision making, job management, performance analysis, business economics, technical skills. Supervisors teach TQM philosophy.</li><li><strong>Teamwork</strong>: quicker, better, more permanent solutions. Three team types:<ul><li><strong>Quality improvement (excellence) teams</strong>: temporary, for recurring problems, <strong>3–12 months</strong>.</li><li><strong>Problem-solving teams</strong>: temporary, <strong>1 week–3 months</strong>.</li><li><strong>Natural work teams</strong>: small groups of skilled workers sharing tasks (employee involvement teams, self-managing teams, quality circles), <strong>1–2 hours a week</strong>.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Leadership</strong>: <em>possibly the most important element</em>. An inspiring vision, clear strategic direction, values; TQM must be <strong>introduced and led by top management</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mortar: communication</h3>\n<p>Links all elements. Credible only if the receiver interprets it as the sender intended. Flows <strong>downward, upward and sideways</strong>, with suppliers and customers too.</p>\n<h3>Roof: recognition</h3>\n<p>Recognize teams and individuals for suggestions and achievements, <strong>best given immediately</strong> after the action. It lifts self-esteem, productivity and quality.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Ways</strong>: personal letters from top management, awards.</li><li><strong>Places</strong>: in front of departments, on performance boards, before top management.</li><li><strong>Times</strong>: staff meetings, annual award ceremonies.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Memory:</strong> <em>EIT foundation, TTL bricks, C mortar, R roof.</em></div>", "ideatools": "<h3>The seven new QC (management) tools</h3>\n<p>The <strong>basic seven</strong> tools use <strong>numerical</strong> data and formulas. The <strong>new seven</strong> work on a predefined problem and organize <strong>verbal</strong> data into structure. The Japanese found them highly effective (the \"second wave\" of quality).</p>\n<ol><li>Affinity diagram 2. Tree diagram 3. Interrelationship diagram 4. Prioritization matrices 5. PDPC 6. Activity network diagram 7. Matrix diagram</li></ol>\n<h3>1. Affinity diagram</h3>\n<p>Pinpoints the problem in a <strong>chaotic situation</strong> by gathering lots of related <strong>verbal data</strong> (ideas, opinions) and <strong>grouping them by natural relationship (affinity)</strong>. <strong>Steps:</strong> state the problem → brainstorm and write each idea on a card → sort cards into groups of similar themes → write an affinity (header) statement for each group → draw the final diagram.</p>\n<h3>2. Tree diagram (systematic diagram, dendrogram)</h3>\n<p>Breaks a <strong>broad goal into increasingly specific means</strong> until reaching action items that can be implemented. <strong>Steps:</strong> record the goal and constraints → generate <strong>primary means</strong> (\"what must happen to achieve this?\") → review both ways (objective ↔ means) → expand to secondary and further means → check from the bottom up: \"will these items achieve the next level?\" <em>Advantage:</em> very systematic and logical.</p>\n<h3>3. Interrelationship diagram</h3>\n<p>Untangles <strong>complex cause-and-effect</strong> links to find <strong>root causes and root effects</strong>. <strong>Steps:</strong> list the issues (from an affinity diagram or brainstorming) → arrange them in a <strong>circle</strong> around the problem → for each pair decide: no, weak or strong relationship → <strong>draw an arrow from cause to effect</strong> (solid = strong, dashed = weak, never two-headed) → <strong>count the arrows</strong>: most <strong>outgoing</strong> arrows = <strong>root cause (driver)</strong>; most <strong>incoming</strong> = <strong>key effect (outcome)</strong>.</p>", "priority": "<h3>4. Prioritization matrices</h3>\n<p><strong>Narrow the team's focus</strong> before detailed planning. Use when root causes must be narrowed to the critical few, issues are complex and interrelated, and <strong>resources are limited</strong>. Needs <strong>team consensus</strong>. <strong>Steps:</strong> agree the goal → list <strong>criteria</strong> (numerical, e.g. 4 of 5, or verbal, e.g. excellent to bad) → make an <strong>L-shaped table</strong> of options vs criteria → rate each (1 = doesn't meet criterion well … 5 = meets it extremely well) → total the scores; <strong>highest total = top priority</strong>. <em>Example (LPG bottling plant):</em> high cost of a centralized compressed-air plant scored 5+4+4+4 = <strong>17</strong>, ranked first.</p>\n<h3>5. Process decision program chart (PDPC)</h3>\n<p>Maps <strong>every conceivable event or contingency</strong> in implementing a plan and prepares <strong>countermeasures</strong>. <strong>Steps (lawn-mower trial example):</strong> set the objective → determine tasks (test wheel, blade, grass box) → list activities and <strong>possible outcomes</strong> (wheels work or skid; blades sharp or corroded) → identify <strong>constraints</strong> (mower ≤ 15 kg) → prepare a <strong>contingency plan</strong> for each failure (if blades corrode, find another material) → check for inconsistencies. <strong>Uses:</strong> implementation plans for MBO and technology projects; showing decision-makers' intentions; forecasting contingencies from past data; countermeasures to reduce nonconformities.</p>", "network": "<h3>6. Activity network diagram (AND / PERT)</h3>\n<p>Finds the <strong>most efficient path and a realistic schedule</strong> by showing total time, task sequence, parallel tasks, <strong>critical tasks</strong> and <strong>slack</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Terms</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Activity</strong>: a part of the project shown by an <strong>arrow</strong>; takes time and resources.</li><li><strong>Event</strong>: the start or end of activities, shown by a <strong>circle/node</strong>; an instant of time.</li><li><strong>Best (B)</strong>, <strong>worst (W)</strong> and <strong>most likely (M)</strong> estimates.</li><li><strong>Expected time: ET = (B + 4M + W) / 6</strong></li><li><strong>Critical path</strong>: the sequence of activities with the <strong>tightest time constraint</strong> on finishing.</li><li><strong>Earliest start time</strong>, slack, project (two or more activities with an aim, a budget and a schedule).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Three construction rules:</strong> (1) each activity is <strong>one and only one arrow</strong>; (2) each activity has <strong>two distinct end nodes</strong>; (3) for each activity ask what must <strong>precede</strong>, <strong>follow</strong> and run <strong>concurrently</strong>.</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> preparing a budget for a new product: A forecast sales (10 days), B study market (7), C design (5, after A), D production schedule (3, after C), E cost estimate (2, after D), F set price (1, after B and E), G prepare budget (14, after E and F).</p>\n<h3>7. Matrix diagram</h3>\n<p>Shows the <strong>presence and strength of relationships</strong> between two (or more) sets of factors at their intersections. Types: <strong>T, Y, X and C</strong>. <em>Restaurant example:</em> factors (food, staff, atmosphere) vs options (better food, better service, more décor budget, more staff, lower prices), each rated 1–3 for impact.</p>", "tqmimplementation": "<h3>Is the organization ready?</h3>\n<p>Three scenarios:</p>\n<ol><li>A track record of <strong>responding well to change</strong>: ideal; TQM is easy.</li><li><strong>Performed well but can't improve</strong> its systems; staff doubt change: needs a <strong>management and leadership development</strong> programme first (the most common case).</li><li><strong>Significant problems</strong> (unstable funding, weak administration, poor skills or morale): TQM is <strong>not appropriate</strong> yet.</li></ol>\n<h3>Two approaches</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Integrated management approach</strong>: TQM fits the organization's own culture. Sub-choice one: the organization already works well and accepts innovation, so TQM becomes the \"accepted advantage\", implemented internally. Sub-choice two: TQM supplies missing processes and competencies (decision-making, measurement) through well-designed TQM initiatives.</li><li><strong>Traditional management approach</strong>: the <strong>most common and the cause of most failures</strong>. TQM is <strong>forced onto existing culture</strong> as a purely technical process by outside experts in a short burst of lectures. Behaviour changes for a while, but culture doesn't (real culture change takes <strong>at least three years</strong>), so TQM is abandoned when money runs out or a new fad arrives.</li></ul>\n<h3>Guidelines for implementing TQM</h3>\n<p>There is <strong>no single right way</strong>. Guidelines: top management <strong>commitment</strong> and a shared direction; <strong>customer identification, surveys and feedback</strong> reflected in quality and timeliness indicators; <strong>quality teams</strong> for specific problems; <strong>organizational assessment and leadership development</strong>; involve line personnel; <strong>identify customers and their requirements</strong>; <strong>make a specific plan</strong>.</p>", "measurement": "<h3>Quality measurement system</h3>\n<p><strong>Measurement</strong> assesses product characteristics or process parameters and gives <strong>feedback</strong> so the operation can adjust. Because TQM needs big investment, firms expect a return; three things block that return:</p>\n<h3>1. High cost of poor quality (COPQ)</h3>\n<p><strong>COPQ</strong> is the cost of <strong>non-value-added activities and waste</strong>, typically <strong>20–40%</strong>. Cutting expenses (laying off a third of staff, selling assets) protects margins but barely touches COPQ. The firm that survives competition is the one that <strong>cuts COPQ</strong>: hard in the short term, but the only route to long-term profit.</p>\n<h3>2. Improper time management</h3>\n<p>People say they lack time. A <strong>Pareto diagram</strong> of time use shows most time spent on <strong>firefighting</strong>, paperwork, meetings and travel, with little on <strong>fire prevention</strong>. Proper management shifts time <strong>to prevention</strong>.</p>\n<h3>3. Lack of score keeping</h3>\n<p>Everyone talks about quality but <strong>no one keeps score</strong>. Managers should plot measures like <strong>defects per unit (dpu)</strong> or COPQ over time; a <strong>scorecard</strong> shows whether the team is winning or losing and stops quality work becoming mere routine.</p>\n<h3>Unit 3 key words</h3>\n<p><strong>Affinity diagram</strong>: groups verbal data by natural relationship. <strong>Activity network</strong>: nodes and arrows for scheduling. <strong>COPQ</strong>: cost of non-value-added activity and waste. <strong>Interrelations diagram</strong>: finds root causes and effects. <strong>Matrix diagram</strong>: rows and columns showing relationship strength. <strong>PDPC</strong>: maps contingencies in implementation.</p>", "iso": "<h3>What is ISO 9000?</h3>\n<p><strong>ISO</strong> is the <strong>International Organization for Standardization</strong>, a federation of national standards bodies of about <strong>130 countries</strong>, developing <strong>voluntary</strong> technical standards since <strong>1947</strong>. <strong>ISO 9000</strong> is about <strong>quality management</strong>: the part of management that sets and implements the <strong>quality policy</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality policy</strong>: the overall quality intentions and direction formally expressed by top management.</li><li><strong>QMS</strong> covers procedures, tools, capability assessment and documentation.</li><li>ISO 9000 is <strong>generic</strong>: it applies to any organization, large or small, goods or services.</li><li>It is <strong>not a product standard</strong> (it concerns <em>how</em> work is done, not the result), <strong>not mandatory</strong> and <strong>not a government regulation</strong>, but it is an important <strong>customer requirement</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h3>History</h3>\n<p>Introduced in <strong>1987</strong>, adopted quickly and went global (90+ countries, including India). Revised in <strong>1994</strong>. Originally six standards:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ISO 8402</strong></td><td>Vocabulary (quality terms)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ISO 9000</strong></td><td>Guidelines for selecting and using the standards</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ISO 9001</strong></td><td>Most complete: <strong>design/development, production, installation and servicing</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>ISO 9002</strong></td><td><strong>Production and installation</strong> only (firms that don't design)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ISO 9003</strong></td><td><strong>Final inspection and test</strong> only, the least detailed</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ISO 9004</strong></td><td>Guidelines for <strong>internal</strong> quality management (technical, administrative, human factors)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Criticism in the 1990s (firms could comply just by following their own manual) led to <strong>ISO 9000:2000</strong>: 9001, 9002 and 9003 merged into <strong>ISO 9001:2000</strong> (the certifiable standard); the 20 elements were reduced to four sections (management responsibility, resource management, product realization, and measurement, analysis and improvement).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>ISO 9000:2000</strong>: concepts and <strong>vocabulary</strong>.</li><li><strong>ISO 9001:2000</strong>: <strong>requirements</strong> for certification or contracts.</li><li><strong>ISO 9004:2000</strong>: <strong>advisory</strong> guidance to go beyond 9001.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why a management standard?</h3>\n<p>It ensures orderly, efficient work; a systematic approach so nothing is missed and everyone knows who does what, when, how, why and where; it is most needed in <strong>large</strong> organizations; and it reduces repeated customer inspections, cutting cost for both supplier and buyer.</p>\n<h3>Benefits of ISO 9000</h3>\n<p>Confidence in own capability, consistency, timely delivery and fewer failures, plus: <strong>improvement in business</strong> (efficiency, less waste), <strong>simplified purchasing decisions</strong> (fewer vendor assessments), <strong>qualifying for new customers</strong> and global markets, <strong>educational experience</strong> and teamwork, <strong>improved market image</strong>, products accepted across countries, and <strong>organizational renovation</strong> (removing obsolete procedures, better calibration and traceability).</p>", "isorequirements": "<h3>Requirements of ISO 9001:2000 (sections 4–8)</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Systematic requirements</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Establish the quality system</strong>: identify and describe processes, implement the QMS, monitor and improve process performance.</li><li><strong>Document the quality system</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>2. Management requirements</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality policy</strong>: easy to understand, relevant to the organization, with ambitious but achievable objectives.</li><li><strong>Demonstrate commitment</strong>: make sure people understand the policy; initiate and follow up implementation; accept no deviations or waste.</li><li><strong>Quality planning</strong>: measurable objectives for functional areas.</li><li><strong>Control the quality system</strong>: appoint a <strong>management representative</strong>; set up communication channels; hold <strong>management reviews</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>3. Resource requirements</strong>: provide <strong>competent, trained people</strong>, suitable <strong>infrastructure</strong> and <strong>work environment</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Realization requirements</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Realization planning</strong>: identify needs and plan quality control during realization.</li><li><strong>Customer processes</strong>: identify requirements and review them <strong>before accepting orders</strong>; control customer communication.</li><li><strong>Product development</strong>: clear responsibilities, managed interfaces, recorded changes, verification.</li><li><strong>Purchasing</strong>: purchased items and suppliers meet requirements (delivery, after-sales); verify at own or supplier premises.</li><li><strong>Operational activities</strong>: control production and service provision.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>5. Remedial requirements</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Monitor and measure</strong> quality.</li><li><strong>Control non-conforming products</strong>: identify them, prevent their delivery or use, correct them, prove corrected products meet requirements, keep records.</li><li><strong>Make quality improvements</strong>: use audits, policy, objectives and reviews; corrective actions must <strong>prevent recurrence</strong>.</li></ul>", "documentation": "<h3>Documentation for an ISO 9000 QMS</h3>\n<p>ISO 9001 requires an organization to <em>\"establish, document, implement and maintain a QMS and continually improve its effectiveness.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>Documents required</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Quality policy and objectives</strong></li><li><strong>Quality manual</strong></li><li><strong>Six documented procedures</strong> (in this edition): <strong>control of documents</strong>, <strong>control of records</strong>, <strong>internal audit</strong>, <strong>control of non-conforming product</strong>, <strong>corrective action</strong>, <strong>preventive action</strong></li><li>Other documents: <strong>specifications</strong> (stating requirements), <strong>quality plans</strong> (which procedures and resources apply, by whom and when, to a project, product or contract), and documents for design, purchasing, work execution and inspection.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Records</strong> are documents that <strong>state results achieved or provide evidence of activities performed</strong>: management reviews; education, training, skills and experience; evidence that realization processes meet requirements, and so on.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><strong>Document vs record:</strong> a <em>document</em> says what to do (information); a <em>record</em> proves it was done (evidence). Memory: <em>say what you do, do what you say, show that you did it.</em></div>\n<h3>Why documentation matters</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Communication</strong> of intent and consistent action (its extent depends on the products, processes, formality, communication skills and culture).</li><li><strong>Evidence of conformity</strong>: proof that what was planned was done.</li><li><strong>Knowledge sharing</strong>: preserving experience (e.g. a technical specification reused for a new design).</li><li>A goal of the revision was a <strong>simplified set of standards</strong>.</li></ul>", "isoimplementation": "<h3>Steps to implement ISO 9000</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Exposure and training</strong>: ISO 9000 familiarization for everyone (and certified vendors); share the plan, e.g. through an internal ISO 9000 booklet.</li><li><strong>Develop the draft quality manual</strong>: study all existing quality systems and practices, collecting charts, records, reports and data sheets.</li><li><strong>Internal audit of existing systems and incorporation of ISO 9000</strong>: task groups study the requirements, find gaps and rectify deficiencies.</li><li><strong>Certification by a third party</strong>: send the manual to an external agency, answer its questionnaire, then host an on-site <strong>audit</strong> of premises and systems.</li></ol>\n<p>(The general path: install a task force → review operations → develop the manual → internal audit → rectify → third-party certification.)</p>\n<h3>Three starting situations</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Preparing to implement a QMS</strong>: use the <strong>process approach</strong>: identify processes, understand their interactions, and document them as needed (process maps are optional).</li><li><strong>Adapting an existing QMS</strong> (e.g. certified to the 1994 version): make modifications for ISO 9001:2000.</li><li><strong>Demonstrating conformity</strong> of an established system.</li></ul>", "osha": "<h3>Why safety and health?</h3>\n<p>Many accidents come from <strong>lack of safety awareness</strong> and <strong>unsafe conditions</strong>; person-days lost to job-related disability are about <strong>ten times</strong> those lost to strikes. Workers face chemical, physical and biological risks (e.g. spray painters without masks, gloves or goggles). Long hours and poor rest harm health. Without safety effort, lost production, wages, medical costs and compensation come straight out of profit.</p>\n<h3>OSHA</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Occupational Safety and Health Act (1970, USA)</strong> set up the <strong>Occupational Safety and Health Administration</strong> in the Department of Labor to:</p>\n<ul><li>encourage employers and employees to <strong>reduce hazards</strong> and improve safety programmes;</li><li>fund <strong>research</strong> on safety and health;</li><li>set <strong>\"separate but dependent\" responsibilities and rights</strong> for employers and employees;</li><li>keep a <strong>reporting and record-keeping</strong> system for injuries and illnesses;</li><li><strong>train</strong> more safety and health personnel;</li><li>develop and <strong>enforce mandatory standards</strong>;</li><li>approve state programmes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Types of OSHA standards</h3>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Specification</strong></td><td>Exact requirements (e.g. sizes, locations and warning symbols of signs)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Performance</strong></td><td>Minimum and maximum limits of achievement</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Particular (vertical)</strong></td><td>Specific to one <strong>industry</strong> or operation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>General (horizontal)</strong></td><td>Apply to <strong>all workplaces</strong>: environmental control, walking surfaces, exits, lighting</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3>Benefits</h3>\n<p>Better community relations, less absenteeism, better morale, steadier work. <strong>Employers</strong> get help identifying and controlling hazards, and inspections focused on the worst hazards. <strong>Employees</strong> get participation in safety programmes and inspections, prompt response to serious hazards, and advice on their rights and responsibilities.</p>\n<h3>Making safety rules work</h3>\n<p>Mass awareness campaigns often <strong>fail to transfer</strong> to real work. Rules work when employees <strong>accept them</strong> (not imposed from above), they <strong>keep pace</strong> with technology and schedules, they <strong>don't cause needless inconvenience</strong>, and they are <strong>specific</strong>, not vague.</p>\n<h3>Assessing a safety and health programme</h3>\n<p><strong>Three methods:</strong> (1) <strong>checking documentation</strong> of activities (e.g. self-inspection records: are the same hazards recurring?), (2) <strong>interviewing employees at all levels</strong> about knowledge and perceptions, (3) <strong>reviewing site conditions</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Four key components assessed:</strong></p>\n<ol><li><strong>Leadership, participation and accountability</strong>: a worksite policy putting safety above production; <strong>visible top-management leadership</strong> (approving inspection reports, regular time for safety concerns).</li><li><strong>Worksite analysis</strong>.</li><li><strong>Hazard prevention and control</strong>: inspection documents and corrective actions.</li><li><strong>Safety and health training</strong>: regular training and periodic awareness checks.</li></ol>", "environment": "<h3>ISO 14000</h3>\n<p>A set of standards on how an organization's activities <strong>affect the environment throughout the life of its products</strong>, from production to <strong>disposal</strong>: pollution, waste, noise, resource depletion and energy use. Uniform standards avoid conflicting regional interpretations.</p>\n<p><strong>It covers:</strong> environmental <strong>management systems</strong>, environmental <strong>auditing</strong>, environmental <strong>performance evaluation</strong>, environmental <strong>labelling</strong> and <strong>life-cycle assessment</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Key features</strong></p>\n<ul><li>An <strong>environmental policy fully supported by senior management</strong> must exist, and be made known to <strong>staff and the public</strong>.</li><li>The policy must commit to <strong>compliance with environmental laws</strong>, <strong>continual improvement</strong> and <strong>prevention of pollution</strong>.</li><li><strong>Voluntary and flexible</strong>; encourages <strong>cost saving</strong> by integrating environmental requirements into design and manufacturing; can give a <strong>market advantage</strong>.</li><li>The system cycle: <strong>planning → implementation and operation → checking and corrective action → management review</strong> (the PDCA idea again).</li></ul>\n<h3>Unit 4 summary</h3>\n<p>ISO 9000 is the worldwide quality standard; implementing a QMS needs strong <strong>documentation</strong>; <strong>health and safety</strong> are integral to TQM, with OSHA guiding employers and workers; <strong>ISO 14000</strong> brings uniform good environmental practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Key words:</strong> <em>Document</em> = information plus its medium (paper, disc, photo). <em>Generic</em> = applicable to any organization. <em>Record</em> = a document stating results achieved, as evidence.</p>\n<h3>How to write the 3-mark SDL answer</h3>\n<p>Define the concept in one line → give 3–4 labelled points (the list from these notes) → add a one-line example or a small diagram (TQM house, PDCA circle, Joiner's triangle, fishbone). That's full marks in under half a page.</p>"}, "sources": [{"id": "m1", "file": "m1.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m1-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "COURSE OUTCOMES HUM 3022 EOM At the end of this course the student must be able to CO1 CO2 CO3 CO4 CO5", "image": "assets/pages/m1-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "COURSE SYLLABUS HUM 3022 EOM By the end of the semester, we will be covering 12345", "image": "assets/pages/m1-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "COURSE ASSESSMENT HUM 3022 EOM During the semester, we will be conducting 1234", "image": "assets/pages/m1-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "ASSIGNMENT [ TERM PROJECT ] Each group consists of 5 members Each group is assigned a Fortune 500 COMPANY as a Topic Each member of the group selects one managerial function to work on the COMPANY ASSIGNED Managerial functions to pick – PLANNING, ORGANIZING, STAFFING, LEADING & CONTROLLING There will be two part of evaluation for this term project 5 MARKS for the REPORT 5 MARKS for the PRESENTATION [ more instructions and guidelines will be shared shortly ]", "image": "assets/pages/m1-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "VERY IMPORTANT As a Department Policy, there won’t be any remedial classes or extra classes engaged at the end of the semester for those falling short of 75% attendance. It is essential that you MANAGE YOUR ATTENDANCE…", "image": "assets/pages/m1-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "BASIS OF MANAGEMENT THEORY AND PRACTICE CHAPTER 1", "image": "assets/pages/m1-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "CHAPTER 1 BASIS OF MANAGEMENT THEORY AND PRACTICE Introduc;on to Business ClassiAca;on of Industries Importance of management for an engineer Manager – DeAni;on & classiAca;on managers and administrators Systems Approach to Management Func;ons of Managers 3 Managerial Skills 14 Principles of Management by Henri Fayol Mintzberg’s 10 roles played by managers External Environment Social Responsibility of managers Ethics in managing – an integrated approach Trust as the Basis for Change Management Interna;onal Business", "image": "assets/pages/m1-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "BUSINESS A business is an organiza4on that strives for a pro:t by providing goods and services desired by its customers. Businesses meet the needs of consumers by providing medical care, autos, and countless other goods and services. Goods are tangible items manufactured by businesses, such as laptops. Services are intangible oBerings of businesses that can’t be held, touched, or stored.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "INDUSTRY Industry refers to economic ac4vi4es, which relate to conversion of resources into useful goods. Industry is concerned with the produc4on or processing of goods and materials as well as breeding and raising of animals. The term industry is also used for ac4vi4es in which mechanical appliances and technical skills are involved.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "INDUSTRY Vs BUSINESS An industry refers to a group of companies or organiza4ons that produce similar goods or services. It represents a speci:c sector of the economy. Industries are characterized by the type of products or services they oBer, and the ac4vi4es involved in their produc4on. Example: Automo4ve industry Technology industry A business, on the other hand, refers to an individual company or organiza4on engaged in commercial ac4vi4es. It is an en4ty that operates within a speci:c industry and aims to generate pro:ts by oBering products or services to customers. Example: Tesla Apple", "image": "assets/pages/m1-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "How can industrial ac4vity be classi:ed? There are many diSerent types of industry. We can classify industry into FOUR main categories: SECONDARY These industries provide a service. These industries extract raw materials directly from the earth or sea. These industries process and manufacture products from raw materials. PRIMARY TERTIARY QUATERNARY INDUSTRIES ?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "These industries incorporate a high degree of research and technology in their processes and employ highly quali:ed people. Biotechnology and computer programming are examples of quaternary industries. What is a quaternary industry?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "Classi:ca4on of industry        ", "image": "assets/pages/m1-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "Importance of Management for an Engineer SNEHA JOHN Listen to this case and you need to decide on which team will you join? And why?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "MANAGERS Managers are individuals in an organiza4on who direct the ac4vi4es of others. So before we can iden4fy who managers are and what they do, we need to de:ne what an organiza4on is: “a deliberate arrangement of people brought together to accomplish some speciAc purpose”", "image": "assets/pages/m1-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "A manager is an individual responsible for planning, organizing, leading, and controlling resources and ac4vi4es within an organiza4on to achieve speci:c goals. Managers play a crucial role in ensuring that their team or department operates eTciently and eBec4vely, aligning with the overall objec4ves of the organiza4on. MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "Types of Managers LEVELS OF MANAGEMENT The levels of management can be classi:ed in three broad categories: 123", "image": "assets/pages/m1-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "MANAGERIAL SKILLS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "20 Skill Requirement in Managers TOP MIDDLE BOTTOM Guess the management Level from the skills shown", "image": "assets/pages/m1-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "Planning Planning involves selecting missions and objectives as well as the actions to achieve them; it requires decision-making, which is choosing future courses of action from among alternatives. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "Organize Organizing is that part of managing which involves establishing an intentional structure of roles for people to Xll in an organization. It is intentional in the sense of making sure that all the tasks necessary to accomplish goals are assigned and, it is assigned to people who can do them best. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "Staing StaYng involves Xlling and keeping Xlled the positions in the organization structure. This is done by identifying workforce requirements; inventorying the people available; and recruiting, selecting, placing, promoting, appraising, compensating, and training so that tasks are accomplished eZectively and eYciently. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "Leading Leading is in[uencing people so that they will contribute to organizational and group goals; it has to do predominantly with the interpersonal aspect of managing. Since leadership implies followership, and people tend to follow those who oZer means of satisfying their own needs, wishes, and desires, it is understandable that leading involves motivation, leadership styles and approaches, and communication. FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "FUNCTIONS OF MANAGERS Controlling Controlling is measuring and correcting individual and organizational performance to ensure that events conform to plans. It involves measuring performance against goals and plans, showing where deviations from standards exist, and helping to correct deviations from standards. In short, controlling facilitates the accomplishment of plans. Although planning must precede controlling, plans are not self-achieving.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-26.webp"}, {"n": 27, "text": "SYSTEMS APPROACH TO MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m1-27.webp"}, {"n": 28, "text": "SYSTEMS APPROACH TO MANAGEMENT • Organization is an open system • Dependent on its external environment for inputs • Various inputs are transformed through the managerial functions • Communication is essential to all phases • Some of the outputs become inputs again", "image": "assets/pages/m1-28.webp"}, {"n": 29, "text": "Systems Approach to Management An organiza4on consists of many sub-systems. All the sub-systems are mutually related to each other. The sub-parts should be studied in their inter-rela4onships rather than in isola4on from each other. The organiza4on provides a boundary, which separates it from other systems. It determines which parts are internal and which parts are external. The organiza4on is responsive to environmental eBects. It is vulnerable to the changes in the environment. An organiza4on is a system consis4ng of many interrelated and interdependent parts or sub-systems. These elements are arranged orderly according to some scheme such that this is more than the sum of the parts. YOUR READING", "image": "assets/pages/m1-29.webp"}, {"n": 30, "text": "Systems Approach to Management As a system an organiza4on draws inputs (energy, informa4on, materials, :nance etc.) from its environment, it transforms these inputs and returns the output back into the environment in the form of goods and services. Every system is a part of a super system. Organiza4on is an open system and it interacts with its environment. It is also a dynamic system as the equilibrium in it is always changing. Management is expected to regulate and adjust the system to secure be^er performance. Management is mul4disciplinary as it draws and integrates knowledge from various disciplines. YOUR READING", "image": "assets/pages/m1-30.webp"}, {"n": 31, "text": "31 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of management Henry Fayol, also known as the Father of Modern Management Theory, gave a new perception on the concept of management. He introduced a general theory that can be applied to all levels of management and every department. He envisioned maximizing managerial eYciency. Today, Fayol’s theory is practiced by the management to organize and regulate the internal activities of an organization", "image": "assets/pages/m1-31.webp"}, {"n": 32, "text": "A PRINCIPLE REFERS TO A FUNDAMENTAL TRUTH. It establishes cause and eSect rela;onship between two or more variables under given situa4on. They serve as a guide to thought & ac4ons. Therefore, management principles are the statements of fundamental truth based on logic which provides guidelines for managerial decision making and ac;ons. These principles are derived on the basis of • observa4on and analysis i.e. prac4cal experience of managers • by conduc4ng experimental studies. HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management 32", "image": "assets/pages/m1-32.webp"}, {"n": 33, "text": "33 1. Division of LABOR 2. AUTHORITY & RESPONSIBILITY 3. Unity of COMMAND (Principle of one boss) 4. Unity of DIRECTION (Principle of one plan) 5. EQUITY (combina4on of fairness, kindness & jus4ce) 6. ORDER (systema4c arrangement of things and people) 7. DISCIPLINE 8. INITIATIVE (eagerness to ini4ate ac4ons without being asked to do so) 9. FAIR Remunera;on 10. Stability of TENURE 11. SCALAR Chain (chain of command) 12. Sub-Ordina;on of INDIVIDUAL INTEREST to General Interest 13. Esprit De’ Corps (team spirit) 14. Centraliza;on & De-Centraliza;on HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management Watch the clip and iden;fy the principles used from HENRY FAYOL’s LIST", "image": "assets/pages/m1-33.webp"}, {"n": 34, "text": "Division of Labor – Henry Fayol has stressed on the specializa;on of jobs. – He recommended that work of all kinds must be divided & subdivided and allo^ed to various persons according to their exper4se in a par4cular area. – Subdivision of work makes it simpler and results in ejciency. – It also helps the individual in acquiring speed & accuracy in his/her performance. – Specializa4on leads to eTciency & economy in spheres of business. 34 1 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-34.webp"}, {"n": 35, "text": "Authority & Responsibility – Authority & responsibility are co-exis4ng. – If authority is given to a person, he should also be made responsible and vice versa. – Authority refers to the right of superiors to get exactness from their sub-ordinates whereas responsibility means obliga;on for the performance of the job assigned. – They must go hand in hand. – Authority without responsibility leads to irresponsible behavior whereas responsibility without authority makes the person ineBec4ve. 35 Compliant means disposed to agree or obey rules, in accordance with rules or standards 2 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-35.webp"}, {"n": 36, "text": "Unity of command (Principle of One Boss) – A sub-ordinate should receive orders and be accountable to one and only one boss at a 4me. – In other words, a sub-ordinate should not receive instruc4ons from more than one person because: – Therefore, dual sub-ordina4on should be avoided unless and un4l it is absolutely essen4al. – Unity of command provides the enterprise a disciplined, stable & orderly existence. – It creates harmonious rela4onship between superiors and sub-ordinates. 36 • It undermines authority • Weakens discipline • Divides loyalty • Creates confusion • Delays and chaos • Escaping responsibili4es • Duplica4on of work • Overlapping of eBorts 3 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-36.webp"}, {"n": 37, "text": "Unity of Direc;on – Fayol advocates ONE HEAD - ONE PLAN which means that there should be one plan for a group of ac4vi4es having similar objec4ves. – Related ac4vi4es should be grouped together. There should be one plan of ac4on for them and they should be under the charge of a par4cular manager. – According to this principle, eBorts of all the members of the organiza4on should be directed towards common goal. – Without unity of direc4on, unity of ac4on cannot be achieved. – In fact, unity of command is not possible without unity of direc;on. 37 4 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-37.webp"}, {"n": 38, "text": "38 Basis UNITY OF COMMAND UNITY OF DIRECTION Meaning It implies that a sub-ordinate should receive orders & instruc4ons from only one boss. It means one head, one plan for a group of ac4vi4es having similar objec4ves. Nature It is related to the func4oning of personnel’s. It is related to the func4oning of departments, or organiza4on as a whole. Necessity It is necessary for :xing responsibility of each subordinates. It is necessary for sound organiza4on. Advantage It avoids congicts, confusion & chaos. It avoids duplica4on of eBorts and wastage of resources. Result It leads to be^er superior sub-ordinate rela4onship. It leads to smooth running of the enterprise. 4 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-38.webp"}, {"n": 39, "text": "Equity – Equity means combina4on of fairness, kindness & jus4ce. – It implies that managers SHOULD BE FAIR AND IMPARTIAL while dealing with the subordinates. – Equity is essen;al to create and maintain cordial rela;ons between the managers and sub-ordinate. – But equity does not mean total absence of harshness. – Fayol was of opinion that, “at 4mes force and harshness might become necessary for the sake of equity”. 39 5 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-39.webp"}, {"n": 40, "text": "Order – This principle is concerned with proper & systema;c arrangement of things and people. – Arrangement of things is called MATERIAL ORDER and placement of people is called SOCIAL ORDER. 40 6 Material order - There should be safe, appropriate and speci:c place for every ar4cle and every place to be eBec4vely used for speci:c ac4vity and commodity. Social order - Selec4on and appointment of most suitable person on the suitable job. There should be a speci:c place for every one and everyone should have a speci:c place so that they can easily be contacted whenever need arises. HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-40.webp"}, {"n": 41, "text": "5S Japanese 5S’s English Translation American 5S Standard Seiri ( !\" ) Put Things in Order Sort Seiton ( !# ) Proper Arrangement Straighten, Set in Order, Simplify Seiso ( $% ) Clean Shine, Scrub, Sweep Seiketsu ( $& ) Purity Standardize Shitsuke ( ' ) Commitment Sustain, Discipline 5S is a workplace organization method that uses a list of five Japanese words 6", "image": "assets/pages/m1-41.webp"}, {"n": 42, "text": "Discipline – “Discipline means sincerity, obedience, respect of authority & observance of rules and regula;ons of the enterprise”. – This principle applies that subordinate should respect their superiors and obey their order. – Discipline is not only required on the part of subordinates but also on the part of management. – Discipline can be enforced if 42 • There are good superiors at all levels. • There are clear & fair agreements with workers. • Sanc4ons (punishments) are judiciously applied. 7 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-42.webp"}, {"n": 43, "text": "Ini;a;ve – Workers should be encouraged to take ini4a4ve in the work assigned to them. It means eagerness to ini;ate ac;ons without being asked to do so. – Fayol advised that management should provide opportunity to its employees to suggest ideas, experiences & new method of work. – It helps in developing an atmosphere of trust and understanding as people then enjoy working in the organiza4on because it adds to their zeal and energy. – They can be encouraged with the help of monetary & non-monetary incen4ves. 43 8 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-43.webp"}, {"n": 44, "text": "Fair Remunera;on – The quantum and method of remunera4on to be paid to the workers should be fair, reasonable, sa4sfactory & rewarding of the eBorts. – As far as possible it should accord sa4sfac4on to both employer and the employees. – Wages should be determined on the basis of cost of living, work assigned, :nancial posi4on of the business, wage rate prevailing etc. – Fayol also recommended provision of other bene:ts such as free educa4on, medical & residen4al facili4es to workers. 44 9 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-44.webp"}, {"n": 45, "text": "Stability of Tenure – Fayol emphasized that employees should not be moved frequently from one job posi4on to another i.e. the period of service in a job should be :xed. – According to Fayol “Time is required for an employee to get used to a new work & succeed in doing it well but if he is removed before that he will not be able to render worthwhile services”. As a result, the 4me, eBort and money spent on training the worker will go waste. Stability of job creates team spirit and a sense of belongingness among workers which ul;mately increase the quality as well as quan;ty of work. 45 10 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-45.webp"}, {"n": 46, "text": "Scalar Chain – Fayol de:nes scalar chain as ’The chain of superiors ranging from the ul4mate authority to the lowest”. – Every orders, instruc4ons, messages, requests, explana4on etc. has to pass through Scalar chain. – But, for the sake of convenience & urgency, this path can be cut short and this short cut is known as Gang Plank. – A Gang Plank is a temporary arrangement between two diBerent points to facilitate quick & easy communica4on. 46 Gang Plank clari:es that management principles are not rigid rather they are very gexible. They can be molded and modi:ed as per the requirements of situa4ons 11 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-46.webp"}, {"n": 47, "text": "Sub-Ordina;on of Individual Interest to General Interest – An organiza4on is much bigger than the individual it cons4tutes therefore interest of the undertaking should prevail in all circumstances. – As far as possible, reconcilia4on should be achieved between individual and group interests. – But in case of congict, individual must sacri:ce for bigger interests. – In order to achieve this altude, it is essen4al that • Employees should be honest & sincere. • Proper & regular supervision of work. • Reconcilia4on of mutual diBerences and clashes by mutual agreement. 12 Reconcilia4on means se^lement, understanding, resolu4on or even some4mes compromise HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-47.webp"}, {"n": 48, "text": "Esprit De’ Corps (can be achieved through unity of command) – It refers to team spirit i.e. harmony in the work groups and mutual understanding among the members. – Esprit De’ Corps inspires workers to work harder. – Fayol cau4oned the managers against dividing the employees into compe4ng groups because it might damage the morale of the workers and interest of the undertaking in the long run. 48 13 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-48.webp"}, {"n": 49, "text": "– To inculcate Esprit De’ Corps following steps should be undertaken - • There should be proper co-ordina;on of work at all levels • Subordinates should be encouraged to develop informal rela;ons among themselves. • EBorts should be made to create enthusiasm and keenness among subordinates so that they can work to the maximum ability. • ETcient employees should be rewarded and those who are not up to the mark should be given a chance to improve their performance. • Subordinates should be made conscious of that whatever they are doing is of great importance to the business & society. – He also cau4oned against the excessive use of Britain communica;on with the subordinates rather he suggests a more face to face communica4on should be developed. The managers should infuse team spirit & belongingness. There should be no place for misunderstanding. People then enjoy working in the organiza4on & oBer their best towards the organiza4on. 13 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-49.webp"}, {"n": 50, "text": "Centraliza;on & De-Centraliza;on – Centraliza4on means concentra4on of authority at the top level. In other words, centraliza4on is a situa4on in which top management retains most of the decision making authority. – Decentraliza4on means disposal of decision making authority to all the levels of the organiza4on. In other words, sharing authority downwards is decentraliza4on. 50 14 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-50.webp"}, {"n": 51, "text": "Centraliza;on & De-Centraliza;on – According to Fayol, “Degree of centraliza4on or decentraliza4on depends on number of factors like size of business, experience of superiors, dependability & ability of subordinates etc. –Anything which increases the role of subordinate is decentraliza6on & anything which decreases it is centraliza6on. – Fayol suggested that absolute centraliza4on or decentraliza4on is not feasible. An organiza4on should strike to achieve a lot between the two. 51 14 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-51.webp"}, {"n": 52, "text": "52 Level 1 Level 2 Level 3 Level 4 Decentralized approach Centralized approach Bomom-to-top-approach Top-to-bomom-approach14 HENRI FAYOL’S 14 PRINCIPLES of Management", "image": "assets/pages/m1-52.webp"}, {"n": 53, "text": "MANAGERIAL ROLES 53 One widely discussed approach to management theory is the managerial roles approach, popularized by Henry Mintzberg of McGill University.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-53.webp"}, {"n": 54, "text": "C B A In this Henry Mintzberg’s model, managers play ten diZerent roles (organized sets of behaviors identiXed with a position) that fall into three basic categories: 54 MANAGERIAL ROLES Interpersonal roles: 1. Figurehead role 2. Leader role 3. Liaison role Informational roles: 4. Monitor role 5. Disseminator role 6. Spokesman role Decisional roles: 7. Entrepreneurial role 8. Disturbance handler role 9. Resource allocator role 10. Negotiator role", "image": "assets/pages/m1-54.webp"}, {"n": 55, "text": "55", "image": "assets/pages/m1-55.webp"}, {"n": 56, "text": "A. Interpersonal roles: 1. Figurehead role 2. Leader role 3. Liaison role 56 MANAGERIAL ROLES", "image": "assets/pages/m1-56.webp"}, {"n": 57, "text": "1. Figurehead role By virtue of his position as head of an organizational unit, every manager must perform some duties of ceremonial nature. a. The president greets the touring dignitaries (attends ribbon cutting ceremonies), b. the foreman attends the wedding of a lathe operator, and c. the sales manager takes an important customer to lunch. Duties that involve interpersonal roles may sometimes be routine, involving little serious communication and no important decision-making. Nevertheless, they are important to the smooth functioning of an organization and cannot be ignored by the manager. 57 MANAGERIAL ROLES A. Interpersonal roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-57.webp"}, {"n": 58, "text": "2. Leader role Because he is in charge of an organizational unit, the manager is responsible for the work of the people of that unit. His actions in this regard constitute the leader role. Some of these actions involve leadership directly – for example, a. in most organizations the manager is normally responsible for hiring and training his staZ. In addition, there is the indirect exercise of the leader role. b. Every manager must motivate and encourage his employees, somehow reconciling their individual needs with the goals of the organization. The in[uence of the manager is most clearly seen in the leader role. Formal authority vests him with great potential power; leadership determines in large part how much of it he will realize. 58 MANAGERIAL ROLES A. Interpersonal roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-58.webp"}, {"n": 59, "text": "3. Liaison role In the liaison role the manager makes contacts outside his vertical chain of command. Managers spend as much time with peers and other people outside their units as they do with their own subordinates, and surprisingly little time with their own superiors. The manager cultivates such contacts largely to 3nd information. In eZect, the liaison role is devoted to building up the manager’s own external information system – informal, private, verbal, but nevertheless eZective. 59 MANAGERIAL ROLES A. Interpersonal roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-59.webp"}, {"n": 60, "text": "B. Informational roles: 4. Monitor role 5. Disseminator role 6. Spokesman role 60 MANAGERIAL ROLES", "image": "assets/pages/m1-60.webp"}, {"n": 61, "text": "4. Monitor role As monitor, the manager continuously scans his environment for information, interrogates his liaison contacts and his subordinates, and receives unsolicited information, much of it as a result of the network of personal contacts he has developed. A good part of the information the manager collects in his monitor role arrives in verbal form, often as gossip, hearsay, and speculation. By virtue of his contacts, the manager has a natural advantage in collecting this soft information for his organization. 61 MANAGERIAL ROLES B. Informational roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-61.webp"}, {"n": 62, "text": "5. Disseminator role The manager must share and distribute much of information. Information he gathers from outside personal contacts may be needed within his organization. In his disseminator role, the manager passes some of his privileged information directly to his subordinates, who would otherwise have no access to it. When his subordinates lack easy contact with one another, the manager will sometimes pass information from one to another. 62 MANAGERIAL ROLES B. Informational roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-62.webp"}, {"n": 63, "text": "6. Spokesman role In his spokesman role, the manager sends some of his information to people outside his unit. In addition, as part of his spokesman role, every manager must inform and satisfy the in[uential people who control his organizational unit. The president of a large corporation may spend a great deal of his time dealing with a host of in[uences. • Directors and Shareholders must be advised about Dnancial performance; • Consumer Groups must be assured that the organization is fulXlling its social responsibility; and • Government OYcials must be satisXed that the organization is abiding by the law. 63 MANAGERIAL ROLES B. Informational roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-63.webp"}, {"n": 64, "text": "C. Decisional roles: 7. Entrepreneurial role 8. Disturbance handler role 9. Resource allocator role 10. Negotiator role 64 MANAGERIAL ROLES", "image": "assets/pages/m1-64.webp"}, {"n": 65, "text": "7. Entrepreneurial role The entrepreneurial role describes the manager as the voluntary initiator of change. As entrepreneur, the manager seeks to improve his unit and to adopt it to changing conditions in the environment. In his monitor role, the president is constantly on the lookout for new ideas. When a good one appears, he initiates a development project. The chief executive appears to maintain a kind of inventory of the development projects that he himself supervises. Like a juggler, he keeps a number of projects in the air: periodically one comes down, is given a new burst of energy, and is sent back into orbit. At various intervals, he puts new projects on-stream and discards old ones. 65 MANAGERIAL ROLES C. Decisional roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-65.webp"}, {"n": 66, "text": "8. Disturbance handler role While the entrepreneurial role describes the manager as the voluntary initiator of change, the disturbance handler role depicts the manager involuntarily responding to pressures. Here change is beyond the manager’s control. He must act because the pressures of the situation are too severe to be ignored: a strike looms, a major customer has gone bankrupt, road accident delays the raw materials etc. Every manager must spend a good part of his time responding to high-pressure disturbances. Disturbances arise not only because poor managers ignore situations until they reach crisis proportions, but also because good managers cannot possibly anticipate all the consequences of the actions they take. 66 MANAGERIAL ROLES C. Decisional roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-66.webp"}, {"n": 67, "text": "9. Resource allocator role Managers have the responsibility of deciding who will receive what in his organizational unit. Perhaps the most important resource the manager allocates is his own time. Access to the manager constitutes exposure to the unit’s nerve center and decision-center. The manager is also charged with designing his unit’s structure, that pattern of formal relationships that determines how work is to be divided and coordinated. Also in his role as resource allocator the manager authorizes the important decisions before they are implemented. 67 MANAGERIAL ROLES C. Decisional roles:", "image": "assets/pages/m1-67.webp"}, {"n": 68, "text": "10. Negotiator role Studies of managerial work at all levels indicate that managers spend considerable time in negotiations. Negotiations are duties of the manager’s job; perhaps routine, they are not to be shirked. 68 MANAGERIAL ROLES C. Decisional roles: Shirked means avoided, dodged, evaded", "image": "assets/pages/m1-68.webp"}, {"n": 69, "text": "External Environment", "image": "assets/pages/m1-69.webp"}, {"n": 70, "text": "Social Responsibility of Managers Society expects organiza4ons and managers to be responsible and ethical. But there are examples of notorious :nancial scandals, scams with quality of materials u4lized, and most of the 4me simple negligence, which proves that managers don’t always act responsibly or ethically.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-70.webp"}, {"n": 71, "text": "Social Responsibility of Managers On one side is the classical or purely economic view that management’s only social responsibility is to MAXIMIZE PROFITS. On the other side is the socioeconomic posi4on, which holds that management’s responsibility goes beyond making pro:ts to include PROTECTING AND IMPROVING SOCIETY’S WELFARE.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-71.webp"}, {"n": 72, "text": "Social Responsibility of Managers When we talk about social responsibility (also known as corporate social responsibility, or CSR), we mean a business :rm’s inten4on, beyond its legal and economic obliga4ons, to do the right things and act in ways that are good for society. Note that this de:ni4on assumes that a business obeys the law and pursues economic interests. But also note that this de:ni4on views a business as a moral agent. In its eBort to do good for society, it must diBeren4ate between right and wrong.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-72.webp"}, {"n": 73, "text": "CORPORATE SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITY (CSR) Corporate social responsibility comes from integra4on of three words: ‘corporate,' 'social,' and 'responsibility.’ Corporate: Organized business Social: Everything dealing with the people Responsibility: Accountability between the two", "image": "assets/pages/m1-73.webp"}, {"n": 74, "text": "CSR DEFINED CSR is the Corporate initiative to assess and take responsibility for the company's effects on the environment and impact on social welfare. The term generally applies to company efforts that go beyond what may be required by regulators or environmental protection groups. Corporate social responsibility may also be referred to as \"corporate citizenship\" and can involve incurring short-term costs that do not provide an immediate financial benefit, but instead promote positive social and environmental change.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-74.webp"}, {"n": 75, "text": "DRIVERS OF CSR 1. Employees and shareholders 2. Brand equity 3. Ethical marke;ng prac;ces and social awareness 4. The environment consciousness 5. Energy conserva;on and global warming 6. Responsibility towards the government Brand equity is the value of a brand, determined by the consumer's percep4on of its quality and desirability.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-75.webp"}, {"n": 76, "text": "• Increased employee satisfaction leads to employee competence which results in superior service delivery and customer satisfaction. • It takes decades for business organizations to build goodwill and trust in the minds of customers and shareholders. • Building an ethically responsible culture can reduce the chances of employees indulging in unfair practices within the organization by emphasizing on professionalism and transparency. • Socially responsible investments, effective communication, employee participation, fairness, and promotions, adequate compensation and personal development.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-76.webp"}, {"n": 77, "text": "ETHICS IN MANAGING – An Integrated Approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-77.webp"}, {"n": 78, "text": "ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach Ethics is de:ned as “the discipline dealing with what is good and bad and with moral duty and obliga4on.” Business Ethics is concerned with a systema4c study of morals. It strives to provide methods to dis4nguish between ac4ons and altudes that are detrimental for business and those that are ethically sound and inspire businesses.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-78.webp"}, {"n": 79, "text": "Ethical concepts have quite open been dismissed in management theory as “sop” factors and open as useless chamer. The fundamental problem stems from the fact that ethics has usually in theory and teaching not consistently been related to other management disciplines such as economics and :nance. Ethical considera;ons have quite open been rejected right away as related to values and religion. ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-79.webp"}, {"n": 80, "text": "ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-80.webp"}, {"n": 81, "text": "Three basic types of moral theories in the :eld of norma4ve ethics have been developed. First, the UTILITARIAN THEORY suggests that plans and ac4ons should be evaluated by their consequences. The underlying idea is that plans or ac4ons should produce the greatest good for the greatest number of people. ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-81.webp"}, {"n": 82, "text": "Three basic types of moral theories in the :eld of norma4ve ethics have been developed. Second, the theory BASED ON RIGHTS holds that all people have basic rights. Examples are the rights to freedom of conscience, free speech, and due process. ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-82.webp"}, {"n": 83, "text": "Three basic types of moral theories in the :eld of norma4ve ethics have been developed. Third, the THEORY OF JUSTICE demands that decision makers be guided by fairness and equity as well as impar4ality. ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-83.webp"}, {"n": 84, "text": "examples A tech company ensures user data privacy by refusing to sell personal informa4on. A media company protects journalists' right to publish unbiased reports without censorship. A :rm provides employees with paid leave for vo4ng to support their civic rights. THESE EXAMPLES ARE BASED ON RIGHTS guess the ethical concept used ?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-84.webp"}, {"n": 85, "text": "A corpora4on ensures equal pay for employees performing the same work, regardless of gender or background. A retailer adopts a fair pricing policy to ensure small vendors are treated equitably. A school provides scholarships to economically disadvantaged students to promote fairness. THESE EXAMPLES ARE FOR THEORY OF JUSTICE examples guess the ethical concept used ?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-85.webp"}, {"n": 86, "text": "A company decides to implement a recycling program that bene:ts the environment and reduces waste for the en4re community. A pharmaceu4cal :rm prices a life-saving drug aBordably to maximize accessibility for more people. A manufacturer shuts down a high-pollu4ng unit to ensure long-term health for surrounding communi4es. THESE EXAMPLES ARE FOR UTILITARIAN THEORY examples guess the ethical concept used ?", "image": "assets/pages/m1-86.webp"}, {"n": 87, "text": "Managers, especially top managers, do have a responsibility to create an organiza4onal environment that fosters ethical decision-making by ins4tu4onalizing ethics. This means applying and integra4ng ethical concepts with daily ac4ons. this can be accomplished in three ways: (1) by establishing an appropriate company policy or a code of ethics, (2) by using a formally appointed ethics commi^ee, and (3) by teaching ethics in management development programs ETHICS IN MANAGING – an integrated approach", "image": "assets/pages/m1-87.webp"}, {"n": 88, "text": "TRUST as the basis for CHANGE MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m1-88.webp"}, {"n": 89, "text": "Trust as the Basis for Change Management Change management is a systema4c approach to dealing with the transi4on or transforma4on of an organiza4on's goals, processes and technologies. The purpose of change management is to implement strategies for eBec4ng and controlling change and helping people to adapt to change.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-89.webp"}, {"n": 90, "text": "Trust as the Basis for Change Management Managers are bombarded with new managerial concepts and old ones oqen are disguised by new terminology-all designed for coping with managerial change demanded by global compe44on, customer expecta4ons, and the need to respond quickly to environmental changes. Although various approaches to managing change in the New Age are available, but the one oqen overlooked concept is trust. Trust is at the center of communica;on, collabora;on, and the willingness to change.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-90.webp"}, {"n": 91, "text": "Trust as the Basis for Change Management Tradi4onally, the concept of trust is equated with integrity, loyalty, caring, and keeping promises in the rela4onships between and among individuals. But trust should go beyond individual rela4onships and extend to the organiza4on through the crea4on of a culture of trust that transcends individual leadership. Leaders come and go; the organiza4on con4nues.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-91.webp"}, {"n": 92, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS", "image": "assets/pages/m1-92.webp"}, {"n": 93, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS Country’s culture impacts on the behaviour of employees and subsequently on the management prac4ces. The behaviours can be categorized into :ve dimensions. (1) individualism vs collec;vism 93", "image": "assets/pages/m1-93.webp"}, {"n": 94, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS Country’s culture impacts on the behaviour of employees and subsequently on the management prac4ces. The behaviours can be categorized into :ve dimensions. (2) large power distance vs small power distance 94", "image": "assets/pages/m1-94.webp"}, {"n": 95, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS Country’s culture impacts on the behaviour of employees and subsequently on the management prac4ces. The behaviours can be categorized into :ve dimensions. (3) uncertainty tolerance vs avoidance 95", "image": "assets/pages/m1-95.webp"}, {"n": 96, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS Country’s culture impacts on the behaviour of employees and subsequently on the management prac4ces. The behaviours can be categorized into :ve dimensions. (4) masculinity vs femininity (aggressive vs passive goal behaviour) 96", "image": "assets/pages/m1-96.webp"}, {"n": 97, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS Country’s culture impacts on the behaviour of employees and subsequently on the management prac4ces. The behaviours can be categorized into :ve dimensions. (5) short-term vs long-term orienta;on 97", "image": "assets/pages/m1-97.webp"}, {"n": 98, "text": "INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS This suggest that managers need to understand the cultural environments and their implica4ons to be successful in the country in which they do business. 98", "image": "assets/pages/m1-98.webp"}, {"n": 99, "text": "Interna4onal Business German Management In the past and to a lesser extent today, the German cultural environment favored reliance on authority in direc4ng the workforce, although it was oqen benevolent authoritarianism (managerial func4on of leading). Even today, while managers may show concern for subordinates, they also expect obedience. 99", "image": "assets/pages/m1-99.webp"}, {"n": 100, "text": "Interna4onal Business Austrian management Management in Austria (and Germany) is characterized by self-realiza4on and leadership. Independence and compe44veness are valued. Tolerance for risk taking is rather low. Australian Management Managing in Australia is inguenced by the country’s moralis4c stance and its emphasis on poli4cal and social values, achievement, and risk taking. 100", "image": "assets/pages/m1-100.webp"}, {"n": 101, "text": "Interna4onal Business Italian Management Italian managers operate in an environment of low tolerance for risks. Italians are very compe44ve, but at the same 4me they like group decision-making. England Management In Britain, job security is important and so are resourcefulness, adaptability, and logic. Individualism is also highly valued. 101", "image": "assets/pages/m1-101.webp"}, {"n": 102, "text": "Interna4onal Business Aspect KOREAN Management Style JAPANESE Management Style AMERICAN Management Style Decision-Making Top-down, hierarchical Consensus-driven, par;cipa;ve Top-down, fast, individual decision-making Employment Prac;ces Focus on loyalty and seniority Life;me employment, loyalty Performance-based, high employee mobility Communica;on Indirect, high-context Indirect, high-context Direct, low-context Workplace Rela;onships Emphasis on hierarchy and respect for elders Emphasis on group harmony and team cohesion Emphasis on individualism and personal achievement Leadership Style Authorita;ve, paternalis;c Facilita;ve, consensus- oriented Authorita;ve, results- oriented Mo;va;on Loyalty to company, respect for authority Group harmony, job security Individual achievement, rewards Consict Resolu;on Avoidance, maintaining face Avoidance, maintaining harmony Confronta;onal, seeking quick resolu;on", "image": "assets/pages/m1-102.webp"}, {"n": 103, "text": "Interna4onal Business Aspect KOREAN Management Style JAPANESE Management Style AMERICAN Management Style Training and Development Emphasis on in-house training, seniority-based Extensive in-house training, long-term orienta;on Formal educa;on, con;nuous professional development Innova;on and Change Slow to adopt, cau;ous Incremental improvements, cau;ous Rapid adop;on, proac;ve Work-Life Balance Long working hours, high dedica;on Long working hours, strong commitment Emphasis on work-life balance, sexible working hours Performance Evalua;on Based on seniority and loyalty Group performance and seniority Individual performance, results-oriented Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) Emerging importance High importance, integrated into business prac;ces High importance, driven by stakeholders and regula;ons", "image": "assets/pages/m1-103.webp"}, {"n": 104, "text": "THEORY Z", "image": "assets/pages/m1-104.webp"}, {"n": 105, "text": "JAPANESE TYPE ORGANIZATION 1. Life4me employment 2. Collec4ve decision making 3. Collec4ve responsibility 4. Slow evalua4on and promo4on 5. Implicit (understood, implied) control mechanisms 6. Non-specialized career path 7. Holis4c concern for employee as a person", "image": "assets/pages/m1-105.webp"}, {"n": 106, "text": "AMERICAN TYPE ORGANIZATION 1. Short-term employment 2. Individual decision–making 3. Individual responsibility 4. Rapid evalua4on and promo4on 5. Explicit (clear, precise, unambiguous) control mechanisms 6. Specialized career path 7. Segmented concern for employee as an employee", "image": "assets/pages/m1-106.webp"}, {"n": 107, "text": "THEORY Z William Ouchi, a management researcher developed this new theory of management in the 1980s. Theory Z is a business management theory that integrates Japanese and American business practices. The Japanese business emphasis is on collective decision making, whereas the American emphasis is on individual responsibility. Theory Z focuses on increasing employee loyalty to the company by providing a job for life with a strong focus on the well-being of the employee, both on and off the job. According to Ouchi, Theory Z management tends to promote stable employment, high productivity, and high employee morale and satisfaction.", "image": "assets/pages/m1-107.webp"}, {"n": 108, "text": "THEORY Z TYPE ORGANIZATION 1. Long-term employment 2. Consensual, participative decision-making 3. Individual responsibility 4. Slow evaluation and promotion 5. Implicit, informal control with explicit, formalized measures 6. Moderately specialized career path 7. Holistic concern, including family", "image": "assets/pages/m1-108.webp"}]}, {"id": "m2", "file": "m2.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m2-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF PLANNING CHAPTER 2", "image": "assets/pages/m2-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "CHAPTER 2 ESSENTIALS OF PLANNING Types of Plans Steps in Planning Objec@ves Nature and Purpose of Strategies and Policies Evolving Concepts in Management by Objec@ves Strategic Planning Process • Cri$cal Ques$on Analysis • SWOT & TOWS Matrix: A Modern Tool for Analysis of the Situa$on • Blue Ocean Strategy: In Pursuit of Opportuni$es in Uncontested • The PorColio Matrix: A Tool for Alloca$ng Resources • Porter’s Industry Analysis and Generic Compe$$ve Strategies", "image": "assets/pages/m2-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "What is Planning? A plan is a blueprint for goal achievement, a blueprint that speciKes the necessary resource alloca$ons, schedules, tasks, and other ac$ons to achieve the purpose.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "Importance of Planning Planning bridges the gap between where we are & where we want to be. Reduces Uncertain$es Provides Direc$on Resource Alloca$on Minimizes impulsive & arbitrary decisions", "image": "assets/pages/m2-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "PLANNING ANSWERS SIX BASIC QUESTIONS in regard to any ac$vity: WHAT NEEDS TO BE ACCOMPLISHED? WHAT ARE THE ALTERNATIVE ROUTES TO IT? WHEN IS THE DEADLINE? WHERE WILL THIS BE DONE? WHO WILL BE RESPONSIBLE FOR IT? HOW WILL IT GET DONE? HOW MUCH TIME, ENERGY, AND RESOURCES ARE REQUIRED TO ACCOMPLISH THIS GOAL?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "Planning is important for the following reasons It helps the management to clarify, focus, and research their businesses or project's development and prospects. It provides a considered and logical framework within which a business can develop and pursue business. It oTers a benchmark against which the actual performance can be measured and reviewed. It plays a vital role in helping to avoid mistakes or recognize hidden opportuni@es. In the business context, it guides the development of products, management, Knances, and most importantly, markets and compe$$on. It helps in forecas@ng the future and makes the future visible to some extent. It bridges between where we are and where we want to go.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "1. PURPOSE / MISSION / VISION 2. GOAL / OBJECTIVE 3. STRATEGY 4. POLICY 5. PROCEDURE 6. RULES 7. PROGRAMS 8. BUDGETS All managers across all levels par$cipate in planning. However, the plans made by the top level manager will diTer from the ones that lower managers make. Plans also diTer from what they seek to achieve and what methods will be used to achieve them. So let us look at the types of plans that managers deal with. TYPES OF PLANS", "image": "assets/pages/m2-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "PURPOSE / MISSION / VISION It iden$Kes the basic func$on or task of an enterprise or agency or any part of it. Example: Oil Company To Search for Oil And to Produce, ReKne, Market Petroleum Product. DUPONT Company BeWer Thing Through Chemistry. 1", "image": "assets/pages/m2-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "PURPOSE / MISSION / VISION 1", "image": "assets/pages/m2-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "Objec$ves are deKned as the important ends towards which organiza$onal and individual ac$vi$es are directed. VERIFIABLE OBJECTIVES - facilitate measurement of the surplus as well as the eTec$veness and eYciency of managerial ac$ons. GOAL / OBJECTIVE 2", "image": "assets/pages/m2-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "A strategy is a complete and all-inclusive plan for achieving said objec$ves. A strategy is a plan that has three speciKc dimensions 1. Establishing long-term objec$ves 2. Selec$ng a speciKc course of ac$on 3. alloca$ng the necessary resources needed for the plan Forming strategy is generally reserved for the top level of management. STRATEGY 3", "image": "assets/pages/m2-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "Policies are also the plans in that they are general statements or understanding which guide or channel thinking in decision making. Not all policies are statement, they are o^en merely implied from the ac$ons of managers. For example – the prac@ce of promo@ng from within. Polices deKne an area within which a decision is to be made and ensure that the decision will be consistent with and contribute to the objec$ve. They help decide issues before they become problems. POLICY 4", "image": "assets/pages/m2-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "EXAMPLES ARE POLICIES OF HIRING UNIVERSITY TRAINED ENGINEERS ENCOURAGE EMPLOYEE SUGGESTION PROMOTING FROM WITH IN SETTING COMPETITIVE PRICES POLICIES HELP IN DECISION MAKING – POLICES OF BUYING FROM THE LOWEST OF THREE QUALIFIED BIDDERS POLICY 4", "image": "assets/pages/m2-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "They are a stepwise guide for the rou@ne to carry out the ac@vi@es. These stepwise sequences are to be followed by all the employees so the ac@vi@es can be fuldlled in an organized manner. THE PROCEDURES ARE DESCRIBED IN A CHRONOLOGICAL ORDER. Take for example the procedure of admission of a student in a college. The procedure starts with Klling out an applica$on form. It will be followed by a collec$on of documents and sor$ng the applica$ons accordingly. As another example consider company policies that may grant vaca$on to employees; procedures to implement this policy will provide for scheduling vaca$on to avoid any disrup$on of work. PROCEDURES 5", "image": "assets/pages/m2-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "Rules are very speciKc statements that deKne an ac$on or non-ac$on. Also, rules allow for no dexibility at all, they are Knal. All employees of the organiza$on must compulsorily follow and implement the rules. Not following rules can have severe consequences. THEY ARE SIMPLEST TYPE OF PLAN. They guide ac@ons without specifying @me sequence. MAY OR MAY NOT BE PART OF PROCEDURE. For Example  -  No smoking zone, quite unrelated to any procedure… No phone zone… RULES 6", "image": "assets/pages/m2-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "Programs are a complex of: goals, polices, procedures, rules, task assignments, steps to be taken, resources to be employed and other elements necessary to carry out a given course of ac$on; they are ordinarily supported by budgets. PROGRAMS 7", "image": "assets/pages/m2-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "Budgets are the statement of expected results expressed in numerical terms, referred as numberized programs. Can be in terms of number of labour hours, units of produc$on, Knancial terms, machine-hours etc. Budgets usually implements a program; it may in itself be a program. VARIABLE OR FLEXIBLE BUDGETS : depends on level of output PROGRAM BUDGETS : the agencies and each dept. within the agencies iden$Kes the goals ZERO BASED BUDGETS : combina$on of variable and program budgets BUDGETS 8", "image": "assets/pages/m2-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "Planning can be classi6ed from di8erent perspectives The Kgure below depicts the perspec$ves of planning.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "The most popular ways to describe plans are in terms of their CONTENT (Strategic, Tactical, and Operational) BREADTH (strategic versus tactical), TIME FRAME (long term versus short term), SPECIFICITY (directional versus specific), and FREQUENCY OF USE (single use versus standing). These classifications are NOT MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE. NOT MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE means that two instances or outcomes can occur simultaneously, and one outcome does not limit the other from being possible.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "Upper-level managers develop STRATEGIC PLANS that apply to the entire organization, establish overall objectives, and position the organization within its environment. Lower-level managers focus on TACTICAL PLANS that specify how the overall objectives will be achieved. These plans differ in time frame and scope. OPERATIONAL PLANS are limited in scope and are measured daily, weekly, or monthly; strategic plans are broader, less specific and encompass five or more years. CONTENT (Strategic, Tactical, and Operational)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "Strategic plans Apply broadly to the en$re organiza$on. Establish the organiza$on’s overall objec$ves. Seek to posi$on the organiza$on in terms of its environment. Provide direc$on to drive an organiza$on’s eTorts to achieve its goals. Serve as the basis for the tac$cal plans. Cover extended periods of $me. Are less speciKc in their details. Tac@cal plans (opera@onal plans) Apply to speciKc parts of the organiza$on. Are derived from strategic objec$ves. Specify the details of how the overall objec$ves are to be achieved. Cover shorter periods of $me. Must be updated con$nuously to meet current challenges. BREADTH (strategic versus tactical)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "Long term • Time frame beyond Kve years. • It speciKes what the organiza$on wants to become in long run. • It involves great deal of uncertainty. • Higher management levels focus on longer $me horizons. • May include a variety of diTerent types of training Some examples Long term Plans: • Sales • Brand awareness of your product • Public reputa$on • Government Plans Short term • Time frame Upto one year • It provide basis for day to day opera$ons. • Meet a par$cular objec$ve in the near future • Cover a limited area of training • Answer the ques$on: Are we doing things right? • Should Kt well within and contribute to long- range plans. Some examples: • Communica$on • Ability to network • AWen$on to detail • Daily rou$nes • Plans for training staT members TIME FRAME (long term versus short term)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "Specidc plans Plans that have clearly deKned objec$ves and leave no room for misinterpreta$on. • “What, when, where, how much, and by whom” (process-focus) Example Lesson Plan Direc@onal plans Flexible plans that set out general guidelines. Example “Go from here to there” (outcome-focus) SPECIFICITY (directional versus specific)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "Single-use plans A plan that is used to meet the needs of a par$cular or unique situa$on Example: Single-day sales adver$sement Standing plan A plan that is ongoing and provides guidance for repeatedly performed ac$ons in an organiza$on Example: Customer sa$sfac$on policy FREQUENCY OF USE (single use versus standing)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "BEING AWARE OF OPPORTUNITIES In light of: The market Compe@@on What customers need/want Our Strengths Our Weakness SETTING OBJECTIVES/GOALS Where we want to be and what we want to accomplish and when CONSIDERING PLANNING PREMISES In what environment (internal or external) will the plans operate? IDENTIFYING ALTERNATIVES What are the most promising alterna@ves to accomplishing our objec@ves? COMPARING THE ALTERNATIVES IN THE LIGHT OF THE GOALS SOUGHT Which alterna@ve will give us the best chance of mee@ng our goals at the lowest cost and highest prodt? CHOOSING AN ALTERNATIVE Selec@ng the course of ac@on we will pursue FORMULATING THE SUPPORTING PLANS Such as plans to: Buy equipment Buy materials Hire and Train workforce Develop a new product NUMBERIZING THE PLANS BY MAKING BUDGETS Develop such budgets as: Volume & Price of Sales Opera@ng Expenses necessary for plans Expenditures for capital equipment STEPS IN PLANNING", "image": "assets/pages/m2-26.webp"}, {"n": 27, "text": "The Nature of Objec@ves Hierarchy of Objec$ves Selng Objec$ves and the Organiza$onal Hierarchy Mul$plicity of Objec$ves How to Set Objec@ves Quan$ta$ve and Qualita$ve Objec$ves Guidelines for Selng Objec$ves OBJECTIVES", "image": "assets/pages/m2-27.webp"}, {"n": 28, "text": "TOP DOWN APPROACH – upper level managers determine the objectives for subordinates BOTTOM UP APPROACH – Relationship of Objectives and the Organizational Hierarchy Source: Essen$als of Management by Harold Koontz, Heinz Weihrich, Mark V. Cannice, McGraw Hill Educa$on(India) Private Limited", "image": "assets/pages/m2-28.webp"}, {"n": 29, "text": "The Nature and Purpose of STRATEGIES and POLICIES Determina$on of the mission or purpose and the basic long-term objec$ves of an enterprise, followed by the adop$on of courses of ac$on and alloca$on of resources necessary to achieve these aims. Strategy General statements or understandings that guide managers’ thinking in decision making. Policies", "image": "assets/pages/m2-29.webp"}, {"n": 30, "text": "MULTIPLICITY OF OBJECTIVES At every level in the hierarchy of objec$ves, goals are like to be mul$ple. That is, the business does not have just one objec$ve. It has several objec$ves. This is because a business must sa$sfy a variety of stakeholders, including owners, employees, consumers, creditors, vendors, society, and so on. The company must establish separate objec$ves for each group.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-30.webp"}, {"n": 31, "text": "QUANTITATIVE OBJECTIVES Quan@ta@ve objec@ves are straighCorward to measure and are represented numerically. These objec$ves are concrete, observable, and quan$Kable. Examples include: Increasing annual revenue by 10% Reducing produc$on costs by 15% QUALITATIVE OBJECTIVES Qualita@ve objec@ves are challenging to measure as they are not represented numerically. Examples include: Enhancing customer experience & sa$sfac$on Improving brand reputa$on and recogni$on", "image": "assets/pages/m2-31.webp"}, {"n": 32, "text": "Evolving Concepts in MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES is a comprehensive managerial system that integrates many key managerial ac$vi$es in a systema$c manner and is consciously directed toward the eTec$ve and eYcient achievement of organiza$onal and individual objec$ves.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-32.webp"}, {"n": 33, "text": "Department of Humani$es & Management Management by Objectives, also known as management by planning, was first popularized by Peter Drucker in his 1954 book The Practice of Management. MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO) Management by objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve the performance of an organization by clearly defining objectives that are agreed to by both management and employees.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-33.webp"}, {"n": 34, "text": "Following are the common features of the organiza@onal objec@ves : VeriKable and quan$Ked objec$ves Hierarchy of objec$ves Network of objec$ves Mul$plicity of objec$ves Process of objec$ve selng & organiza$onal hierarchy MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-34.webp"}, {"n": 35, "text": "comprehensive managerial system that integrates many key ac$vi$es managerial ac$vi$es arranged in a systema$c manner directed towards eTec$ve and eYcient achievement of objec$ves organiza$onal and individual objec$ves addressed managers and employees deKne objec$ves for every department, project, and person, and use them to monitor subsequently performance monitored and achieved MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-35.webp"}, {"n": 36, "text": "MBO emphasizes on the following: Performance appraisal Short term objec$ves and mo$va$on Long term planning MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-36.webp"}, {"n": 37, "text": "Selng preliminary objec$ves at the top Clarifying organiza$onal roles Selng subordinates objec$ves Recycling of objec$ves Process of MBO MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-37.webp"}, {"n": 38, "text": "Key Result Areas for Business (Peter Drucker) • Market standing • Innova$on • Produc$vity • Physical and Knancial resources • ProKtability • Managerial performance and development • Worker performance and altude • Public responsibility MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO) Addi$onal Areas: 1. Service 2. Quality What do you mean by “Key result areas”? An area in which performance is essen@al for the success of the enterprise.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-38.webp"}, {"n": 39, "text": "Enterprise Objec$ves Planning Premises Key Result Areas Appropriate Organiza$on SUPERIOR’s Objec$ves SUPERIOR’s preliminary recommenda$on of Objec$ves for SUBORDINATE SUBORDINATE’S preliminary statement of Objec$ves Available needed resources SUBORDINATE’s Objec$ves Subordinate’s Ongoing Performance Periodic review of process by SUPERIOR Correc$ve measures and SUPERIOR’s assistance Final Performance by SUBORDINATE FINAL REVIEW & APPRAISAL OF PERFORMANCE NEW INPUTS AGREEMENT MAY EFFECT", "image": "assets/pages/m2-39.webp"}, {"n": 40, "text": "Examples of non-veri1able and veri1able Objectives Non-Veridable Objec@ves 1. To make a reasonable proKt 2. To improve communica$on 3. To improve produc$vity 4. To develop beWer managers 5. To install a computer system Veridable Objec@ves 1. To achieve a return on investment of 12% at the end of the current Kscal year 2. To issue a two-page monthly newsleWer beginning July 1, involving not more than 40 work hours of prepara$on $me (a^er Krst issue) 3. To increase produc$on output by 5% by December 31, without addi$onal costs and while maintaining current quality level. 4. To design and conduct a 40-hour in house program on the “Fundamentals of Management” to be conducted this year involving not more than 200 working hours of the management development staT with 90% of the managers passing this exam. 5. To install a computerized control system in the produc$on department by December 31, requiring not more than 500 working hours of system analysis and opera$ng with not more than 10% down$me during the Krst 3 months of installa$on.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-40.webp"}, {"n": 41, "text": "SETTING EMPLOYEE OBJECTIVES • Iden$fy an employee’s key job tasks. • Establish speciKc and challenging goals for each key task. • Allow the employee to ac$vely par$cipate. • Priori$ze goals. • Build in feedback mechanisms to assess goal progress. • Link rewards to goal aWainment. MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-41.webp"}, {"n": 42, "text": "OBJECTIVES of MBO • To iden$fy problems and opportuni$es in business. • To convert iden$Ked opportuni$es into clear goals. • To set up a system to convert these goals into achievements. • To review the organiza$on in the light of the objec$ves. • To establish the objec$ves of each job and unit. • To clarify the policies and systems to accomplish the objec$ves. • To set up a review system. MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-42.webp"}, {"n": 43, "text": "BENEFITS of MBO • The need for planning will be recognized. • It provides for objec$ves and accountability for performance. • It encourages par$cipa$ve management. • It helps in job enrichment. • It provides for a good feedback system. • Leader/subordinate eTorts are focused on ac$vi$es that will lead to goal aWainment • Performance improved up and down the chain • Mo$vated people MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-43.webp"}, {"n": 44, "text": "WEAKNESSES OF MBO Failure to teach the philosophy of MBO Failure to give guidelines to goal seWers DiYculty of selng goals Emphasis on short run goals Danger of indexibility Desire to make goals veriKable people may use quan$ta$ve goals and aWempt to use numbers in areas where they are not applicable. MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO)", "image": "assets/pages/m2-44.webp"}, {"n": 45, "text": "MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES (MBO) PROBLEMS of MBO – Change adversely aTects planning – Low morale reduces eTec$veness – Lower level goals take precedence – Over-centralized authority limits par$cipa$on – Paperwork", "image": "assets/pages/m2-45.webp"}, {"n": 46, "text": "STRATEGIC PLANNING TOOLS STRATEGIC PLANNING is the process of developing the strategy or direction and action plan to achieve the goals of an organization.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-46.webp"}, {"n": 47, "text": "Strategic Planning Process INPUTS People Capital Managerial Skills Technology Skills GOALS Employees Consumers Suppliers Stockholders Governments Community Others Purpose & Major Objectives of the organization Enterprise profile Top management Orientation (Values + Vision) Development Of Alternate Strategies Internal Strengths & Weaknesses Present & Future External Threats & opportunities Leadership Control Medium & short-run planning Evaluation & Strategic choice Consistency testing & Contingency planning INDUSTRY ANALYSIS Implementation Re-engineering organization structure (staffing) Source: Essen$als of Management by Harold Koontz, Heinz Weihrich, Mark V. Cannice, McGraw Hill Educa$on(India) Private Limited", "image": "assets/pages/m2-47.webp"}, {"n": 48, "text": "3 LEVELS OF STRATEGY Corporate Level Strategy What business we are in? Business Level Strategy or Compe$$ve Strategy How do we compete? Func$onal Level Strategy How do we support business level strategy? STRATEGY", "image": "assets/pages/m2-48.webp"}, {"n": 49, "text": "STRATEGIC PLANNING TOOLS", "image": "assets/pages/m2-49.webp"}, {"n": 50, "text": "TOOLS TO DEVELOP STRATEGY These 5 strategy development tools are related but dis$nct. Managers can use any of the tool or a combina$on of tools that seem most appropriate for them and their organiza$ons. CRITICAL QUESTION ANALYSIS BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY SWOT ANALYSIS & TOWS MATRIX BUSINESS PORTFOLIO ANALYSIS PORTER’S GENERIC MODEL", "image": "assets/pages/m2-50.webp"}, {"n": 51, "text": "Cri$cal Ques$on Analysis • What is our business? Glass boWle manufacturers missed their opportuni$es by seeing themselves for too long as glass boWle makers rather than as liquid container manufacturers while plas$c and metal containers come to replace glass in many cases. • Who are our customers? • What do our customers want? • How much will our customers buy and at what price? • Do we wish to be a product leader? • Do we wish to develop our own new products?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-51.webp"}, {"n": 52, "text": "An example of cri?cal ques?on analysis: The key ques$ons that serve as guides for establishing a marke$ng strategy are: • Where are our customers and why do they buy? • How do our customers buy? • How is it best for us to sell? • Do we have something to oTer that compe$tors do not? Cri$cal Ques$on Analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-52.webp"}, {"n": 53, "text": "TOOLS TO DEVELOP STRATEGY These 5 strategy development tools are related but dis$nct. Managers can use any of the tool or a combina$on of tools that seem most appropriate for them and their organiza$ons. CRITICAL QUESTION ANALYSIS BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY SWOT ANALYSIS & TOWS MATRIX BUSINESS PORTFOLIO ANALYSIS PORTER’S GENERIC MODEL", "image": "assets/pages/m2-53.webp"}, {"n": 54, "text": "By :W. Chan Kim and Renée Mauborgne. The Blue Ocean Strategy is a unique approach to business • growth that encourages companies to create new market spaces instead of competing in saturated markets • success can be found in ‘blue oceans’ – untapped, uncontested market spaces ripe for growth • emphasizes the simultaneous pursuit of differentiation and low cost to open up a new market space and create new demand. It is about creating and capturing uncontested market space, making the competition irrelevant.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-54.webp"}, {"n": 55, "text": "Blue Ocean Strategy pivots on two main principles: 1. value innovation • value innovation involves delivering superior value to customers through differentiation and low cost • beating the competition but making it irrelevant by redefining the industry boundaries 2. creation of uncontested market space", "image": "assets/pages/m2-55.webp"}, {"n": 56, "text": "ADVANTAGES 1. Businesses are able to create new demand and tap into new markets 2. Businesses can focus on creating unique value propositions that are appealing to customers 3. Businesses can avoid the cutthroat competition of traditional markets 4. Businesses can achieve sustained profitability", "image": "assets/pages/m2-56.webp"}, {"n": 57, "text": "CHALLENGES 1. high risk of failure 2. significant resource investment, and 3. the uncertainty of unexplored markets", "image": "assets/pages/m2-57.webp"}, {"n": 58, "text": "", "image": "assets/pages/m2-58.webp"}, {"n": 59, "text": "APPLICATIONS Hindustan Unilever In 2001, India’s Hindustan Unilever launched Project Shakti, an initiative to provide rural India with basic hygiene and sanitation products. The company used a direct-selling model, which not only increased sales but also helped bring safe drinking water and sanitation products to communities that needed them the most. Airtel The launch of Airtel’s mobile banking services in 2010 and the expansion of the Flipkart e-commerce platform into grocery delivery in 2015. These examples show that the Blue Ocean strategy can be used to tap into new markets and create value for both businesses and consumers.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-59.webp"}, {"n": 60, "text": "APPLICATIONS Flipkart When they 1rst started out, they were a small startup competing against much bigger and well-established companies. They used the Blue Ocean strategy to focus on the needs of the Indian consumer and oOer a unique shopping experience. This helped them to quickly become one of the leading e-commerce companies in India. PayTm It created a new market in India by providing a convenient and accessible way to pay for goods and services via mobile phone. Before PayTm the concept of digital and mobile payments was fairly non-existent or ill- adopted.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-60.webp"}, {"n": 61, "text": "Four Actions Framework BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY", "image": "assets/pages/m2-61.webp"}, {"n": 62, "text": "Identify and categorize factors / attributes / features / services etc into the 4 groups mentioned below…. Eliminate the factors on which the company has been compe$ng Reduce several factors below the company standards Raise key factors that will assist in making the compe$$on irrelevant above the standards Create some new ideas, factors, and steps that the company needs to achieve its blue ocean goal", "image": "assets/pages/m2-62.webp"}, {"n": 63, "text": "Competitive Factor analysis : an example", "image": "assets/pages/m2-63.webp"}, {"n": 64, "text": "The Four Actions Framework analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-64.webp"}, {"n": 65, "text": "", "image": "assets/pages/m2-65.webp"}, {"n": 66, "text": "", "image": "assets/pages/m2-66.webp"}, {"n": 67, "text": "CRITICAL QUESTION ANALYSIS BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY SWOT ANALYSIS & TOWS MATRIX BUSINESS PORTFOLIO ANALYSIS PORTER’S GENERIC MODEL TOOLS TO DEVELOP STRATEGY These 5 strategy development tools are related but dis$nct. Managers can use any of the tool or a combina$on of tools that seem most appropriate for them and their organiza$ons.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-67.webp"}, {"n": 68, "text": "68 Acronym for Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportuni@es, and Threats. Technique is credited to Albert Humphrey who led a research project at Stanford University in the 1960s and 1970s. Planning tool used to understand Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportuni$es, & Threats involved in a project / business. Used as framework for organizing and using data and informa$on gained from situa@on analysis of internal and external environment. Technique that enables a group / individual to move from everyday problems / tradi$onal strategies to a fresh perspec@ve.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-68.webp"}, {"n": 69, "text": "STRENGTHS STRENGTHS Characteris$cs of the business or a team that give it an advantage over others in the industry. Posi$ve tangible and intangible aWributes, internal to an organiza$on. BeneKcial aspects of the organiza$on or the capabili$es of an organiza$on, which includes human competencies, process capabili$es, Knancial resources, products and services, customer goodwill and brand loyalty. Examples - Abundant Knancial resources, Well-known brand name, Economies of scale, Lower costs [raw materials or processes], Superior management talent, BeWer marke$ng skills, Good distribu$on skills, CommiWed employees. What is SWOT Analysis?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-69.webp"}, {"n": 70, "text": "WEAKNESSES WEAKNESSES Characteris$cs that place the Krm at a disadvantage rela$ve to others. Detract the organiza$on from its ability to aWain the core goal and induence its growth. Weaknesses are the factors which do not meet the standards we feel they should meet. However, weaknesses are controllable. They must be minimized and eliminated. Examples - Limited Knancial resources, Weak spending on R & D, Very narrow product line, Limited distribu$on, Higher costs, Out-of-date products / technology, Weak market image, Poor marke$ng skills, Limited management skills, Under-trained employees. What is SWOT Analysis?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-70.webp"}, {"n": 71, "text": "OPPORTUNITIES OPPORTUNITIES Chances to make greater proKts in the environment - External aWrac$ve factors that represent the reason for an organiza$on to exist & develop. Arise when an organiza$on can take beneKt of condi$ons in its environment to plan and execute strategies that enable it to become more proKtable. Organiza$on should be careful and recognize the opportuni$es and grasp them whenever they arise. Opportuni$es may arise from market, compe$$on, industry/government and technology. Examples - Rapid market growth, Rival Krms are complacent, Changing customer needs/tastes, New uses for product discovered, Economic boom, Government deregula$on, Sales decline for a subs$tute product . What is SWOT Analysis?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-71.webp"}, {"n": 72, "text": "SWOT ANALYSIS - THREAT ! ! THREATS THREATS External elements in the environment that could cause trouble for the business - External factors, beyond an organiza$on’s control, which could place the organiza$on’s mission or opera$on at risk. Arise when condi$ons in external environment jeopardize the reliability and proKtability of the organiza$on’s business. Compound the vulnerability when they relate to the weaknesses. Threats are uncontrollable. When a threat comes, the stability and survival can be at stake. Examples - Entry of foreign compe$tors, Introduc$on of new subs$tute products, Product life cycle in decline, Changing customer needs/tastes, Rival Krms adopt new strategies, Increased government regula$on, Economic downturn. What is SWOT Analysis?", "image": "assets/pages/m2-72.webp"}, {"n": 73, "text": "Assume that a car manufacturing company has recently launched its electrical vehicle (EV) products. Perform a SWOT analysis for the same. 10 EXERCISE", "image": "assets/pages/m2-73.webp"}, {"n": 74, "text": "INTERNAL EXTERNAL HELPFUL HARMFUL STRENGTHS • Eco-friendly • Silent engine • Simpler mechanism • Low Cost of maintenance and Cheaper to run • Energy Savings – achieved from regenera$ve braking system THREATS • Entry of compe$tors • Rise in cost of electricity • Availability of hybrid vehicles adding to compe$$on WEAKNESSES • High Price • needs $me to recharge • BaWeries change is expensive • Lack of recharging infrastructure OPPORTUNITIES • Huge untapped EV market • Growing demand of green technologies • Rising fuel costs • Lower taxes • Government subsidy to promote eco-friendly vehicles EXERCISE", "image": "assets/pages/m2-74.webp"}, {"n": 75, "text": "TOWS Matrix TOWS Analysis refers to the interlinking of the internal strengths and weaknesses of a company with the threats and opportuni$es that it faces in its external environment. It is a categoriza@on of the strategies which are available to a company. It does this by focusing on aligning internal elements with external factors in an appropriate manner. DEFINITION", "image": "assets/pages/m2-75.webp"}, {"n": 76, "text": "It has four alterna?ve strategies • SO Strategy • ST Strategy • WO Strategy • WT Strategy TOWS Matrix", "image": "assets/pages/m2-76.webp"}, {"n": 77, "text": "Internal strengths (S) e.g., strengths in management opera$ons, Knance, marke$ng, R&D, engineering WO strategy: Mini-Maxi e.g., Developmental strategy to overcome weaknesses in order to take advantage of opportuni$es WT strategy: Mini-Mini e.g., retrenchment, liquida$on, or joint venture to minimize both weaknesses and threats Internal weaknesses(W) e.g., weaknesses in areas shown in the box of “strengths” SO strategy: Maxi-Maxi Poten$ally the most successful strategy, u$lizing the organiza$on’s strengths to take advantage of opportuni$es External Opportuni@es (O) (Consider risks also) e.g., current and future economic condi$on, poli$cal and social changes, new products, services and technology External threats (T) e.g., lack of energy, compe$$on, and areas similar to those shown in the “opportuni$es” box above ST strategy: Maxi-Mini e.g., use of strengths to cope with threats or to avoid threats Internal factors External factors TOWS Matrix for Strategy Formulation • SO Strategy • ST Strategy • WO Strategy • WT Strategy", "image": "assets/pages/m2-77.webp"}, {"n": 78, "text": "A strategy used by corpora$ons to reduce the diversity or the overall size of the opera$ons of the company. This strategy is o^en used in order to cut expenses with the goal of becoming a more dnancial stable business. Typically the strategy involves withdrawing from certain markets or the discon$nua$on of selling certain products or service in order to make a beneKcial turnaround. TOWS Matrix RETRENCHMENT STRATEGY WT strategy: Mini-Mini", "image": "assets/pages/m2-78.webp"}, {"n": 79, "text": "Story of an apple TOWS Matrix for Strategy Formula@on", "image": "assets/pages/m2-79.webp"}, {"n": 80, "text": "TOWS Matrix Strategies Combining STRENGTHS + OPPORTUNITIES (SO) Apple has strong brand awareness and loyalty from its customers. It also has strong capabili$es in product design and aesthe$cs. • Combining these factors (which are internal strengths) with the growing popularity of touchscreen smartphones (a good market opportunity) is what led to the launch of the Krst iPhone. • This was, of course, an immediate and massive hit, breaking various records for phone sales at the $me.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-80.webp"}, {"n": 81, "text": "Strategies Combining STRENGTHS + THREATS (ST) • Some of the core strengths of Apple are its product design and strong brand loyalty which it commands from its consumers. It has taken advantage of these strengths to push the use of its proprietary ligh$ng cable connec$on on iPhones and iPads. This boosts its revenue while most other phone manufacturers have already adopted Type C as a standard connec$on for their devices. • However, Apple faces a threat in some markets like the European Union where the local regulators are pushing for the company to also adopt Type C in its devices to reduce unnecessary electronic waste. Adop$ng the Type-C standard might poten$ally lead to a par$al loss of revenue for the company. However, by properly using its strength in product design and capitalizing on brand loyalty, a sizable por$on of its consumers will con$nue to buy Apple’s Type C charger rather than cheaper alterna$ves. TOWS Matrix", "image": "assets/pages/m2-81.webp"}, {"n": 82, "text": "Strategies Combining WEAKNESSES + OPPORTUNITIES (WO) Apple typically has a compara$vely smaller product porColio compared to its compe$tors, and this is one of its few weaknesses (although this may be arguable). • When new opportuni$es arise in the market in the form of the latest trends, Apple is not always the quickest to capitalize on it. However, the company has s$ll made good use of WO strategies in its ascent to commercial success. • The wearables sector Krst started gaining trac$on in 2010 and this represented a strong market opportunity. Compe$tors like Google, Samsung, Motorola had all launched gadgets in this space in the next few years and were seeing returns. Apple then overcame its weakness and entered this market with its Apple Watch in 2015, which proved to be a success. TOWS Matrix", "image": "assets/pages/m2-82.webp"}, {"n": 83, "text": "Strategies Combining WEAKNESSES + THREATS (WT) Apple has been in the digital media sector since the launch of its Apple TV device in 2007. • However, despite upda$ng this device over the years with several upgraded versions, it was not a commercial success. • The weakness it had in this area was mainly due to limited func$onality and interconnec$vity compared to compe$ng products like Roku or Fire S$ck. • This was because you needed to already be in the Apple ‘ecosystem’ to make full use of the device. • At the same $me, the threat in the digital media space was growing day by day with more and more streaming service launches. • The introduc$on and launch of its own streaming service (Apple TV+) is a WT strategy. This is because it helps the company reduce its weakness and overcome the growing threat to a certain extent.. TOWS Matrix", "image": "assets/pages/m2-83.webp"}, {"n": 84, "text": "S WO WT W SO O T ST S WO WT W SO O T ST S WO WT W SO O T ST S WO WT W SO O T ST Time Dynamics of the TOWS Matrix Internal factors External factors PAST PRESENT PRESENT + T1 PRESENT + T2", "image": "assets/pages/m2-84.webp"}, {"n": 85, "text": "CRITICAL QUESTION ANALYSIS BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY SWOT ANALYSIS & TOWS MATRIX BUSINESS PORTFOLIO ANALYSIS PORTER’S GENERIC MODEL TOOLS TO DEVELOP STRATEGY These 5 strategy development tools are related but dis$nct. Managers can use any of the tool or a combina$on of tools that seem most appropriate for them and their organiza$ons.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-85.webp"}, {"n": 86, "text": "The Business PorColio Matrix or the Growth-Share Matrix was developed by BOSTON CONSULTING GROUP (BCG) in 1970s. According to this technique, businesses or products are classiKed as low or high performers depending upon their MARKET GROWTH RATE and RELATIVE MARKET SHARE. Business Portfolio Analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-86.webp"}, {"n": 87, "text": "87 Business Portfolio Analysis Boston Consul$ng Group (BCG) Matrix grid is broken into two quadrants. It has a four-cell matrix – ques@on mark, star, cash cow & dogs The Boston Consul$ng Group (BCG) growth/share matrix is among the best known approaches. It is one of the most renowned corporate porColio analysis tool. In the BCG approach, each of the Krm’s Strategic Business Units (SBUs) is ploWed on a two-dimensional grid in which the axes are rela$ve market share and industry growth rate.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-87.webp"}, {"n": 88, "text": "According to the BCG Matrix, business could be divided into high or low depending upon their industry growth and relative market share. Relative Market Share = SBU Sales this year / leading competitors sales this year. Market Growth Rate = Industry sales this year - Industry Sales last year. The analysis requires that both measures be calculated for each SBU. The dimension of business strength, relative market share, will measure comparative advantage indicated by market dominance. The key theory underlying this is existence of an experience curve and that market share is achieved due to overall cost leadership. 88 Business Portfolio Analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-88.webp"}, {"n": 89, "text": "Why BCG Matrix? To assess : ProKles of products/businesses The cash demands of products The development cycles of products Resource alloca$on and divestment decisions Business Portfolio Analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-89.webp"}, {"n": 90, "text": "PRODUCT LIFECYCLE", "image": "assets/pages/m2-90.webp"}, {"n": 91, "text": "Main Steps in BCG 1. Iden$fying and dividing a company into SBUs. 2. Assessing and comparing the prospects of each SBU according to two criteria : SBUs rela$ve market share. Growth rate of SBUs industry. 3. Classifying the SBUs on the basis of BCG matrix. 4. Developing strategic objec$ves for each SBUs. Business Portfolio Analysis", "image": "assets/pages/m2-91.webp"}, {"n": 92, "text": "QUESTION MARKS High growth, Low market share • Most businesses start of as question marks. • They will absorb great amounts of cash if the market share remains unchanged (i.e. low). Why question marks? • Question marks have potential to become star and eventually cash cow but can also become a dog. • Investments should be high for question marks. Business Portfolio Analysis…", "image": "assets/pages/m2-92.webp"}, {"n": 93, "text": "STARS High Growth, High Market Share • Stars are leaders in business. • They also require heavy investment, to maintain its large market share. • It leads to large amount of cash consumption and cash generation. • Attempts should be made to hold the market growth otherwise the star will become a CASH COW. Business Portfolio Analysis…", "image": "assets/pages/m2-93.webp"}, {"n": 94, "text": "CASH COWS Low growth , High market share • They are foundation of the company and often the stars of yesterday. • They generate more cash than required. • They extract the profits by investing as little cash as possible • They are located in an industry that is mature, not growing or declining. Business Portfolio Analysis…", "image": "assets/pages/m2-94.webp"}, {"n": 95, "text": "DOGS Low growth, Low market share • Dogs are the cash traps. • Dogs do not have potential to bring in much cash. • Number of dogs in the company should be minimized. • Business is situated at a declining stage. Business Portfolio Analysis…", "image": "assets/pages/m2-95.webp"}, {"n": 96, "text": "Each of the four quadrants of the grid has different implication for the SBUs that fall into the category Stars are SBUs competing in the high-growth industries with relatively high market shares. These firms have long-term growth potential and should continue to receive substantial investment funding. These are SBUs with high market shares in low-growth industries. These units have limited long-run potential but represent a source of current cash flows to fund investments in “stars” and “question marks”. Dogs are SBUs with weak market shares in low-growth industries. Because they have weak position and limited potential, most analysts recommend that they be divested. Question marks are SBUs competing in high-growth industries but having relatively weak market shares. Resources should be invested in them to enhance their competitive positions. Business Portfolio Analysis…", "image": "assets/pages/m2-96.webp"}, {"n": 97, "text": "BCG Matrix application The BCG Matrix method can help to understand a frequently made strategy mistake: having a one size fits all strategy approach, such as a generic growth target or a generic return on capital for an entire corporation. Cash Cows Business Units will reach their profit target easily. Their management have an easy job. Even worse, they are often allowed to reinvest substantial cash amounts in their mature businesses Dogs Business Units are fighting an impossible battle and, even worse, now and then investments are made. These are hopeless attempts to \"turn the business around\" As a result all Question Marks and Stars receive only mediocre investment funds. In this way they can never become Cash Cows. Inadequate invested sums of money are a waste of money Either these SBUs should receive enough investment funds to enable them to achieve a real market dominance and become Cash Cows (or Stars), or otherwise companies are advised to disinvest. They can then try to get any possible cash from the Question Marks that were not selected 97 APPLICATIONS OF BCG MATRIX", "image": "assets/pages/m2-97.webp"}, {"n": 98, "text": "BCG Matrix Benefits BCG MATRIX is simple and easy to understand. It helps you to quickly and simply screen the opportunities open to you, and helps you think about how you can make the most of them. It is used to identify how corporate cash resources can best be used to maximize a company’s future growth and profitability. 98 BENEFITS OF BCG MATRIX", "image": "assets/pages/m2-98.webp"}, {"n": 99, "text": "Limita@ons • BCG matrix uses only two dimensions: Rela@ve Market Share and Market Growth Rate • Problems arise while gelng data for market share and market growth • High market share does not mean proKts all the $me • Business with low market share can be proKtable too • It neglects the eTects of synergy between business units • Market growth is not the only indicator for aWrac$veness of a market • There is no clear deKni$on of what cons$tutes a “market” • The model neglects small compe$tors that have fast growing market shares LIMITATIONS OF BCG MATRIX", "image": "assets/pages/m2-99.webp"}, {"n": 100, "text": "", "image": "assets/pages/m2-100.webp"}, {"n": 101, "text": "Business Portfolio Analysis… Four strategies emerged from BCG Matrix: Build/Grow Hold/Maintain Harvest Divest", "image": "assets/pages/m2-101.webp"}, {"n": 102, "text": "Growth Strategies… • Direct Expansion • Involves increasing a company’s size, revenues, opera$on, or workforce. • Merger • Occurs when two companies, usually of similar size, combine their resources to form a new company. • Acquisi$on • Occurs when a larger company buys a smaller one and incorporates the acquired company’s opera$ons into its own.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-102.webp"}, {"n": 103, "text": "CRITICAL QUESTION ANALYSIS BLUE OCEAN STRATEGY SWOT ANALYSIS & TOWS MATRIX BUSINESS PORTFOLIO ANALYSIS PORTER’S GENERIC MODEL TOOLS TO DEVELOP STRATEGY These 5 strategy development tools are related but dis$nct. Managers can use any of the tool or a combina$on of tools that seem most appropriate for them and their organiza$ons.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-103.webp"}, {"n": 104, "text": "The fundamental basis of AVERAGE PROFITABILITY IN THE LONG RUN is SUSTAINABLE COMPETITIVE ADVANTAGE… There are two basic types of compe@@ve advantage a drm can possess: LOW COST OR DIFFERENTIATION DIFFERENTIATION LEADERSHIP COST LEADERSHIP FOCUS DIFFERENTIATION FOCUS COST FOCUS !\"#$ &\" #' &($)' र+, ? The two basic types of COMPETITIVE ADVANTAGE combined with the MARKET SCOPE of a drm, lead to three generic strategies for achieving average performance in an industry: 1 COST LEADERSHIP 2 DIFFERENTIATION & 3 FOCUS • cost focus • diTeren@a@on focus", "image": "assets/pages/m2-104.webp"}, {"n": 105, "text": "• An organiza$on implemen$ng an overall cost leadership strategy aWempts to gain a compe@@ve advantage by reducing its cost below the costs of compe@ng drms. • Such organiza$ons keep a close watch on costs in areas such as research and development, sales and services. • By keeping costs low, the organiza?on is able to sell its products at low prices and s?ll make a proIt. Example: Timex. For decades, this Krm has specialized in manufacturing rela$vely simple, low cost watches for the mass market. Overall Cost leadership strategy", "image": "assets/pages/m2-105.webp"}, {"n": 106, "text": "Di8erentiation strategy • An organiza$on that pursues a diTeren$a$on strategy seeks to dis?nguish from compe?tors by oKering something unique through the quality of products or services. • Firms that are successfully able to implement a diTeren$a$on strategy are able to charge more than compe$tors because customers are willing to pay more to obtain the extra value they perceive. Example: ROLEX WATCHES are handmade of gold and stainless steel and are subjected to strenuous tests of quality and reliability. PORSCHE SPORTS CAR are indeed special; so is the CATERPILLAR COMPANY, which is known for its prompt service and availability of spare parts. The Krms reputa$on enables it to charge thousands of dollars for its products.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-106.webp"}, {"n": 107, "text": "Focused Strategy • A company adop$ng a focused strategy concentrates on a speci1c regional market, product line, or group of buyers. • This strategy may have either a diTeren$a$on focus, whereby the Krm diTeren$ates its products in the focus market, or on overall cost leadership focus, whereby the Krm manufactures and sells its products at low cost in the focus market. Example: In the watch industry, LONGINES follows a focus diTeren$a$on strategy by selling highly jeweled watches to wealthy female customers. FISHER-PRICE uses focus diTeren$a$on strategy to sell electronic calculators with large, bright colored buWons to the parents of preschoolers.", "image": "assets/pages/m2-107.webp"}, {"n": 108, "text": "A company that tries to engage in each generic strategy but fails to achieve any of them, is considered ‘stuck in the middle’. STUCK IN THE MIDDLE Such a company has no compe@@ve advantage regardless of the industry it is in. As a maper of fact, such a company will compete at a disadvantage because the ‘cost leader’, the ‘diTeren@ators’ and the ‘focusers’ in the industry will be beper posi@oned to compete. It may be the case, however, that a company that is stuck in the middle s@ll earns interes@ng prodts simply because it is opera@ng in a highly aprac@ve industry or because its compe@tors are stuck in the middle as well. FOR YOUR READING & BETTER UNDERSTANDING Only", "image": "assets/pages/m2-108.webp"}]}, {"id": "m3", "file": "m3.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m3-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "NATURE OF ORGANIZING CHAPTER 3", "image": "assets/pages/m3-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "CHAPTER 3 NATURE OF ORGANIZING Process Urwick’s Principles of Organizing Span of Management Factors AEecFng the Span Various Methods of DepartmentaFon Line and StaE Concepts (Line, StaE and FuncFonal StaE authority) DelegaFon - DeRniFon Principles and Steps", "image": "assets/pages/m3-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "4 What is an Organiza.on? Organiza.on refers to a collec.on of people, who are involved in pursuing de;ned objec.ves. The organiza.on encompasses division of work among employees and alignment of tasks towards the ul.mate goal of the company. It can also be referred as the second most important managerial func.on, that coordinates the work of employees, procures resources and combines the two, in pursuance of company’s goals.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "5 Importance of Organiza.ons – Bring together resources to achieve desired goals – Produce goods and services eHciently – Facilitate innova.on – Use modern manufacturing & informa.on technologies – Adapt to and inMuence a changing environment – Create value for owners, customers and employees – Accommodate ongoing challenges of diversity, ethics etc – mo.va.on and coordina.on of employees", "image": "assets/pages/m3-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "Veri;able objec.ves. A clear idea of the major du.es and ac.vi.es involved. An understood area of discre.on or authority so that the person ;lling the role knows what he can do to accomplish goals. ORGANIZATION IMPLIES A FORMALIZED, INTENTIONAL INTERNAL STRUCTURE OF ROLES AND POSITIONS Nature and Purpose of Organizing", "image": "assets/pages/m3-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "IDENTIFICATION OF ACTIVITIES OF THE ORGANIZATION ORGANIZING THE ACTIVITIES CLASSIFICATION OF THE AUTHORITY BUILDING RELATIONSHIP BETWEEN AUTHORITY AND RESPONSIBILITY 3 2 1 4 4PROCESS ORGANIZING STEPS IN AUTHORITY RESPONISIBILITY", "image": "assets/pages/m3-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "Formal OrganizaFon means the inten.onal structure of roles in a formally organized enterprise. Informal OrganizaFon is a network of personal and social rela.ons not established/required by the formal organiza.on but arising spontaneously as people associate with one another. FORMAL AND INFORMAL ORGANIZATION", "image": "assets/pages/m3-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "Formal OrganizaFon This is one which refers to a structure of well de;ned jobs each bearing a measure of authority and responsibility. It is a conscious determina.on by which people accomplish goals by adhering to the norms laid down by the structure. This kind of organiza.on is an arbitrary set up in which each person is responsible for his performance. Formal organiza.on has a formal set up to achieve pre- determined goals. FORMAL AND INFORMAL ORGANIZATION", "image": "assets/pages/m3-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "Informal OrganizaFon It refers to a network of personal and social rela.onships which spontaneously originates within the formal set up. Informal organiza.ons develop rela.onships which are built on likes, dislikes, feelings and emo.ons. Therefore, the network of social groups based on friendships can be called as informal organiza.ons. There is no conscious eTort made to have informal organiza.on. It emerges from the formal organiza.on and it is not based on any rules and regula.ons as in case of formal organiza.on. FORMAL AND INFORMAL ORGANIZATION", "image": "assets/pages/m3-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER INFORMAL ORGANIZATION - GOLF CLUB PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER INFORMAL ORGANIZATION – BOWLING TEAM INFORMAL ORGANIZATION – MORNING COFFEE REGULARS PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER INFORMAL ORGANIZATION – WEEKEND ADVENTURE SPORTS FORMAL ORGANZATION PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER VICE PRESIDENT VICE PRESIDENT MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER DIVISIONAL MANAGER", "image": "assets/pages/m3-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "12 Formal organiza.on Informal organiza.on 1. Designed and created by the top management 2. Planned one 3. Authority and responsibility are ;xed and de;ned 4. Requires an oHce to func.on 5. Is rigid, de;nite, and has a wricen cons.tu.on 1. Comes up on its own 2. Not a planned one, it is created spontaneously 3. Authority is generally based on personal acceptance. 4. Func.ons through people. 5. Is Mexible and has no such ;xed Rigid, wricen cons.tu.on.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "TALL AND FLAT ORGANIZATIONS Flat structures have fewer levels and wide spans of control Tall structures have many levels of authority and narrow spans of control", "image": "assets/pages/m3-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "14 Refers to the number of subordinates who report directly to an execu.ve or supervisor. The diTerences in the span of control have direct implica.ons on the shape of the organiza.on. TO BE EFFECTIVE… Organiza.ons must ;nd the op.mal span of control – Narrow enough to permit managers to MAINTAIN CONTROL OVER SUBORDINATES – Wide enough so that the possibility of MICROMANAGING (INTERFERING) IS MINIMIZED SPAN OF CONTROL", "image": "assets/pages/m3-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "SPAN OF CONTROL FACTORS EFFECTING The OPTIMAL SPAN OF CONTROL is dependent on the following 8 factors SUBORDINATES TRAINING DELEGATION OF AUTHORITY PLANNING RATE OF CHANGE COMMUNICATION TECHNIQUES KIND OF ACTIVITY KIND OF ORGANIZATION ORGANIZATION LEVEL", "image": "assets/pages/m3-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "16", "image": "assets/pages/m3-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "TEN PRINCIPLES OF ORGANIZING BY URWICK Lyndall Urwick Businessperson 08/24/2026 17 Lyndall Fownes Urwick MC was a Bri.sh management consultant and business thinker. He is recognized for integra.ng the ideas of earlier theorists like Henri Fayol into a comprehensive theory of management administra.on. He wrote an inMuen.al book called The Elements of Business Administra.on, published in 1943.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "1. Principle of Unity of ObjecFves 2. Principle of OrganizaFonal E`ciency 10. Principle of FuncFonal DeRniFon 9. Principle of Flexibility 8. Principle of Parity of Authority and Responsibility 7. Principle of DelegaFon 6. Principle of Unity of Command 5. Principle of Scalar Chain 4. Principle of Span of Management 3. Principle of SpecializaFon 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "1. PRINCIPLE OF UNITY OF OBJECTIVES An organiza.on structure is eTec.ve if it enables individuals to contribute to enterprise objec.ves. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick) A common goal so devised for the business as a whole and the organiza.on is set up to achieve that goal. In the absence of a common aim, various departments will set up their own goals and there is a possibility of conMic.ng objec.ves for diTerent departments. So there must be an objec.ve for the organiza.on", "image": "assets/pages/m3-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "2. PRINCIPLE OF ORGANIZATIONAL EFFICIENCY An organiza.on is eHcient if it is structured to aid the accomplishment of enterprise objec.ves with a minimum of unsought consequences or costs. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "3. PRINCIPLE OF SPECIALIZATION The whole work should be divided amongst the subordinates on the basis of quali;ca.ons, abili.es, and skills. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "4. PRINCIPLE OF SPAN OF MANAGEMENT The number of persons an individual can eHciently and eTec.vely manage is referred to as the span of management. There is a limit to this number. The exact number will depend on the impact of underlying variables. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "5. PRINCIPLE OF SCALAR CHAIN A scalar chain is a chain of command or authority that Mows from upper organiza.onal level to the lowest levels and clariRes who reports to whom. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "6. PRINCIPLE OF UNITY OF COMMAND Every subordinate is answerable and accountable to one boss at one .me. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "7. PRINCIPLE OF DELEGATION The authority must be delegated as far down in the organiza.on as possible. Adequate authority should be delegated to all individuals so that they accomplish the results expected. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "8. PRINCIPLE OF PARITY OF AUTHORITY AND RESPONSIBILITY The responsibility for ac.ons cannot be greater than that implied by the authority delegated, nor should it be less. The responsibility of subordinates to their superiors for performance is absolute, and superiors can't escape responsibility for the organiza.onal ac.vi.es of their subordinates. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-26.webp"}, {"n": 27, "text": "9. PRINCIPLE OF FLEXIBILITY The organiza.onal structure must be simple to understand and Mexible. The structure should be adaptable to the changes in the nature of business and technology and procedures. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-27.webp"}, {"n": 28, "text": "10. PRINCIPLE OF FUNCTIONAL DEFINITION Every posi.on in an organiza.on and every department in an organiza.on must have a clear de;ni.on of the results expected, ac.vi.es to be undertaken and authority delegated. 10 Principles of ORGANIZING (Urwick)", "image": "assets/pages/m3-28.webp"}, {"n": 29, "text": "DEPARTMENTATION", "image": "assets/pages/m3-29.webp"}, {"n": 30, "text": "is to divide whole work into a number of jobs to ensure that no important ac.vity is lek out THE FIRST STEP IN DESIGNING AN ORGANIZATION STRUCTURE ORGANIZATION STRUCTURE is to bring together homogeneous jobs into groups and to decide their rela.on to each other THE NEXT STEP IN DESIGNING AN ORGANIZATION STRUCTURE THIS PROCESS IS KNOWN AS DEPARTMENTATION it involves many decisions, concerning a logical division of work to be done, and leads to the establishment of a number of manageable units.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-30.webp"}, {"n": 31, "text": "PATTERNS OF DEPARTMENTATION 1. Departmenta.on by Simple numbers 2. Departmenta.on by Time 3. Departmenta.on by Enterprise func.ons 4. Departmenta.on by Territory/Geography 5. Departmenta.on by Product 6. Process/Equipment Departmenta.on 7. Customer Departmenta.on 8. Matrix Departmenta.on", "image": "assets/pages/m3-31.webp"}, {"n": 32, "text": "BY NUMBERS This is a structural departmenta.on which include – grouping all persons who are to perform the same du.es and func.ons and pumng them together under the supervision of a manager. Age old method and rapidly falling into disuse. 1", "image": "assets/pages/m3-32.webp"}, {"n": 33, "text": "Reasons for the DECLINE: 1. The technology is advancing very fast, demanding more specialized and diTerent skills. 2. Groups composed of specialized personnel are more eHcient than merely based on numbers. It is useful only at the lowest level of the organiza.on structure. BY NUMBERS 1", "image": "assets/pages/m3-33.webp"}, {"n": 34, "text": "BY TIME Oldest form structuring organiza.on Suitable for lower level of the organiza.on Grouping of ac.vi.es according to basis of .me The use of shiks is common in many enterprise because normal workloads become insuHcient and ineTec.ve E.g.: Hospital, Produc.on facili.es 2", "image": "assets/pages/m3-34.webp"}, {"n": 35, "text": "ADVANTAGES LIMITATIONS BY TIME 2 • Services oTered 24X7 instead of 8 hrs shik • Process need not be interrupted which needs a con.nuous cycle of opera.on • Expensive capital equipment can be used more than 8hrs/day • Students acending classes during the day’s .me can work in night shik to sustain their living. • Supervising during night shik becomes diHcult • There is fa.gue factor, since it is diHcult for most employees to change their biological clock • Having several shiks may cause problems with coordina.on and communica.on • Payment of over.me can increase the cost of produc.on or services rendered.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-35.webp"}, {"n": 36, "text": "Func.onal departmenta.on The most widely used form of departmenta.on Groups ac.vi.es by func.on - the jobs to be done. Consistent with the idea of specializa.on and division of work, ac.vi.es that are alike or similar are placed together in one department and under a single chain of command. BY FUNCTION 3", "image": "assets/pages/m3-36.webp"}, {"n": 37, "text": "Func.onal departmenta.on also facilitates coordinaFon since a supervisor is in charge of one major area of ac.vity. It is easier to achieve coordina.on this way than to have the same func.ons performed in diTerent departments under diTerent supervisors. BY FUNCTION 3", "image": "assets/pages/m3-37.webp"}, {"n": 38, "text": "STRENGTHS WEAKNESS BY FUNCTION 3 • Allows economies of scale within func.onal departments • Enables in-depth knowledge and skill development • Enables organiza.on to accomplish func.onal goals • Is best with only one or a few products • Slow response .me to environmental changes • May cause decisions to pile on top, hierarchy overload • Leads to poor horizontal coordina.on among departments • Results in less innova.on • Involves restricted view of organiza.onal goals", "image": "assets/pages/m3-38.webp"}, {"n": 39, "text": "Grouping ac.vi.es on the basis of territory. If an organiza.on's customers are geographically dispersed, it can group jobs based on geography. For example, Coca-Cola has reMected the company’s opera.on in two broad geographic areas – the North American sector and the interna.onal sector, which includes the Paci;c Rim, Europe, Africa and La.n America groups. BY TERRITORY / GEOGRAPHY 4 Geographical departmenta.on", "image": "assets/pages/m3-39.webp"}, {"n": 40, "text": "ADVANTAGES LIMITATIONS BY TERRITORY / GEOGRAPHY 4 • Places responsibility at the lower levels. • Places emphasis on local markets & local problems. • Improves co-ordina.on in a region. • Takes advantages of economies of local opera.on. • Becer face to face communica.on with local interests. • Furnishes measurable training ground for general managers. • Requires more persons with G.M abili.es. • Tends to make maintenance of economic central services diHcult. • Increases problem of top management control.", "image": "assets/pages/m3-40.webp"}, {"n": 41, "text": "BY PROCESS / EQUIPMENT 5", "image": "assets/pages/m3-41.webp"}, {"n": 42, "text": "Customer departmentaliza.on - Grouping ac.vi.es on the basis of common customers or types of customers. The assump.on is that customers in each department have a common set of problems and needs that can best be met by specialists. BY CUSTOMER 6", "image": "assets/pages/m3-42.webp"}, {"n": 43, "text": "Product departmenta.on can also be a useful guide for grouping ac.vi.es in service businesses. E.g.: A food product company may choose to divide its opera.ons into a frozen food department, a dairy products department, a produce department, and the like. BY PRODUCT / SERVICE 7", "image": "assets/pages/m3-43.webp"}, {"n": 44, "text": "A matrix structure is a type of departmentaliza.on that superimposes a horizontal set of divisional repor.ng rela.onships onto a hierarchical func.onal structure. The essence of a matrix organiza.on is the combina.on of func.onal and product or project pacerns of Departmenta.on in the same organiza.on structure. BY MATRIX STRUCTURE 8 Matrix structure departmenta.on", "image": "assets/pages/m3-44.webp"}, {"n": 45, "text": "BY MATRIX STRUCTURE 8", "image": "assets/pages/m3-45.webp"}, {"n": 46, "text": "FUNCTIONAL DEPARTMENTATION PRODUCT DEPARTMENTATION IDENTIFY THE DEPARTMENTATION GEOGRAPHICAL DEPARTMENTATION PROCESS DEPARTMENTATION", "image": "assets/pages/m3-46.webp"}]}, {"id": "m4", "file": "m4 - L22 TO 26 - TALENT ACQUISITION AND MANAGEMENT.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m4-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "TALENT ACQUISITION AND MANAGEMENT CHAPTER 4", "image": "assets/pages/m4-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "CHAPTER 4 TALENT ACQUISITION AND MANAGEMENT Difference between HRM and HRD Job Analysis Job Description and Job Specification Recruitment Methods and Sources Selection Process Techniques and Instruments Induction and Orientation Systems Approach to Staffing Approaches to Manager Development and Training.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "4 HRM (Human Resource Management) focuses on managing people within an organization and dealing with administrative tasks, policies, and procedures. HRD (Human Resource Development) emphasizes enhancing employees’ skills, knowledge, and capabilities to foster growth and organizational success through learning and development initiatives.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "5 HRM differs with HRD in a sense that HRM is associated with management of human resources while HRD is related to the development of employees. Human Resource Management is a bigger concept than Human Resource Development The former encompasses a range of organizational activities like planning, staffing, developing, monitoring, maintaining, managing relationship and evaluating whereas the latter covers the development part i.e., training, learning, career development, talent management, performance appraisal, employee engagement and empowerment.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "FUNCTIONS OF HRM FUNCTIONS OF HRD 6", "image": "assets/pages/m4-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "4 Benefits of integrating HRM and HRD Practices 1. Holistic Employee Development: Integrating HRM and HRD ensures a comprehensive approach to immediate support and long-term skill enhancement. 2. Strategic Alignment: HR practices align directly with organizational goals, enhancing the company’s strategic objectives. 3. Improved Productivity: Well-rounded development boosts employee skills and engagement, leading to increased efficiency. 4. Enhanced Talent Retention: Investing in employee development fosters loyalty and reduces turnover rates. 7", "image": "assets/pages/m4-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "The functions of Human Resource Management can be broadly divided into managerial and operational functions 8 1 MANAGERIAL function 2 OPERATIVE function", "image": "assets/pages/m4-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "DEFINITIONS Job - Consists of a group of tasks that must be performed for an organization to achieve its goals Position - Collection of tasks and responsibilities performed by one person; there is a position for every individual in an organization Job Analysis - Systematic process of determining the skills, duties, and knowledge required for performing jobs in an organization Job Description – document providing information regarding tasks, duties, and responsibilities of job Job Specification – minimum qualifications to perform a particular job 9", "image": "assets/pages/m4-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "5 Systematic process of determining the skills, duties, and knowledge required for performing jobs in an organization Studies are conducted to answer: • What skills are needed? • What should be the qualification? • What are desirable abilities? • What should be the work experience? • What are his/her responsibilities? • Job analysis includes the preparation of Job descriptions and Job specifications. JOB ANALYSIS", "image": "assets/pages/m4-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "5 Systematic process of determining the skills, duties, and knowledge required for performing jobs in an organization JOB ANALYSIS Tasks Responsibilities Duties JOB ANALYSIS JOB DESCRIPTIONS JOB SPECIFICATIONS Knowledge Skills Abilities ❑Human Resource Planning ❑Recruitment ❑Selection ❑Training and Development ❑Performance Appraisal ❑Compensation and Benefits ❑Safety and Health ❑Employee and Labor Relations ❑Legal Considerations ❑Job Analysis for Teams JOB DESCRIPTIONS JOB SPECIFICATIONS", "image": "assets/pages/m4-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "12 Job analysis includes the preparation of JOB DESCRIPTIONS and JOB SPECIFICATIONS JOB ANALYSIS", "image": "assets/pages/m4-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "Job description and Specification 13", "image": "assets/pages/m4-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "14 RECRUITMENT SELECTION PLACEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m4-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "15 1 2 3 Recruitment means search for the prospective employees to suit the job requirements as represented by job specification. Out of the prospective applicants right type of persons to match the jobs are selected for the job. Under placement the selected person is placed on the job which is most suited to him.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "1 THE OVERVIEW RECRUITMENT Process of locating, identifying, and attracting capable candidates. Can be for current or future needs. Critical activity for some corporations.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "Understanding the RECRUITMENT PROCESS The recruitment process is a strategic framework that organizations follow to find and attract the best candidates for their available positions. Variations in the process may exist based on specific requirements and industry practices. 17", "image": "assets/pages/m4-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "a simple 10-step process 10 RECRUITMENT 18 Identifying Hiring Needs Job Analysis and Job Description Sourcing Candidates Screening and Shortlisting Interviews Assessments and Tests Background Checks Decision Making Offer and Negotiation Onboarding 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9", "image": "assets/pages/m4-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "TYPES OF RECRUITMENT 1. Internal Recruitment 2. External Recruitment 3. Campus Recruitment 4. Executive Search 5. Contingent Recruitment 6. Remote Recruitment 7. Diversity and Inclusion Recruitment 8. Referral Recruitment 9. Social Media Recruitment 10. Full Cycle Recruitment 11. Digital Recruitment 19 Full Cycle Recruitment: A comprehensive recruitment process covering all stages, from sourcing to onboarding. Internal Recruitment: Hiring candidates from within the organization for a vacant position. External Recruitment: Sourcing candidates from outside the organization to fill open positions. Campus Recruitment: Recruiting fresh graduates directly from universities or educational institutions. Executive Search: A specialized recruitment process focused on hiring senior-level or executive positions. Contingent Recruitment: Payment-based recruitment where agencies are paid only if they successfully fill a position. Remote Recruitment: Hiring candidates who will work remotely, outside of a traditional office setting. Diversity & Inclusion Recruitment: Focused hiring strategies to ensure a diverse and inclusive workforce. Referral Recruitment: Hiring candidates recommended by current employees or network connections. Social Media Recruitment: Using platforms like LinkedIn, Twitter, or Facebook to attract and recruit candidates. Full Cycle Recruitment: A comprehensive recruitment process covering all stages, from sourcing to onboarding. Recruitment can take various forms depending on the specific needs and strategies of an organization. Here are some common types of recruitment approaches:", "image": "assets/pages/m4-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "CHALLENGES IN RECRUITMENT 1. Talent Shortage 2. High Competition 3. Time and Resource Constraints 4. Evolving Job Market and Skills Gap 5. Diversity and Inclusion 6. Candidate Experience 7. Technology and Automation 8. Compliance and Regulations 9. Retention and Turnover 10.Market and Industry Dynamics 20", "image": "assets/pages/m4-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "21 S O U R C E S O F RECRUITMENT INTERNAL RECRUITMENT EXTERNAL RECRUITMENT 1 2 A. PROMOTION B. TRANSFER C. DEMOTION D. RETIRED EMPLOYEES E. NOTICE BOARDS F. JOB POSTING A. PRESS ADVERTISEMENTS B. EDUCATIONAL INSTITUTES C. PLACEMENT AGENCIES D. EMPLOYMENT AGENCIES E. UNSOLICITED OBLIGANTS F. EMPLOYEE RECOMMENDATION", "image": "assets/pages/m4-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "2 SELECTION Selection refers to picking from among the candidates the one that best meets the position requirements.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "23 1. Initial or Preliminary interview 2. Application form 3. Reference checks SELECTION PROCEDURE Various steps of the 4. Employment tests 5. Interview 6. Selection decision", "image": "assets/pages/m4-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "24 An initial or preliminary interview is an initial meeting to evaluate a candidate's basic qualifications and fit for a position. INITIAL OR PRELIMINARY INTERVIEW 1", "image": "assets/pages/m4-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "25 This is used as one of the selection tools. It provides factual information needed for evaluating the candidate’s suitability. Once hired, the data can become a part of the employee’s records. It contains written record of: • Identifying information • Information regarding education • Information regarding experience • Expected salaries and allowances and other fringe benefits • Information regarding community activities APPLICATION FORM 2", "image": "assets/pages/m4-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "26 REFERENCE CHECKS 3 The applicant is asked to mention the names and addresses of his former employees and also of two or three persons known but not related to him. Referees may be called upon to give detailed information about candidate’s capabilities. If references are checked in the correct manner, a great deal can be learned about a person that an interview or tests can’t elicit.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-26.webp"}, {"n": 27, "text": "27 EMPLOYMENT TESTS 4 Tests are instruments designed to measure selected qualities and abilities in terms of job specifications. Such tests provide a sample behavior that is used to draw inferences about the future behavior or performance of an individual. i. MEASURES OF HUMAN CHARACTERISTICS ii. MEASURES OF HUMAN PSYCHOLOGY", "image": "assets/pages/m4-27.webp"}, {"n": 28, "text": "28 MEASURES OF HUMAN CHARACTERISTICS EMPLOYMENT TESTS 4 1 2 3 4", "image": "assets/pages/m4-28.webp"}, {"n": 29, "text": "29 •Intelligence / Mental tests •Emotional Quotient •Skill tests •Aptitude tests •Psychomotor tests •Clerical Aptitude test Aptitude •Job Knowledge test •Work Sample test Achievement •Group Discussion •In Basket Situational Interest •Objective tests •Projective tests •Situation tests Personality Multi- Dimensional EMPLOYMENT TESTS 4 MEASURES OF HUMAN PSYCHOLOGY 1 2 3 4 5 6", "image": "assets/pages/m4-29.webp"}, {"n": 30, "text": "30 Preliminary interview • Informal interview • Unstructured interview Core interview • Background information interview • Job and probing interview • Stress interview • The group discussion interview • Formal and structured interview • Panel interview • Depth interview Decision-Making interview INTERVIEW 5", "image": "assets/pages/m4-30.webp"}, {"n": 31, "text": "31 SELECTION DECISION 6 Once everyone has been interviewed and you’ve received feedback from the interviewers, the hiring manager will narrow the pool down to the best fit for the job. Sometimes, a second candidate will be identified as a runner-up in case the first pick declines the job offer. Since the next phase involves testing/training, having a second candidate in mind is helpful if the first choice doesn’t pass/clear.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-31.webp"}, {"n": 32, "text": "32", "image": "assets/pages/m4-32.webp"}, {"n": 33, "text": "TRAINING Training refers to acquiring specific knowledge and skills for a particular job or task. It is usually a short-term activity concerned with improving an employee’s current job performance. It includes formal training courses, on-the-job training, or coaching sessions. 33", "image": "assets/pages/m4-33.webp"}, {"n": 34, "text": "5 Processes of Training & Development 34 Needs Assessment Design and Development Delivery Evaluation Follow-Up 1 2 3 4 5", "image": "assets/pages/m4-34.webp"}, {"n": 35, "text": "Current Trends in Training & Development 1. Digital Learning 2. Personalized Training Programs 3. Microlearning 4. Gamification 5. Diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) Training 6. Soft skills development", "image": "assets/pages/m4-35.webp"}, {"n": 36, "text": "Examples of Companies with Training and Development Programs Microsoft allows a wide variety of training and development programs, including technical training, leadership development, and career development workshops. The company’s annual Hackathon event lets employees collaborate and develop new ideas and solutions. 36", "image": "assets/pages/m4-36.webp"}, {"n": 37, "text": "Examples of Companies with Training and Development Programs Google offers a range of training and development programs for employees, including leadership development, technical skills training, and employee wellness programs. The company’s specially designed program enables employees to teach and learn from each other in their areas of expertise. 37", "image": "assets/pages/m4-37.webp"}, {"n": 38, "text": "Examples of Companies with Training and Development Programs Amazon provides extensive on-the-job training for employees to develop skills in various areas like customer service, leadership and technology. In addition, their unique Career Choice program pays 95% of tuition and fees for employees to learn and excel in high-demand fields. 38", "image": "assets/pages/m4-38.webp"}, {"n": 39, "text": "Examples of Companies with Training and Development Programs Starbucks is known for its comprehensive employee training program, the Starbucks Experience. The program includes online and in- person training sessions, usually covering customer service, coffee knowledge, and drink preparation. The employees receive continuous coaching and feedback from managers to improve their performance. 39", "image": "assets/pages/m4-39.webp"}, {"n": 40, "text": "Examples of Companies with Training and Development Programs Walmart’s training programs lay stress on areas like customer service, compliance, and leadership development. They also provide tuition assistance to pursue education and training in high- demand fields. 40 These are a few examples of training and development programs adapted by the top brands. Effective training and development programs are essential to help employees perform their jobs effectively.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-40.webp"}, {"n": 41, "text": "TRAINING METHODS 41 ON-THE JOB METHODS 1 OFF-THE JOB METHODS 2", "image": "assets/pages/m4-41.webp"}, {"n": 42, "text": "INDUCTION & ORIENTATION 42", "image": "assets/pages/m4-42.webp"}, {"n": 43, "text": "INDUCTION The formal process of introducing a new employee to the organization, its policies, and culture. ORIENTATION A structured program to familiarize new employees with their job roles, teams, and work environment.", "image": "assets/pages/m4-43.webp"}, {"n": 44, "text": "SENSITIVITY TRAINING is a form of training with the goal of making people more aware of their own goals as well as their prejudices, and more sensitive to others and to the dynamics of group interaction. SENSITIVITY TRAINING", "image": "assets/pages/m4-44.webp"}, {"n": 45, "text": "SYSTEMS APPROACH TO STAFFING", "image": "assets/pages/m4-45.webp"}, {"n": 46, "text": "46", "image": "assets/pages/m4-46.webp"}, {"n": 47, "text": "MANAGER DEVELOPMENT AND TRAINING 47", "image": "assets/pages/m4-47.webp"}, {"n": 48, "text": "major 8 considerations in ensuring the DEVELOPMENT OF EXCELLENT MANAGERS: 1. Instilling a Willingness to Learn 2. Accelerating Management Development 3. Planning for Innovation 4. Measuring & Rewarding Management Effectively 5. Tailoring Information 6. Expanding Research & Development in Tools & Techniques 7. Developing more Managerial Inventories 8. Creating Strong Intellectual Leadership", "image": "assets/pages/m4-48.webp"}]}, {"id": "m5", "file": "m5 - L27 TO L36 - LEADERSHIP AND MOTIVATION, CONTROLLING.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT", "image": "assets/pages/m5-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "LEADERSHIP AND MOTIVATION CHAPTER 5 CONTROLLING", "image": "assets/pages/m5-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "CHAPTER 5 LEADERSHIP AND MOTIVATION Leader and Manager Differences between: Leading and Managing, Differences between: motives, motivators and motivation Theories of Motivation Maslow’s Need Hierarchy Herzberg’s Two-Factor Theory McGregor X and Y Motivational Techniques Leadership Styles Likert’s Four Systems of Management Leadership Grid Theories Committees, Teams, and Group Decision-Making Communication Difference between General and Managerial Communication Types of Communication Barriers of Communication CONTROLLING Process (steps) Management Control Techniques (Budgetary, Non-budgetary & Network) Types of control", "image": "assets/pages/m5-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "4 LEADERSHIP is the art or process of influencing people so that they will strive willingly and enthusiastically toward the achievement of group goals. Ideally, people should be encouraged to develop not only willingness to work but also “willingness to work with zeal and confidence”. Zeal is ardor, earnestness, and intensity to work; Confidence reflects experience and technical ability.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "5 What is LEADERSHIP LEADING people INFLUENCING people COMMANDING people GUIDING people", "image": "assets/pages/m5-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "Managers vs. Leaders 6 MANAGERS Focus on things Do things right Plan Organize Direct Control Follows the rules LEADERS Focus on people Do the right things Inspire Influence Motivate Build Shape entities", "image": "assets/pages/m5-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "Ingredients of Leadership 7 ➢The ability to USE POWER EFFECTIVELY and in a RESPONSIBLE MANNER. ➢The ability to UNDERSTAND that human beings have different MOTIVATION FORCES at different times and different situations. ➢The ability to INSPIRE. ➢The ability to act in a manner that will DEVELOP A CLIMATE conducive to responding to and affecting motivations.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "Qualities of an effective leader 8 Shouldn’t be arrogant Shouldn’t be miserly Should be as open as possible with subordinates Should ensure his troops understand what is expected of them Should promote competency and inspire Must be able to see things from others point of view Must consider the needs of the organization above his or her own", "image": "assets/pages/m5-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "MOTIVE A motive is an inner state that energizes, activates, or moves and directs behavior towards goals. It is the driving force behind human actions. MOTIVATOR The technique used to motivate people. SATISFACTION The contentment experienced when a want is satisfied. MOTIVATION The drive and effort to satisfy a want or a goal. The process of stimulating people to action to accomplish desired goals.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "MOTIVATION Theories of", "image": "assets/pages/m5-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "Maslow's HIERARCHY OF NEEDS", "image": "assets/pages/m5-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "Maslow's hierarchy of needs is a theory of psychology explaining human motivation based on the pursuit of different levels of needs. PHYSIOLOGICAL SAFETY SOCIAL ESTEEM and SELF-ACTUALIZATION as each need is substantially satisfied, the next need becomes dominant. THE HIERARCHY NEEDS THEORY Self-Actualization The drive to become what one is capable of becoming.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "HIGHER ORDER NEEDS are satisfied INTERNALLY LOWER-ORDER NEEDS are satisfied EXTERNALLY THE HIERARCHY NEEDS THEORY", "image": "assets/pages/m5-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "Organizations help employees meet their self-actualization needs by providing them with opportunities for skill development, the chance to be creative, promotions and the ability to have complete control over their jobs. Organizations meet employees’ esteem needs with merit pay rises, recognition, challenging tasks, participation in decision making and opportunity for advancement. Organizations meet employees’ social needs by providing them with the opportunity to interact with others, to be accepted and to have friends. Many organizations schedule employee parties, picnics, trips and support office sport teams Organizations meet employees’ safety needs by providing safe working conditions, job security, and fringe benefits (medical insurance / sick pay / pensions) Organization meet employees’ physiological needs by providing adequate salary, work breaks, and safe working conditions.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "HERZBERG'S TWO FACTOR THEORY", "image": "assets/pages/m5-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "HERZBERG TWO FACTOR THEORY The two-factor motivation theory, otherwise known as HERZBERG’S MOTIVATION-HYGIENE THEORY or DUAL-FACTOR THEORY, argues that there are separate sets of MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE FACTORS in the workplace that either cause JOB SATISFACTION OR DISSATISFACTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "HERZBERG TWO FACTOR THEORY", "image": "assets/pages/m5-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "COMPARISON MASLOW VS. HERZBERG", "image": "assets/pages/m5-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "THEORY X AND THEORY Y McGregor’s", "image": "assets/pages/m5-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "Theory X and theory Y are part of motivational theories. Both the theories, which are very different from each other, are used by managers to motivate their employees. Theory X gives importance to supervision, while theory Y stresses on rewards and recognition. THEORY-X AND THEORY-Y", "image": "assets/pages/m5-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "THEORY-X AND THEORY-Y Assumptions of Theory X (Traditional Belief) An average employee intrinsically does not like work and tries to escape it whenever possible. Since the employee does not want to work, he must be persuaded, compelled, or warned with punishment so as to achieve organizational goals. A close supervision is required on part of managers. The managers adopt a more dictatorial style. Many employees rank job security on top, and they have little or no aspiration/ ambition. Employees generally dislike responsibilities. Employees resist change. An average employee needs formal direction.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "THEORY-X AND THEORY-Y Assumptions of Theory Y (Belief based on research) Employees can perceive their job as relaxing and normal. They exercise their physical and mental efforts in an inherent manner in their jobs. Employees may not require only threat, external control and coercion to work, but they can use self-direction and self-control if they are dedicated and sincere to achieve the organizational objectives. If the job is rewarding and satisfying, then it will result in employees’ loyalty and commitment to organization. An average employee can learn to admit and recognize the responsibility. In fact, he can even learn to obtain responsibility. The employees have skills and capabilities. Their logical capabilities should be fully utilized. In other words, the creativity, resourcefulness and innovative potentiality of the employees can be utilized to solve organizational problems.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "BASIS FOR COMPARISON THEORY X THEORY Y Meaning Theory X is a motivational theory, which involves high supervision and control over the subordinates, and greater degree of centralization. Theory Y, is an advanced theory, wherein it is assumed that the workers are self-directed and self-motivated, for growth and development and takes active part in decision making. Work Dislikes work Work is natural Ambition Little to no ambition Highly ambitious Responsibility Avoids responsibility. Accept and seek responsibility. Leadership style Autocratic Democratic Direction Constant direction is required. Little to no direction is required. Control Tight Lenient Authority Centralized Decentralized Self-motivation Absent Present Focuses on Psychological needs and Security needs Social needs, esteem needs and self-actualization needs.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "MOTIVATIONAL TECHNIQUES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "• Praise the workers and give them due credit for all good work . • Take sincere interest in subordinates as individual persons. • Promote healthy competition among the individual employees. • Find ways to develop and utilize the appeal of pride in or about the workplace. • Delegate substantial amount of responsibility to the subordinates. • Fix fair wages and monetary individual or group incentives for employees. • Formulate a suitable suggestion system. • Provide opportunities for growth and promotion. Motivational Techniques (Positive)", "image": "assets/pages/m5-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "• Reprimanding the employees • Demotion • Lay-offs • Discharge • The proper proportioning of positive and negative techniques is the mark of a skillful manager. Motivational Techniques (Negative) Reprimanding means Scolding, Lecturing", "image": "assets/pages/m5-26.webp"}, {"n": 27, "text": "Money (Economists and most managers have tended to place money high on the scale of motivators, but behavioral scientists place it low) Positive reinforcement Job enrichment Participation Special Motivational Techniques", "image": "assets/pages/m5-27.webp"}, {"n": 28, "text": "LEADERSHIP STYLES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-28.webp"}, {"n": 29, "text": "29 LEADERSHIP BASED ON THE USE OF AUTHORITY LIKERT’S FOUR-SYSTEMS OF MANAGING THE MANAGERIAL GRID 1 2 3 THREE THEORIES on LEADERSHIP BEHAVIOR AND STYLES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-29.webp"}, {"n": 30, "text": "1. Styles based on USE OF AUTHORITY 30 Leadership style is the manner and approach of providing direction, implementing plans, and motivating people. Kurt Lewin (1939) led a group of researchers to identify different styles of leadership. This early study has been very influential and established three major leadership styles. ✓ AUTOCRATIC LEADER ✓ DEMOCRATIC/PARTICIPATIVE LEADER ✓ FREE REIN LEADER", "image": "assets/pages/m5-30.webp"}, {"n": 31, "text": "1.1 Autocratic Leadership 31 The Autocratic leader commands and expects compliance, is positive and leads by ability to withhold or give rewards and punishment.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-31.webp"}, {"n": 32, "text": "1.1 Autocratic Leadership 32 This style is used when leaders tell their employees what they want and how they want it accomplished, without getting the advice of their followers. Some of the appropriate conditions to use it is when you have all the information to solve the problem, you are short on time, and your employees are well motivated.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-32.webp"}, {"n": 33, "text": "1.1 Autocratic Leadership 33 The autocrat leadership style manages the direction of all goals and work, with little to no input from the team. They have all the power to make decisions, and they use it. They don’t worry about input, and do not leave room for subordinates to sub-manage. The authoritarian style should normally only be used on rare occasions. For example: Military, manufacturing, construction", "image": "assets/pages/m5-33.webp"}, {"n": 34, "text": "1.2 Democratic / Participative leader 34 The Democratic/Participative leader consults with subordinates on proposed actions and decisions and encourages participation from them.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-34.webp"}, {"n": 35, "text": "1.2 Democratic / Participative leader 35 This style involves the leader including one or more employees in the decision making process (determining what to do and how to do it). However, the leader maintains the final decision making authority. Using this style is not a sign of weakness, rather it is a sign of strength that your employees will respect.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-35.webp"}, {"n": 36, "text": "1.2 Democratic / Participative leader 36 This is normally used when you have part of the information, and your employees have other parts. Note that a leader is not expected to know everything  -  this is why you employ knowledgeable and skillful employees. Using this style is of mutual benefit  -  it allows them to become part of the team and allows you to make better decisions.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-36.webp"}, {"n": 37, "text": "1.3 Delegative / Free Reign Leader 37 The Free-rein leader uses his/her power very little, if at all giving subordinates a high degree of independence in their operations. Such leaders depend largely on subordinates to set their own goals and the means of achieving them.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-37.webp"}, {"n": 38, "text": "1.3 Delegative / Free Reign Leader 38 In this style, the leader allows the employees to make the decisions. However, the leader is still responsible for the decisions that are made. This is used when employees are able to analyze the situation and determine what needs to be done and how to do it.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-38.webp"}, {"n": 39, "text": "1. Styles based on USE OF AUTHORITY LEADER LEADER LEADER Autocratic Leadership Democratic / Participative leader Delegative / Free Reign Leader EMPLOYEES EMPLOYEES EMPLOYEES POWER STLYE POWER WITH LEADER POWER SHARED POWER WITH EMPLOYEE", "image": "assets/pages/m5-39.webp"}, {"n": 40, "text": "Forces that influence the style to be used : 40 How much time is available. Are relationships based on respect and trust or on disrespect? Who has the information  -  you, your employees, or both? How well your employees are trained and how well you know the task. Internal conflicts. Stress levels. Type of task. Is it structured, unstructured, complicated, or simple? Laws or established procedures such as training plans.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-40.webp"}, {"n": 41, "text": "2. Likert’s 4 Systems of Managing 41 Rensis Likert along with his associates in Michigan University, USA conducted research to study the patterns and styles of managers over three decades, across 200 organizations and developed a four-fold model of the management system that helped in understanding the leadership behavior. The proposed four systems of management, can also be thought of as leadership styles. Each describes the relationships, involvement, and roles of managers and subordinates in an industrial setting. System 1 Management “EXPLOITATIVE AUTHORITATIVE” System 2 Management “BENEVOLENT AUTHORITATIVE” System 3 Management “CONSULTATIVE” System 4 Management “PARTICIPATIVE”", "image": "assets/pages/m5-41.webp"}, {"n": 42, "text": "2.1 Exploitative Authoritative 42 ➢Managers are highly autocratic with no trust nor confidence in subordinates ➢Motivate people through fear & punishment and only occasional rewards ➢Engage in downward communication and limit decision making to the top ➢Responsibility lies in the hands of the people at the upper levels of the hierarchy. ➢The decisions are imposed on subordinates and they do not feel free at all to discuss things about the job with their superior. System 1 Management “EXPLOITATIVE AUTHORITATIVE” The teamwork or communication is very little and the motivation is based on threats.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-42.webp"}, {"n": 43, "text": "2.2 Benevolent Authoritative 43 ➢Managers have a patronizing confidence and trust in subordinates ➢Motivate with rewards and some fear and punishment ➢Permit some upward communication ➢Ask some ideas and opinions from subordinates ➢Allows some delegation of decision-making but with close control ➢Responsibility lies at the managerial levels but not at the lower levels of the organizational hierarchy ➢Subordinates do not feel free to discuss things about the job with their superior System 2 Management “BENEVOLENT AUTHORITATIVE” The teamwork or communication is very little and motivation is based on a system of rewards. patronizing confidence to treat others in a manner that shows you consider yourself to be better or more important than they are.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-43.webp"}, {"n": 44, "text": "2.3 Consultative 44 ➢Managers have substantial but not complete confidence and trust in subordinates ➢Usually try to make use of subordinates ideas and opinions ➢Use rewards for motivation with occasional punishment and some participation ➢Engage in communication flow both up and down ➢Make broad policy and general decisions at the top while allowing specific decisions to be made at lower levels, and act consultatively in other ways. ➢Responsibility is spread widely through the organizational hierarchy. System 3 Management “CONSULTATIVE” Has fair amount of teamwork, motivation is based on rewards and involvement in the job.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-44.webp"}, {"n": 45, "text": "2.4 Participative 45 ➢Managers have complete trust and confidence in subordinates in all matters ➢Seek ideas & opinions from subordinates and constructively use them ➢Engage in downwards and upwards communication ➢Encourage decision making throughout the organization ➢Operate among themselves and with their subordinates as a group. ➢Responsibility for achieving the organizational goals is widespread throughout the organizational hierarchy. System 4 Management “PARTICIPATIVE” There is a high level of teamwork, communication, & participation.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-45.webp"}, {"n": 46, "text": "2. Likert’s 4 Systems of Managing 46 In general, Likert found that those managers who applied the SYSTEM 4 approach to their operations had greatest success as leaders. Moreover, he noted that departments and companies managed by the system 4 approach were more effective in setting goals and achieving them and were generally more productive.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-46.webp"}, {"n": 47, "text": "3. The Managerial Grid 47 The managerial grid model is a self-assessment tool by which individuals and organizations can help identify a manager's or leader's style. The grid was originally developed by Robert R. Blake and Jane S. Mouton in the 1960s and has evolved in subsequent decades.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-47.webp"}, {"n": 48, "text": "3. The Managerial Grid 48 \"CONCERN FOR PRODUCTION\" runs along the horizontal axis on a 9-point scale. A 1, or low concern for production, is placed to the far left of the horizontal line, close to the right angle; a 9, or high concern for production, is located on the far right of the horizontal line. \"CONCERN FOR PEOPLE\" runs along the vertical axis on a 9-point scale. A 1, or low concern, falls at the bottom of the vertical line, close to the base near the right angle, while a 9, or high concern for people, is placed at the top of this vertical axis. Each dimension ranging from low (1) to high (9), thus creating 81 different positions in which the leadership’s style may fall.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-48.webp"}, {"n": 49, "text": "49 1.9 COUNTRY CLUB MANAGEMENT Thoughtful attention to needs of people for satisfying relationship leads to a comfortable, friendly organization atmosphere and work tempo. 5.5 MIDDLE OF THE ROAD MANAGEMENT Adequate organization performance is possible through balancing the necessity to get out work with maintaining morale of people at a satisfactory level. 9.1 AUTHORITY-COMPLIANCE MANAGEMENT Efficiency in operations results from arranging conditions of work in such a way that human elements interfere to a minimum degree. 9.9 TEAM MANAGEMENT Work accomplishment is from committed people; interdependence through a “common stake” in organization purpose leads to relationships of trust and respect. 1.1 IMPOVERISHED MANAGEMENT Exertion of minimum effort to get required work done is appropriate to sustain organization membership.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-49.webp"}, {"n": 50, "text": "3. The Managerial Grid 1.1 MANAGEMENT PEOPLE PRODUCTION minimum effort is required to get work done and sustain organizational morale 1.9 MANAGEMENT PEOPLE PRODUCTION thoughtful attention to needs of people leads to a friendly and comfortable organizational atmosphere and work tempo. 9.1 MANAGEMENT PEOPLE PRODUCTION efficiency results from arranging work in such a way that human elements have little effort. 5.5 MANAGEMENT PEOPLE PRODUCTION adequate performance through balance of work requirements & maintaining satisfactory morale. 9.9 MANAGEMENT PEOPLE PRODUCTION work accomplished is from committed people with interdependence through a common stake in organization purpose & with trust and respect", "image": "assets/pages/m5-50.webp"}, {"n": 51, "text": "Styles of Managing 51 1.1 MANAGEMENT Referred to as “IMPOVERISHED MANAGEMENT” Here managers concern themselves very little with either people or production and have minimum involvement in their jobs, to all intents and purposes, they have abandoned their jobs and only act as messengers communicating information from superiors to subordinates. PEOPLE PRODUCTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-51.webp"}, {"n": 52, "text": "Styles of Managing 52 1.9 MANAGEMENT Referred to as “COUNTRY CLUB MANAGEMENT” It is one in which managers have little or no concern for production but are concerned only for people. They promote an environment in which everyone is relaxed, friendly and happy and no one is concerned about putting forth coordinated effort to accomplish the enterprise objectives. PEOPLE PRODUCTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-52.webp"}, {"n": 53, "text": "Styles of Managing 53 5.5 MANAGEMENT Referred to as “ MIDDLE OF THE ROADS” Here the managers have medium concern for production and for people. They obtain adequate, but not outstanding morale and production. They do not set goals too high and they are likely to have rather benevolently autocratic attitude toward people. PEOPLE PRODUCTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-53.webp"}, {"n": 54, "text": "Styles of Managing 54 9.1 MANAGEMENT Referred to as “AUTOCRATIC TASK MANAGEMENT” Here the managers are concerned only with developing an efficient operation, who have little or no concern for people, and who are quite autocratic in their style of leadership. PEOPLE PRODUCTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-54.webp"}, {"n": 55, "text": "Styles of Managing 55 9.9 MANAGEMENT Referred to as “TEAM MANAGEMENT” This is on the other extreme. The managers here display in their actions the highest possible dedication both to people and to production. They are real team managers who are able to mesh the production needs of the enterprise with the needs of the individuals. PEOPLE PRODUCTION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-55.webp"}, {"n": 56, "text": "3 4 5 56 AUTOCRATIC LEADER DEMOCRATIC/PARTICIPATIVE LEADER FREE REIN LEADER SYSTEM 1 MANAGEMENT “EXPLOITATIVE AUTHORITATIVE” SYSTEM 2 MANAGEMENT “BENEVOLENT AUTHORITATIVE” SYSTEM 3 MANAGEMENT “CONSULTATIVE” SYSTEM 4 MANAGEMENT “PARTICIPATIVE” 1.1 MANAGEMENT “IMPOVERISHED MANAGEMENT” 1.9 MANAGEMENT “COUNTRY CLUB MANAGEMENT” 5.5 MANAGEMENT “MIDDLE OF THE ROADS” 9.1 MANAGEMENT “AUTOCRATIC TASK MANAGEMENT” 9.9 MANAGEMENT “TEAM MANAGEMENT” LEADERSHIP BASED ON THE USE OF AUTHORITY LIKERT’S FOUR-SYSTEMS OF MANAGING THE MANAGERIAL GRID 1 2 3 summary….", "image": "assets/pages/m5-56.webp"}, {"n": 57, "text": "Leadership as a CONTINUUM 57 Developed by Robert Tannenbaum and W. H. Schmidt. They see leadership as involving a variety of styles, ranging from one that is highly boss centred to one that is that is highly subordinate centred. The styles vary with the degree of freedom a leader / manager grants to subordinates. Thus instead of suggesting a choice between the two styles of leadership – Authoritarian and Democratic – this approach offers a range of styles.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-57.webp"}, {"n": 58, "text": "58 The continuum theory recognizes which style of leadership is appropriate, depending on the leader, followers and the situation faced by the organization. Ellipse is placed around the model to represent the influences on style imposed by both the organizational environment and societal environment. AREA OF FREEDOM for managers AREA OF FREEDOM for non-managers DEMOCRATIC Leadership as a CONTINUUM AUTHORITARIAN FREE REIGN Organizational Environment Societal Environment", "image": "assets/pages/m5-58.webp"}, {"n": 59, "text": "DECISION MAKING COMMITTEE, TEAMS & GROUPS", "image": "assets/pages/m5-59.webp"}, {"n": 60, "text": "COMMITTEE A committee is a group of persons to whom, as a group, some matter is committed. It is also referred to as a board, commission, task force, team, self- managing team, self-managed work group, or autonomous work group. However, its essential nature is similar.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-60.webp"}, {"n": 61, "text": "FOUR STAGES OF GROUP DEVELOPMENT Groups go through four stages Forming when the members of the group get to know each other Storming when the members of the group determine the objective of the meeting and conflict arises Norming when the group agrees on norms and some rules of behavior Performing when the group gets down to the task 1 2 3 4", "image": "assets/pages/m5-61.webp"}, {"n": 62, "text": "FUNCTIONS AND FORMALITY • Planning • Organizing • Staffing • Leading • Controlling In addition to this, Some people in the committee • have the authority to make recommendations to a manager • receive information without making recommendations and decisions", "image": "assets/pages/m5-62.webp"}, {"n": 63, "text": "REASONS FOR USING COMMITTEES 1. Group Deliberation and Judgment 2. Dominant Logic 3. Fear of Too Much Authority in a Single Person 4. Representation of Interested Groups 5. Coordination of Departments, Plans, and Policies 6. Transmission and Sharing of Information 7. Consolidation of Authority 8. Motivation through Participation", "image": "assets/pages/m5-63.webp"}, {"n": 64, "text": "DISADVANTAGES AND MISUSE OF COMMITTEES 1. Committees are costly. 2. They may result in compromises at the least common denominator rather than in an optimal decision. 3. They may lead to indecision. 4. They also can split responsibility. 5. It can lead to a situation in which a few persons impose their will on the majority, not allowing the participation of other members. 1. The committee has often fallen into disrepute through misuse. 2. Committees should not be used as a replacement for a manager, for research study, for unimportant decisions, and for decisions beyond the participants’ authority.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-64.webp"}, {"n": 65, "text": "GROUPS Two or more people acting interdependently in a unified manner toward achieving common goals. Characteristics of Groups ➢Group members share one or more COMMON GOALS, such as the goals of a product group to develop, manufacture, and market a new product. ➢Group members normally require interaction and communication among members. ➢Group members within a group assume ROLES (designing, producing, selling, or distributing a product, etc) ➢Groups usually are a part of a LARGER GROUP. ➢Groups develop NORMS, which refer to the expected behavior of the group members.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-65.webp"}, {"n": 66, "text": "FUNCTIONS AND ADVANTAGES OF GROUPS • Groups are powerful in changing behavior, attitudes, and values and in disciplining members. • Groups are used for decision-making, negotiating, and bargaining. • Thus, group members with diverse backgrounds may bring different perspectives to the decision-making process • Different group structures influence communication patterns.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-66.webp"}, {"n": 67, "text": "FUNCTIONS AND ADVANTAGES OF GROUPS • Effective group interaction may also affect motivation. • E.g, group members participating in setting objectives may become committed to the achievement of group goals • An understanding of groups is important for carrying out all managerial functions, particularly the function of leading • Groups do provide social satisfaction for their members, a feeling of belonging, and support for the needs of individuals. • Another benefit of groups is that they promote communication.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-67.webp"}, {"n": 68, "text": "TEAMS A team can be defined as “a small number of people with complementary skills who are committed to a common purpose, set of performance goals, and approach for which they hold themselves mutually accountable.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-68.webp"}, {"n": 69, "text": "In teams, some make recommendations; others have the power to make decisions, and few others run operations. Some teams are created to solve problems, such as quality circles, and others engage in cross-functional activities such as design, marketing, finance, manufacturing, personnel, and so on.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-69.webp"}, {"n": 70, "text": "TEAM BUILDING Team members must be convinced that the team’s purpose is worthwhile, meaningful, and urgent. Team members should also be selected according to the skills needed to achieve the purpose. Teams should have the right mix of skills, such as functional or technical skills, problem-solving and decision-making skills, and, of course, human relations skills.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-70.webp"}, {"n": 71, "text": "TEAM BUILDING The team needs to be guided by rules for group behavior, such as regular attendance, confidentiality, discussions based on facts, and everyone contributing. Goals and required tasks should be identified early in team formation. Members should encourage each other through recognition, positive feedback, and rewards.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-71.webp"}, {"n": 72, "text": "TYPES OF TEAMS Self-managing team A group with members with various skills needed to carry out a relatively complete task. Virtual management Running a team whose members are not in the same location, do not report to the person managing it, and may not even work for the same organization.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-72.webp"}, {"n": 73, "text": "COMMUNICATION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-73.webp"}, {"n": 74, "text": "COMMUNICATION Communication is viewed as the means by which people are linked together in an organization to achieve common goals. Group activities in organizations are impossible without proper communication because coordination and change cannot be affected.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-74.webp"}, {"n": 75, "text": "COMMUNICATION Communication is essential for the INTERNAL FUNCTIONING of the organization, because it integrates the managerial functions of planning, organizing, staffing, leading, and controlling. Communication also relates an enterprise to its EXTERNAL ENVIRONMENT. It is through communication that managers become aware of the needs of the customers, the availability of suppliers, the claims of stockholders, the regulations of the government and the concerns of the community", "image": "assets/pages/m5-75.webp"}, {"n": 76, "text": "", "image": "assets/pages/m5-76.webp"}, {"n": 77, "text": "COMMUNICATION PROCESS FEEDBACK NOISE TRANSMISSION OF MESSAGE THOUGHT ENCODING RECEPTION DECODING UNDERSTANDING SENDER RECEIVER The communication process involves the sender, the transmission of messages through a selected channel, and the receiver. Communication begins with the sender, who has a thought or an idea that is then encoded in a way that can be understood by both the sender and the receiver. The information is transmitted over a channel that links the sender with the receiver. The message may be oral or written, and it may be transmitted through a memorandum, a computer, the telephone, a telegram, or television. TRANSMISSION OF MESSAGE The receiver decodes the information received. The degree to which the receiver decodes the message depends on his knowledge of the subject matter, experience, trust and relationship with the sender. An effective communication occurs only if the receiver understands the message in exactly the same way as it was intended by the sender. The Feedback is the final step of the process that ensures the receiver has received the message and interpreted it correctly as it was intended by the sender. The response of the receiver can be verbal or non-verbal. The act of communication can be derailed by the following types of noise, which deflect your audience’s focus away from your message: Physical noise | Physiological noise | Technical noise | Organizational noise Cultural noise | Psychological noise | Semantic noise (language, words) COMMUNICATION PROCESS", "image": "assets/pages/m5-77.webp"}, {"n": 78, "text": "NINE TYPES OF COMMUNICATION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-78.webp"}, {"n": 79, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Formal communication is an officially created procedure for the flow of communication between the various positions in the organizational set-up. Memos Intranet Meetings Conferences Formal One-on-Ones Bulletin Boards Handouts Letters Presentations Speeches Notice Boards Organizational blogs Emails from managers and leaders", "image": "assets/pages/m5-79.webp"}, {"n": 80, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Informal communication is one that is outside the formed, recognized communication system, such as conversations among workers and the grapevine (gossip). Single Strand – a form of informal communication wherein each person communicates with the next in a single sequence. Cluster - a very common form of informal communication, in cluster networks a person will receive information and choose to pass it on to their cluster network or keep the information to themselves. Each individual will pass on the information to the next cluster network Probability Chain – each individual randomly tells another individual the same piece of information.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-80.webp"}, {"n": 81, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Downward communication flows from people at higher levels to those at lower levels in the organizational hierarchy. • This kind of communication exists especially in organizations with an authoritarian atmosphere. • The kinds of media used in downward oral communication include instructions, speeches, meetings, telephone, loudspeakers etc. • Written downward communication includes letters, memoranda, pamphlets, policy statements, procedures etc. • The downward flow of communication through the different levels of the organization is time-consuming. • Information is often lost/distorted as it comes down the chain of command.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-81.webp"}, {"n": 82, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Upward communication travels from subordinates to superiors and continues up the organizational hierarchy. • Unfortunately, this flow is often hindered by managers in the communication chain that filters the messages and does not transmit all the information, especially unfavorable news to their bosses. • Upper management needs to know specifically about production performance, marketing information, financial data, what lower-level employees are thinking etc. • Upward communication is primarily non-directive and is usually found in participative/demographic organizational environments. • Effective upward communication requires an environment in which subordinates feel free to communicate", "image": "assets/pages/m5-82.webp"}, {"n": 83, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Crosswise communication or Horizontal communication refers to communication between individuals who are at the same or similar levels within an organization but have different areas of responsibility. Horizontal communication is slightly more fluid and dependent on cross-individual communication. Typical examples exist as communication between managers of different departments (HR, Marketing, Sales, etc.).", "image": "assets/pages/m5-83.webp"}, {"n": 84, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Diagonal flow among people at different levels who have no direct reporting relationships. This kind of information flow is used to speed up the process of communication, to improve understanding and to coordinate efforts for the achievement of organizational objectives. Communication between a floor manager and a Sales team is a prime example of diagonal communication.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-84.webp"}, {"n": 85, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Written communication has the advantage of providing records, references, and legal defense. The message can be carefully planned, prepared, and then directed to a large audience through mass mailing. It can promote uniformity in policy, and procedure. The disadvantages are that written messages may create mountains of paper, maybe poorly expressed by ineffective writers, and may not provide immediate feedback.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-85.webp"}, {"n": 86, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Oral communication can occur in a face-to-face meeting of two people or in a manager’s presentation to a large audience, which can be formal or informal, or it can be planned or accidental. The advantage being it makes possible speedy interchange with immediate feedback. People can ask questions and clarify points.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-86.webp"}, {"n": 87, "text": "TYPES OF COMMUNICATION Non-verbal communication – what a person says can be reinforced (or contradicted) by non- verbal communication, such as facial expressions, and body gestures. Non-verbal communication is expected to support verbal, but it does not always do so.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-87.webp"}, {"n": 88, "text": "COMMUNICATION Lack of planning Un-clarified assumptions Semantic distortion - can be deliberate or accidental. “We sell for less” is deliberate (less than what?) Poorly expressed messages. Loss by transmission Poor retention Poor listening and beforehand evaluation Distrust, threat, and fear Insufficient period for adjustment to change Information overload BARRIERS TO EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION", "image": "assets/pages/m5-88.webp"}, {"n": 89, "text": "Other communication barriers • Selective perception • Attitude influence • Differences in status and power • A large number of levels in the organizations", "image": "assets/pages/m5-89.webp"}, {"n": 90, "text": "TOWARDS EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION Following are the guidelines for effective communication / improving communication: • Senders of messages must clarify in their minds what they want to communicate. • Encoding and decoding be done with symbols that are familiar to the sender and receiver of the message. • Planning for communication should not be done in a vacuum. • It is very important to consider the needs of the receivers of the information. • In communication tone of voice, choice of language, and the congruency between what is said and how it is said to influence the reactions of the receiver of the message. • Communication is complete only when the message is understood by the receiver of the information. • The function of communication is more than transmitting information. • It also deals with emotions that are important in interpersonal relationships between superiors & subordinates.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-90.webp"}, {"n": 91, "text": "Effective communication is the responsibility of both the sender and the receiver of the information. “Listening is the key to understanding.” Listening is a skill that can be developed by following the guidelines listed below: • Stop talking • Put the talker at ease • Show the talker that you want to listen • Remove distraction • Empathize with the talker • Be patient • Hold your temper • Go easy with arguments • Ask questions", "image": "assets/pages/m5-91.webp"}, {"n": 92, "text": "guidelines to improve written communication Following are the guidelines to improve written communication: • Use simple words and phrases • Use short and familiar words • Use personal pronouns (Such as I, You etc) whenever applicable. • Give illustrations and examples. • Use short sentences and paragraphs • Use active verbs (Such as the manager plans) • Avoid unnecessary words and sentences.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-92.webp"}, {"n": 93, "text": "as a function of management CONTROLLING", "image": "assets/pages/m5-93.webp"}, {"n": 94, "text": "CONTROLLING 94 Controlling is verifying whether everything occurs in conformity with the plan adopted, the instruction issued and principles established. It has for object to point out weaknesses and errors in order to rectify them and prevent recurrence. It appears on everything, THINGS, PEOPLE, and ACTIONS.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-94.webp"}, {"n": 95, "text": "CONTROLLING 95 The managerial function of controlling is the measurement & correction of performance in order to make sure that enterprise objectives and the plans devised to attain them are closely related. Planning and controlling are closely related. Controlling is the function of every manager from president to supervisor in an organization.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-95.webp"}, {"n": 96, "text": "ESTABLISHING STANDARDS 96 Plans are the yardsticks against which managers device controls. Hence controls require plans. Since plans vary in detail & complex and since managers can not watch everything, special standards are established. STANDARDS ARE SIMPLY… CRITERIA OF PERFORMANCE. They are the selected points in entire planning program at which measures of performance are made so that managers can receive signals about how things are going & thus do not have to watch every step in the execution of plans.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-96.webp"}, {"n": 97, "text": "ESTABLISHING STANDARDS 97 For the purpose of CONTROL, a standard is defined as a unit of measurement that can serve as a REFERENCE POINT FOR EVALUATING RESULTS. Thus, in a broad sense, goals, objectives, quotas, and performance targets will also serve as ‘standard’ in the control process. Some specific standards are – SALES QUOTAS, BUDGETS, JOB DEADLINES, MARKET SHARE and PROFIT MARGINS.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-97.webp"}, {"n": 98, "text": "98 Establishment of STANDARDS Actual PERFORMANCE MEASURE actual performance COMPARE Actual performance vs. Standard IMPLEMENTATION of corrections Program of CORRECTIVE ACTIONS Analysis of CAUSES OF DEVIATIONS Identification of DEVIATIONS 8 STEPS IN CONTROLLING", "image": "assets/pages/m5-98.webp"}, {"n": 99, "text": "ESTABLISHING STANDARDS 99 PHYSICAL STANDARDS TECHNICAL STANDARDS MONETARY STANDARDS MANAGERIAL STANDARDS TIME STANDARDS", "image": "assets/pages/m5-99.webp"}, {"n": 100, "text": "100 3 TYPES OF CONTROL", "image": "assets/pages/m5-100.webp"}, {"n": 101, "text": "101 FEED FORWARD CONTROLS Control that allows managers to anticipate problems before they arise CONCURRENT CONTROLS Give managers immediate feedback on how efficiently inputs are being transformed into outputs FEEDBACK CONTROLS Control that gives managers information about customers’ reactions to goods and services 3 TYPES OF CONTROL", "image": "assets/pages/m5-101.webp"}, {"n": 102, "text": "SELF STUDY MATERIAL Included for end semester exam", "image": "assets/pages/m5-102.webp"}, {"n": 103, "text": "103 Classified under three basic categories: 1. BUDGETARY CONTROLS 2. NON-BUDGETARY CONTROLS 3. NETWORK TECHNIQUES MANAGEMENT CONTROL TECHNIQUES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-103.webp"}, {"n": 104, "text": "104 A process of finding out what is being done and comparing the actual results with the budgeted. This is done in order to approve accomplishments or to remedy differences by adjustment or correction. Types: Fixed budget, Flexible budget, Master budget, Sales budget, Production budget, Materials budget, Labor budget, Capital expenditure budget. 1. BUDGETARY CONTROLS", "image": "assets/pages/m5-104.webp"}, {"n": 105, "text": "105 BUDGET is a single-use plan that details the resources – usually financial, that will be required to complete a program, project, or other organizational activity. The report can be viewed by the manager and can take required action. BUDGET: DEFINITION & CONCEPT", "image": "assets/pages/m5-105.webp"}, {"n": 106, "text": "106 BUDGET: TYPES Financial Budget Cash flow, or cash budget Capital expenditure budget Balance sheet budget Operating Budget Sales or revenue budget Expense budget Profit budget Non-monetary Budget Labor budget Space budget Production budget i ii iii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-106.webp"}, {"n": 107, "text": "107 Financial Budget Sources and uses of cash Cash flow, or cash budget All sources of cash income or expenditure in monthly, weekly, or daily periods Capital expenditure budget Costs of major assets such as plant, machinery, or land Balance sheet budget Forecast of the organization’s assets and liabilities in the event all other budgets are met A FINANCIAL BUDGET indicates the organization expects to get its cash for the common time period and how it plans to use it. BUDGET: TYPES i", "image": "assets/pages/m5-107.webp"}, {"n": 108, "text": "108 Operating Budget Planned operations in financial matters Sales or revenue budget Income the organization expects to receive from normal operations Expense budget Anticipated expenses for the organization during the coming time period Profit budget Anticipated differences between the sales or revenues and expenses AN OPERATING BUDGET is concerned with planned operations within the organization. BUDGET: TYPES ii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-108.webp"}, {"n": 109, "text": "109 Non-monetary Budget Planned operations in non financial terms Labor budget Hours of direct labor available for use Space budget Square feet of space available for various functions Production budget Number of units to be produced during the coming time period A NON-MONETARY BUDGET is a budget expressed in non financial terms, such as units of outputs, hours of direct labor, machine hours or square foot allocations. BUDGET: TYPES iii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-109.webp"}, {"n": 110, "text": "▪OVER-BUDGETING – There is a danger of over-budgeting through spelling out minor expenses in detail and depriving managers of needed freedom in managing their departments. ▪OVER-RIDING ENTERPRISE OBJECTIVES/GOALS – Another danger lies in allowing budgetary goals to become more important than enterprise goals. In this zest to keep within budget limits, managers may forget that they owe primarily to enterprise objectives. ▪HIDING INEFFICIENCY – it may be used to hide inefficiencies of managers. ▪CAUSING INFLEXIBILITY – it is one of the greatest dangers in budgets. flexibility in decisions will be lost due to budgets and decision will become rigid. 110 DANGERS IN BUDGETING", "image": "assets/pages/m5-110.webp"}, {"n": 111, "text": "111 VARIABLE BUDGET Dangers arise from inflexibility in budgets because maximum flexibility consistent with efficiency underlies good planning. Attention has been increasingly given to VARIABLE / FLEXIBLE BUDGETS BUDGET: TYPES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-111.webp"}, {"n": 112, "text": "112 VARIABLE BUDGET These budgets are designed to vary usually on as the volume of sales / some other measure of output varies. Some costs do not vary with volume of output, particularly in the short period of time. Costs that vary with volume of output range from those that are completely variable to those that are only slightly variable. BUDGET: TYPES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-112.webp"}, {"n": 113, "text": "VARIABLE BUDGET A fixed budget will work as well with good plans and sales forecasts, a variable budget forces study of & preoccupation with, factors which translate workload into labour and expense need. A typical variable budget is shown. BUDGET: TYPES Item of Expense Monthly Sales Volume (in 000s) 20 40 60 80 100 Material Labour Over Heads X Y Z 5X 7Y 3Z Cost of Production A 2A Engineering Research & Development Sales & Distribution B C D 2B 2C 4D Total Cost ` ``` Profit % Profit to Sales", "image": "assets/pages/m5-113.webp"}, {"n": 114, "text": "114 ZERO BASED BUDGETING Another type of budgeting, the purpose of which has much in common with the purpose of a well operated system of variable budgeting, is ZERO BASE BUDGETING. The idea behind this technique is to divide enterprise programs into “packages” composed of goals, activities & needed resources & then to calculate costs for each package from the ground up. By starting the budget of each package from base zero, costs are calculated afresh for each budget period, thus avoiding the common tendency in budgeting of looking only at changes from the previous period. BUDGET: TYPES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-114.webp"}, {"n": 115, "text": "115 There are many traditional control strategies that are not connected with budgets. i Statistical data ii Special reports and analysis iii Operational audit iv Personal observation 2. NON-BUDGETARY CONTROL DEVICES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-115.webp"}, {"n": 116, "text": "116 i STATISTICAL DATA 2. NON-BUDGETARY CONTROL DEVICES Since no manager can do anything to history, it is essential that statistical reports show trends so that the viewer can extrapolate where things are going. Hence, most data when presented on charts, should be made available as averages to rule out variations due to accounting periods, seasonal factors, and the periodic variations. Most managers understand statistical data the best when the data is presented in chart or graphic form, since trends and relationships are then easier to see. Moreover if data are to be meaningful, even when presented on charts, they should be formulated in such a way that comparisons to some standard can be made.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-116.webp"}, {"n": 117, "text": "117 ii SPECIAL REPORTS AND ANALYSIS 2. NON-BUDGETARY CONTROL DEVICES In control, special reports & analysis help in particular problem areas. Routine accounting & statistical reports furnish a good share of necessary information, there are often areas in which they are inadequate. It may be that some of the funds spent for elaborate information programs could be more profitably spent for special analysis.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-117.webp"}, {"n": 118, "text": "118 iii OPERATIONAL AUDIT 2. NON-BUDGETARY CONTROL DEVICES It is the regular and independent appraisal by a staff of internal auditors, of the accounting, financial & other operations of an enterprise. Although often limited to the auditing of accounts, in its most useful form operational auditing includes appraisal of operations in general, weighing actual results against planned ones. iv PERSONAL OBSERVATION It is also called as “Managing by Walking Around” by some organizations.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-118.webp"}, {"n": 119, "text": "119 PERT – Program Evaluation & Review Technique. CPM – Critical Path Method. Both are meant for planning and control of projects. 3. TIME-EVENT NETWORK ANALYSIS CPM is a deterministic model i.e. it does not take into account the uncertainties involved in the estimation of time for execution of a job or an activity. PERT is a probabilistic model i.e. It takes into account uncertainties involved in the estimation of time of a job or an activity. It uses three estimates of the activity time – optimistic, pessimistic and most likely.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-119.webp"}, {"n": 120, "text": "120 GANTT Charts – show the time relationships between “events” of a production system. 3. TIME-EVENT NETWORK ANALYSIS Gantt chart, commonly used in project management, is one of the most popular and useful ways of showing activities (tasks or events) displayed against time. On the left of the chart is a list of the activities and along the top is a suitable time scale. Each activity is represented by a bar; the position and length of the bar reflects the start date, duration and end date of the activity. This allows you to see at a glance:", "image": "assets/pages/m5-120.webp"}, {"n": 121, "text": "121 Such controls apply to a PART OF ENTERPRISE and do not measure TOTAL ACCOMPLISHMENTS against total odds. OVERALL CONTROL Controls are designed for specific things: policies, employee selection, training, etc. where we wish the performance to conform the plans.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-121.webp"}, {"n": 122, "text": "122 There are three major reasons for control of overall performance. OVERALL CONTROL just as overall planning must apply to enterprise or major division goals, so must overall controls be applied. 1 decentralization of authority – especially in product or territorial division creates semi-independent units, and these must be subjected to overall controls to avoid the chaos of complete independence. 2 OVERALL CONTROLS ARE GENERALLY FINANCIAL IN NATURE. overall controls permit measure of integrated area manager’s total effort, rather than parts of it. 3", "image": "assets/pages/m5-122.webp"}, {"n": 123, "text": "123 I BUDGET SUMMARIES & REPORTS II PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL III RETURN ON INVESTMENT OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES", "image": "assets/pages/m5-123.webp"}, {"n": 124, "text": "124 BUDGET SUMMARIES & REPORTS A budget summary is a resume of all the individual budgets, reflects company plans so that sales volume, costs, profits, utilization of capital, and return on investment may be seen in a proper relationship. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES i", "image": "assets/pages/m5-124.webp"}, {"n": 125, "text": "125 BUDGET SUMMARIES & REPORTS In these terms it shows, top management how well the company as a whole is succeeding in meeting its objectives. For the best control through budget summary, a manager must first be satisfied that total budgets are an accurate and reasonably complete portrayal of the company’s plans. Minor discrepancies should receive appropriately little attention. The purpose of a control system is to draw attention to important variations, and both the budget reports & the attention paid to them should reflect this. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES i", "image": "assets/pages/m5-125.webp"}, {"n": 126, "text": "126 BUDGET SUMMARIES & REPORTS Managers should not under-estimate the value of the budget summaries in providing an effective means for overall control in situations of decentralized authority. Budget summaries furnish a means whereby enterprise objectives can be clearly & specifically defined & departmental plans can be made to contribute toward such objectives. The summaries hence, furnish a useful guide for Corrective action. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES i", "image": "assets/pages/m5-126.webp"}, {"n": 127, "text": "127 PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL Since the survival of a business usually depends upon profits & since profits are a definite standard against which to measure success, many company’s use the income statement for divisional & departmental control. Since income statement is a statement of all revenues and expenditures for a given time, it is a true summary of the results of business operations. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES ii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-127.webp"}, {"n": 128, "text": "128 PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL P & L control, when applied to divisions/departments, is based on the premise that if it is the purpose of the entire business to make a profit, each part of the enterprise should contribute to this purpose. Hence, ability of a part to make an expected profit becomes standard for measuring its performance. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES ii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-128.webp"}, {"n": 129, "text": "129 PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL In P & L control, each major department/division details its revenue & expenses – normally including a proportionate share of company overhead – and calculates periodically its profit/loss. Some units have their own accounting groups , in others, the statement is prepared by the central accounting department. P & L control usually is practicable only for major segments of a company, since the paperwork involved in building up P & L statements for smaller departments tend to be too heavy. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES ii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-129.webp"}, {"n": 130, "text": "130 PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL Also P & L control usually implies that the managers of a division/department have fairly wide authority to run their part of the business as they see fit, with profit the primary standard of success. The more integrated & complete the organizational unit, the more accurate a measuring stick of P & L control can be. Hence it works best in product & territorial divisions. Companies organized on a functional basis do occasionally employ P & L control. In most instances, P & L control is not applied to central staff & service departments. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES ii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-130.webp"}, {"n": 131, "text": "131 PROFIT AND LOSS CONTROL : LIMITATIONS High cost of accounting & paper transactions involving intra-company transfer of costs & revenues. If it is carried very far in the organization, is that departments may come to compete with an aggressive detachment not helpful to enterprise coordination. P&L control may be inadequate for overall performance. Duplication of accounting records in several places. 1 2 3 4", "image": "assets/pages/m5-131.webp"}, {"n": 132, "text": "132 RETURN ON INVESTMENT ROI technique is used to measure both absolute and relative success of a company or a company unit by determining the ratio of earnings to investment of capital. This tool regards profit not as a absolute but as a return (generally %) on capital employed in the business. The goal of a business is seen, accordingly not necessarily as optimizing profits but as optimizing returns from capital devoted to business purposes. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES iii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-132.webp"}, {"n": 133, "text": "133 RETURN ON INVESTMENT The ROI control is best summarized in the chart form shown in the next slide. Hence analysis of variations in rate of return leads to every financial aspect of the business. In any control through ROI, the number of ratios and comparisons behind the yardstick figure can not be overlooked. Although, improvement of rate of return can come from higher % of profit to sales, improvement may also come from increasing the rate of turnover by lower price & reducing return on sales. OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES iii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-133.webp"}, {"n": 134, "text": "134 RETURN ON INVESTMENT : FACTORS INFLUENCING ROI CONTROL OVERALL CONTROL DEVICES iii", "image": "assets/pages/m5-134.webp"}, {"n": 135, "text": "RETURN ON INVESTMENT : ADVANTAGES If the ROI control is complete and shows all the factors affecting the return, then it enables managers to locate the weaknesses. Ex: If inventories are rising, the rate of return will be affected. Like P & L control, it focuses managerial attention on the central/core objective of the business – making the best possible profit on the capital available. It is very effective where the authority is decentralized. It is not only an absolute guide to capital efficiency, but also offers possibility of comparing efficiency in the use of capital within the company and with other enterprises also. 1 2 3", "image": "assets/pages/m5-135.webp"}, {"n": 136, "text": "136 RETURN ON INVESTMENT : LIMITATIONS This method of control is not foolproof. Major difficulties involve availability of information on sales, costs and assets & proper allocation of investments and return for commonly sold or produced items. Greater danger in ROI control, is that it can lead to excessive pre-occupation with financial factors within the firm or the industry. Undue attention on ratios and financial factors may lead to overlooking environmental factors such as social and technical developments. Over emphasis on the rate of return may lead to undesirable inflexibility (or rigidity) in investing capital for new ventures. What constituents a reasonable return? Comparisons of rate of return are hardly enough, because they do not tell the top managers, what the rate of return should be? 1 2 3 4", "image": "assets/pages/m5-136.webp"}, {"n": 137, "text": "Direct Control & Preventive Control", "image": "assets/pages/m5-137.webp"}, {"n": 138, "text": "138 Future actions can be taken broadly in 2 different ways. Direct Control and Preventive Control One way is… to trace the cause of an unsatisfactory result back to the persons responsible for it & get them to correct their practices & ways of doing. This is referred to as DIRECT CONTROL. The other way is… to develop better managers who will skillfully apply concepts, techniques, principles etc. and who will look at managing from a system point of view, thus eliminating considerable results caused by poor management. This is referred to as PREVENTIVE CONTROL.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-138.webp"}, {"n": 139, "text": "139 In every enterprise, hundreds and even thousands of standards are built to compare the actual performance, in terms of quantity, quality, time and cost – with plans. NEGATIVE DEVIATION INDICATES – in terms of goal achievement, cost, price, personnel, labour hours, machine hours etc. – that performance is less than good/ normal/ standard and the results are not confirming to plans & require control. This is the case of DIRECT CONTROL. DIRECT CONTROL", "image": "assets/pages/m5-139.webp"}, {"n": 140, "text": "140 7 common causes of NEGATIVE DEVIATIONS Assumption that the person responsible will take corrective action suitably at right time. Uncertainty Lack of knowledge, experience or judgment. Assumption that performance can be measured. Assumption that personal responsibility exists. Assumption that time expenditure is warranted. Assumption that mistakes can be discovered in time. 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 in terms of goal achievement, cost, price, personnel, labour hours, machine hours etc.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-140.webp"}, {"n": 141, "text": "141 Principle of PREVENTIVE CONTROL states that: For adapting preventive control in organizations, they must have wider understanding of managerial principles, functions, techniques and managerial philosophy. PREVENTIVE CONTROL “the higher the quality of managers and their subordinates, the less will be the need for direct control”", "image": "assets/pages/m5-141.webp"}, {"n": 142, "text": "142 The desirability of PREVENTIVE CONTROL rests upon 3 assumptions : PREVENTIVE CONTROL 123 Assumption that qualified, experienced managers make a minimum errors. Assumption that management fundamentals can be used to measure performance. Assumption that the application of management fundamentals can be evaluated.", "image": "assets/pages/m5-142.webp"}, {"n": 143, "text": "143 ADVANTAGES PREVENTIVE CONTROL PSYCHOLOGICAL ADVANTAGE Subordinate managers know what is expected of them, understand the nature of managing and feel a close relationship between performance and measurement. HIGHER ACCURACY is achieved in assigning personal responsibility. ENCOURAGES CONTROL BY SELF CONTROL. Knowing that errors will be uncovered in an evaluation; managers will themselves try to determine their responsibility correctly & make corrections voluntarily. LIGHTENS THE MANAGERIAL BURDEN caused by direct control. Preventing problems from occurring often requires less effort than correcting them after deviations been detected. 1 2 3 4 of", "image": "assets/pages/m5-143.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq1", "file": "pyq/1. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - REGULAR NOV 2024.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) 1B) 1C) 2A) 2B) 2C) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 21-Nov-2024 (02:30 PM - 05:30 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION FIFTH SEMESTER B.TECH. (COMPUTER SCEINCE & ENGINEERING/ INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) EXAMINATIONS - NOVEMBER 2024 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3122/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM 3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. Different managerial activities are listed in the table below. With reference to Mintzberg's managerial roles, mention the role category and specific role for the following activities. (4) Compare and contrast the managerial practices in Japan and the USA, focusing on cultural influences and organizational behavior. (3) Five years ago, Mr. Rahul Modi completed his degree in food processing. He worked for one year in a company manufacturing noodles, masala powders, and pasta. He was not happy in the company and decided to have his own organic food processing unit for the same. He set the objectives and the targets and formulated an action plan to achieve the same. One of his objectives was to earn a 10% profit on the amount invested in the first year. It was also decided that raw materials like fruits, vegetables, spices, etc. will be purchased on three months' credit from farmers cultivating organic crops only. He also decided to follow the steps required for marketing the products through his own outlets. He appointed Ms. Mamta Patel as a production manager who decides the exact manner in which the production activities are to be carried out. Ms. Mamta Patel also prepared a statement showing the number of workers that will be required in the factory throughout the year. Mr. Rahul Modi informed Ms. Mamta Patel about his sales target for different products, area-wise for the forthcoming quarter. While working on the production table a penalty of 100 rupees per day for not wearing the caps, gloves, and apron was announced. Question to be answered: Quoting lines from the above paragraph, outline any four types of plans discussed, and briefly explain them. (3) As the leader of the organization, you are inevitably responsible for the direction and development of your organization. With this regard, it is important to consistently design a strategic plan for your operations. Proficiency in several tools and methodologies is necessary to accomplish this task. Using appropriate representation and examples, discuss any two strategic tools that you will implement to facilitate the development of your firm. (4) Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in organizing with a suitable representation. (3) Propose a scheme of departmentation for a MNC with a field network of sales all over the country. Discuss its merits and limitations. (3) Page 1 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq1-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "3A) 3B) 3C) 4A) 4B) 4C) 5A) 5B) 5C) A new team leader in the customer service department is training an employee to handle customer escalations independently. Following the Flow of Delegation model, analyze how the team leader would apply each step in training the employee on handling escalated calls. (3) Analyze the differences between Human Resource Management (HRM) and Human Resource Development (HRD). How do these functions complement each other in achieving long-term organizational success? (3) Analyze McGregor's THEORY X AND THEORY Y motivational theory that guides the Manager to develop strategies to motivate his/her employees toward organizational goals. Give corporate examples for the same. (4) Evaluate three types of recruitment methods commonly used by modern businesses. For each method, provide relevant examples and assess how it aligns with organizational goals and talent needs. (3) Identify and examine three current trends in Training & Development. For each trend, analyze its potential impact on employee performance and organizational growth, providing examples from recent industry practices. (3) TechNova Corporation is a rapidly growing software development company that has expanded into various sectors, including mobile applications, cloud services, and cybersecurity. The company has two main departments: Product Development and Technical Support. The Product Development team consists of creative software engineers and designers who work collaboratively on innovative projects. Recently, the team has expressed frustration with strict guidelines and constant oversight from their manager. They believe their creativity is stifled, and they could produce better results if given more freedom to explore their ideas. The team flourishes on brainstorming sessions and values input from all members. The Technical Support department handles customer inquiries and troubleshooting issues. The team works in a fast-paced environment, where quick responses are essential to maintaining customer satisfaction. The manager has been directive and emphasizes adherence to scripts and procedures, ensuring all team members follow the same protocol for handling customer issues. Based on the scenarios presented for each department, identify which leadership style would be most suitable for each department and justify your reasoning. Consider the unique needs and dynamics of the teams when providing your analysis (4) How can 'systems approach to staffing' help the HR managers' activities of a manufacturing plant? Provide a labeled sketch to illustrate its main components. (3) In a technology organization, various barriers can hinder effective communication among team members. Identify and discuss three common barriers to effective communication in the workplace and suggest ways to address them. (3) TechGear is a mid-sized manufacturer specializing in high-quality consumer electronics, including smartwatches and fitness trackers. The company has recently advertised the launch of a new smartwatch model that includes advanced health monitoring features. The market response has been overwhelmingly positive, leading to a significant increase in orders. The supply chain manager proactively orders additional components and materials needed for production. The human resources team hires 25 temporary workers and schedules 3 hours of overtime for existing employees to ramp up production capacity. As the launch date of the new smartwatch approaches, the marketing team at TechGear conducts extensive market research. They project that the demand will exceed the company's planned production capacity (2000 units) due to high pre-orders and promotional activities. After launching the smartwatch, 850 customers provided feedback through surveys and online reviews. While most feedback is positive, some customers report issues with battery life and software glitches, which affected the company's goal of 100 % customer satisfaction. The product development team analyzed customer feedback and identified that a software update is needed to fix the glitches, and there is a need to enhance battery optimization in the next production run starting in the 1st week of March. The engineering team develops a software patch and begins working on hardware changes for the next iteration of the smartwatch. Questions to be answered: a) Identify and explain the TWO types of control being practiced by quoting the lines. b) Identify and explain TWO critical point standards by quoting the line. (4) -----End----- Page 2 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq1-2.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq2", "file": "pyq/2. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - MAKEUP DEC 2024.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) 1B) 1C) 2A) 2B) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 27-Dec-2024 (02:30 PM - 05:30 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION FIFTH SEMESTER B.Tech. (COMPUTER SCEINCE & ENGINEERING/ INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) EXAMINATIONS - DECEMBER 2024 / JANUARY 2025 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3122/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (MAKEUP) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM 3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. Identify and explain the managerial functions performed by a newly joined CEO of State Bank of India. Explain which of the three managerial skills is the top priority for this position. (4) \"A business firm's intentions, beyond its legal and economic obligations\". What does this intention mean and what are the drivers that ensure these intentions being fulfilled? (3) \"The plans made by the top-level manager will differ from the ones that lower managers make.\" Elaborate by giving suitable examples for six types of plans. (3) Illustrate how Porter's Generic Strategy is incorporated in the cases below. Case#1: Maruti Suzuki, India's leading car manufacturer adopts lean manufacturing and co-locates suppliers near its plants to minimize logistics expenses. Its strategic focus on cost-effective R&D and an extensive distribution network ensures affordable pricing without compromising quality. This approach has secured Maruti Suzuki over 40% of the market share, sustained profitability, and strong customer loyalty in a highly price-sensitive market. Case#2: Patanjali Ayurved targets the niche market of health-conscious consumers seeking natural and Ayurvedic products. By tightly controlling its supply chain and distribution, the company kept prices competitive while ensuring wide availability through exclusive stores and partnerships. (4) EcoEarth, a growing startup in sustainable packaging, recently expanded its operations to cater to the increasing demand for eco-friendly products. To manage this growth, EcoEarth's founders, Maya and Arjun, recognized the need to refine the organization's structure. They started by analyzing the core functions their team handles daily, from sourcing materials to customer support. They observed that while the company had strong values and a mission, many tasks overlapped, causing confusion and delays. Maya and Arjun decided to bring in an experienced consultant, Priya, to help clarify and streamline the workflow. Priya suggested dividing tasks based on expertise and reorganizing the team's workflow for better coordination. Following her advice, they formed dedicated teams for research and development, marketing, customer service, and logistics, each with its own supervisor. These supervisors would have the autonomy to make decisions within their departments but would coordinate major decisions with Maya and Arjun. As the company grew, Maya and Arjun realized that, although they had distributed responsibilities, there was a need to formalize decision-making processes. They worked with Priya to develop a hierarchy that balanced authority and responsibility across teams. This also meant setting clear boundaries so that every team understood its role and whom to report to, creating accountability and smoother communication across departments. Identify and explain the steps taken by EcoEarth in organizing their operations, following the sequence of organizing activities. For each identified step, cite specific lines from the case to support your explanation. (3) Page 1 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq2-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "2C) 3A) 3B) 3C) 4A) 4B) 4C) 5A) 5B) 5C) In an organization that serves diverse customer groups, explain how customer departmentalization can be used to tailor services and improve responsiveness. Provide an organizational structure that illustrates customer departmentalization and discuss its advantages and potential challenges. In an organization that serves diverse customer groups, explain how customer departmentalization can be used to tailor services and improve responsiveness. Provide an organizational structure that illustrates customer departmentalization and discuss its advantages and potential challenges. (3) Draw an organizational chart for a company of your choice, showing both line and staff functions. Then, describe how each line and staff function contribute to the company's goals. Provide examples of situations where line and staff departments would need to collaborate to solve a specific organizational problem. (3) You are an executive at the management level of a reputable firm. You are assigned with a critical task to : \"Prepare a comprehensive job description and job specification for a Graduate Engineer Trainee for the position in an R&D department\". (3) Innovatech Solutions, a growing tech company, is experiencing productivity and engagement challenges across its departments. The CEO, M J Nair, wants to improve motivation but is unsure how to approach two very different teams: Team#1: Innovation Team: This group of developers, designers, and project managers tackles complex, creative projects. They perform well with flexibility and minimal supervision but have recently shown frustration with unclear project goals and a lack of growth opportunities, leading to scattered productivity. Team#2: Support Team: This team handles structured, repetitive tasks like data processing and customer support. They've shown inconsistent performance and low engagement, often expressing dissatisfaction with the routine nature of their work. Question to be answered: Given the situation, advise the CEO on which Motivational theory he needs to apply to his two teams to meet the organizational goals and why. Justify your answer. (4) Describe the 10-step recruitment process for finding the right candidate for a job. (3) As your organization's human resource manager, you are responsible for your employees' training and development. Hence, discuss the processes of training and development. Also, shed light on current trends in training and development through suitable examples and/or representations. (3) Explain, with a neat sketch, Likert's Four Systems of Managing and examine the effect of each system on employee motivation and productivity. (4) Using a clear diagram, describe the Systems Approach to Staffing and explain its significance in the recruitment/selection process. (3) Summarize the different stages of group development. What distinguishes a committee, group, and team from one another? (3) With the help of a block diagram, list the 8 steps involved in the controlling process and discuss the steps with the help of a common example. (4) -----End----- Page 2 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq2-2.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq3", "file": "pyq/3. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - REGULAR MAY 2025.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) 1B) 1C) 2A) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 05-May-2025 (02:30 PM - 05:30 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION SIXTH SEMESTER B.TECH. (ELECTRONICS AND COMMUNICATION ENGINEERING) DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - APRIL / MAY 2025 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. In a leading software firm in Udupi, employees are assigned roles based on their expertise, ensuring that developers, designers, and analysts focus on specialized tasks. This structured approach has led to faster project turnaround and improved software quality. The company maintains a professional work environment where expectations around deadlines and performance are clearly communicated and upheld. While high standards are enforced, leadership values long- term employee retention, offering career development programs and mentorship to reduce turnover. The firm also fosters a culture where employees are encouraged to take ownership of their ideas- those who propose workflow improvements or innovative product features are given opportunities to lead their implementation. Identify and analyze the Henry Fayol's management principles by citing the relevant lines from the case study to support your explanation. (4) Critically analyze the three fundamental moral theories in normative ethics and their role in fostering ethical decision-making within organizations. Using real-world examples demonstrate how companies can institutionalize ethics through these frameworks. (3) As the Supply Manager of a leading organization, you play a critical role in ensuring efficient inventory management to support the company's operational and financial goals. To optimize supply chain performance in the upcoming financial year, you are required to develop an 8-STEP PLANNING PROCESS for inventory management that enhances efficiency, cost-effectiveness, and resource utilization. (3) Categorize the following Nestlé products into the BCG matrix with a brief explanation of the matrix. Also create strategies for each of these Nestlé products based on the BCG Matrix. Nescafé: Nestlé dominates the instant coffee segment, while its ready-to-drink cold coffee is gaining popularity among young consumers. With the market still growing, continued investment in flavors, packaging, and marketing will sustain its dominance. Nestlé A+ Curd Alternatives: The plant-based dairy market in India is expanding due to health trends and lactose intolerance. Nestlé is still establishing itself in this space, facing competition from brands like Epigamia and Sofit. Milkmaid: While Milkmaid has strong brand nostalgia, its market is shrinking due to changing consumer preferences and declining home baking trends. Nestlé may reduce investments and focus on seasonal and niche demand. (4) Page 1 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq3-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "2B) 2C) 3A) 3B) 3C) 4A) 4B) 4C) 5A) 5B) 5C) Maggi: A market leader in instant noodles and seasonings, Maggi enjoys strong consumer loyalty despite competition from Yippee and Wai Wai. The mature market limits growth, but it remains highly profitable. IDENTIFY the URWICK's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief. (i) Vikram entrusted key project decisions to senior team leaders while retaining overall accountability. By assigning authority with clear responsibilities, he empowered his team to take ownership of tasks, leading to faster decision-making and improved efficiency. (ii) Priya restructured the workforce by assigning tasks based on employees' expertise. This division of labor improved efficiency, as each team member focused on specific functions they were skilled at, reducing errors and increasing productivity. (iii) Rohan ensured that all departments aligned their project goals with the company's vision. By clearly defining organizational priorities, he created a unified direction that helped teams work toward common objectives without conflicts. (3) This kind of organization occurs frequently in construction (e.g., building bridges), in aerospace (e.g., designing and launching satellite weather), in marketing (e.g., an advertising campaign for a major new product), in the installation of an electronic data processing system, or in management consulting firms where experts from different fields work together. IDENTIFY this departmentation structure and explain it with a neat diagram mentioning the pros and cons. (3) Analyze the four features of line organization with the help of a neat block diagram representing a startup in FMCG product. (3) A leading company in the FMCG sector needs to fill a team leader position quickly. Instead of hiring externally, they decide to promote an experienced team member. Discuss why opting for an internal source of recruitment is beneficial in this scenario. Support your answer with relevant reasoning. (3) Analyze the corporate approach to employee motivation which aligns with Herzberg's Two Factor Theory. Identify specific corporate strategies that fulfill each factor and evaluate their impact on employee retention and performance. (4) Describe the 10-step recruitment process for finding the right candidate for a job. (3) As your organization's human resource manager, you are responsible for your employees' training and development. Hence, discuss the processes of training and development. Also, shed light on current trends in training and development through suitable examples and/or representations. (3) Explain, with a neat sketch, Likert's Four Systems of Managing and examine the effect of each system on employee motivation and productivity. (4) Using a clear diagram, describe the Systems Approach to Staffing and explain its significance in the recruitment/selection process. (3) Summarize the different stages of group development. What distinguishes a committee, group, and team from one another? (3) With the help of a block diagram, list the 8 steps involved in the controlling process and discuss the steps with the help of a common example. (4) -----End----- Page 2 of 2", "image": "assets/pages/pyq3-2.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq4", "file": "pyq/4. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - MAKEUP JUNE 2025.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 1B) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 1C) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2A) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2B) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Understand\" Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 10-Jun-2025 (02:00 PM - 05:00 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION SIXTH SEMESTER B.TECH. (ELECTRONICS AND COMMUNICATION ENGINEERING) DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - JUNE 2025 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (MAKEUP) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. With reference to an organization, sketch and justify how managerial functions of management and their functions work in the systems approach. (4) Compare and contrast the managerial practices in Japan and the USA, focusing on cultural influences and organizational behaviour. (3) After working as a software developer for a fintech startup, Miss Cleopatra decided to start her own company specializing in cybersecurity solutions for small and medium-sized businesses. She set an initial goal of acquiring 15 clients in the first quarter and achieving a 25% return on investment by the end of the year. Miss Cleopatra chose to hire independent contractors with expertise in cybersecurity and negotiated flexible payment terms based on project completion. She appointed Dr. Banrakas as the project manager to create a workflow, assign tasks, and monitor progress. Cleopatra developed a marketing strategy targeting small business owners through social media and tech conferences, while Banrakas set quality benchmarks for project deliverables. They also introduced a late submission penalty of ₹500/day for team members who missed project deadlines. Question to be answered: Quoting lines from the above paragraph, outline any four types of plans discussed and briefly explain them. (3) As the leader of the organization, you are inevitably responsible for the direction and development of your organization. With this regard, it is important to consistently design a strategic plan for your operations. Proficiency in several tools and methodologies is necessary to accomplish this task. Using appropriate representation and examples, discuss any two strategic tools that you will implement to facilitate the development of your firm. (4) Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in organizing with a suitable representation. (3) Page 1 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq4-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "2C) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3A) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Understand\" 3B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4C) Propose a scheme of departmentation for an MNC with a field network of sales all over the country. Discuss its merits and limitations. (3) Analyze the four features of line and staff organization with the help of a neat block diagram applied to a large multi-specialty hospital. (3) You have been appointed as the HR manager of a company planning to recruit freshers. Draft a Job Description and a Job Specification for the entry-level position in your own respective domains (eg. Mechanical, Aeronautical, Chemical etc) (3) Analyze the corporate approach to employee motivation which aligns with McGregor's Theory X And Theory Y. Identify specific corporate strategies that fulfill each factor and evaluate their impact on employee retention and performance. (4) As a Talent Acquisition Manager in today's competitive job market, you are facing multiple recruitment challenges. Identify three major challenges and explain how you would address each of them to ensure an effective and inclusive hiring process. (3) Differentiate between a Preliminary Interview and a Core Interview in the recruitment process. Explain the purpose of each stage in the recruitment process. (3) PSN Corporation, a leading player in the technology sector, operates across multiple regions, serving diverse markets. As the company was a family business, most of the employees were friends and relatives. A few were old folks too. This made the top management prioritize employee well-being over productivity, fostering a relaxed and friendly work environment. Over time, managers adopted a hands-off approach, avoiding difficult decisions and failing to set clear expectations, leading to a further decline in performance. Employees lacked direction, motivation, and accountability, causing the earlier deteriorating productivity to stagnate state. After running the business with loads of financial burdens, top management finally decided to let go of the non-performing employees and recruit more skilled based personnel. The new managers were very target-oriented, and they focused solely on developing efficient operations, building up production capacities while displaying little concern for employee well-being. This gave them better returns in production but choked creativity and teamwork, leading to dissatisfaction among several team members and leading to higher attrition rates. Top management was not happy to invest in training employees who would leave them in a year or so. So, they had to come up with a better management technique. They decided to demonstrate unwavering dedication to both people and production, fostering a collaborative work environment where individual needs align with organizational goals. This approach results in high-performance teams and innovative solutions. It also was appreciated by the employees with high aspiration as this style gave them opportunities and career development. IDENTIFY and EXPLAIN the 4 leadership styles used by CITING the lines. Shed some light on the pros and cons associated with each identified style. (4) Page 2 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq4-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 5A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5B) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Understand\" 5C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" Differentiate between orientation and induction programs conducted by organizations, highlighting the objective and content. (3) Using well-structured block diagrams, elaborate on the concept of corporate communication and explain its process in a business environment. (3) SmartGadgets Inc., a leading manufacturer of wireless earbuds and smart home devices, learns about an impending global semiconductor shortage. To secure the company's production capacity, the manager negotiates long-term contracts with multiple-chip suppliers across different regions, reducing dependence on a single source. Additionally, they increase the semiconductor inventory by 75%, ensuring buffer stock to sustain production. During production, real-time monitoring detects that one of the assembly lines is producing a higher-than-usual number of defective earbuds. The production manager immediately halts the faulty line, identifies a calibration issue in the semiconductor component attachment machine, and rectifies the problem before its escalation. IDENTIFY and EXPLAIN the TYPE of CONTROL(s) used by SmartGadgets Inc by QUOTING THE LINES from the case. What is the significance of these control types? (4) -----End----- Page 3 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq4-3.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq5", "file": "pyq/5. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - REGULAR NOV 2025.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) 1B) 1C) 2A) 2B) 2C) 3A) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3B) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 19-Nov-2025 (02:00 PM - 05:00 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION FIFTH SEMESTER B.TECH. DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - NOVEMBER 2025 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (SPL: COMPUTER SCIENCE & ENGINEERING / INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM 3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. What are the principles of Henri Fayol? Explain any four of its importance in the system approach of management. (4) What is Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR)? Explain the role of drivers of CSR in detail. (3) The Samsung group is a large Korean conglomerate that focuses on electronics and financial services. In the past, many Korean companies encountered difficulties because they diversified into unrelated fields. In its new approach to management, Samsung tries to avoid the pitfalls of other companies. At the turn of the century, Samsung initiated a policy designed to make the company a leader in its field, competing against firms such as Sony of Japan. The company is driven by its values and its philosophy. Samsung realizes the importance of its people and the use of latest technologies for achieving success in the marketplace. At the same time, it is aware of the responsibility to contribute to society not only in Korea, but also worldwide. Chairman Lee Kun-Hee laid the groundwork for a new approach to management that considers defects a crime. Quality, superior products and excellent customer service are considered key factors for success in the very competitive environment that has been dominated by Japanese firms. With its \"digital management\" approach, Samsung attempts to exploit the opportunities created by the information age technologies. Samsung's values, philosophy and policies as well as its goal of becoming a global leader in its field have attracted many Korean students. Samsung will use the talents of these young people, to successfully compete in the global environment. Analyse this case in terms of Porter's Generic strategies. (3) What are the various types of plans used in an organization? Briefly describe any four of them. (4) Explain the nature and purpose of organizing function in management. Also discuss the steps in organizing with a suitable representation. (3) Propose a scheme of departmentation for an MNC with a field network of sales all over the country. Discuss its merits and limitations. (3) As the founder of a small manufacturing company producing steel bolts, you are considering adopting a Line Organizational structure. Apply the features of a line organization to explain how authority, communication, and decision-making would function in your company to ensure operational efficiency and draw the organizational chart. (3) You are the HR manager at an esteemed Nestlé (Food and beverages) with its production factory in Bengaluru. You need to create a comprehensive job specification on 6 factors for a Graduate Engineer Trainee (GET) reporting to the Production & Maintenance Manager (Technical) at their Bengaluru factory. (3) Page 1 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq5-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 5A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5B) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" Lenskart, India's leading eyewear retailer, wants to understand why some of its store employees report high job satisfaction while others feel disengaged despite similar working conditions. As an HR analyst, analyze 4 factors each for how Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory can help Lenskart, and explain how addressing these factors can improve overall employee morale and productivity. (4) You are the Chief Human Resources Officer (CHRO) for Tata Motors - one of India's largest automotive manufacturers, and your task is to pick 3 each of the best internal sources and external sources for the next recruitment drive. You need to justify your selections to the Board of Directors. (3) You are the founder of a 'Tech Startup' that leverages technology to develop innovative products or services, often aiming for rapid growth and scalability. You would be recruiting new employees shortly and thus researching the Current Trends. Elaborate on 3 current trends in Training & Development that you will be considering for your startup. (3) Quote the lines from the Tata Consultancy Services (TCS) Project Scenarios to identify and explain the different Leadership Styles incorporated based on use of authority. In the planning phase of a new software solution, the same manager conducts brainstorming sessions, encourages team members to share ideas, and collectively decides on the technology stack and workflow. Once the project enters the development stage, experienced developers are given autonomy to choose coding practices and manage their schedules, with minimal supervision, trusting their expertise. During a critical client deadline, the project manager takes full control, assigns tasks without discussion, and enforces strict timelines to ensure timely delivery and facilitate quick decisions without team input. In the testing phase, senior QA engineers are given full freedom to choose tools, create test cases, and schedule their work, with minimal managerial intervention. (4) As the HR manager at PhonePe, a leading digital payments and financial services platform in India, apply your understanding of induction (3 factors) and orientation (3 factors) to design a program for newly recruited employees. Explain how your program will help newcomers quickly adapt to the company's culture, policies, and job responsibilities while enhancing their engagement and productivity. (3) Apply your understanding of the communication process to explain how information flows in an organization. Illustrate your answer with a neat block diagram. (3) Analyze how four types of standards can be applied during the managerial function - control in a company like Micromax (an Indian consumer electronics company known for affordable smartphones and mobile devices) to evaluate performance and improve operational efficiency. Explain the impact of each standard on decision-making and organizational outcomes for Micromax. (4) -----End----- Page 2 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq5-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "Event Name Subject END TERM EXAM-ODD SEMESTER 2025 MIT MANIPALHUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Course Outcome Description Marks 1 3 CO 3 3 2 4 CO 4 12 3 5 CO 5 15 Total 30 * NA - CO Not Selected Event Name Subject END TERM EXAM-ODD SEMESTER 2025 MIT MANIPALHUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Level No of Questions Marks (%) 1 Remember 0 0(0%) 2 Understand 0 0(0%) 3 Application 5 15(50%) 4 Analysis 4 15(50%) 5 Create 0 0(0%) 6 Evaluate 0 0(0%) Total 9 30 (100%) Page 3 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq5-3.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq6", "file": "pyq/6. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - MAKEUP DEC 2025.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 1B) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 1C) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 2A) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 23-Dec-2025 (02:00 PM - 05:00 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION FIFTH SEMESTER B.TECH. DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - DEC 2025/JAN 2026 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (SPL: COMPUTER SCIENCE & ENGINEERING/INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM 3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. Explain the three levels of management. Examine the interrelationship between the three levels of management and the varying importance of managerial skills at each level. Illustrate your answer with suitable corporate examples for each level and skill. (4) Analyze three cultural dimensions that can influence management practices and employee behavior in a multinational company operating across diverse countries. Give one example for each dimension. (3) Given the following corporate examples, apply your knowledge of types of plans to identify and justify which type each scenario represents. i) Zomato implements a hyper-local delivery plan focusing on tier-2 and tier-3 cities to increase its customer base. ii) Infosys enforces a work-from-home plan to ensure employee safety and operational continuity during crises. iii) HDFC Life follows a step-by-step claims approval plan to process customer insurance claims efficiently. iv) Flipkart restricts sellers from listing counterfeit products as per its strict compliance. v) Reliance Foundation runs the \"Digital Udaan\" plan to provide rural youth with digital literacy training. vi) Asian Paints allocates an annual marketing budget to support nationwide promotional campaigns and product launches. (3) Zomato a comprehensive online platform for food delivery, restaurant discovery, and dining experiences across India and several international markets. Perform a SWOT analysis for Zomato and create two TOWS strategies to capitalize on the opportunities. i) Strong brand recognition and a large, loyal customer base across India. ii) High operational costs and dependence on third-party delivery personnel. iii) Expansion into tier-2 and tier-3 cities with growing digital penetration. iv) Intense competition from Swiggy, Blinkit, and other hyper-local delivery startups. v) Wide range of services including food delivery, dining-out, and subscription-based offerings. vi) Limited profitability despite high revenue due to aggressive discount strategies. vii) Diversification into grocery delivery, cloud kitchens, and international markets. viii) Regulatory changes related to labor laws and food safety compliance. (4) Page 1 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq6-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2B) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 2C) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3A) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4A) 4B) 4C) 5A) ix) Advanced technology platform for real-time tracking and efficient logistics management. x) Vulnerability to service quality issues and customer complaints affecting brand image. xi) Leveraging AI and data analytics to personalize customer experience and improve efficiency. xii) Economic downturns reducing consumer spending on discretionary dining and deliveries. Explain the steps involved in organizing. Compare on four grounds between a startup and an established multinational company in terms of formal and informal structure. (3) As the HR manager of a mid-sized manufacturing company, determine an appropriate span of control for the production managers using the four factors. Explain how you would apply these factors to decide the number of direct reports for each manager to ensure effective supervision and minimize micromanagement. (3) Imagine you are the founder of a large automobile firm planning to introduce a Line and Staff Organizational structure to improve technical decision-making and managerial control. Demonstrate how the roles of line executives and staff specialists would operate in your firm to enhance efficiency and specialization without creating conflicts of authority. Also draw an organizational chart. (3) As the HR Manager of a mid-sized IT company, apply the benefits of integrating HRM and HRD practices to design a strategy that improves employee productivity and retention. (3) Nykaa, India's leading beauty and lifestyle retailer, has approached you to suggest them on how they can use the Maslow's 'Hierarchy of Needs' to motivate employees at different career stages. You need to make a report, explaining Nykaa how fulfilling each 'level-of-need' influences employee engagement, performance, and retention within the organization. (4) How are employment tests instrumental in the selection procedure? With suitable representations, list out the measures of human psychology and human characteristics. (3) As the human resource manager of your technical organization which primarily consults investment and banking sectors, you hold the essential responsibility for the training and development of your staff. Occasionally, it is necessary to send your staff members for both on-the-job and off-the-job training. Therefore, list all the on-the-job and off-the-job training methods. Illustrate one on-the-job training method and one off-the-job training method suitable for the current business context, using relevant examples and/or representation. (3) Analyse Authority leadership styles and evaluate their impact on employee motivation and productivity. (4) Company, a leading Indian infrastructure developer, is confronting a critical decline in employee motivation and productivity. This trend, evidenced by rising attrition and widespread dissatisfaction, has been traced to four core issues: poor working conditions, non-competitive compensation, limited career growth, and a weak organizational culture. In response, management has initiated a comprehensive review of its employee motivation policies to address these systemic concerns. Identify the levels of Maslow's hierarchy of needs not being met at X Company. Explain how the application of Maslow's theory can improve employee motivation and performance. Critically evaluate the sufficiency of Maslow's theory in this context and consider the relevance of other motivational models. (3) Page 2 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq6-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "5B) 5C) Analyse Authority leadership styles and evaluate their impact on employee motivation and productivity. (3) Illustrate the eight steps of the controlling process using a block diagram and explain each step with a common example. (4) -----End----- Event Name Subject MAKE-UP EXAM-ODD SEMESTER 2025 MIT MANIPAL HUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Course Outcome Description Marks 1 1 CO 1 7 2 2 CO 2 7 3 3 CO 3 9 4 4 CO 4 3 5 5 CO 5 4 Total 30 * NA - CO Not Selected Event Name Subject MAKE-UP EXAM-ODD SEMESTER 2025 MIT MANIPAL HUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Level No of Questions Marks (%) 1 Remember 0 0(0%) 2 Understand 0 0(0%) 3 Application 5 15(50%) 4 Analysis 4 15(50%) 5 Create 0 0(0%) 6 Evaluate 0 0(0%) Total 9 30 (100%) Page 3 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq6-3.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq7", "file": "pyq/7. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - REGULAR MAY 2026.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 1B) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 1C) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2A) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2B) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 2C) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 06-May-2026 (02:00 PM - 05:00 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION SIXTH SEMESTER B.TECH. DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - MAY 2026 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (SPL: ELECTRONICS & COMMUNICATION ENGINEERING) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM 3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. A hospital faces a sudden shortage of critical supplies during an emergency. i) Assess which of Mintzberg's roles are most critical. ii) Find out how the manager should prioritize actions. Suggest a step-by-step response plan using at least 3 roles. (3) Critically analyze the three fundamental moral theories in normative ethics and their role in fostering ethical decision-making within organizations. Using real-world examples demonstrate how companies can institutionalize ethics through these frameworks. (3) An EdTech company is expanding globally. Classify the required plans into strategic, tactical, and operational plans with examples. (4) Michael Porter's Generic Model suggests that a company attempting to engage in both Cost Leadership and Differentiation simultaneously risks being \"Stuck in the Middle\" with no competitive advantage. Contrast this with the Blue Ocean Strategy, which explicitly pivots on \"Value Innovation\". (3) Identify the Urwick's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief. i) Neha ensured that each manager oversaw a limited number of employees to maintain effective supervision. By keeping a balanced span of control, she enabled better communication, timely decision-making, and efficient coordination within teams. ii) Arjun established a clear reporting structure where each employee received instructions from only one direct supervisor. This eliminated confusion, reduced conflicting orders, and improved accountability across teams. iii) Ravi assigned project leaders the necessary decision-making authority along with their responsibilities. By ensuring that authority and responsibility were balanced, he prevented delays, improved efficiency, and made individuals more accountable for their work. (3) Design an organizational structure for a multinational company using appropriate departmentation (4) Page 1 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq7-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3A) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4B) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 5A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5B) methods. Explain the four steps in the process of delegation with a suitable example. (3) Explain any three benefits of integrating HRM and HRD practices with a suitable example for each. (3) Omega Solutions is an IT company. The HR manager conducted an exit interview with Sameer, who was resigning. Sameer said: \"My salary is good and the office is comfortable. But I never receive any appreciation for my work. I have been doing the same tasks for three years with no growth. My manager never gives me any challenging responsibilities.\" Based on the case above, identify the factors affecting Sameer's motivation according to Herzberg's Two-Factor Theory. Explain the theory briefly. (4) You are a recruitment consultant hired by AB Corporation - a mid-sized manufacturing company. Your task is to recommend 3 internal sources and 3 external sources for their upcoming recruitment drive. Justify your recommendations. (3) You are the founder of a digital marketing agency that helps small businesses grow their online presence. You plan to hire fresh graduates and train them to meet industry standards. Elaborate on any three current trends in Training & Development that you will consider for your agency. (3) Metro Logistics is a courier company. The regional manager observed four branch managers: AK is extremely focused on delivery targets. He pushes his team to work faster and meet deadlines. He rarely asks about their personal problems or work-life balance. Employee complaints are high, but his branch has the best on-time delivery record. BK is very popular among her team. She organizes team lunches, listens to personal issues, and avoids conflicts. Her employees are happy, but her branch often misses delivery deadlines. CK maintains a balanced approach. He sets reasonable targets and also checks on employee well- being. His branch meets most deadlines, and employee turnover is low. DK took over a branch that was performing poorly. She set high targets and also started weekly one-on-one meetings with each employee to understand their concerns. She introduced recognition for good performance and team-building activities. Within six months, her branch achieved the highest on-time delivery record and the lowest employee turnover in the company. Based on the case above: i) Identify the leadership style of AK, BK, CK, and DK using Blake and Mouton's Managerial Grid. ii) Draw the Managerial Grid and label all five leadership styles. (2+2 = 4 marks) (4) Explain the systems approach to staffing with a labeled sketch. Why is this approach important for organizations? (3) Explain the four stages of group development with a brief explanation of each stage using relevant examples. (3) Page 2 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq7-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" Professor Mehta teaches a management course to 60 students. At the start of the semester, he asks the students sitting at the last bench, \"Can you hear me clearly? Do you understand my accent?\" He then tells them, \"If you understand me now, it will be fine. Otherwise, after 36 classes, you can tell me my faults. I can only improve next semester.\" During the semester, the class has monthly committee meetings where students discuss issues like teaching pace, assignment deadlines, and classroom facilities. The professor reviews these feedback points and makes adjustments immediately. To avoid losing teaching days on Fridays and Saturdays due to holidays, the institute sometimes swaps the timetable - for example, Thursday follows Friday's schedule, and Monday follows Saturday's schedule. This ensures all subjects get their required number of classes. Identify and explain the three types of control used by Professor Mehta and the institute by quoting lines from the case. What is the significance of each control type? (4) -----End----- Event Name Subject END TERM EXAM - EVEN SEMESTER 2025-2026 MIT MANIPALHUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Course Outcome Description Marks 1 1 CO 1 6 2 2 CO 2 7 3 3 CO 3 10 4 4 CO 4 9 5 5 CO 5 18 Total 50 * NA - CO Not Selected Event Name Subject END TERM EXAM - EVEN SEMESTER 2025-2026 MIT MANIPALHUM 3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Level No of Questions Marks (%) 1 Remember 0 0(0%) 2 Understand 0 0(0%) 3 Application 6 18(36%) 4 Analysis 9 32(64%) 5 Create 0 0(0%) 6 Evaluate 0 0(0%) Total 15 50 (100%) Page 3 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq7-3.webp"}]}, {"id": "pyq8", "file": "pyq/8. HUM 3022 EOM - END SEMESTER QUESTION PAPER - MAKEUP JUNE 2026.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "1A) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 1B) Course Outcome: \"CO1\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 1C) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 2A) Course Outcome: \"CO2\" / Cognitive Level: \"Understand\" 2B) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 2C) Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3A) Question Paper Exam Date & Time: 11-Jun-2026 (02:00 PM - 05:00 PM) MANIPAL ACADEMY OF HIGHER EDUCATION SIXTH SEMESTER B.TECH. DEGREE EXAMINATIONS - JUNE 2026 SUBJECT: HUM 3022/HUM_3022 - ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT (SPL: ELECTRONICS & COMMUNICATION ENGINEERING) ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT [HUM3022] Marks: 50 Duration: 180 mins. A Answer all the questions. Predict how does the \"Systems Approach\" view an organization. (4) Analyze the three basic moral theories in normative ethics. (3) Analyze Management by Objectives (MBO) as proposed by Peter Drucker. Discuss its advantages, limitations, and relevance in modern organizations. (3) Explain the \"Dynamics of the TOWS Matrix\" in relation to a company's progression through time. Specifically, analyze how a WO Strategy can eventually transition into a high-success SO Strategy. Use a suitable example to show a company overcame its initial weakness to illustrate this strategic evolution. (4) Identify the URWICK's principles from the following cases and explain them in brief. i) Meera streamlined project workflows and eliminated redundant steps, ensuring optimal resource utilization. Her efforts reduced turnaround time and improved overall productivity without increasing costs. ii) Rajesh introduced a dynamic project management framework that allowed teams to quickly adapt to market changes. This ensured that the company remained competitive without disrupting ongoing operations. iii) Kavita clearly outlined roles and reporting structures within the organization. This minimized confusion, improved accountability, and enhanced coordination between different departments. (3) A healthcare organization wants to improve patient care and operational efficiency. Suggest a departmentation strategy integrating multiple approaches. (3) You are the founder of a small steel bolt manufacturing company. You decide to adopt a Line organizational structure. Explain how authority, communication, and decision-making will work in (3) Page 1 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq8-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO3\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 3B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 3C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 4B) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 4C) your company under this structure. Draw the organizational chart. You have been appointed as the HR manager of a company planning to recruit freshers. Draft a Job Description and a Job Specification for the entry-level position in your own respective domains (eg. Mechanical, Aeronautical, Chemical etc). (3) ConnectCall Solutions is a BPO company handling customer support for multiple clients. The company has 800 employees working in night shifts. The HR director, Mr. Mehta, conducted an internal survey to understand why employee turnover was high. He compiled the following real statements from employees: Employee A (Ravi, age 22, works for 8 months): \"I earn just enough to pay for my shared room and food. But last month, the company delayed salaries by two weeks. I had to borrow money. Also, there's no transport facility at night. I fear walking to the bus stop after the shift. I'm thinking of quitting.\" Employee B (Neha, age 25, works for 2 years): \"I have a decent salary and the company provides cab service at night. But honestly, I feel invisible here. My team leader never says 'good job.' I have won 'Employee of the Month' twice, but no one celebrated it. My colleagues don't include me in their WhatsApp group. I eat lunch alone every day.\" Employee C (Arjun, age 30, works for 5 years): \"I have a good salary, my own house, and a supportive team. But for the last two years, I have been doing the same troubleshooting calls. I have asked for cross-training in chat support and voice process, but my request is always ignored. I feel my career has stopped growing.\" Employee D (Priya, age 40, works for 10 years): \"I am a senior team lead. I have a high salary, a car, respect from my team, and challenging projects. Last year, I completed an MBA while working. Now I want to design a new training module for freshers. I have ideas to improve the entire onboarding process. But my manager says, 'Just follow the existing manual.' I feel I have more to offer.\" Employee E (Sunil, age 28, works for 3 years): \"I have no complaints about salary or safety. My team is friendly and my work is interesting. But last month, I was passed over for a promotion despite having better numbers than the person who got promoted. My manager said, 'Seniority matters more than performance.' I feel humiliated and underappreciated.\" Based on the case of ConnectCall Solutions: i). Identify the highest level of Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs that is currently unfulfilled for each of the five employees (A, B, C, D, E). ii) For employees A and D, explain what specific action the company should take to fulfill their unmet need. Justify your answer using Maslow's theory. (2½+1½ = 4 marks) (4) You are the Chief Human Resources Officer (CHRO) for Tata Motors - one of India's largest automotive manufacturers, and your task is to pick 3 each of the best internal sources and external sources for the next recruitment drive. You need to justify your selections to the Board of Directors. (3) You are the founder of a 'Tech Startup' that leverages technology to develop innovative products or services, often aiming for rapid growth and scalability. You would be recruiting new employees shortly and thus researching the Current Trends. Elaborate on 3 current trends in Training & Development that you will be considering for your startup. (3) Quote the lines from the Reliance Retail Expansion Project Scenarios to identify and explain the (4) Page 2 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq8-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" 5A) Course Outcome: \"CO4\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5B) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Application\" 5C) Course Outcome: \"CO5\" / Cognitive Level: \"Analysis\" different Leadership Styles incorporated based on Likert's 4 System. For long-term strategy, Reliance forms cross-functional teams to co-create solutions for customer experience. Decision-making is collaborative, and employees feel empowered to innovate. As operations stabilize, senior leaders start offering incentives and recognition for meeting sales goals. While decisions remain centralized, managers are allowed minor flexibility in implementing promotional strategies, fostering a sense of goodwill. At the initial stage of launching stores in Tier-3 cities, top management imposes strict targets and policies without consulting store managers. Decisions are centralized, and employees have little autonomy, creating pressure to meet deadlines. During the planning of a new digital retail platform, management actively seeks input from regional managers and tech teams. Suggestions are considered before final decisions, improving engagement and ownership among employees. Apply the systems approach to staffing to explain with a neat diagram how the State Bank of India (SBI) can effectively meet its organizational goals and deliver quality banking services. (3) As the communication manager at ICICI Bank, apply your understanding of 'barriers to effective communication' to identify three barriers involved in the workspace and suggest practical strategies to overcome them for smoother information flow in the organization. (3) Analyze how three types of controls can be implemented in a company like Reliance Jio to enhance operational efficiency and customer satisfaction. Explain with examples how each type of control helps managers anticipate problems, monitor ongoing processes, and evaluate outcomes. (4) -----End----- Event Name Subject MAKE UP EXAM - EVEN SEMESTER 2025-2026 MIT MANIPALHUM3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Course Outcome Description Marks 1 1 CO 1 7 2 2 CO 2 7 3 3 CO 3 9 4 4 CO 4 12 5 5 CO 5 15 Total 50 * NA - CO Not Selected Event Name Subject MAKE UP EXAM - EVEN SEMESTER 2025-2026 MIT MANIPALHUM3022:ESSENTIALS OF MANAGEMENT # Level No of Questions Marks (%) 1 Remember 0 0(0%) 2 Understand 1 4(8%) 3 Application 6 19(38%) 4 Analysis 8 27(54%) 5 Create 0 0(0%) 6 Evaluate 0 0(0%) Total 15 50 (100%) Page 3 of 3", "image": "assets/pages/pyq8-3.webp"}]}, {"id": "sdl1", "file": "SDL/1. QUALITY MANAGEMENT - Week 1 - Unit1 - Introduction to Quality Control.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "5 Introduction to Quality Control UNIT 1 INTRODUCTION TO QUALITY CONTROL Structure 1.1 Introduction Objectives 1.2 Definition of Quality 1.3 Concept of Quality 1.3.1 Quality of Design 1.3.2 Quality of Conformance 1.3.3 Quality of Performance 1.3.4 Achieving and Maintaining Quality 1.4 Quality Control 1.5 Stakeholders and Their Expectations 1.6 Quality Assurance 1.6.1 Quality Audit 1.6.2 Quality Survey 1.6.3 Product Audit 1.7 Recognition of Quality Effects 1.8 Summary 1.9 Key Words 1.10 Answers to SAQs 1.1 INTRODUCTION The opening of international trade market has brought many opportunities for improved products and services. Consequently, for almost every product or service, there is more than one organization trying to make a sale. Price may be a major issue in whether a sale is made or lost, but another important factor is quality. In fact quality is often the major issue and poor quality can be very expensive for both the producing firm and customer. Consequently, the producer employs quality control (QC) tactics to ensure that a quality product can be delivered at the right place and right time with the right price. Quality is not only concerned with manufactured goods but also with services like banking, health care, education etc. This unit starts with the definition of quality. An outline of how quality is established, why quality control is necessary and who are affected by quality is provided. The elements of quality assurance are discussed in detail. The unit ends with a section on highlighting the need of recognizing the effects of quality. Objectives After studying this unit, you should be able to • understand the concept of quality and its control, and • explain quality assurance and its importance. 1.2 DEFINITION OF QUALITY In the beginning, a high-quality product was understood as one that is stronger and is, in general, more durable than other products. In some cases, this is a good definition of a quality product, but not always. A good fuse (circuit breaker), for example, is not one that lasts longer during periods of high current or voltage. So the quality of a product is", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "6 Quality Systems the degree to which the product meets specifications. This definition of quality is also true for the service sector industry. In fact, today, quality is more visible in service sector such as hospitals, restaurants, banks, etc. The health of an organizations producing physical commodities thrives not only on manufactured products but also on the services (annual maintenance, defect replacement, product updates, etc.). In today’s society, organizations like insurance companies do not produce anything physical yet they are crucial in providing security to the commodities (e.g., vehicle) acquired by an individual. Increasingly, the definitions of quality include an added emphasis on meeting these new needs of an individual. Several pioneers of quality have provided various definitions of quality. Feigenbaum defines quality as “The total composite product and service characteristics of Engineering, Manufacturing, Marketing and Maintenance through which the product and service in use meet the expectation of the customer”. According to Juran, (i) Quality consists of those product features which meet the needs of customers and thereby provide product satisfaction. (ii) Quality consists of freedom from deficiencies. “Product” is the output of any process. It consists of mainly goods, software and services. A “product feature” is a property which is possessed by a product and which is intended to meet certain customers’ needs. A “customer” is someone who is impacted by the product. Customers may be external or internal. External customers are impacted by the product but are not members of the company which produces the product. External customers include clients who buy the product, government regulatory bodies, the public etc. Internal customers are members of company who require the product for further processing or in-house use. Some other definitions of quality quoted in the literature are as follows : • According to ANSI/ASQC Standard A3-1987 (draft), quality means “The totality of features and characteristics of a product or service that bear on its ability to satisfy stated or implied needs.” • The European Organization for Quality Control Glossary (1981) defines quality as “The totality of features and characteristics of a product or service that bear on its ability to satisfy a given need. For example, with manufactured products quality is mainly determined by quality of design and quality of manufacture.” • The Soviet encyclopedia defines quality as follows: “Quality of Products is the aggregate of properties of a product determining its ability to satisfy the needs it was built to satisfy.” 1.3 CONCEPT OF QUALITY The concept of quality has evolved from one that is produced (product) or provided (service) by an organization to an individual or society. Quality is a demand of customer either internal or external. A product can be said to possess a good quality if it fulfills the following requirements : Fitness for Purpose The product or service fulfils the purpose for which it has been produced or provided. Conformance to Requirements The product performs satisfactory in an application intended by the user. Grade The product possesses distinguishing feature in terms of appearance, performance, durability, safety, affordability and maintainability.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "7 Introduction to Quality Control Degree of Performance The product scores over competitive products of equivalent grade, based on comparative test by customers. Degree of Excellence The product gains a value derived from functions performed with the product, the timeliness of product’s availability, aesthetics associated with ownership, quality of performance and its cost. With the principle that “customer is the king”, the producer or service provider strives to meet the expectations of the customer. From the producer’s point of view, quality must be observed in the aspects of designing, conformance and performance. In the next sections, we shall study how quality is observed in the above three areas in the product industry. The implications of quality in service sector shall be discussed side by side. 1.3.1 Quality of Design Prior to producing a product, it is essential for the producer or manufacturer to know “what are the demands of the user? This information can be obtained by conducting surveys, past market trends, etc. (These shall be discussed in detail in a later section.) With this database, a collective assessment of user needs is made and the requirements are quantified. These quantified requirements are called ‘specifications’ that decide the characteristics of the product. The quality of design is concerned with the tightness of specification for manufacture of the product. In other words, it refers to the appropriateness of the process involved with the manufacturing of the product. For example, a part with tolerance of ± 0.001 mm would be considered a better design of quality than another with tolerance of ± 0.01 mm. The manufacturer is said to deliver a good quality of design if the product performs consistently within the stipulated life span. The performance can be measured in terms of rated output, efficiency, overload capacity and continuous or intermittent operation as per specific application. The quality of design is controlled by several factors : (a) Type of Customer in Market The pre-requisites to the design of a product involve conducting a market survey because the taste and needs of customer vary with location, socio-economic status and personal preference. For consumer goods, it is important to study the consuming habits, prices the customer is willing to pay and the choice of design of product i.e., its size, shape and packaging etc. For example, a 10-seater 3-wheeler would be the best choice for commuting in rural and semi-urban areas because people would share the auto rather than hire the entire vehicle, whereas a small auto-rickshaw would serve the purpose of a city commuter where he has to travel to a particular place. Thus, the priority in designing a three-wheeler for travel in rural areas is capacity whereas a city-vehicle should be compact and must run at higher speeds on city roads. Similarly, for capital goods such as heavy machineries, the decision to design for quality is governed by factors such as reliability, trouble-free service and maintainability. (b) Profit Consideration It is important that the manufacturer minimizes internal waste besides realizing the customer’s expectation. For example, if the customer is demanding a product whose functional utility is of greater importance than the physical attributes, the manufacturer can produce a quality design without investing too much on aesthetics. Producing different grades of products, catering to various segments of customers, also contributes to increasing of the profit margin. (c) Environmental Condition", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "8 Quality Systems A good design considers the effect of environment on the performance. A well-designed bus body fails to provide ride comfort in village roads and hilly tracks whereas a jeep makes it suitable for rough terrains and muddy roads also. Similarly, laboratory appliances often fail to perform satisfactorily in real-life situation because of absence of proper temperature, humidity, etc. Increasing research has been called for designing appliances that can perform in harsh conditions and inside factories. (d) Special Requirements of Product Generally, greater the requirements for strength, fatigue resistance, interchangeability of parts, etc. tighter should be the tolerance to give goods of better quality. In case of electronic items, where the breakdown of a part is unavoidable due to voltage fluctuation, designs are made to enable quick replacement. The service sector is very sensitive to the changing needs of the customer. Often the customer would like to avail the services as per his convenience. In general, quality of design in service is viewed from the point of consumption and flexibility in service. For example, medical aid can be sought at home if patient cannot be transported to hospital. Although such design of service demands greater resources and time nevertheless it ensures that customers are delighted. The coming of personalized insurance schemes is an example of introducing flexibility in services. Such organizations deal with the customer on an individual basis. The customer himself is in charge of designing his needs (type of insurance) thereafter the organization modifies its working policy towards the customer. 1.3.2 Quality of Conformance The quality of design is an indicator of what we plan to do. The quality of conformance is an indicator as to how well the manufactured product conforms to the plan. A product can be designed by considering most of the factors affecting it. However, implementation of those design concepts might fail during production planning or manufacturing. Some of the key factors that ensure conformance of product to design specification are discussed below. (a) Quality of Incoming Raw Materials Any compromise in any part of the product which may be due to poor quality of the raw material is reflected in its use and period of use. Besides, the machines and tools for producing the product and measuring instruments must be kept at high level of maintenance. (b) Selection of Process and Process Control The conceptual design of a product is realized by transforming the raw materials through appropriate processes and continuous monitoring of the process. Thus the proper selection of process and adequate process control is utmost essential to observe quality. (c) Work Force Well-trained, experienced and motivated operators, technicians and engineers form the root of the organization. A proper work force at proper place improves productivity and quality of product. (d) Safe Transport and Storage A well-designed product cannot achieve conformance to quality if proper care is relaxed in shipment and storage of finished goods. Thus delivery process to customer is an essential step in achieving the quality mark. (e) Implementing Statistical Techniques", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "9 Introduction to Quality Control Conformance to quality must put a check in the variability of process. This variability arises due to reasons – seen and unseen. Provision of statistical techniques is an important step in controlling variability. (f) Inspection Programme When the quality of product is said to conform to design and other factors, it also indicates that defectives are minimal. This is accomplished by inspection and feedback from user and implementing remedial procedures. In the service sector, quality of each unit of service counts to provide the seal of conformance. The service is said to conform to quality if the service provider not only provides the appropriate facilities but also reverts to the customer based on customer feedback. For example, a passenger traveling by an airline continuously assesses the organizations resources ranging from ticket reservation to check-in, security checks to luggage arrival. If the services in any section dissatisfy the passenger, he makes a quick response and asks for the cause. A good service provider handles customer complaints and takes action on the feedback from customer. Conformance to quality is dependent on the service provider who has the knowledge and proper communication skills to deal with the customer queries. 1.3.3 Quality of Performance The quality of performance is concerned with how well the manufactured product gives its performance. It depends upon quality of design and quality of conformance. The product can be a best possible design, but poor conformance to design can cause poor performance. Conversely, the best conformance cannot make the product function correctly if design itself is not right. The performance of a product is observed from experience of the customer. The approval of customer is essential for a product, well designed and conforming to quality (stated by producer). The satisfaction of the customer is immediately reflected in the number of sales and the organization can be said to achieve the level of performance. 1.3.4 Achieving and Maintaining Quality Quality can be viewed as a duty dictated by a customer and performed by the manufacturer or service provider. The approval of customer is necessary for the manufacturer to evaluate the manufacturing process. The functions concerned with quality are performed by individuals and departments of the manufacturing organization or a service company. A brief description of function of various departments, individuals, specialists in the organization is listed below. (i) Marketing This department conducts market survey to create a database of customer demands, the associated features of the demand, the type of service expected, the frequency of use etc. The experience of the customer with existing product also becomes the basis for a redesign or improvement in product. (ii) Research and Development In this department, specialists conceptualize a product, which can meet the quality needs of user. (iii) Design In this department, design engineers design product and specify materials considering quality requirements. (iv) Process Planning Process engineers specify process, machines and instruments capable of producing products through specified manufacturing processes. (v) Purchase", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "10 Quality Systems Purchasing specialist buy materials and components possessing appropriate qualities specified by designers. (vi) Production Operators are trained to use process and instruments to make the product as per design. (vii) Inspection Inspectors examine product to reduce defects and process to reduce variability. (viii) Retail Houses Retailer makes the products accessible to customer and also provides maintenance service. Thus, it is seen that the quality is everybody’s business. 1.4 QUALITY CONTROL It is a process through which we measure the actual performance, compare it with some standard and take corrective action if there is a deviation in observed performance from standard. It is a systematic procedure for meeting the quality goals. Fiegenbaum defines Total Quality Control as : “….an effective system for integrating the quality development, quality maintenance and quality improvement effects of various groups in an organization to enable the production to be carried out at most economical level and to achieve satisfaction of the customer.” The reason for carrying out quality control is that processes are subjected to variations. The variations can be due to materials, tools, machines, labours, working conditions, skill of operator; anything that is involved with the creation of product. Some variations can be rectified immediately but others cannot be and their causes are also not easy to trace. This translates into deteriorated performance of the product and a loss of quality. Quality control strives to match the targeted performance by minimizing the variation. The processes involved in the production are bound to vary in time. Since the deviation can take place in any stage of production, it is both economical as well as scientific to introduce quality control at all levels of production. Before understanding the basic objectives of quality control, it is important to question who sets the standards of quality. The standards of quality are set according to its specification i.e., its design. The design remains a concept in the mind of designer until it is defined through quantified description such as drawing. Specifications are of following types : (a) Standard Specification For example, Bureau of Indian Standard (BIS) formulates specifications of a variety of product. The bodies that prescribe standards comprise members drawn from industry, research organization, government department and interested experts. (b) Customer Specification When standard specifications are not available for a particular customer needs, the customer provides specification to meet his particular needs. (c) Company Specification These are a compromise decided by contract between the customer and manufacturer because of constraints in manufacturing process, supply or availability of raw materials etc. Following are the basic objectives of quality control :", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "11 Introduction to Quality Control (i) The foremost objective is to improve the company’s income by making the product more acceptable to customer. In other words, quality control (QC) is concerned with quality of future production, so that products provide versatility, better manufacturability etc. (ii) The second important objective is reduction of losses due to defects. Defects can arise in production machinery, instruments raw material and also processes. Inspection is the obvious answer for sorting defectives. But Q.C. tries to achieve less sorting, less reworks, less shutdowns, few customer returns and ultimately lower scrap. QC also puts a check to the flaws that creep into service because of negligence or ignorance. (iii) Quality control acts as a tool to judge the conformity of the process to established standards and take suitable action when there are deviations. (iv) Obtaining loyal customers in the long run is an important objective of QC. In the deep understanding of quality, the efforts of an organization are described as “customer-centric”. The phenomenon of “profit escalation” or rise in profits is bound to occur once the organization has a base of loyal customers. (v) A major objective of quality control is associating a value to the product. A manufacturing organization recognizes that the requirements of customer change over time. In order to maintain the same value of the product, it is necessary to obtain feedback from the customer on the present worth of product in terms of usefulness and also price. This process is accompanied by experimentation and modification of process. In fact, maintaining a value is a step toward developing good customer relations. The ultimate aim of QC is to ensure satisfaction of customers with products and services of high quality level, to build customer’s goodwill, confidence, and reputation of manufacturer. The principle behind quality control is based on assumption that key quality characteristics can be defined and that measures of these characteristics can be observed. The key characteristics can be as simple as length of one part or percent impurities in a chemical solution. Once the key characteristics are defined, systems are established for controlling the mean value of the characteristics. In later units, you will learn that word “control” refers to a statistical inference and hence the procedure of QC may not catch all defectives arising of variation but can significantly reduce the number of defects to a few parts per million. The key characteristics are not restricted to technological data such as lengths, frequency or viscosity. Quality engineering recognizes four other characteristics. (a) Psychological (sensory) – Taste, odour, beauty etc. (b) Time Oriented – Reliability, maintainability, life etc. (c) Contractual – Guarantee, safety etc. (d) Ethical – Honesty, integrity etc. In the health industry, key characteristics are based on emotional grounds such as trust (on doctors). Although these are non-measurable, the one important feature of items identified by such characteristics is that by QC, quality of future product or services can be regulated. The quality policy of an organization is formulated on the basis of these characteristics. The policy involves working out details of product requirement, setting standard (specification) selecting inspection plans, detecting deviation, setting procedure for checking and taking corrective action. Also it involves controlling the salvage value. Quality Control affects the operational policy of an organization. It is a fact that the ultimate aim of an organization is to produce optimum quality at minimum price and ensure customer satisfaction. The competitiveness of an organization would be determined by degree of QC undertaken. A strict quality policy involves monitoring all observable characteristic and controlling the process at mean level. This involves investment in the form of techniques and capital. This guarantees a higher quality performance. Thus the working policy of the organization revolves about improving its", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "12 Quality Systems income without compromising on quality and improving the usefulness of future products. In other words, quality policy dictates the working policy of an organization. SAQ 1 (a) Define the term quality control and explain its objectives. (b) Differentiate between inspection and quality control. (c) What is the meaning of quality of design? 1.5 STAKEHOLDERS AND THEIR EXPECTATIONS The people who are directly and indirectly affected in varying degrees by the operation of companies or institutions are usually thought of as Stakeholders. Stakeholders are those who have an interest in a particular decision, either as individuals or representatives of a group. This includes people who influence a decision, or can influence it, as well as those affected by it. The stakeholder may represent from the community of investors, consumers, employees, surveyors, consumer panels and management-labour negotiators. The relations between the organization and stakeholders are provided in the codes of practice. The codes usually open with a statement that in its dealings, the corporation (or member of a professional body) will always act with integrity, fairly and reasonably, the acceptance of a social responsibility to the community. Thus the existence of codes implies a sense of obligation for the organization to behave in certain ways towards groups of people. As such the stakeholders have the following main roles : Plan and Policy Development The key stakeholder must play the role for the project to be successful. The stakeholders have specific interests in an organization because it benefits him. Or the project activities might cause damage or conflict for the stakeholder. A stakeholder is involved in drawing up action plan(s) in light of review and new guidance. They share their views with key stakeholders via established forums or networks. Quality Improvement Initiative The role of stakeholder is very important in early stages of quality improvement initiative. In case, the stakeholder senses the quality initiative as a hamper to his interests, he might withdraw from the organization. Hence, the organization develops strategies for obtaining support from the stakeholders. Performance Monitoring for the Forward Planning Function Periodic meetings are held between the organization and stakeholders for monitoring the performance and taking corrective actions. 1.6 QUALITY ASSURANCE The term “assurance” refers to protection of a product performance under actual conditions. Quality is a fitness for use. To derive the best performance of a product, it", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "13 Introduction to Quality Control must be used only as stated. Thus, it is essential to recognize the early warning of trouble ahead. With quality assurance, the management makes available to the customer the proof that product is fit for use. Thus quality assurance is the activity of providing the evidence needed to establish confidence that the quality function is being performed. The type of evidence differs, widely according to the persons requiring the assurance and the nature of product. As an example, freshness of vegetable in the village market is attained through direct sensory examination. In the case of packaged items, the evidence must be supplemented by laboratory testing. The expiry date in tinned products, cosmetics and medicine is mentioned after laboratory inspection. For long-life products, favourable environmental conditions must be stated. The manager can assure quality performance if he has the answer to the following questions : (i) Is the product safe? (ii) Are we complying with legal requirements? (iii) Is the product(s) performing within warranted life period? (iv) Is the product marketable? Now, we shall discuss three forms of company practiced quality assurance used to provide evidence. (a) Quality audit, (b) Quality surveys, and (c) Product audit. 1.6.1 Quality Audit A quality audit is an independent review conducted to compare some aspect of quality performance with a standard for that performance. An independent audit provides an unbiased picture of performance. The usual purpose of quality audits is to provide independent assurance that : • Plans for attaining quality are such that, if followed, intended quality will, in fact, be attained. • Products are fit and safe for use. • Laws and regulations are being followed. • There is a conformance to specifications. • Data provide accurate and adequate information on quality to all concerned. The purpose of an audit is valuable to the organization because it covers various aspects, viz. (i) Policies and procedures regarding : (a) Operating. (b) Quality control. (c) Administration. (ii) Operating effectiveness involving : (a) Records, interpretations, corrective action. (b) Equipment control. (c) Inter-departmental co-ordination. (d) Assessment of product quality. (iii) System effectiveness covering : (a) Storage and Handling practices.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "14 Quality Systems (b) Field complaints and corrective action. (c) Tool and Gauge control. (d) Product design changes. It is only up to the need of client what sort of auditing he would like to conduct. The various types of audits generally carried out are : The Adequacy Audit It is called as management audit. It determines the extent to which the documented system, represented in the quality manual and the associated procedures, work instruction and forms, adequately meets the requirements of the standards. A Compliance Audit It is carried out to establish the extent to which the documented system is implemented and observed by the workforce. An External Audit External audit is carried out by an organization or another with which, the organization has either entered into contract for sale of goods or services. It may be an adequacy and/or a compliance audit. An Extrinsic Audit This is an external audit carried out by an independent third party that may be accredited, using a national or international standard, such as ISO: 9000 series to provide assurance on the effectiveness of the quality systems. Internal Audit It is the audit carried out by the company on its own quality systems. Its purpose is to give assurance to the management that its quality system is effectively achieving the planned quality objectives. These audits are carried out by the organizations’ own staff, provided that they are independent of the system being audited or by an outside agency. The most common reasons for conducting an audit include: • To determine if in-house field personnel are following Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) correctly. • To determine if there are any deficiencies in how SOPs have been prepared and how best to correct field practices. • To determine if there are any misunderstandings by field personnel regarding expectations of SOPs. • To validate or dispute outside audit results (such as those of a regulatory agency). • To assist in the development of new SOPs where new sampling equipment or techniques are being used. • To conduct annual quality control checks. In an engineering company the complete quality audit brings together the data from the following investigations. (A) To examine whether • The design meets the functional requirements completely. • Design specifications are clear without ambiguity. • The design fulfils the customer’s requirements.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "15 Introduction to Quality Control (B) To examine whether • Manufacturing specification conforms to the functional design specifications. • The manufacturing specifications are complete and clear cut. (C) To examine • Customers quality complaints. • The adequacy of corrective action taken by the company. (D) To examine activities in shops as • The adequacy of gauges and test equipment used. • Completeness and sequence of performance of inspection procedure, data collection system and action. The design of an audit is tied directly to the objectives defined by the client. Some clients simply want to find out if their contractors are following the SOPs outlined in their work plans at a single site. This type of audit is simple and straightforward, consisting mainly of filling out a detailed checklist. Other clients want to evaluate the field equipment used by their field staff. While in the field, the auditors will make detailed observations about actual field activities being conducted during the audit. Depending upon the objectives of the audit, these observations may be evaluated against required SOPs, against best available standard methods and practices; or against corporate field procedure guidance. The timing of an audit is best decided by discussions between the auditors and the client. In some cases, audits may be conducted as an unannounced visit to permit observation of actual field practices. In other cases, the field team is well aware of the audit and works with the auditors to structure field activities that will cover all key practices and procedures defined in the SOPs. In still other instances, where the audit is conducted as an independent third party review of field procedures, the field team may be given a window of dates for the audit to be conducted. The final product of an audit can vary from a simple checklist report to a very detailed technical evaluation of the sampling team being audited. The client requesting the audit makes the decision on report format. In addition to written reports, photographic documentation of field activities can also be included in the report pending security clearance with the field site. An auditing task consists of the following steps : Audit Initiation The basic right to conduct audits is derived from the ‘charter’, which has been approved by upper management. A specific audit is initiated on request of the manager of activity. Audit Planning Element of a plan must include definition of scope and objective of audit, identification of area to be audited and auditors, a schedule including expected start and completion date, standard and audit documentations. In all, the plan should be prepared to inform the manager of activity and the auditors regarding details. Audit Implementation This phase involves collection, analysis and evaluation of factual information and drawing of conclusion from these facts. Audit Reporting After the audit, the audit report is prepared. The report is viewed as credible if it is balanced in reporting and depersonalized. A common criticism to the audit report is the tendency for the report to emphasize deficiencies that are minor in nature. A", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "16 Quality Systems balanced report must determine the impact of deficiencies on activities. On the other hand an audit that reports only deficiencies is bound to be unacceptable because nothing is said about those elements that have performed well. There are certain deficiencies associated with quality audits. Quality audits are not sufficient to provide full assurance to upper management because they cannot address the following things. (i) Relative standing of the product in the marketplace with respect to quality. (ii) Feedback of user. (iii) Opportunities for reducing costs of poor quality. The review of entire elements of an organization demands time, money and coordination. The following points prove effective in conducting quality audits. (i) Prioritize the Quality related Activities : Before preparing for an audit, it is essential to estimate the improvement versus cost of performing the audit. (ii) Define Clearly the Subject Matter of Audits : Identify broad areas and establish a detailed checklist of features to be studied and question to be raised. (iii) Switch Resources : The audit resources should be switched to other areas as soon as one area is improved. (iv) Reference Standards : In absence of any reference, the auditing task becomes very large. A Quality Assurance Manual (Reference IS, 10201-1988) incorporates : Quality objective, Quality policy, Control of measuring tools, gauges and instruments, Material management, Vendor evaluation, inspection plans, System of handing of rework, rejection and deviations, Storage, packaging and packing, Handling of customer complaints, Quality costs and Quality reporting and review. Thus in absence of any company audit, it is best to follow according to the manual. 1.6.2 Quality Survey Quality Survey enables the manager of an organization to assess the quality of the products and/or services of the organization by viewing the process from different perspectives. A series of investigations are being carried out by full time staff specialists who distribute forms and handouts to customers or the employees of an organization from whom reasonable information can be derived. The quality surveys are very common in production as well as service sector. A quality survey goes further than discovering discrepancies as in auditing process. A good survey should discover opportunities and unexpected threats. A quality survey allows to : • understand where quality improvement efforts are succeeding, • involve employees in quality improvement efforts from the beginning of the process, • pinpoint regions where quality improvement needs to occur, and • improve customer satisfaction with increased quality. Surveys can be conducted by distributing the forms to customer or by interviewing them. A reference standard helps to define the activities that are needed to achieve quality objectives, provide criteria for evaluation and thereby increase the objectivity of the audits. The regulatory bodies issuing the standard surveys are ISO 9000 series, BS 5750 and MIL-Q-9858A.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "17 Introduction to Quality Control 1.6.3 Product Audit Product auditing is an independent evaluation of product quality to determine fitness of a product for use and conformance to specification. Product auditing takes place after inspections have been completed. The purpose of products auditing includes : (i) Estimating the quality level as delivered to customers. (ii) Evaluating the effectiveness of the inspection decisions in determining conformance to specifications. (iii) Providing information useful in improving the outgoing product quality level and improving the effectiveness of inspection. (iv) Providing additional assurance beyond routine inspection activities. It may be performed by : • One of the customer • Internally as ‘Final Inspection’ • External product audits are typically oriented to a specific customer. The main task in Product Audit is sampling for inspecting defects in a product. For products manufactured by mass production, sample size is determined by statistical procedures. For products manufactured as large unit such as automobiles, the number of units might be small but the total number of characteristics that are sampled becomes very large. However, certain products demand independent sampling, for example, drugs. A sample of product audit is shown below : (1) Name of Manufacturer _________________________________________ (2) Licence/Conditional Licence Number _____________________________ (3) Name of Product ______________________________________________ (4) End Use Product Formulation ____________________________________ • Were specifications consistent with registered particulars Yes  No  N/A  • Was the formula on the batch record consistent with registered formula particulars Yes  No  N/A  (5) Product Stability Profile • Is an SOP for developing stability data available? Yes  No  N/A  • Is it adequate? Yes  No  N/A  • Is it being implemented? Yes  No  N/A  (6) Is stability data on selected products available? Yes  No  N/A  (7) Are there change control procedures in place for controlling formulation change? • Are changes internally approved? Yes  No  N/A  • Is there a procedure for notifying the necessary changes where appropriate? Yes  No  N/A ", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "18 (8) Product Label Quality Systems • Is the label approved Yes  No  N/A  • Is it current Yes  No  N/A  • Is copy attached Yes  No  N/A  The Product Audit is highly customized so as to reflect target market segments and key competitors. As the product gets complex, it is essential to carry out the auditing process in multiple stages. Some of the key stages that have been identified are tabulated in Table 1.1 with its merits and demerits. Table 1.1 : Potential Stages for Product Auditing (as Described by Juran) Stage at which Product Auditing is Conducted Merits and Demerits of Using this Stage After acceptance by inspectors Most economical, but does not reflect effect of packing, shipping, storage, or usage After packing but before shipment to fields Requires unpacking and repacking, but evaluates effect of original packing On receipt by dealers Difficult to administer at such multiple locations, but reflects effect of shipping and storage On receipt by users Even more difficult to administer, but evaluated the added effects of dealer handing and storage plus effects of shipment to user and unpacking Performance in service The ideal, but also the most difficult to administer because of the number and variety of usages; can be simplified through sampling In a report of product audit, the product is assigned a rating or a score. This running score is used as one of the inputs for the executive reports on quality. One of the ways managers prefer to rate the product is based on several levels of seriousness such as critical, major, minor, incidental. The purpose of such auditing is that inspection reports appear in the form of presence of defects, failures and other factors. Because these discrepancies are unequal in nature a summary of % defectives does not present a complete picture of the acceptable quality. The most direct method of quantifying the seriousness is by assigning weights. Usually a weight of 100 “demerits” is arbitrarily assigned to the most serious defect class and weight then assigned to the other classes. For instance : Seriousness Classification Weight or Demerit Value A (Critical) 100 B (Major) 40-70 C (Minor) 10-25 D (Incidental) 1-10 To summarize, defects are converted to demerits to arrive at a composite demerits per unit. For example, the result of product audit on one product line for 1 month were as per Table 1.2. If there were 5000 units of products audited in a month, the demerits per unit totaled 856/5000 = 0.17. Table 1.2: Results of Product Audit Defect Class Weight Number of Defects Found Total Demerits", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "19 Introduction to Quality Control A (Critical) 100 2 200 B (Major) 50 7 350 C (Minor) 10 28 280 D (Incidental) 1 26 26 Total 856 In addition to summarizing the defects found (both number and relative seriousness), the audit result can be tallied by functional responsibility (i.e., design, purchasing, production). Audit result can be summarized to show the effectiveness of the previous inspection activities. Typically, a simple ratio is used such as the percentage of total defects, which are detected by inspection. For example, if the previous inspection revealed a total of 40 defects in a sample of N pieces and if the product audit inspection revealed 10 additional defects, the inspection effectiveness would be 40/50 100 × , or 80 percent. The usual standard for comparison of product audit is previous practice; the managers want to see whether quality is improving or worsening. To quantify past practice, the auditors conduct the product audit over a sufficient number of months to acquire stable data. This period is known as the base period. In calculating the base period, it is usual to exclude known abnormalities such as new- product troubles and the effect of temporary crises. The resulting “refined” base period then becomes a basis for comparison, mush as previous year’s costs or expenses. Alternatively, the standard for comparison may be “market quality”. In such cases, the product audit plan is applied to competitive products in the same way that it is applied to the company’s own products. 1.7 RECOGNITION OF QUALITY EFFECTS Quality affects a supplier’s economy in the following two principal ways : The Effect on Costs With respect to its effects on costs, the word ‘quality’ is used in the sense of freedom from deficiencies. In this sense, higher quality means lower cost as the defects are minimized. The cost of poor quality affects two parties : the manufacturer and the users. Manufacturer suffers loss as a result of waste of material, labour time and processing cost. Poor quality also increases the cost of the user of the product in the form of repair costs after warrantee period, various losses due to downtime etc. The Effect on Income With respect to its effect on income, the word quality is used in the sense of the features of the product, which respond to customer needs. Those features make the product saleable and provide “product satisfaction” to customers. With superior quality, the company can earn higher share of market, firmer prices, a higher percentage of successful bids, and still other benefits of income. The effect of improved quality can be observed by observing the following : (i) Increased production. (ii) Reduction in scrap and re-work cost. (iii) Increased sales of good quality product. (iv) Reputation of manufacturer and goodwill of the customer. (v) Psychological stability in the enterprise. SAQ 2 (a) What is quality audit? Name and describe the various types of quality audits.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "20 Quality Systems (b) An inspection revealed total 18 defects in a sample. Product audit found 2 additional defects. What is the inspection effectiveness? 1.8 SUMMARY In this unit, an introduction to quality control is provided. First, quality is defined. It is the total composite product and service characteristics of Engineering, Manufacturing, Marketing and Maintenance through which the product and service in use meet the expectation of the customers. For satisfactory quality of performance, quality of design and conformance should be good. Quality control is the process through which we measure the actual quality performance, compare it with the standards and take corrective actions if there is a deviation. Quality assurance deals with the question of assuring the desired quality, reliability, service and other aspects in the manufactured product through scientific techniques. Quality audit, quality survey and product audit are the main tools for achieving quality assurance. Finally, the effects of quality are described. 1.9 KEY WORDS Customer : A customer is someone who is impacted by the product or service. Grade : A popular name for degrees of product satisfaction is grade. It is sometimes called “quality of design”. Product whose features are perceived as meeting customer needs to a superior degree are called “higher-grade” products. Income : It refers to the gross receipts from sale. Inspection : Inspection is an act of checking materials, parts, components or products at various stages in production and sorting out the faulty or defective items from the good items. Product : It is the output of any process. Product Auditing : Product auditing is an independent evaluation of product quality to determine its fitness for use and conformance to specifications. Quality : It is the totality of features and characteristics of a product or service that bear on its ability to satisfy stated or implied needs. Quality Assurance : All those planned or systematic actions necessary to provide adequate confidence that a product or service will satisfy for given requirements of quality. Quality Audit : A quality audit is an independent review conducted to compare some aspect of quality performance with a standard for that performance.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "21 Introduction to Quality Control Quality Control : Quality control is the process through which we measure the actual quality performance, compare it with standards and take corrective actions if there is a deviation. Quality Engineering : It is the branch of engineering, which deals with the principles and practices of product and service quality assurance and control. Quality Policy : The overall intention and direction of an organization as regards quality as formally expressed by top management. Service : Service is work performed for someone else. The service may be provided to consumer (e.g. haircutting), to an institution (e.g., security service) or to both. Specification : The document that prescribes the requirements with which the product or service has to conform. Testing : A means of determining the capability of an item to meet specified requirements by subjecting the item to a set of physical, chemical, environmental, or operating actions and conditions. 1.10 ANSWERS TO SAQs SAQ 2 (b) 90% Answer to all other SAQs may be found in the text. QUALITY SYSTEMS Quality has been a very important activity from the dawn of civilization. The goal of high quality is common to all countries. As the international trade is increasing, need for standardization of products and maintaining their quality is increasing. Application of managerial tools to quality planning and quality control has gained much importance. Early system of managing for quality was dependent on factory workers supervised by a foreman. Quality was assured through the skills of the workers supplemented by supervisory audit or by a departmental inspection. Top management involvement was limited. Gradually, inspection departments took the form of quality control and quality assurance departments. After World War II, the Japanese embarked on a course of", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "22 reaching national goals by trade rather than by military means. The Japanese adopted a variety of strategies for improving the quality. The upper managers personally took charge of leading the quality revolution. The functionaries at all levels underwent training in managing for quality. Quality improvement was undertaken at a continuing, revolutionary pace. Other nations started emulating Japanese way of managing quality. Today Total Quality Management (TQM) has become a keyword. Quality Systems In 1987, ISO 9000 standards were introduced. These pertain not to the quality of product but to the practice of quality assurance methods within a company. ISO 9000 covers varied activities such as design, manufacturing, software development, service activities, auditing and methods for improving quality on the shop floor. This block on Quality System contains four units. Unit 1 provides an introduction to quality. Unit 2 presents information on total quality management and different approaches. It also provides an idea of zero defect program, Poka-Yoke and concurrent engineering. Unit 3 describes various tools of quality. Unit 4 is about the role of ISO and OSHA. QUALITY ENGINEERING Quality is all of the features and characteristics of a product or service that contribute to the satisfaction of a customer’s needs. These needs involve price, safety, availability, maintainability, reliability and usability. Price is easily defined by the amount of money to be spent for purchasing a particular product. The other needs are defined by translating the features and characteristics for the manufacturer of a product or the delivery of a service into specifications. Conformance of the product or service to these specifications is measurable and provides a quantifiable definition of quality. Therefore, simply stated, quality is conformance to specifications and the degree of conformance is the measure of quality.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "23 Introduction to Quality Control From the viewpoint of the customer, who has to use the product or service delivered to him, it is fitness for use. From the manufacturer’s point of view, it is conformance to those specifications that would result in customer satisfaction ultimately. In very general terms, quality may also be defined as affordable excellence. Quality then is simply meaning the customers requirements. Now let us try to understand more about quality through the following definitions : • Fitness for the purpose or use. • The totality of features and characteristics of a product or service that beer or its ability to satisfy stated or implied needs. • Quality should be aimed at the needs of the consumer, present, and future. • The total composite product and service characteristics of marketing, engineering, manufacturing and maintenance through which the product and service in use will meet the expectation by the customer. • Conformance to requirements. We shall be dealing more about Quality Engineering aspects in the course. The course comprises four blocks. Block 1 deals with Quality Systems. In this block, you will be introduced to quality control as well as concept of TQM. It is well known that happy people alone can produce outstanding quality. ISO 9000 standards are quality management systems, standards which are the bases for implementation assessment and verification of the quality system in the production as well as service organization. The standards include a number of ‘Good practices’ which must be followed to ensure the effectiveness of the quality system. Block 2 will introduce you the Concept of Quality Tools-Statistical. Statistical quality control is a branch of quality control. It is the collection, analysis, and interpretation of data for use in quality control activities. We shall learn more about Quality Tools and others in Block 3. The evolution of quality management stimulated, at least in part, by the impact of the demand by customer for higher quality conformance levels and the increasing competitiveness in world markets has resulted in the need to address quality management in all aspects of a business, including customers and suppliers. This development is known as total quality management (TQM). In Block 4, you will see by these statements that TQM is a philosophy and set of guiding principles for managing an organisation.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl1-19.webp"}]}, {"id": "sdl2", "file": "SDL/2. QUALITY MANAGEMENT - Week 2 - Unit 2 - Concept of Total Quality Management.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "23 Concepts of Total Quality Management UNIT 2 CONCEPTS OF TOTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT Structure 2.1 Introduction Objectives 2.2 What is Total Quality Management? 2.3 Principle of Objectives 2.4 Reasons for Popularity of TQM 2.5 Axioms of TQM 2.6 Valuable Tools for Quality 2.7 Concepts of Kaizen in TQM 2.7.1 Total Quality Control 2.7.2 Kanban Just-in-Time System 2.7.3 Quality Circles 2.7.4 Total Predictive Maintenance 2.8 Deming Approach to TQM 2.8.1 14 Points to Quality Management 2.8.2 The Seven Deadly Sins 2.8.3 The PDCA Cycle 2.9 Juran on Quality 2.9.1 Quality Trilogy Model 2.9.2 Achieving Quality through Breakthrough 2.10 Crosby and Quality Treatment 2.10.1 The Zero Defect Concept 2.10.2 The Four Absolutes of Quality 2.10.3 Crosby’s 14 Points for Quality Improvement 2.11 Steps in Implementing Zero Defect Program 2.12 Poka-Yoke 2.13 Concurrent Engineering 2.14 Summary 2.15 Key Words 2.16 Answers to SAQs 2.1 INTRODUCTION Total Quality Management (TQM) bursts on the international scene in the early 1980s. Individuals such as Juran and Deming achieved guru status by extolling its virtues. Edward Deming introduced the process of statistical measures during the period 1939-45 to control quality. He showed that process operator could eliminate 15% of the causes of variation and that the management was accountable for remaining 85% of the problems. However, American managers did not appreciate this conclusion initially. Deming philosophy was, however, taken seriously by the Japanese industrialists and they applied his teachings enthusiastically. The outcome was turnaround of Japan’s industrial", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "24 Quality Systems fortunes. Since then western managements are attempting to follow the Japanese example by adopting a consistent approach to quality. This new mantra that a consistent and persistent investment in quality will eventually pay off is deeply imbibed in the philosophy of TQM. Objectives After studying this unit, you should be able to • understand the features of TQM, • explain the axioms of TQM through Joiners triangle, • know the tools for improving quality, and • implement zero defect programme. 2.2 WHAT IS TOTAL QUALITY MANAGEMENT? Total quality management refers to the total involvement of staff in an organization together in bringing about quality satisfaction by promoting a participating culture. The fundamental point in TQM is the definition of a customer. Earlier definitions of a customer included one who was impacted by the product. TQM philosophy extends the concept of impact to every individual or department within the organization. The external customer is only the final part in the customer chain. This makes everyone a customer of, and a supplier to, someone else in the chain. This new definition of customer asks for altogether different outlook to the question about responsibility of quality. Since quality inputs and outputs are demanded and expected at every point on the chain, no one is spared from performing the quality function. In fact quality becomes a routine duty of every individual. This is the new culture of TQM. There are interesting features in this work culture. Firstly, that in problem solving efforts, TQM transcends across organizational hierarchial boundaries. This implies that manager and the worker are equally involved in decision making and performing a job. This also implies that both of them have a potential to introduce creativity and innovation in problem solving. Secondly, TQM invokes the leaders of an organization to create a vision and establish a mission. The vision is the hope of a better tomorrow and even better, of being the number one, the best in the class tomorrow. The top managers themselves are the advocate of change and also the motivators. The process of change includes commitment to the technical challenges (new methods and techniques) and social tasks. This is called strategic planning. Thirdly, TQM gives a sense of ownership to all the employees thus empowering them to make decisions. This motivates the morale of the people in customer chain and in effect, breeds teamwork too. And lastly, TQM philosophy advocates that tools through which quality is achieved; technology, methods and innovators, are always updating and improving. Thus, an organization practicing TQM achieves quality with a consistent and persistent effort 2.3 PRINCIPLE OF OBJECTIVES TQM employs a common sense system for measuring and analyzing a given process in order to find a better way of doing work. TQM should help to do a better job by : (i) Bringing people together for discussion and exchange of ideas.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "25 Concepts of Total Quality Management (ii) Developing an alert open-minded, questioning attitude on the part of all employees. (iii) Uncovering and eliminating wasteful and time-consuming methods, thereby leaving more time for the development of effective procedures. (iv) Eliminating fear at all levels of organization. The nature of current worldwide competition generally demands from any corporation four ability characteristics. (i) To understand what the customers want and to provide it, immediately on demand, at the lowest cost. (ii) To provide product and services of high quality and reliability. (iii) To keep up with the pace of change, technological as well as political and social. (iv) To be one step ahead of the customer’s needs; that is, to predict what the customer will want one year or ten years from now. The principal objectives of TQM catering to the above characteristics are : (i) To monitor performance and customers satisfaction levels. (ii) To identify improvements necessary in the customer interface. (iii) To deliver improved products and services at the lowest cost. (iv) To assess and agree to the customer’s requirement. (v) To tailor output to the customer’s demand. There are two salient features of TQM that make it unique : (i) Continuous Improvement and Innovation : This helps to enhance the value of the organization’s operation and output. Continuous improvement means that new products are launched or existing products and services are improved. This can be accomplished by • reducing errors, defects and waste, • improving responsiveness and cycle time performance, and • improving productivity and effectiveness in use of all resources. (ii) Application of Quality Methods : In TQM culture, quality methods are applied to entire organization, from management, to accounting, to manufacturing, service, and every other sub-entity in the organization. This ensures that every one in customer chain within an organization understands the company motives. 2.4 REASONS FOR POPULARITY OF TQM Total quality management has become the synonym of the success for most of the organizations in the world and this is gaining popularity day by day. The reasons can be attributed to the beneficial consequences of TQM. Guaranteed Customer Satisfaction and Increased Market Share In TQM culture, the customer is the most important part of process line and if process quality is attained, customer satisfaction is guaranteed. Quality product and services ensure not only the customer’s future return but also fewer complaints and lower warranty costs. Higher profits improve reputation and hence increased market share. Cost Reduction", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "26 Quality Systems Decrease in cost under warranty is not the only cost reduction to be achieved. The higher the quality, the lower the number of inferior products. The reasons for reduction in cost due to high quality are : • less consumption of energy and saving of working hours, • less replacement of materials, and • less stoppage of machines for inspection of final output. The effect of cost reduction leads to prompt deliveries and higher productivity levels, and simultaneously lower cost for repair and reworking. Conducive Environment TQM provides an environment where fear is eliminated. When fear is absent, it leads to the consequences where : • employees take pride in their work because they can admit to their mistakes and provide opinion, • employees feel respected and accepted because they feel as part of the team, and • employees strive not only for their own interest but also for interest of organization. New Definition to Success Success in a TQM practiced organization is measured not only by customer satisfaction, profit and market share, but also by • improved employee morale, • efficiency in operation, • fewer complaints and improved industrial relations, and • happier working environment. On the whole, investment in continuous total quality improvement will guarantee the survival and propriety of the business, the protection of investment, future dividends and more jobs. 2.5 AXIOMS OF TQM The essential elements of TQM as a process-oriented approach can be represented by only three main principles. (i) Commitment (to never ending quality improvement & innovation). (ii) Scientific knowledge (of the proper tools and techniques for the ‘technical change’). (iii) Involvement (all in one team for the social change). Also called the axioms of TQM, the above three fundamental characteristics are of equal significance and hence can be represented at the corners of an equilateral triangle (also called Joiner’s Triangle) as shown in Figure 2.1. Axiom 1: Commitment", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "27 Concepts of Total Quality Management Axiom 3: Involvement Axiom 2: Scientific Knowledge Figure 2.1: Joiner’s Triangle of TQM Axioms With the help of these cornerstones, the realization of the TQM culture is possible. The interdependence of these cornerstones makes it easy to characterize the TQM environment fully, as well as to define it clearly. Commitment When TQM is being practiced within the organization, the aim of organization is to be the best in its kind. For this it needs a change in attitudes and culture, something that is impossible unless there is a real commitment to quality and innovation from the senior management. The management must regard the workforce as an asset and must be willing to invest in the future development of workforce. However the commitment should not be restricted in the form of slogans and meetings only. Slogans demanding quality from everyone are not likely to achieve anything but frustration, anxiety and isolation. Some of the ways to motivate the workforce include : • proper working conditions, • adequate education and training, good communication and cooperation, • modern leadership rather than strict supervision, • good incoming material and equipment, appropriate quality tool, and • job satisfaction and incentives to workforce for good work. Senior managers should be the ones to plan, initiate and coordinate the quality improvement process and keep up the momentum when enthusiasm dies down. Thus, a management commitment to continually improving the quality of products and service is an obvious necessity. Scientific Knowledge The best way forward for improving processes is use of scientific approach, not opinion or emotion. A proper scientific approach helps managers focus their attention on individuals and take decisions based on facts unlike conventional wisdom. Scientific procedures like statistical methods help exploit information generated by processes and are used to anticipate, identify and correct mistakes. The main advantages of scientific knowledge are as follows : (i) It provides a common language, which can facilitate communication between the different departments and between different individuals. (ii) Scientific knowledge helps employees in owning the responsibility for quality and certain quality problems as they arise. The role of TQM quality department should be that of co-ordination, education and support of scientific quality tools throughout the organization. Additional duties should include continuous research and development of innovative methods or further development of existing ones. Involvement This cornerstone is concerned with the social change of feeling that all are part of one team in the organization. The capacity of problem solving increases many times when there is a common effort. Due to synergy, the output of the group is much more than the individual output of its members. Employees will contribute and innovate if they know that genuine effort will always be successful in their endeavours; this is because teamwork provides a strong capacity to absorb occasional failures. The team spirit efforts should extend outside the company, to include the subcontractors and suppliers.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "28 Quality Systems 2.6 VALUABLE TOOLS FOR QUALITY At present there are more than 40 quality tools. In this section, seven most commonly used quality tools used in conjunction with TQM shall be discussed. Cause and Effect Diagram A cause and effect diagram (Figure 2.2) is used when the need is to identify, explore, and display the possible causes of a specific problem or condition. They are also called “Ishikawa” diagrams after its originator or “Fishbone” diagram because of its shape. Major causes (A, B, C, D, E) are the primary causes. Contributing factors or minor problems (A1, B1) within a major cause are listed as the small “bones” attached to the large “bones”. Figure 2.2: Ishikawa Diagram Flowchart A flowchart is a pictorial representation showing all the steps of a process. A flowchart consists of a various symbols arranged in a sequential order that represents the flow of information. Flowcharts are used to document a process. By creating and studying flowcharts the management can often uncover sources of trouble as well as solution to problems. The meanings of some symbols are described below. 1. The activity symbol is a rectangle that designates an activity. 1 2. The decision symbol is a diamond which designates point from which the process branches into two more paths. The path taken depend on the answer to question which appears inside diamond. 2 3. The terminal symbol is a rounded rectangle which identifies the beginning or end of a process. 3 4. The document symbol represents a special task to the process. 4 5. The flow line represents a process with which we connect process elements, e.g., activities, decisions, etc. 5 Pareto Chart The pareto analysis is a chart based on the pareto principle. This principle states that the major part of the problems may be due to a small number of causes. For example, 80% of the problems can be traced to 20% of the causes. The chart shows the factors or causes arranged in descending order of magnitude from left to right. This helps to determine which problem to solve in what order. For example, Figure 2.3 shows that out of the five causes of defects in casting, the majority of Problem to be Solved Major Problem B Major Problem A Major Problem E Major Problem D Major Problem C Minor Problem B1 Minor Problem", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "29 Concepts of Total Quality Management 50 40 30 20 10 0 1 2 3 4 5 Causes of Failure (Defects) Total Rejected castings are rejected due to blowholes. Hence, this problem needs to be tackled first because it causes the most defects. Figure 2.3 : Pareto Diagram Run Chart A run chart (Figure 2.4) is used to monitor a process to see whether or not the long-range average is changing. This is the simplest tool to construct and use. Figure 2.4: Run Chart Control Chart A control chart is simply a run chart with statistically determined upper and lower control limit lines (UCL and LCL, respectively) drawn on either side of the process average. Being in control simply means that the process is consistent. This is observed in chart when no data points fall outside the control lines. The control limits are calculated as follows. Calculate arithmetic mean for the means of ‘n’ samples. If the mean of ith sample is Xi, then the mean of means, called grand mean, is given by n X X X X X n) ... ( 3 2 1 + + + + = Calculate the standard deviation, n X X X X X X s n 2 2 2 2 1 ) ( ... ) ( ) ( − + + − + − = Calculate the upper control limit, UCL = s X 3 + Calculate the lower control limit, LCL = s X 3 − Figure 2.5 shows a typical control chart. 1. Blow holes 50% 2. Hard spots 30% 3. Scabs 10% 4. Shifts 7% 5. Cold shuts 4%", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "30 Quality Systems Figure 2.5 : Control Charts with Lower and Upper Limits Correlation Chart A correlation (Figure 2.6) chart measures the performance of one factor compared to another and helps determine what relationship, if any, exists. (a) Positive relationship (b) No relationship (c) Negative relationship Figure 2.6: Correlation Chart Histogram A histogram is a bar graph that gives a pictorial representation of data. The intervals are shown on the X-axis and the number of scores in each interval is represented by the height of a rectangle located above the interval. The following example illustrates how to construct a histogram. The data below are the spelling test scores for 20 students on a 50 word-spelling test. The scores (number correct) are: 48, 49, 50, 46, 47, 47, 35, 38, 40, 42, 45, 47, 48, 44, 43, 46, 45, 42, 43, 47. The largest number is 50 and the smallest is 35. Then, the range, R = 50 − 35 = 15. The 20 students are divided into 5 classes. Hence the interval width = R/5 =15/5 = 3. The first class has a lower limit of 35. Therefore, the first upper limit is 35+3 or 38. The second class will have a lower limit of 38 and an upper limit of 41. Data in tabular form is shown in Table 2.1. Corresponding histogram is shown in Figure 2.7. Table 2.1 : Frequency Table Class Lower Limit Upper Limit Frequency 1 35 38 1 2 38 41 2", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "31 Concepts of Total Quality Management 3 41 44 4 4 44 47 5 5 47 50 8 Figure 2.7 : Histogram 2.7 CONCEPT OF KAIZEN IN TQM Kaizen is a new concept in modern quality, which is necessary to understand and implement TQM with positive results. Kaizen is a Japanese word, which literally means gradual, continuous improvement mainly in small matters, in all aspect of working life. Kaizen signifies the constant and gradual improvement no matter how small, which should be taking place all time, in every process, involving everyone from all the ranks of management and workforce. Kaizen begins with the recognition that any corporation can have problems and improvements can be made as long as perfection is lacking. The distinct features inherent in the kaizen philosophy are as follows : (i) The uniqueness of Kaizen is that it solves problems by establishing a culture in which everyone can freely admit to problems. In any organizational environment, the kaizen approach can help towards continuous improvement by contribution of small changes throughout the organization. (ii) There is urgency about the never-ending effort for improvement and gradual change for better. Repetitiveness is considered as negative and as an inhibitor to progress. (iii) There is an emphasis on the process rather than on the output. This is based on the realization that mass inspection of the output is a non-value added activity, incapable of improving quality. A management-oriented kaizen is crucial if the morale and the momentum are to be kept up. Kaizen includes many practices that are outlined below. 2.7.1 Total Quality Control Total Quality Control (TQC) is the high road to kaizen. TQC categorizes quality cost into three major categories, i.e. failure costs, appraisal cost and prevention cost and manages them separately. The underlying principle was that investment in proper quality planning and prevention activities can lead to a substantial reduction in the failure costs (scrap, reworking, firefighting etc.) and even in appraisal cost (inspection, audit etc.). Following are the failure costs: Internal Failure Costs These are the costs associated with defects that are found prior to transfer of products to the market or customer. Examples include scrap, spoilage, defectives and rework. Some other costs that contribute to internal costs are :", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "32 Quality Systems (i) Failure Analysis Costs : Cost of analyzing defective product to determine causes. (ii) 100% Sorting Costs : Cost of finding defective items in a lot with unacceptably high level of defects. External Failure Costs These are the costs associated with defects that are found after product is shipped to the customer. The components of external cost are : (i) Warranty Charges : Costs in replacing or making repairs to product that are within warranty period. (ii) Complaint Adjustment : Cost of investigation of complaints attributed to defective product or installation. (iii) Allowances : Costs of concessions made to customer due to substandard products. Appraisal Costs These are the costs incurred to determine the degree of conformance to quality requirements. These include : (i) Incoming Inspection : Costs of determining the quality of purchased product by inspection on receipt or by inspection at source. (ii) Final Inspection and Test : Costs of evaluation of conformance to requirements. (iii) Product Quality Audits : Costs of performing quality audits on in-process or finished product. Costs of maintaining instruments and equipments. (iv) Cost of maintaining instruments and equipment in calibration. Prevention Costs These are the costs incurred to keep failure and appraisal costs to a minimum. The various techniques used to minimize failure or appraisal costs are : quality planning, process planning and control, audits, quality training, supplier quality evaluation and products review. 2.7.2 Kanban Just-in-time System Kanban is a communication procedure in the production process aimed at elimination of all waste by just-in-time collection, production and delivery. Just-in-time or JIT is a discipline organized to improve quality and productivity through the elimination of all waste. Its goals are : (i) Maximize customer service levels through delivery, quality, price, design and flexibility. (ii) Minimize cost to the corporation through overhead, material cost, scrap, inventory holding cost, and productivity. Kanban is a special method of achieving JIT production and scheduling. It literally means “card” and is sometimes referred to as the “pull” system. The kanban system was pioneered by Taiichi Ohno in Toyota’s plant in order to reduce unnecessary waste. The different levels of waste include overproduction, waste in motion and transportation of units, waste in processing and machine time, waste in inventory and waste in defects. The process of Kanban involves pulling parts and replacing with cards through the manufacturing sequence as needed. There are two types of kanban – withdrawal and a production ordering. For example, A, B and C are the final products of a factory, each", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "33 Concepts of Total Quality Management requiring a definite number of ‘a’ and ‘b’ parts (screws and washers). The worker producing A goes to the machining line where parts ‘a’ are being prepared. If A requires 3 number of a’s, he withdraws 3 from machining line and replace with 3 production ordering kanbans. This kanban is picked up by a worker from machining line and this serves as an order to produce 3 parts of ‘a’. The withdrawal kanban shows the quantity of items that subsequent process should withdraw from the preceding one. The production ordering kanban shows the quantity that preceding process should produce. Machining Line Assembling Line Figure 2.8 : Kanban in the Production Process The benefits of kanban are : (i) Necessary parts are received and delivered promptly only when they are needed, cutting down on waste, storing space and in inventory. (ii) Elimination of non-value added activities and waste movement of materials. (iii) Kanban helps in changing the plant and machinery layout for better efficiency. If necessary, whole factory space can be redesigned. 2.7.3 Quality Circles These are informal groups whose members meet occasionally on a voluntary basis, with the main purpose of problem solving. A circle usually consists of a maximum 10 members and their supervisor who works as a leader. Quality circles aim towards the educational development of their members. The members of these groups are people from management and the workforce, as well as supplier and subcontractors. These members are initially subjected to a training course. The course consists of : (i) Study of simple problem solving technique like brainstorming, cause and effect diagrams, histograms, control charts, etc. (ii) Study of reports of improvement projects carried out by other QC circles. (iii) Participation in an actual project and implementation of the lessons. The underlying principle guiding the working of a quality circle is that the members of a QC are the best to know about the problem. Hence, with a little management tactics, they can solve the problem on their own. The benefits arising of working through a quality circle are many, but they can be regarded to fall in two categories : measurable savings and improvement in the attitudes and behaviour of workers. (i) Since people work together on an equal basis; this strengthens team spirit and improves morale and communication. (ii) It promotes initiatives and substantially develops the ability to solve problems. The effects of QC fall into three categories : (i) Effect on characteristics of individual. (ii) Effect on relationship among individuals. (iii) Effect on attitudes of workers toward the organization. 2.7.4 Total Predictive Maintenance (TPM) TPM is defined as activities aimed at : (i) eliminating breakdown defects, and all other equipment losses,", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "34 Quality Systems (ii) gradually increasing equipment effectiveness, (iii) improving company profits, and (iv) creating a satisfying workplace environment. TPM emphasizes on effective use of existing resources to minimize waste. This is a preventive approach to bring in changes. TPM can be an important cost saver because : • further capital investment of new machines can be avoided, • time consuming activities like breakdowns, repairs etc. can be prevented, and • simple maintenance activities like oiling equipment on a routine basis, improve the plant condition. TPM programs depend on simple skills and include daily checking, equipment tuning, adjustments, trouble-shooting and repair. The above-discussed four Kaizen practices act as a versatile tool because : (i) It assists in identification and elimination of sources of potential problems. (ii) At any time an indication of the current capability of the equipment can be assessed. The kaizen improvement process is in sharp contrast with the type of improvement that is usually the result of innovation. Top management’s responsibility is however to maintain a balance between the two. Table 2.2 compares these two approaches toward quality improvement. Table 2.2 : Comparison of Innovative and Kaizen Approaches to Improvement Innovation Kaizen 1. Innovation is a short-term, dramatic improvement 1. Kaizen implies slow, step-by-step improvement. 2. Requires a large initial investment but little effort to maintain. 2. Requires effort to maintain but minimal investment. 3. Innovation is result-oriented 3. Kaizen is process-oriented. 4. It is a prerequisite for achieving and sustaining superior company performance. 4. It is a prerequisite to exceptional care for the customer, through superior quality and service for the external customer, and through proper leadership for internal customer. 2.8 DEMING APPROACH TO TQM Dr. W. Edwards Deming was a statistician by training and largely involved in rebuilding of Japanese industry after World War II. He is largely known for his application of statistical theory to manufacturing variation. Deming defines TQM as “A culture inherent in which there is total commitment to the quality and an attitude expressed by everybody’s involvement in the process of continuous improvement of products and services through the use of innovative scientific methods”. Deming’s contribution to TQM is listed in his 14 points to quality improvement, seven deadly sins and PDCA cycle.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "35 Concepts of Total Quality Management 2.8.1 14 Points to Quality Management The principles by which TQM can be incorporated into an organization is given by Deming’s 14 points for Transformation of Management. These include Create Constancy of Purpose Create constancy of purpose towards improvement of product and service with the aim to become competitive, to stay in business, and to provide jobs. The management must take immediate action on any deviation from the drive towards achieving constancy. Adopt a New Philosophy A change is necessary in the old management methods, which are no longer effective for today’s business environment. If current management practices are not in tandem with the type of business then the cost is bound to be loss of competitive position. Cease Dependence on Mass Inspection to Achieve Quality Build quality into the product in the first place. If possible, everything has to be done right at first time so that there is no need for rectification later. There is a need to replace the practices of mass inspection and defect detection with practices of defect prevention and continuous improvement End the Practice of Awarding Business on the Basis of Price Alone The low price of incoming materials does not ensure performance. It is possible that the future supplies are not consistent as regards quality level. Therefore, it is always worthwhile to stick to a single supplier for any one item in a long-term business association of loyalty and trust. Constantly Improve the System of Production and Service The company should always try to reduce variability in order to improve quality and productivity constantly and decrease costs. The manager should keep an eye on the system, maintenance and process improvement and training. He should anticipate trouble and not become content. Institute Training on the Job Training and education are the cornerstones of consistency. Investment in proper training of all new employees including managers will ensure that newcomers will immediately start contributing to their full capacity. Adopt and Institute Methods of Supervision and Leadership The aim of supervision should be to help people and machines do a better job, that employees take more interest in work and come forward in handling responsibilities. Modern leadership means that people leading the company must show the employees ways of improving productivity; the leaders should be a motivating factor; they should teach and counsel rather than judge. Drive Out Fear In order that everyone works effectively and productively for the company, a two-way communication should be encouraged. When fear is driven away, it leads to successful joint working relationships, based on respect and cooperation. An atmosphere of fear is always counter-productive and reduces competitiveness and innovation. Break Down Barriers between Departments and Individuals", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "36 Quality Systems People from all departments contribute to a common goal of company and eventually success. This is possible when individual barriers breakdown and lead to mutual trust and cooperation. Eliminate Slogans Slogans and poster campaign demanding for new levels of excellence do not yield positive results because the worker cannot correct the causes of low quality and unsatisfactory productivity. No substantial improvement can be made through slogans unless management takes an initiative or employee training is done. Eliminate Numerical Goals or Work Standards The only way to increase quality and productivity is to replace work standards with competent leadership. If target is reasonable and eventually achieved, the employee involved will become complacent and put no more efforts. On the other hand if target is unreasonable, either it will not be attained or it will be attained by manipulating the figures and/or lowering standards. Thus, any methods relying on numerical goals are detrimental to the future of the whole company. End Annual Reviews or Merit Rating Deming strongly recommends the abolition of the annual merit rating because it destroys teamwork, increases variability in the performance of employee and focuses on short term. Besides, it causes excessive internal competition and isolation, reduces initiatives and risk-taking. Institute a Rigorous Program of Education and Self-improvement This refers to continuing re-education and self-improvement for everyone. Retraining is as essential as providing training to new employees because it gives the people a sense of security that their work carries a value. Re-education assures that if the position, the employee is currently holding, ceases to hold a value, then he would be able to change into a more valuable position within the company. Define Top Management’s Permanent Commitment to ever Improving Quality and Productivity Management should immediately take action to accomplish the transformation by implementing the preceding 13 points. Once a manager fully understands Deming’s philosophy, he will become capable of coordinating actions for continuous improvement. 2.8.2 The Seven Deadly Sins Deming, in addition to putting forward his philosophy in the 14 points, also identified seven deadly diseases that, if unattended, cause harm in the organization and prevent it from becoming a “world-class” organization. The seven deadly sins are : Lack of Constancy A constancy of purpose provides the company charter for planning of products and services that will have a market and keep the company in business and provide jobs. The absence of constancy of purpose has a bad effect on the overall environment of organization. Short-term Profits An emphasis on short-term profits is accompanied by short-term thinking. Profits can be obtained by cutting down and training on investments originally intended for employee benefits. The reasons of such action are: fear of unfriendly takeover and push from investors for dividends.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "37 Concepts of Total Quality Management Performance Appraisal The meaning of performance appraisal is evaluation of performance by merit rating. If the merit is based on system parameters that are against the norms and ethics of present and without comparison to standard reference, the effects could be devastating. Rewards based on short-term performance destroy long-term planning, builds fear, demolishes teamwork, nourishes rivalry and politics. Often, a feeling of depression is inevitable making the employee unfit for work for weeks after receipt of rating. Job-hopping Job-hopping refers to the constant change in management due to continuous replacement or exchange of people in leadership positions. This leaves everyone wondering about stability, having to deal with new style of leadership and changes in direction. Use of Visible Figures Only When a company presents its progress report, it is often subjected to manipulation. One can cut down on research, education, training and quality improvement efforts in order to show profits. The use of only visible figures do not present the true image of the company’s standing in the market. For example, what are the figures for failing to satisfy the customer, improving quality and productivity etc. Excessive Medical Costs The presence of excessive medical costs, unsafe products, unsafe processes, unsafe work place, and job stress puts a negative effect to the work attitude. Excessive Costs of Liability The liability of legal requirements to be met during work costs a lot of time to the worker. This puts him under psychological pressure and he is often involved more with dealing how to complete the legalities. 2.8.3 The PDCA Cycle There is an ongoing cycle of activities that Deming suggests as a procedure to assist in the establishment and long-term existence of a quality organization. The cycle, called the Deming cycle (or Shewhart cycle), consists of four main stages, one following another in a specific order that is repeated continuously. This is shown in Figure 2.9. The name PDCA cycle is often used as an acronym for all four stages: plan, do, check, act. Figure 2.9 : PDCA Cycle PDCA cycle reflects the basis of a self-sustaining quality programme. It should drive any process or activity in the company, howsoever small. The four elements are explained below. Plan The process of improvement should start with careful planning of the route of action. This could involve a great deal of understanding, documenting, evaluating and assessing the tools to be used for an effective quality programme. Change", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "38 Quality Systems should be gradual and not for the sake of change. At this stage, decisions should be taken on the objectives to aim for, the changes that are needed, the types of performance measure to be used, and who should be responsible for what. Also, a determination should be made of the availability of resources or data, the possible implications of the actions and the contingency measures to be taken when something goes wrong. Do Implementation of the plan should follow with the involvement of everybody. This will include training in scientific methods, survey of the customer’s needs and expectations, identification of core processes, collection of statistical information, understanding of process control and variation, identification of projects with improvement objectives, formation of project teams and communication of resulting successes. At this stage, small-scale implementation of any planned change will take place and resulting data will be collected. Check Checking how the plan is evolving is an important part of the cycle. This involves measuring and observing the ‘effects’ of any change or test carried out at the do stage, analysis of results, feedback and review. Using actual data, deviations from original expectations should be evaluated and investigated. Act Corrective action can follow, based on the lessons learned so far. A study of the current situation should be made, standardizing any improvements and properly documenting any good new processes and innovative ideas. The review at the checking stage can be used as a learning process, so that knowledge is built into a new plan. Planning requires prediction; the result of any change at the action stage can enhance the ability to predict in the future. The Deming cycle of Planning, Implementation, Checking, Action, (New) Plan is thus ready to start again. This should never stop. SAQ 1 (a) What are the differences between Kaizen and Innovation? (b) What is a PDCA cycle? 2.9 JURAN ON QUALITY Dr. Joseph Juran was one of the first persons to incorporate the involvement of people within quality. Juran’s point was that organizations were well aware of the aspects of quality control but did not know how to manage for quality. The ideas for which he was best known are: (i) Top management involvement. (ii) Placed emphasis on training process supervision and operators.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "39 Concepts of Total Quality Management (iii) The application of Pareto principle. (iv) The project-by-project approach to quality improvement. Juran places more of the quality responsibilities to middle management and quality professionals. The role of upper managers is restricted to guidance and policy making. Management for quality is done by the use of three managerial processes – planning, control and improvement which are three members of Juran’s Quality Trilogy model. 2.9.1 Quality Trilogy Model The model is shown in Figure 2.10. Figure 2.10 : Sequence of Events in Quality Trilogy Model Quality Planning The important aspects in planning for quality are (i) Identifying the customer and needs. (ii) Translating the customer’s needs into a language that every employee of organization can understand, and develop a product. (iii) Optimizing the product by developing and optimizing the process. (iv) Once operating conditions have been established and proved as optimal, transferring the process to operate. Cost considerations play important role in planning. Quality Control Control is the process of detecting and correcting adverse changes. Changes occur inadvertently due to effect of negative influence on the set level of quality. It is crucial to take action when standards are not met in the from of audits, control limits etc. Juran states that the majority (80%) of the current quality problems are the fault of poor management rather than poor workmanship on the factory floor. Only a process in control is predictable and amenable to further improvement. Quality Improvement Negative Influences Apply Process to Operation T f New Level of Quality Quality Plan • Who is customer? • What are his needs? • Convert needs into specifications • Develop and optimize product • Establish new operating Set Quality Control Present? Degradation of Quality Level Yes No Quality Improvement Schemes Higher Level of Quality", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "40 Quality Systems Juran believes that improvement can be realized by organized procedures and priority. To achieve a breakthrough, Juran suggests the following steps: (i) Accept the responsibility for making improvement. Management must be involved. (ii) Understand the universal sequence of events for making the improvements. (iii) Apply the universal sequence to actual company problems. 2.9.2 Achieving Quality through Breakthrough Juran emphasizes that quality results can be attained by making a breakthrough. A breakthrough indicates a “beneficial change” from status quo. Such a change is needed for overcoming chronic quality problems. The organization often are busy in fire fighting to overcome sporadic quality problems but would be immensely benefited if chronic quality problems are removed. But quality does not happen by accident. It has to follow a sequence of plan. Juran dictates the following steps by which this universal breakthrough sequence is to be carried out in an organization. Proof of the Need Appropriate level of management should be convinced of the significance of the quality issue which will require a new approach. The top management easily understands a proposal in terms of money. For example, the problem of waste should be converted into money lost in order to provide a proof to the management, i.e. the management must acknowledge that a lot of waste is being generated under the current practices of operation. Project Identification The only way of achieving a breakthrough is by converting chronic problems into specific projects. The project provides a way of separating budgets and facilities to conduct experiments. In order to correctly identify project, prioritizing is done by pareto analysis (discussed in Section 2.6.3). Organization for Improvement This stage requires that clear responsibilities are established for three levels of activities : (i) Guiding improvement programmes that are done by a team of upper managers. (ii) Project-by-project improvement. (iii) Analyzing each project. The Diagnostic Journey The diagnosis involves discovering the causes of defect by proper experimentation, data collection, interpretation of results and finally testing any theories. Errors are bound to occur either from operator side or management side or both. Juran categorizes them as : (i) Inadvertent errors if these are unintentional and unpredictable; beyond the realm of operator awareness. (ii) Those errors arising out of lack of knowledge are technical errors. (iii) The last one is willful errors so called because operator makes intentionally and persistently and is aware of making the errors but ignores. Remedial Action", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "41 Concepts of Total Quality Management For problems arising due to operator controllable error, Juran suggests the remedies, some of which are as follows : (i) One should not give orders to other and both should take orders from the situation. (ii) Establish accountability. Find out who the worker is and who did the job. (iii) Provide assistance to workers in identifying defects from operator’s side. (iv) Reassign work by separating critical work from rest. (v) Improve communications among workers and supervisors to promote the mutual interests and understanding. (vi) After the problem is diagnosed, a proper choice to solution should be considered. The best method of doing this is to quantify for each alternative the cost to organization. (vii) Practice preventive maintenance. (viii) Practice open communication and feedback between different departments. Quick access to procedures, instructions, policies, keywords, specification etc. (ix) Establish clear responsibilities for quality-oriented decision. (x) Practice sampling instead of 100% inspection. (xi) Separate problems using Pareto principle. Resistance to Change There is a resistance to change. Juran recommends certain actions to overcome the social and culture factors. (i) Provide Participation : People who are affected by the change should be allowed to participate both in planning and in execution of change. This provides opportunity to evaluate merits as well as impact and threat to current beneficial practices. Participant should be treated with dignity so that favourable social climate is developed which makes change more acceptable. (ii) Provide Enough Time : In order to absorb and evaluate the merits and demerits of change, adequate time must be allowed. Conducting a small tryout of the yet-to-incorporate change reduces risks and provides the needed proof to the advocates of change as well as those giving resistance. Holding the Gains The final step in a breakthrough sequence is to hold the gains achieved so far. Operating forces must engage in control activities when out of control situations occur. The methods of doing this are • establishing new standards and making it known to operators of what is expected from them from now on, • training of operator so they know what exactly has been changed, and • establishing a new system of control consisting of early warning signals, techniques for analysis of data and rules for decision making and reporting. When a breakthrough in new level of performance has been achieved and gains have been held as the new standards, the sequence of event is ready to start again.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "42 Quality Systems 2.10 CROSBY AND QUALITY TREATMENT Philip B. Crosby was first to popularize quality for top management. He claimed that all quality improvements pay for themselves, i.e. that quality is free. Crosby’s most important contribution has been the development of a 14-point programme of quality improvement and zero defect concept. 2.10.1 Zero Defect Concept The zero defect concept has two meanings which are explained below. Defect-Free Product This concept has value as a long- range objective, since it implies the need for never-ending improvement. The concept rejects the idea that we can relax our efforts short of perfection. In contrast, if defect-free product is viewed as a short- range objective, it becomes counterproductive by shutting off efforts to reach attainable goals. Zero Defects as a Slogan In this meaning, the term is adopted as a kind of banner, which motivates the company to improve quality. In its best usage, this is comparable to adopting an appealing brand name to help sell a product. If the drive is well conceived (i.e., a “good” product), then a good brand name is assured. If the drive is ill-conceived (e.g., an attempt to solve the company’s quality problems by pressurizing the work force), then it will fail no matter how clever is the slogan. 2.10.2 The Four Absolutes of Quality Crosby provides the following four absolutes regarding quality. Quality Means Conformance, Not Elegance Quality is the conformance of a product or a service to requirements. The definition of quality can never make any sense unless it is based on exactly what the customer wants. As a consequence, the management has the following three tasks to perform. (i) Establish the requirements to be met and communicate them to all employees of organization. (ii) Provide appropriate tools and techniques, and the necessary training in them. (iii) Provide continuous support and management. The System of Quality is Prevention Before carrying out the operation, it is beneficial to identify all opportunities for error. At least a contingency plan can be drawn up so that if the problem really occurs, the damage is controlled and restricted to minimum possible. Inspection is inevitable. But a company that relies on mass inspection of the final output cannot achieve progress. The Measurement of Quality is the Price of Non-conformance The price of non-conformance (PONC) indicates the costs involved with doing wrong things – rejects, reworking, warranty costs etc. The sum costs of such actions amounts to 20-40% of the total operating costs. This is the reason why Crosby stressed that quality is more of a management function rather than a technical operation. When management realizes the damage done, it is prompted to apply professional quality functions, prevention efforts and quality education to bring down the loss. This cost of effort is called the cost of conformance or COC.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "43 Concepts of Total Quality Management Thus, a company whose products and services are rated as of high quality is paying a very low price of non-conformance. The Only Performance Standard is Zero Defects The concept of zero-defect is based on the fact that any deviation from target causes a loss. Hence, it is always cheaper to do the job right first time, on time, every time. Only that management, which continuously strives to produce a product free of any errors, meets the performance standard. 2.10.3 Crosby’s 14 Points for Quality Improvement According to Crosby, quality is a never-ending process. Crosby laid down the following 14-points to assist in the establishment of a quality ethics. Management Commitment Quality should be the first agenda of all management meetings. Senior management must demonstrate their commitment to quality by making clear how it is going to deliver defect free products and service on time. Quality Improvement Team A quality improvement team will consists of people who will assist in the changing of the system to enable quality to flourish. Its main task will be to • change the attitude and practices of those who run the company, • set up required educational activities, and • coordinate the efforts of team members and upper management. Measurement A clear method of measurement for every part of an activity should be decided. Once decision is taken, a level of reference is created, reasonable targets can be set, progress can be monitored and comparisons can be made. The Cost of Quality The management needs to monitor the cost of quality and then treat it as a positive item in the management process. If the costs are high, it indicates the returns made by current quality practices are not favourable. Those processes accounting for the maximum consumption of resources needs to be given prior treatment and modifications. Quality Awareness People should be continuously reminded about the management commitment to quality, training facilities. Crosby advocates the use of slogans or posters also as a reminder to the workforce of importance of quality. Corrective Action Any corrective action must be executed only after analysis of past data or determination of the cause so that the problem is treated permanently. The organization must be wary of undertaking any corrective action, which takes the form of reworking or replacement activity because it does not carry any value. Zero Defects Planning Proper planning is required for the concept of zero defect to be embedded in the company culture. It will have to start with a zero defect commitment from the top management. Crosby suggests the awareness training including the celebration of “Zero Defects Day”. Employee Education", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-21.webp"}, {"n": 22, "text": "44 Quality Systems The subjects addressed during education process are concerned with the company’s activities and ways of achieving quality. A classroom environment, where both workers and management participate, results in greater understanding and break down barriers leading to collaborative work. Crosby states six elements in the entire education process which is also referred to as 6 Cs. • Comprehension (understanding of what is necessary, abandonment of the old way of thinking and of outdated practices). • Commitment (management-led dedication to cultural change). • Competence (methodical and scientific implementation of improvement process). • Communication (complete cooperation throughout the production process, including suppliers and customers). • Correction (elimination of all causes of problems and prevention of new ones arising). • Continuance (never-ending effort for improvement). Zero Defects Day Crosby recommends the celebration of zero defects day to reward serious efforts. This act will be a reminder of importance of quality and as a demonstration of commitment towards zero defect principle. Goal Setting When it comes to quality, the ultimate goal is that of zero defects, and all intermediate goals should move in that direction. Error Cause Removal The permanent removal of causes of error requires a team effort. The identification of error is possible when a proper communication exists between the members of team. Communication ensures sharing of knowledge and experience. Each team member can contribute by submitting a defect cause removal proposal. Recognition Crosby considers recognition as a reference point to help the improvement efforts in right direction. The recognition process needs to be planned carefully and developed gradually. When an employee is decorated with a quality award for his efforts, it must set an example to others and not rivalry. Quality Council The people who define the mission, visions, values and policies necessary for managing the improvement process can be brought together in one group under the name of quality council. A council serves a common platform where they exchange their knowledge and the coordinated expertise can be effective in providing support to quality improvement teams in their efforts towards elimination of hassle and achievement of zero defects. Do it All Over Again TQM can happen if the effort is continuous. The process of learning, participating and experimenting with new methods should never end process. The process can gain speed and get embedded in the work culture when done again and again.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-22.webp"}, {"n": 23, "text": "45 Concepts of Total Quality Management SAQ 2 What is the difference between price of conformance and price of non- conformance? 2.11 STEPS IN IMPLEMENTING ZERO DEFECT PROGRAM The zero defect concept seeks the voluntary participation of all workers in understanding personal responsibility for the quality of task they perform. Zero Defect (ZD) programs can be implemented in the following ways. Personal Challenge The employees are challenged to adopt work culture and error-free performance. They are requested to voluntarily sign pledge cards – a promise that they will strive to observe the highest standards of quality and performance. Identification of Error-concept The problems which obstruct ZD aim are identified, defect sources traced out and their causes rectified. This phase provides employees with a background of the problem. They are encouraged to be alert and report potential sources of defects. Inspection The employee or worker is trained about specific quality points he is expected to monitor and encouraged to report any out-of-control conditions promptly. The use of ZD breakdown sheet is taught to him for error-free work performance. The sheet has two columns, one showing the logical sequence of operations and the other critical factors, which determine whether the job is being carried out without defect. Motivation The management should try to make the workers realize that ‘Quality is important to the company’s future and employee’s future, depends on the company’s future; therefore quality is important for every person working in the company. The following two methods are used for creating psychological climate favourable for quality mindset. Attract Attention With the help of slogans such as “Up with quality, Down with rejects”, letter to employee’s homes from company president, articles with pictures in newspaper etc. Generate Interest With the help of displays on how company products are used and highest quality workmanship award etc. A well-planned programme on quality education to highlight the impact of quality on the image of the company in the market is needed.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-23.webp"}, {"n": 24, "text": "46 Quality Systems 2.12 POKA-YOKE The concept of Poka-Yoke was pioneered by Shingeo Shingo of Japan to produce zero defect products. It is basically a mistake-proofing system. Originally called “fool proofing” and later changed to “mistake proofing”, poka-yoke translates into English as to avoid inadvertent errors. Such a system employs inspection actively to identify errors during the process. The result is a business that wastes less energy, time and resources. A poka- yoke employed in a production system works by detecting the errors and stopping the production. Only after finding out the cause of error and eliminating error, the production system is resumed. The use of poka-yoke in a production system is extremely valuable because it prevents the occurrence of the same error repeatedly. Poka-yoke uses a bunch of small devices to either detect or prevent defects. The device can be developed by anyone from manager to line employee. A poka-yoke device is any mechanism that either prevents a mistake from being made or makes the mistake obvious at a glance. The ability to find mistakes at a glance is essential. Suppose a person picks up a pen at a bank to sign a cheque. But when the top is depressed to extend the writing part the pen malfunctions because the inside spring is absent. A simple poka-yoke to prevent such a situation could have been built into the assembling process of pen. This is as follows : (i) Present all the pieces of pens in one dish and springs in another dish. (ii) The worker counts out springs from the dish, puts springs inside pen and places them in a small tray. (iii) After assembly is complete, if a spring remains in the dish, an error has occurred. The operator knows a spring has been omitted and can correct the omission immediately. The cost of this inspection (looking at the dish) is minimal, yet it effectively functions as a form of inspection. The cost of rework at this point is also minimal, although the preferred outcome is still to find the dish empty at the end of assembly and to avoid rework even when its cost is small. The effects of employing poka-yoke are observed in increased productivity and decrease of error rate. Poka-yoke devices are an effective alternative to demands for greater worker diligence. Hence, the morale and worker attitudes also improve when a poka-yoke is employed. There are certain disadvantages associated with installation of poka-yoke. • It demands a certain level of quality built into system. For example, if a production line is malfunctioning at several points, the in-built poka-yoke will cause a large number of stoppages of the line. This is not desirable, as productivity will be hampered. • The devices do not work properly if the production rate is very fast. This is because the devices need some time to respond to an error and if the production line moves rapidly, it is possible to bypass the error. For example, by the time, error in certain dimension of a component is noted and the information is conveyed to production line, many quantities of the product would have been produced. 2.13 CONCURRENT ENGINEERING Concurrent engineering requires performing the various steps and processes in managing a project in tandem rather than in sequence. This means that engineering; research and development, production, and marketing are all involved at the beginning of a project, before any work has been done. Combining concurrent engineering with TQM and project management may produce the following benefits", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-24.webp"}, {"n": 25, "text": "47 Concepts of Total Quality Management • New product development times are reduced. • Average life of the product is increased. • Sales and revenues are increased. • The number of customers increases. Organizations with separate methodologies for each process may end up with duplication of effort, duplication of resources and even facilities (Figure 2.11(a)). As organizations begin recognizing the synergetic effects of putting several of these under a single methodology, the first two process to become partially coupled are project management and TQM (Figure 2.11(b)). As the benefits of synergy and integration become apparent, organizations choose to integrate all the processes (Figure 2.11(c)). (a) (b) (c) Figure 2.11 : Integration of Project Management and TQM with Concurrent Engineering Managing a project in tandem rather than in a sequential manner is not easy and superior project control is required to avoid increasing the level of risk later in the project. The most serious risks are delays in bringing product to market and costs when reworking is needed due to poor planning or poor project control. Improved planning, scheduling and control are essential to modern project management, so it is obvious for top companies to integrate concurrent engineering, project management systems and TQM. 2.14 SUMMARY In this unit, an introduction to the various elements of TQM is provided. The views of Deming are explained through Joiner’s triangle, 14 steps to Quality Management and PDCA cycle. Juran’s road to improving quality is presented through Juran’s Trilogy Model. The concept of Zero Defect and 14 points of Quality Improvement form the basis of Crosby’s philosophy. In addition, a description of various quality tools were also explained. The practices of Kaizen and Poka-Yoke were addressed to show how productivity could be increased. Finally, the concepts of Concurrent Engineering were introduced to show the benefits of integrating different management practices in the quest to quality. 2.15 KEY WORDS Continuous Improvement : It means that new products are launched or existing products and services are improved. Control Limits : These are statistically determined limits, which indicate the maximum variability of a process. Ishikawa : A pictorial representation of possible causes (major and minor) of a specific problem in the form of a skeleton of fish. JIT : Just-in-time is a method of producing necessary parts in quantities needed at a particular time only. TQM Concurrent Engineering Project Management", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-25.webp"}, {"n": 26, "text": "48 Quality Systems Kaizen : The pursuit of continuous improvement through identifying and eliminating non-value added activity. Kanban : It is an instruction on card requiring the operator to produce the products. Pareto Principle : This principle states that the major part of the problems may be caused by small number of possible causes. PDCA : The continuous cycle of plan-do-check-act to assist in establishment and long terms existence of an organization. Poka-yoke : Poka-yoke, also called mistake proofing, is a simple method to prevent defects from occurring in your business processes. TQM : TQM is management and control activities based on the leadership of top management and based on the involvement of all employees and all departments from planning and development to sales and service. These management and control activities focus on quality assurance by which those qualities, which satisfy the customer, are built into products and services during the above processes and then offered to consumers. TQC : Total Quality Control is an effective system of integrating quality development, quality maintenance and quality improvement efforts of the various groups in an organization, so as to enable production and services at the most economical ways, which provides customer satisfaction. 2.16 ANSWERS TO SAQs Please refer the preceding text for Answers to SAQs.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl2-26.webp"}]}, {"id": "sdl3", "file": "SDL/3. QUALITY MANAGEMENT - Week 3 - Unit 3 - Tools of Quality.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "49 Tools of Quality UNIT 3 TOOLS OF QUALITY Structure 3.1 Introduction Objectives 3.2 Elements of TQM 3.3 Seven QC Tools for Improvement 3.3.1 Affinity Diagram 3.3.2 Tree Diagram 3.3.3 Interrelations Diagram 3.3.4 Prioritization Matrices 3.3.5 Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC) 3.3.6 Activity Network Diagram 3.3.7 Matrix Diagram 3.4 Implementation of TQM 3.4.1 Integrated Management Approach 3.4.2 Traditional Management Approach 3.4.3 Guidelines while Implementing TQM 3.5 Quality Measurement System 3.5.1 High Cost of Poor Quality 3.5.2 Improper Time Management 3.5.3 Lack of Score Keeping 3.6 Summary 3.7 Key Words 3.8 Answers to SAQs 3.1 INTRODUCTION Total Quality Management is a management approach that originated in the 1950s and has steadily become more popular since the early 1980s. Total Quality is a description of the culture, attitude and organization of a company that strives to provide customers with products and services that satisfy their needs. This culture requires quality in all aspects of the company’s operations, with processes being done right the first time and defects and waste eradicated from operations. Scientific methodology can be employed for the implementation of TQM. For this purpose, a number of well-established tools/methodologies can be used. Objectives After studying this unit, you should be able to • explain the various elements of TQM, • know the various types of quality tools for improvement, • describe the process of implementing TQM in an organization, and • understand certain aspects of a quality measurement system. 3.2 ELEMENTS OF TQM", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "50 Quality Systems TQM has been coined to describe a philosophy that makes quality the driving force behind leadership, design, planning, and improvement initiatives. To be successful in implementing TQM, an organization must concentrate on the following eight key elements : (i) Ethics, (ii) Integrity, (iii) Trust, (iv) Training, (v) Teamwork, (vi) Leadership, (vii) Recognition, and (viii) Communication. These elements can be divided into four groups according to their function. These groups namely foundation, bricks, binding mortar and roof constitute the necessary features of a house as shown in Figure 3.1. Figure 3.1: The 8 Elements of TQM Foundation TQM is built on a foundation of ethics, integrity and trust. It promotes openness, fairness and sincerity and allows involvement by everyone. These three elements move together, however, each element offers something different to the TQM concept. Ethics Ethics is the discipline concerned with good and bad in any situation. It is a two-faced subject represented by organizational and individual ethics. Organizational ethics establish a business code of ethics that outlines guidelines that all employees are to adhere to in the performance of their work. Individual ethics include personal rights or wrongs. Integrity Integrity implies honesty, morals, values, fairness, and adherence to the facts and sincerity. These are the characteristic that the Recognition Training Teamwork Leadership Ethics, Integrity and Trust Communication Communication Communication", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "51 Tools of Quality customers (internal or external) expect and deserve to receive. People see the opposite of integrity as duplicity. TQM will not work in an atmosphere of duplicity. Trust Trust is a by-product of integrity and ethical conduct. Without trust, the framework of TQM cannot be built. Trust promotes full participation of all members. It allows empowerment that encourages pride in ownership and also encourages commitment. It allows decision making at appropriate levels in the organization, promote individual risk-taking for continuous improvement and helps ensure that measurements focus on improvement of process. Trust is essential to ensure customer satisfaction. So, trust builds the cooperative environment essential for TQM. The interrelationship between ethics, integrity and trust is shown in Figure 3.2. Figure 3.2: Interrelationship between Ethics, Integrity and Trust Bricks On the basis of strong foundation of trust, ethics and integrity, bricks are placed to reach the roof of recognition. It includes training, teamwork and leadership. Training During the creation and formation of TQM, employees are trained so that they can become effective employees for the company. Training that employees require are interpersonal skills, the ability to function within teams, problem solving, decision making, job management, performance analysis, business economics and technical skills. Supervisors play an important role in implementing TQM within their departments, because they impart training and teach the employees the philosophies of TQM. Teamwork Teamwork is a key element of TQM to bring success. With the use of teams, the business will receive quicker and better solutions to problems. Teams also provide more permanent improvements in processes and operations. In teams, people feel more comfortable bringing up problems that may occur, and can get help from other workers to find a solution and put into place. There are mainly three types of teams that TQM organizations adopt. These are : • Quality Improvement Teams or Excellence Teams : These are temporary teams with the purpose of dealing with specific problems that often re-occur. These teams are set up for a period of three to twelve months. • Problem Solving Teams : These are temporary teams to solve certain problems and also to identify and overcome causes of problems. They generally last from one week to three months. Integrity Ethics Trust", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "52 Quality Systems • Natural Work Teams : These teams consist of small groups of skilled workers who share tasks and responsibilities. These teams use concepts such as employee involvement teams, self-managing teams and quality circles. These teams generally work for one to two hours a week. Leadership It is possibly the most important element in TQM. Leadership in TQM requires the manager to provide an inspiring vision, make strategic directions that are understood by all and to encourage values that guide subordinates. For TQM to be successful in the organization, the supervisor must be committed in leading his employees. A supervisor must understand TQM, believe in it and then demonstrate the belief and commitment through the daily practices of TQM. The supervisor makes sure that strategies, philosophies, values and goals are transmitted down throughout the organization to provide focus, clarity and direction. A key point is that TQM has to be introduced and led by top management. Commitment and personal involvement is required from top management in creating and deploying clear quality values and goals consistent with the objectives of the company and in creating and deploying well-defined systems, methods and performance measures for achieving those goals. Binding Mortar From foundation to roof of the TQM house, everything is bonded by the strong mortar of communication. Communication Communication acts as a vital link between all elements of TQM. Communication means a common understanding of ideas between the sender and the receiver. The role of sender and receiver can be played by anyone who is actively participating to realize the goal of achieving TQM. The success of TQM demands communication with all personnel within the department or among departments, the suppliers and customers. Supervisors must keep open the means through which employees can send and receive information about the TQM process. It is vital that ideas, thoughts and results of actions are being shared correctly. The communication process can be said to be credible only if receiver interprets in the way the sender intended. Communication follows different ways such as : • Downward Communication : This is the dominant form of communication in an organization. Presentations and discussions basically do it. By this the supervisors are able to make the workers conversant with TQM. • Upward Communication : By this the lower level of employees are able to provide suggestions to upper management of the effects of TQM. As employees provide insight and constructive criticism, supervisors must listen effectively to correct the situation that comes about through the use of TQM. This forms a level of trust between supervisors and workers. • Sideways Communication : This type of communication is important because it breaks down barriers between departments. It also allows dealing with customers and suppliers in a more professional manner. Roof", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "53 Tools of Quality The roof of the TQM house is built with recognition. Recognition Recognition is the last and final element in the entire system. A team or an individual should be given the recognition for the suggestions and achievements. Recognition comes in its best form when it immediately follows an action that an employee has performed. Recognition can be a positive factor to changes in self-esteem, productivity, quality and the amount of effort to the task at hand. Recognition comes in different ways, places and time such as, • Ways : It can be by way of personal letter from top management or by awards. • Places : Good performers can be recognized in front of departments, on performance boards and also in front of top management. • Time : Recognition can be given at staff meetings, annual awards ceremony, etc. It is very clear from the above discussion that TQM without involving integrity, ethics and trust would be an incomplete mission. Training is the key by which the organization creates a TQM environment. Leadership and teamwork go hand in hand. Communication between departments, supervisors and workers create the liveliness in the whole TQM process. Last but not the least, recognition should be given to people who contributed to the overall completed task. Hence, lead by example, train employees to provide a quality product, create an environment where there is no fear to share knowledge, and give credit where credit is due should be the motto of a successful TQM organization. 3.3 SEVEN QC TOOLS FOR IMPROVEMENT The new seven quality tools were adopted with the aim of developing quality control techniques with a design approach. Design approach implies that the best way of tackling a problem is decided before actually solving the problem. Since these tools are capable of identifying a problem from verbal facts they are used mainly for the qualitative problems in nature. When used in conjunction with original Basic Seven Tools (Flowcharting, Cause and Effect Diagrams, Pareto Analysis, Run Charts, Control Charts and Histograms), it increases TQM effectiveness. Figure 3.3 illustrates the difference in approach between the use of Basic Seven Tools and the Seven New Tools. FACTS Data Verbal Data Define problem before Collecting numerical data Define problem after Collecting numerical data § General Ideas § Formulate Plans § Analytical Approach The Seven New Tools The Basic Seven Tools Numerical Data", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "54 Quality Systems Figure 3.3 : Relation between New Seven QC Tools and Basic Seven Tools The basic seven tools rely on numerical data to give shape to the problem and make use of mathematical formulas. The new tools, on the other hand, act on a predefined problem and are used to organize verbal data into a more structured form. The Japanese realized the new seven quality tools were the most effective, which entered them into the second wave of quality. Following are the Seven New Quality Control tools. 3.3.1 Affinity Diagram An Affinity diagram is used to pinpoint the problem in a chaotic situation and generating solution strategies. It involves gathering of large amounts of inter-related verbal data (ideas, opinions) concerning a product or process. This is followed by organizing the data into groups based on natural relationship or affinity. This affinity makes it feasible for further analysis and to find a solution to the problem. The steps for constructing an affinity diagram are as follows : (i) State the issue or problem. (ii) Brainstorm and record all verbal data including ideas. Write each idea on a card. Pile data cards into groups of similar themes (natural affinity for each other). (iii) Combine statements on data cards to new affinity statement. (iv) Make new card with affinity statement. Continue to combine until less than five groups remain. (vi) Lay the groups out, keeping the affinity clusters together. Complete the diagram as shown in Figure 3.4. Figure 3.4: Affinity Diagram The advantages of Affinity diagrams are : • It facilitates breakthrough in thinking and stimulates fresh ideas. • It permits the problem to be pinned down accurately. 3.3.2 Tree Diagram The tree diagram is a systematic methodology that helps develop a succession of strategies for achieving objectives. The purpose of making a tree diagram is to generate Main idea A Main idea B Main idea C Subgroup of idea A1 Subgroup of idea A2 Subgroup of idea B1 Subgroup of idea B2 Subgroup of idea B3 Subgroup of idea C1 Subgroup of idea C2 Subgroup of idea C3 Subgroup of idea C4 Organize Information", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "55 Tools of Quality Limitations Development of the Means Confirmation of Objectives the most specific level of action items that can be implemented to accomplish a broader goal. They are also known as Systematic diagrams or Dendrograms. The steps to construct the tree diagram are as follows : (i) Record the problem or goal and identify constraints on how objective can be achieved. (ii) Generate the primary mean of achieving objective by asking questions like : “What needs to happen to achieve this goal or solve this problem?” (iii) Review each system of means in both directions, i.e. from objective to means and means to objective. Figure 3.5 shows how the development of means and confirmation of objective are carried out through the entire process of building a tree diagram. (iv) Take each primary mean and ask questions of achieving it (secondary means). Continue expanding to next level of means by asking more specific questions. (v) Review the tree diagram. Start with the last level and ask: “Will this set of items achieve the next higher level?” If the answer is yes for every level, then the diagram is complete. If not, then redo the diagram. Figure 3.5 : Tree Diagram Advantages of tree diagrams are: • Tree diagrams are very systematic and approaches each objective logically, hence it is less likely that items are omitted. • Tree diagrams facilitate agreement among team. 3.3.3 Interrelations Diagram Interrelations diagram is a tool for finding solution to problems that have complex relationships. The purpose of making it is to identify the root causes and root effects of a problem. Root causes are those factors or aspects of a problem, which primarily influence other factors and root effects are those factors of a problem, which are primarily influenced by other factors. Following are the steps on how to construct an inter- relationship diagram : Develop the Problem Statement The problem is presented as a complete sentence and is made clear to all team members. Develop Issues Related to the Problem Goal or Objective Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Ways and Means Question : How? Question : How? Question : Why? Question : Why?", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "56 Quality Systems These issues may be the result of a previous activity such as an affinity diagram or a brainstorming session. Each member lists five causes affecting problem. Arrange the Issues in a Circle The problem statement is noted at the center of a piece of paper and circle drawn around it. The header cards from the affinity diagram or the causes found after brainstorming are arranged in a circular pattern around the problem statement. Identify Cause-and-effect Relationships Using any of the issues as a starting point, the relationships are worked in sequence. For each pair of issues, determine if there is • no cause/effect relationship, • a weak cause/effect relationship, • a strong cause/effect relationship. Draw Arrows to Indicate Directions of Influence For each relationship pair, draw an arrow from the issue that is the cause to the issue that is influenced. For strong relationships, use a solid line. For weaker relationship, draw a dashed line. If some relationships may seem evenly balanced, the arrow must point from stronger influence. Never draw two-headed arrows. Figure 3.6 illustrates how the arrows are used to draw a cause and effect relation. Count the Arrows Once the analysis state is complete, the number of arrows going in and out of each category is counted. The numbers are noted above each category in the form number in/number out. Identify the Root Causes and Effects The root causes are the categories with the greatest number of arrows going out and the effects are the categories with the greatest number of arrows going in. The advantages of interrelationship diagrams are: • Useful at planning stage for obtaining perspective on overall situation. • Facilitates consensus among team. • Assists to develop and change people’s thinking. • Enables priorities to be identified accurately. We don’t know how to solve problems We don’t think it is important to resolve this problem In = 0.5 | Out = 1.5 In = 0.5 | Out = 1 In = 1.5 | Out = 1 We are afraid to take action", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "57 Tools of Quality Figure 3.6: Interrelationship Diagram 3.3.4 Prioritization Matrices Prioritization matrices are designed to narrow down the focus of any team before detailed implementation planning can happen. These tools should be used when : • Key root causes have been identified and most critical causes must be narrowed down. • When the issues are complex and they have strong interrelationships. • There are limited resources for improvement activities so efforts must be concentrated on the critical few. This approach helps to quickly identify basic disagreements. The approach to construct the prioritization matrices is dependent upon willingness of team to decide on a consensus. Unless consensus is understood and utilized in the decision process, the approach to prioritization will not be effective. When a consensus has been reached, the task of creating a prioritization matrices is started. Following are the steps : • Firstly, the team involved must agree on the goal to be achieved; the agreement must be clear, concise, and exact. • Next, a list of criteria is created to rate the various possible solutions. The criteria can be a numerical score such as (3 out of 5 or 4 out of 5) or a verbal score (such as excellent, good, satisfactory, bad, worse). • Make an L-shaped table as shown in Table 3.1. The various elements of the problem are noted in the leftmost column, that reflects the needs of the group. The various options are categorized into plans and arranged horizontally. • Now, compare all options relative to each criterion and mark a score. • Sum individual ratings to establish overall ranking. • Choose the best option(s) across all criteria. Table 3.1 : L-shaped Prioritization Matrices for Comparing the Options Options Elements of Problem A B C Problem No.1 A1 B1 C1 Problem No.2 A2 B2 C2 Problem No.3 A3 B3 C3 Combined Score A total B total C total The following example illustrates how to use prioritization matrices for solving multiple problems. Assume that a LPG bottling plant has been facing a number of problems in the last three months. In a special upper level corporate meeting, the board of managers have short-listed 5 problems that are supposed to cause difficulties in the plant’s operation. These problems are listed in the second column of Table 3.3. Each problem was subjected to a round of analysis by comparing them against certain criterion or conditions Root Cause We don’t identify our objective In = 0.5 | Out = 2.5 We change priorities too frequently In = 4 | Out = 0 Effect", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "58 Quality Systems (A, B, C and D). In order to decide which problem to tackle first a rating scale is devised. The rating scale is a numerical scoring system used to represent how closely the criteria are met. This is shown in Table 3.2. Table 3.2 : Rating Scale for Matching Criterion 1 2 3 4 5 Does not meet Criterion well Meets criterion fairly well Meets criterion well Meets criterion very well Meets criterion extremely well Now a matrix is prepared as shown in Table 3.3. The rated score of each criterion is filled in the cells corresponding to a problem topic. For example, the scores of criterions for problem 1 are 5, 4, 4 and 4. Finally, the total score is calculated. The highest score represents the project of greatest priority. Table 3.3 : Prioritizing Problems in Order to Identify a Schedule Plan Criteria Sl. No. Problem Topic A B C D Total Rank 1 High cost in maintaining centralized compressed air plant 5 4 4 4 17 1 2 Frequent duplicated order of gas cylinders 4 3 5 4 16 2 3 High cost involved in imported metal materials 4 3 4 3 14 4 4 Long process in registration of customer 5 2 3 3 13 5 5 Non-standardization of safety valve in mechanical workshop 3 3 5 4 15 3 Based on this rating, the board of LPG Gas bottling plant decides to give priority to the problem of high cost in maintaining centralized compressed air plant. The advantages of drawing prioritization matrices are as follows : • It forces a team to focus on best things to do, rather than everything they could do. • It limits private agendas since all the criteria identified are team generated. • It increases participation and ownership of both problems and solutions. 3.3.5 Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC) The Process Decision Program Chart (PDPC) is a very useful and powerful method to overcome a problem or a goal to be achieved which are not familiar. With the help of PDPC it is possible to map out all the conceivable events or contingencies that can occur in the implementation stage and also find out feasible counter measures to overcome these problems. The idea behind drawing PDPC chart is that “implementation plans do not always progress as anticipated”. When problems, technical or otherwise, arise, solutions are frequently not apparent. The PDPC method, in response to these kinds of problems, anticipates possible outcomes and prepares countermeasures that will lead to the best possible solutions. For this reason, PDPC is a way of contingency planning. The steps to construct this chart are explained with an example. Figure 3.7 illustrates how contingency planning is carried when testing a lawn mower machine. The various steps in carrying out this test are as follows :", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "59 Tools of Quality • Determine proposed implementation tasks. In this example, the tasks are testing of wheel, blade and grass box independently. • List activities to achieve the tasks as well as possible outcomes of each task. While testing the wheel, there are two outcomes, either the wheels are working correctly or the wheels skid. Similarly for the blades, either the blades have retained their sharpness or the blades have corroded. • Identify constraints of objective. For example, the weight of lawn mower must not exceed 15 kg. • Prepare contingency plan for each task and review what action is needed if task is not performed well. If the blades have corroded, find out alternative material that cuts wet grass as well. • Examine carefully to check for inconsistencies and ensure that all important factors are included. Figure 3.7: The PDPC Chart The PDPC method can be used to : • establish an implementation plan for management by objectives, • establish an implementation plan for technology-development themes, • enable those involved to understand decision-makers intentions, • facilitate forecasting because it uses past data to anticipate contingencies, and • implement countermeasures to minimize nonconformities in the manufacturing process. Lawn Mower Trial Test Wheel Test Cutting Blades Test Grass Box Wheel Skids Corroded Blades Distorted Shape due to Low Strength Wheel OK Blades OK Grass Box OK Test with Different Wheel Tyres Try Different Blade Material Redesign Number of Reinforcing Ribs Contingencies", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "60 Quality Systems 3.3.6 Activity Network Diagram The most popular activity network diagram (AND) is also known as the Program Evaluation Review Technique (PERT). It is used to find the most efficient path and a realistic schedule for the completion of any project by graphically showing the total completion time, the necessary sequence of tasks, those tasks that can be done simultaneously, the critical tasks to monitor, and the slack time of tasks within the project. In order to properly utilize an activity network diagram, it is essential to understand some specific terms used. Some of them are explained below. Activity Part of a project represented by an arrow, which has a defined beginning and end, and which requires time and resources. Event Indicates the start or completion of one or more activities, and is shown graphically as a circle or square. Theoretically, an event is an instant of time. Best Estimate (B) Earliest possible completion time. Worst Estimate (W) Time required if all goes wrong. Most Likely Estimate (M) Length of time most likely needed. Expected Time (ET) The average duration time. 6 4 W M B ET + + = Critical Path (CP) The sequence of activities imposing the most rigorous time constraint on attainment of the final event. Earliest Start Time (EST) Earliest possible time that an activity can begin. Before any activity may begin, all activities preceding it must be completed. The EST is determined by a forward pass through the network, allowing the expected time (ET) for each activity. Latest Start Time (LST) Latest possible time an activity can begin and still be completed by the time the next activity must begin. The LST is calculated by starting at the end of the network and working backwards through the network. Float (Slack) Latest start time (LST) minus earliest start time (EST) for a given activity. Network A graphical representation of a project plan, which shows activities and the relationship between them.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "61 Tools of Quality Project Two or more activities with a given aim. A project must have a beginning, budget, schedule of completion, and an end. To actually construct the AND, the following 3 rules are necessary : Rule 1 Each activity is represented by one and only one arrow in the network. Rule 2 Each activity must be identified by two distinct end nodes. Rule 3 To maintain the correct relationship, the following questions must be answered as each activity is added to the network : • What activity must immediately precede the current activity? • What activity must follow the current activity? • What activities must occur concurrently with the current activities? For example, consider the task of preparing a budget for launching a new product. Table 3.4 provides the duration of each activity and the sequence of preceding activities essential for making a budget. Table 3.4 : Activities and Duration to Prepare a Budget for a New Product Activity Precedence Duration (Days) A Forecast sales volume ------------- 10 B Study competitive market --------------- 7 C Design item and facilities A 5 D Prepare production schedule C 3 E Estimate cost of production D 2 F Set sales price B,E 1 G Prepare budget E,F 14 It can be noted that (i) Activities A and B have no activity preceding them. (ii) Activity C can start immediately after A is completed. (iii) Activities D and E can start immediately only after C and D respectively are completed. (iv) Activities E and F each have two immediately preceding activities, i.e (B, E) and (E, F) respectively. With the above conditions and denoting an activity by an arrow and an event by a node, the network can be constructed. To account for the fact that E immediately precedes both F and G while E and F immediately precede G, dummy activities are used. The activity network diagram for the above problem is shown in Figure 3.8. The dotted lines represent dummy activities.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "62 Quality Systems 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 C(5) D(3) E(2) B(7) F(1) G(14 ) A(10) 3/5/9 4/5/12 7/9/15 Task 1 Task 2 Task 3 Figure 3.8 : Activity Network Diagram 3.3.7 Matrix Diagram The matrix diagram method is designed to seek out principal factors from many characteristics concerning a subject under study. The matrix diagram helps to advance the process of problem solving by indicating the presence and degree of strength of a relationship between two sets of factors. By using the intersecting points of the factors, the experimenter may determine the strength of the relationship. A matrix diagram can be used to compare the results of implementing a new manufacturing process to the needs of a customer. The common matrix diagrams are T-type, Y-type, X-type and C-type. As an example of matrix diagram, assume that a restaurant manager wants to improve the customer’s dining experience. The factors by which he can bring about an improvement are food, staff and atmosphere of the restaurant. There are six types of options to be considered: Improve the quality of food, improve the quality of service, increase budget for decoration, add staff, and decrease menu prices. The factors and options are tabulated as shown in Table 3.5. Impact of each option is rated as 1, 2 or 3, 3 indicating a high impact and 1 indicating a low impact. From the table, it is seen that maximum impact is possible in the case of staff, followed by food and atmosphere. Table 3.5 : An Example of a Matrix Diagram OPTIONS FACTORS Improve Quality of Food Improve Quality of Service Increase Budget for Decoration Add Staff Decrease Menu Prices Total Food 3 1 4 Staff 3 2 5 Atmosphere 2 2 SAQ 1 (a) The best estimate, most likely estimate and worst estimate for the three tasks are shown in the figure below. If the first task begins on day zero, on what day can the third task be expected to complete? 0 0 10 10 15 15 18 18 21 21 21 21 35 20 20 Start a b End", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "63 Tools of Quality (b) What are the eight elements of TQM? 3.4 IMPLEMENTATION OF TQM An organization should be healthy before beginning TQM. Basically, three types of scenario exist in any organization. (i) The organization has a track record of effective responsiveness to the environment. In the past, it has successfully modified its operation when needed. Such an organization is an ideal case where TQM will be easier to implement. (ii) The organization has been performing well in the past but has no skill at improving its operating systems to current standards. No one has seen what changes can bring about. The employee looks into change with doubt. If this condition prevails, a comprehensive program of management and leadership development may be necessary. A majority of industries face this situation. (iii) The organization has significant problems such as a very unstable funding base, weak administrative systems, lack of managerial skill, or poor employee morale. In this scene, TQM would not be appropriate. In case the organization is inexperienced with dealing in change, a program of management and leadership development can update the knowledge base. This makes the employees slowly praise the benefits of change. For example, if the existing quality of working life of the worker improve with change, he will appreciate change. If the current reality of an organization does not indicate good health, TQM implementation should be delayed until the organization is in a state in which TQM is likely to succeed. In the next two sub-sections, you will come to know of two types of approaches used to implement TQM in an organization. 3.4.1 Integrated Management Approach In this approach, the principles of TQM are blended and balanced with existing practices in both organizational and human resource (employee) management systems. The management asks for a third party assistance, who is an expert in the field, in order to establish TQM in the company. This approach represents the 20% success rate of TQMs. It is divided into two sub-choices, depending upon managerial resources, readiness, acceptance, and competencies. In both sub-choices one and two, TQM finds a way to blend both bottom-line organizational realities with individual human realities into a unique set of quality beliefs and actions. Sub-choice One TQM can be approached as a concept of management that adjusts and restores management in producing end product or services resulting in customer satisfaction. In this case, management and individuals of the organization are", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "64 Quality Systems usually operating fairly effectively and the culture is supportive of accepting innovation. In such a scenario, TQM becomes the “accepted advantage” in beating competition for gaining customers, loyalties etc. Thus, successful TQM implementation can be done internally without much external help. Sub-choice Two TQM can also be approached as a system of management that provides processes and competencies currently lacking and needed by management. Some of the basic areas include decision-making and traditional areas of measurement. In these cases, companies benefit from well-designed “TQM Initiatives” that have the external power for changing culture to a supportive role. The TQM’s role in this instance is much greater, as it must enlighten the individuals and systems in the organization in areas essential to healthy TQM. 3.4.2 Traditional Management Approach This is the most common approach, and is also the cause of most of the failures. It involves overlaying (or forcing) of TQM system (s) and processes onto (or into) existing cultures without negotiating with or gaining commitment to their impact on individual’s lives. The management sees TQM as a technical process only. As usual, a third party expert group is called for making the change. Since these groups are expensive to employ, the companies try to extract maximum benefit within a limited period. In most cases, the employees are subjected to a series of training lectures. This quick spell on TQM aims to change the behaviour of employees but does not aim for a cultural change that supports the behaviour change. In fact, with proper guidance and training, it is possible to change attitudes of employees within weeks and months. However, sustainable culture change takes a minimum of three years before total impact is seen. The consequence of such a limited focus is that employees tolerate the changes in the initial period, but as there is no real agenda of what change to bring into the personal lives of employees or the health of the organization, it remains a concept. When money runs out or a new idea comes along the company quickly abandons this concept too. 3.4.3 Guidelines while Implementing TQM There is no one right way to implement TQM successfully in an organization. Due to the variation of cultures, each organization is unique and as such, there is no guaranteed formula for success. The steps, which help in implementation of TQM, are as follows : Recognize at the Beginning that the Organization is Unique As a consequence, one should not borrow someone else’s experience and try to fit into it. It would not work even though the model organization was very successful with it. Conduct a Need Assessment It is not fruitful to develop improvement plans before conducting a needs assessment. The need assessment helps develop a strategy for improvement. This is done by identifying vital processes to be targeted for change. It also provides a baseline measurement for judging progress. Start Small The best plans are those that result in action, which improve the processes of the organization and result in better services and products for the customer. A simple plan that generates action and gets results is better than an elaborate plan that does not give conclusive results. Some initial TQM actions might consist of specific projects designed to address problem that have potential for expanding to other processes of organization. Examples of such efforts might include :", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "65 Tools of Quality • Conduct of customer identification, survey, and feedback efforts to be reflected in quality and timeliness indicators. • Designate quality teams to address specific operating problems. • Conduct organizational assessment and leadership development. • Directly involve some line personnel in implementing some form of quality improvement effort reflected in the overall strategic plan. Identify Customers and Their Requirements A review of the customer’s requirements serves quality indicators on the areas where TQM would be implemented. Make a Plan The more specific a plan is, the more likely the organization will be successful. SAQ 2 (a) Why traditional management approach is not successful in implementing TQM? (b) What guidelines must be followed while implementing TQM? 3.5 QUALITY MEASUREMENT SYSTEM Measurement is the process of assessing the performance of a product characteristics or process parameters. The measurement of a quality system provides feedback of the process so that adjustment can be made to allow the operation to continue on its current course. In recent years, quality measurement has become as important as practicing and implementing TQM. This is because installing TQM demands huge investment. Therefore, the organization expects a good return on investment (ROI). When results are less than satisfactory, the temptation is to blame the training or the whole total quality movement. This section shall discuss three major root causes as to why some companies do not receive substantial ROI from their TQM efforts. 3.5.1 High Cost of Poor Quality The price of any product or service is driven by profits and total cost to produce. This is shown in Figure 3.9(a). The total cost to produce can be broken into two categories as shown in Figure 3.9(b). Theoretical Cost The cost associated with doing only the right things right the first time. Cost of Poor Quality The waste or non-value added cost is referred to as COPQ. According to quality gurus such as Deming, Juran, etc., the percentage of COPQ in most companies is 20-40%. This Cost of Poor Quality is due to high cycle times, long development times, high defect rates, poor productivity, excessive inventories, training without", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "66 Quality Systems ROI, lack of customer focus, and many other non-value added activities. COPQ serves as a metric or scale to measure the amount of waste generated. In order to survive global competition, the company must come out with a lower and more competitive price. Figure 3.9(c) shows that the company has cut down its expenditure in order to attract customers. Removing 1/3 of its work force and selling off non-profitable assets have made the cut in expenditure (Figure 3.9(d)). This ensures the company a profit margin but it has done very little to cut the COPQ. In other words, the company has not put emphasis on waste reduction, and proper metrics. The company that will survive competition is depicted in Figure 3.9(e). It has cut its COPQ significantly. The key to competitiveness and survival is tied to reducing the 20-40% waste. Lowering COPQ is a difficult strategy in the short term, but it is the only way to long-term profitability, competitiveness, and the ability to bear hard times. Figure 3.9 : Impact of Competition and Lowering COPQ 3.5.2 Improper Time Management The second reason for not receiving ROI is a claim by people in industry that they do not have enough time. This claim can be verified by drawing a Pareto Diagram. A Pareto Diagram shows that the major part of the problems may be caused by small number of possible causes. Consider the case of the fire department in an iron and steel factory. If the company spends majority of its time fighting fires in the blast furnace section, it leaves little opportunities for the management to instruct the employees about quality measures. There might be situations also in which new technology cannot be installed because of frequent fires in the blast furnace. Such a situation is shown in Figure 3.10(a). Thus the scientists, engineers, technicians, and operators who although have gone through a proper training do not have time to implement what they have learned. A manager must learn where his people are spending their time, and then develop a way to increase the amount of time spent on quality improvement. Figure 3.10(b) illustrates that a majority of time is allocated to teaching and training the employees about fire prevention. Such an education is the first constructive step towards quality improvement. Although the installation of fire preventive program cannot completely rule out fires lesser fires indicate that more time and money is saved. The time saved is utilized for managers to bring forward the new quality schemes and employees to practice their teaching.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "67 Tools of Quality Fire Fighting Paper Work Meetings Travel Other Fire Prevention % of Time (a) Improper Time Management Fire Fighting Paper Work Meetings Travel Other Fire Prevention % of Time (b) Proper Time Management Figure 3.10 : Pareto Diagram for Time Management 3.5.3 Lack of Score Keeping The third aspect of the ROI problem is the fact that everyone in the company seems to be talking quality improvement, but no one is keeping score. Many corporate vice presidents claim that they are not getting the needed support from middle management. Commonsense would indicate that they are not keeping score or, at the very least, not keeping the right score on middle management. For example, if middle managers are responsible for processes that have computable measures of quality, such as defects per unit (dpu) or COPQ, they should be plotting these measures over time. If a plot of dpu or COPQ appears as shown in Figure 3.11, it will be difficult convincing anyone that the process demonstrates quality improvement. The scorecard does not show it. Score keeping is mandatory to prevent the daily routine from becoming nothing more than mere practice. Keeping score must be used in order to know how the process is doing (i.e., whether the efforts are bringing the company to a winning or losing position). In addition, management must ensure that scorecards are implemented in a way that motivates people to ethically play to win rather than motivating them to falsify data to avoid looking bad. Figure 3.12 depicts a winning scorecard. (a) Defects per Unit versus Time (b) COPQ versus time Figure 3.11 May 2003 May 2004 dpu COPQ May 2003 May 2004 COPQ", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-19.webp"}, {"n": 20, "text": "68 Quality Systems Figure 3.12: A Winning Score Card SAQ 3 (a) What is meant by cost of poor quality? (b) Giving an example, explain how time management affects quality. 3.6 SUMMARY The eight elements are the key in ensuring the success of TQM in an organization. Without these elements, the business entities cannot be successful TQM implementers. These are : (i) Ethics (ii) Integrity (iii) Trust (iv) Training (v) Teamwork (vi) Leadership (vii) Recognition and (viii) Communication. There are seven quality control tools for Improvement. These are : (i) Affinity Diagram (ii) Tree Diagram (iii) Interrelationship Diagram (iv) Prioritization Matrices (v) PDPC Chart (vi) Activity Network Diagram (vii) Matrix Diagram. Two common approaches for implementing the TQM are : Integrated Management Approach and Traditional Management Approach, though there is no unique way to implement TQM. Some guidelines for implementing the TQM are described in this unit. In recent years, quality measurement has become as important as practicing and implementing TQM. The measurement of a quality system provides feedback of the", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-20.webp"}, {"n": 21, "text": "69 Tools of Quality process so that adjustment can be made to allow the operation to continue on its current course. 3.7 KEY WORDS Affinity Diagram : It is a tool or method that gathers large amount of inter-related verbal data and organizes the verbal data into groups based on natural relationship. Activity Network Diagram : It is a technique using nodes for events and arrows for activities for project planning, scheduling and monitoring. This is very useful for finding the completion date of the various activities within a complex task or project. Cost of Poor Quality : It is the cost associated with non-value added activities and waste management. Interrelations Diagram : It is a tool for finding solution to problems that have complex causal relationship in order to identify the root causes and root effects of a problem. Management by Objective : An art of management and technique whereby the actions of analysis, direction and control are focused on the end result. Matrix Diagram : A Matrix diagram consists of a number of columns and rows whose intersections are checked up, to find out the nature and strength of the problem. PDPC : PDPC stands for Process Decision Program Charts. The PDPC helps to find out all the conceivable events or contingencies that can occur in the implementation stage and also find out feasible counter measures to overcome these problems. Score Keeping : It is the task of monitoring the levels of activities that do not bring returns or cause loss (financial or materialistic). 3.8 ANSWERS TO SAQs SAQ 1 (a) The expected completion date for task 1 can be found from the formula 6 4 W M B ET + + = = (3 + 4 × 5 + 9) / 6 = 5.333. Similarly, the completion date for task 2 and task 3 are 6 and 9.67 respectively. So the expected duration of the project is 5.33 + 6 + 9.67 or 21 days. Answers to all other questions may be found in the text.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl3-21.webp"}]}, {"id": "sdl4", "file": "SDL/4. QUALITY MANAGEMENT - Week 4 - Unit 4 - Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality.pdf", "pages": [{"n": 1, "text": "71 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality UNIT 4 ROLE OF ISO AND OSHA IN QUALITY Structure 4.1 Introduction Objectives 4.2 What is ISO 9000? 4.2.1 History of ISO 9000 4.2.2 Necessity of Management Standard 4.3 Components of ISO 9000 4.4 Benefits of ISO 9000 4.5 Requirements of ISO 9000-9004 4.6 Documentation for ISO 9000 QMS 4.7 Implementation of ISO 9000 QMS 4.8 Need for Safety and Health in Industry 4.9 Safety Approaches and Safety Management as per OSHA 4.10 ISO 14000 4.11 Summary 4.12 Key Words 4.13 Answers to SAQs 4.1 INTRODUCTION The business environment has changed during the past few decades. Rising expectations worldwide and open market economies are now forcing businesses to compete with each other. This open competition has created new enterprises, reputed brand names, and superior product designs. The improved performance of today’s automobiles, plastics, and machine tools, for example, are noteworthy. The vigor in trade across national borders has placed the customer in a strong position where he can ask for better gains for what he pays. In fact, businesses are desperate to conform to customers’ needs and make them satisfied. All these developments have made quality a strategic choice for many producers. However, the approaches to provide quality products and services still vary significantly around the world. This is because different industries have different perceptions and requirements of quality. These differences become especially apparent when a producer attempts to carry trade across borders. In such instances, standardization, formation of association, special arrangements for contract management, and professionally managed transfer of technology have evolved as solutions. The ISO 9000 standards for quality management, in particular, were proposed to meet the growing needs for international standardization by the joint action of 26 of the largest consumer and producer nations including India, under the sponsorship of the ISO. These standards embody comprehensive quality management concepts and guidance. The recent years have also seen a growing concern for safety of employees in the industries. This has evolved into strict norms for provision of safe-accident free environment in the work place. In 1987, a revolution occurred in the management of quality worldwide when the International Standards Organization (Geneva) brought out a set of standards pertaining not to the quality of products but rather to the practice of quality assurance methods within a company. Termed as ISO 9000 standards, these proved their value (particularly", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-1.webp"}, {"n": 2, "text": "72 Quality Systems to buyers) rather quickly and went global. Today, over 90 countries have recognized these standards. Many countries including India have adopted them as their own standards for benchmark quality management practices. Today this series covers varied activities such as design, manufacturing, software development, service activities, auditing, and methods for improving quality on the shop floor. This module begins with the background and evolution of ISO. The succeeding section brings in the principles of Quality Management Systems (QMS) and its relation to ISO 9000. Next, the components of ISO are discussed followed by the benefits of ISO 9000. The details for establishment of ISO 9000 are provided under the section QMS implementation. The last three sections will bring the issue of safety and health in industry and what are the approaches to implement safety management. This unit ends with the description of ISO 14000. Objectives After studying this unit, you should be able to • understand the QMS system, • explain the types of ISO 9000 standards, • know the requirements for implementation of ISO 9000, • appreciate the necessity for safety and health in industry, and • describe ISO 14000. 4.2 WHAT IS ISO 9000? ISO is the word that represents the International Organization for Standardization. It is the worldwide federation of national standards bodies for approximately 130 countries. ISO has been developing voluntary technical standards over almost all sectors of business, industry and technology since 1947. These standards are documented agreements containing technical specifications to be used consistently as rules, guidelines, or definitions of characteristics to ensure that materials, products, processes and services are fit for the purpose of customer. The vast majority of ISO standards are highly specific to a particular product, material, or process. ISO 9000 is primarily concerned with “quality management”. Quality management is that aspect of the company’s overall management function that determines and implements the company’s quality policy. Quality Policy is the overall quality intentions and direction of an organization as regards quality, as formally expressed by the top management. A Quality Management Standards (QMS) covers the areas of procedures, tools, the assessment of capability, documentation procedure, etc. ISO 9000 is a generic management system standard. Generic means that the same standards can be applied to any organization, large or small, whatever its product, including service in any sector of activity. It also applies to a business enterprise, a public administration or a government department. 4.2.1 History of ISO 9000 In the 1970s, many major organizations published their own quality management standards (Ford’s Q101, for example). It introduced the idea that confidence in a product could be gained from an approved quality management system and quality manuals. By the late 70s it was decided that, for the first time, there would be a national standard on what constituted a quality system. This led to the development of BS5750 series (British Standard). By 1980s, there was a need for some kind of internationally recognized quality system. In 1987, BS5750 series was also issued as ISO 9000 and EN 2900 (Euro-norm", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-2.webp"}, {"n": 3, "text": "73 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality series in the European Union). Several additional standards have been issued subsequently by ISO, including a major revision of the original series done in 1994 also known as ISO 9000:1994. ISO 9000 was originally issued as a series of six internationally agreed upon standards. They were named as ISO 9000, ISO 9001, ISO 9002, ISO 9003, ISO 9004, and ISO 8402 respectively. 4.2.2 Necessity of Management Standard In a very small organization, it may not be necessary to document the working procedure of the system, because the manager or owner runs the organization as he wishes. The larger the organization, and more the people involved, greater the likelihood that there will be some written procedures, instructions, forms or records. Management system standards provide the organization with a model to follow in setting up and operating the management system. This model incorporates the features on which experts in the field have reached a consensus as representing the international state of the art. Some of the important features of a management standard are as follows : (i) The standards help ensure that the organization goes about its business in an orderly and structured way, so that time, money and other resources are utilized efficiently. (ii) A systematic approach ensures that nothing important is left out and that everyone is clear about who is responsible for doing what, when, how, why and where. (iii) The need for management systems is needed most in large organizations. Companies in such fields as automobiles, defense, or health care services have been operating management systems for years. (iv) The customer demands special inspections and checks to make sure that what he receives from a supplier, meets his own requirements. A standardized QMS addresses procedures, tools, assessment of capability, documentation methods, etc. Hence, it reduces the burden of cost of the supplier, which in turn the buyer ends up paying. ISO 9000 is not a product standard. It only concerns the way an organization goes about its work, and does not concern the result of this work. ISO 9000 is not a mandatory system nor is it a government regulation. However, it is a very important customer regulation. The development and adoption of this system of standards have been motivated by the need to provide assurance to the widest variety of customers about an organization’s quality assurance methods and management practices. SAQ 1 (a) Explain the term quality management system. (b) Why is a management standard necessary? 4.3 COMPONENTS OF ISO 9000 The ISO 9000 series of quality management system consists of six distinct standards. The first, ISO 8402, is the terminology standard. The next document is the ‘ISO 9000 standard’, which provides the basic concepts such as what is quality, what is meant by a", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-3.webp"}, {"n": 4, "text": "74 Quality Systems quality policy, what is traceability, etc. This section explains four standards 9001, 9002, 9003 and 9004. • ISO 9001 specifies quality system requirements for use where a contract between the supplier and the buyer requires the demonstration of the supplier’s capability in designing and developing products or services, production, installation and servicing. The requirements aim primarily at preventing nonconformity at each of these steps. Conformity means that there is an agreement between the desired specifications of customer and the produced item by manufacturer. A key role can be played here by statistical Quality Control methods. The documentation of quality management procedures used in the company is another important requirement. • ISO 9002 is used in a situation when only production and installation conformance is to be ensured. It is the appropriate standard for organizations that do not design and develop products. It requires demonstration of the supplier’s ability to control the process that determines the acceptability of products supplied. The focus is on detecting any nonconformity during production and installation, and the implementation of the means to prevent the recurrence of nonconformity. Again, statistical Quality Control methods will play an important part here. • ISO 9003 is the least detailed standard and it only requires that conformance in final test and inspection be ensured. ISO 9003 is the appropriate standard for an organization whose business processes do not include design control, process control, purchasing or servicing. It focuses on inspection and testing to ensure that final products and services meet specified requirements. • ISO 9004 contains guidelines on the technical, administrative and human factors affecting the quality of products and services for internal use only. ISO 9004 describes the elements by which a company can develop good quality management systems and implement them. A quality system includes the organizational structure, responsibilities, procedures and resources for implementing quality management. In the 1990s, ISO 9000 was increasingly criticized on the grounds that it did not really assure the delivery of a quality product or service since organizations could comply simply by demonstrating that they had consistently followed the procedures laid down in their quality manual. A review process resulted in the publication of ISO 9001:2000. Three previous standards, ISO 9001, 9002 and 9003 have been reduced to one standard, ISO 9001:2000 for certification purposes. The twenty main elements of ISO 9000:1994 were reduced to four main sections covering : • Management Responsibility • Resource Management • Product Realization • Measurement, Analysis and Improvement The new family of quality management standards comprises three individual standards : (i) ISO 9000:2000 (ii) ISO 9001:2000 (iii) ISO 9004:2000", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-4.webp"}, {"n": 5, "text": "75 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality ISO 9000:2000 is the logical starting point. It begins with an explanation of the overall approach to developing a quality management system. The main part of ISO 9000:2000 is its comprehensive vocabulary section, which contains definitions of all the terms used in ISO 9001:2000 and ISO 9004:2000. ISO 9001:2000 specifies the requirements for a quality management system that can be used for certification or contractual purposes. It could also be used internally as a sound basis for managing quality without ever having a system audited or assessed by any external body. It is designed to facilitate assessment and is the standard to which one must demonstrate compliance if he wishes to be certified by an accredited assessment body. ISO 9004:2000 was developed to complement ISO 9001:2000. ISO 9004:2000. It takes the organizations beyond ISO 9001 and should be viewed more as an advisory document than as a specification. It is not intended to be used as a guideline for how to implement ISO 9001:2000 but as a source of information, ideas and advice about how the organization can move beyond the requirements of ISO 9001:2000. 4.4 BENEFITS OF ISO 9000 The gains of an organization receiving ISO 9000 certification are numerous. First and foremost, the organization becomes sure of its own capabilities, its commitment to quality, assurance of consistency in quality, timely delivery, and the reduced incidence of product failure. Such an organization is accepted by most customers internationally. Several other benefits are listed below. Improvement in Business Most companies implementing ISO 9000 report increases in business process efficiencies, reductions in waste, and improved product quality. The preparation for certification forces combing of most shop practices of the organization. This process streamlines operations which results in the measurement and control of waste, re-work, etc. This is the ‘by-product’ impact of implementing ISO 9000. Simplified Purchasing Decisions Working with an ISO 9000 accredited company greatly simplifies purchasing decisions. The customer saves time and money by eliminating numerous vendor assessments such as incoming inspections. Qualify for New Customers Many corporations see ISO 9000 certification as an essential requirement for conducting business with a new vendor. To enter global markets, ISO 9000 standards are required in many countries. Educational Experience It is a response of many organizations that the registration process is a very good educational experience and creator of teamwork. It allows employees to understand the overall work process and their roles in it. Improved Image in Market Industry studies indicate that an ISO certified company experiences positive cultural changes, and increased productivity and efficiency. These changes encourage greater quality awareness with the effect that marketing positioning of the company is improved. Competitive Tool The competitive market created by the globalization of trade across continents has necessitated ISO 9000 certification. ISO 9000 facilitates mutual recognition of", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-5.webp"}, {"n": 6, "text": "76 Quality Systems products legally produced in one country to be accepted in another country. This is possible due to a uniform technical specification. Organizational Renovation ISO 9000 is the way forward for an organization seeking renovation. ISO 9000 certification rids companies of numerous obsolete documentation and procedures. Instead, they have fool-proof systems of instrument calibration and excellent systems for traceability to troubleshoot customer problems. 4.5 REQUIREMENTS OF ISO 9000-9004 The requirements of ISO 9000 are presented in sections 4 to 8 of ISO 9001:2000. In this section of the unit, the following contents of ISO 9001:2000 shall be discussed : • Systematic Requirements • Management Requirements • Resource Requirements • Realization Requirements • Remedial Requirements Systematic Requirements Establish Quality System In order to establish a quality system, the tasks of development, implementation and improvement are done in the following ways. • The processes that make up the quality system are identified and quality management processes are described. • The quality management system (QMS) is implemented by use of quality system processes and managing process performance. • Process performance is continuously monitored to improve QMS. Document the Quality System This includes developing documents that reflect what the organization does. The tasks associated with preparing a document are as follows : • The operation procedures are documented in order to prepare a quality system. • The processes of interaction between several activities and how they affect the operation of the organization are also documented. • The correct version of documents is provided at points of use. Those documents that come from external sources are carefully monitored and then integrated with the quality system records of organization. Management Requirements Management requirements shall include the following : Support Quality The importance of quality can be focused by management by promoting the need to meet customer requirements and regulatory requirements. The personnel must be encouraged to meet quality system requirements.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-6.webp"}, {"n": 7, "text": "77 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality Establish a Quality Policy When defining and documenting its quality policy, management should consider the following points: • The quality policy should be expressed in easy to understand language. • The quality policy should be relevant to the organization, its other policies, the products or services provided, and the organization’s people. • The objectives should be ambitious and achievable. Demonstrating Commitment The management should demonstrate commitment by activities such as : • Ensuring that concerned people understand the organization’s quality policy. • Initiating, managing, and following up on the implementation of the quality policy, including implementation of the quality system. • Not accepting deviations from quality policy or wasted resources in any part or aspect of the organization. Carry Out Quality Planning The management should ensure that objectives are set for functional areas. They must take into account that objectives will facilitate product realization and that the objectives are measurable. Control Quality System In order to support the improvement of QMS, management representative must be appointed that reports status of the improvement and pace of activities. A proper communication channel must be established throughout the organization. Perform Management Reviews A management review is performed to evaluate whether the present quality system should be improved. The inputs required from a review can be gathered from information based on purchaser feedback, internal feedback (such as results of internal audits), process performance, and product (including services) performance. Based on these facts, management can generate review outputs to improve quality system. The quality system review process and the reasons behind it should be known and understood by the organization. Resource Requirements Supporting a quality system requires providing quality resources. The resources should be used by competent personnel with adequate experience. The various tasks associated with resource requirements are : • Delivering training and awareness programmes to achieve acceptable levels of competence and evaluating effectiveness of training. • Providing quality environment in order to ensure products requirements. • Providing quality infrastructure: This includes providing workspace, equipment, utilities, support services, hardware and software. Realization Requirements", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-7.webp"}, {"n": 8, "text": "78 Quality Systems The tasks associated with planning the product realization processes include : Control Realization Planning This means identifying the needs and requirements of product realization, developing methods to control quality during product realization and documenting the process. Control Customer Processes It includes identifying customers’ product requirements and reviewing requirements before accepting orders from customers. For this purpose, it is essential to develop a process to control communications with customers. Control Product Development There are several stages in product design and development phase. First and foremost, it is essential to clarify design and development responsibilities. Interactions between design and development groups need to be managed effectively. The changes in product design and development must be recorded. Equally essential is to perform product design and development verifications. Control Purchasing Function The purchased products must meet specified requirements. Equally important is to ensure that suppliers also meet requirements such as timely delivery and after sales services. The purchased products must be verified at the premises of organization and if required at the premises of the supplier. Control Operational Activities The factors that need to be kept in control during actual production are information, equipments and measurement activities. The proof of capability must be demonstrated by producing planned outputs through process equipments. In case property is supplied by customers it must be safeguarded properly. The products must be preserved during internal processing and final delivery. Control Monitoring Devices Choice of monitoring and measuring devices must meet the operation requirements. Periodic calibration is very essential. Moreover, devices must be protected from unauthorized adjustment and improper environment. Before using monitoring and measuring software, they must be properly validated. Remedial Requirements Remedial tasks are essential to assure conformity of products to requirements. They should be used to improve quality management system. The various methods of remedial procedures are: Monitor and Measure Quality This is the task of monitoring customer satisfaction by conducting surveys. Performing internal audits can help discover flaws that normally remain undetected. Prompt action must be taken when processes fail to achieve planned results. Control Non-conforming Products", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-8.webp"}, {"n": 9, "text": "79 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality The first task is to define how non-conforming products should be identified. Once detected, action must be taken to prevent the delivery or use of non- conforming products. After the events that led to a non-conforming product have been traced out, corrections must be initiated. A proof must be generated that corrected products now meet requirements. A record of non-conforming products must be maintained. Make Quality Improvements The information needed to improve the QMS are obtained from audits, quality policy, quality objectives, and management reviews. The corrective actions undertaken must prevent recurrence. For this purpose, it is essential that the effectiveness of actions be carefully examined and recorded. The rest of the sections in ISO 9000 deals with introduction and vocabulary. ISO 9002 is now obsolete and replaced by ISO 9000:2000. In the next section, the documentation procedure of ISO shall be discussed in detail. 4.6 DOCUMENTATION FOR ISO 9000 QMS The implementation of ISO 9001 require an organization to “establish, document, implement, and maintain a quality management system and continually improve its effectiveness in accordance with the requirements of this International Standard”. In the terminology of ISO 9001:2000, document refers to information and its supporting medium. The documents may be in the form of paper, magnetic, electronic or computer disc, photograph and master sample. The documentation includes : A Quality Manual This is a document specifying the quality management system of an organization. The quality policy and quality objectives are also mentioned in this manual. The format and structure of the manual is a decision for each organization, and will depend on the size, culture and complexity of the organization. For example, a small organization may find it appropriate to include the description of its entire QMS within a single manual, including all the documented procedures required by the standard. Documented Procedures The International Standard specifically requires a “documented procedure”. A procedure is a specified way to carry out an activity or a process. The procedure has to be established, documented, implemented and maintained. ISO 9001:2000 specifically requires the organization to have “documented procedures” for the following six activities : (i) Control of documents (ii) Control of records (iii) Internal audit (iv) Control of non-conforming product (v) Corrective action (vi) Preventive Action Some organizations may find it convenient to combine the procedure for several activities into a single documented procedure (for example, corrective action and preventive action). Others may choose to document a given activity by using more than one documented procedure (for example, Internal audits). Documents Needed by the Organization to Ensure Effective Planning, Operation and Control of its Processes", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-9.webp"}, {"n": 10, "text": "80 Quality Systems These include specifications in form of a document stating requirements. The Quality plans are documents specifying which procedures and associated resources shall be applied by whom and when to a specific project, product, process or contract. Document control should include those documents, pertinent to design, purchasing, work execution, quality standards, inspection of materials and documents of suppliers. The supplier’s documents are accompanied with materials. These are written procedures with all the contents that describe how documentation for these functions should be controlled, who is responsible for the control, what is to be controlled and where and when the control is to take place. Other than documented procedures, it may be necessary to develop other documents to demonstrate the effective implementation of its QMS. Records These are documents required by International Standard that state results achieved for providing evidence of activities performed. Organizations are free to develop other records that may be needed to demonstrate conformity of their processes, products and quality management system. The records as required by ISO:9000 are : • Management reviews. • Education, training, skills and experience. • Evidence that the realization processes and resulting product fulfill requirements. • Results of design and development, verification and validation relating to product requirements. • Results of supplier evaluations and any necessary actions arising from the evaluations. • Customer property that is lost, damaged or otherwise found unsuitable for use. • Results of calibration and verification of measuring equipment. Validity of the previous measuring results when the measuring equipment is found not to conform to requirements. Basis used for calibration or verification of measuring equipment where no international or national measurement standards exist. • Internal audit results and follow-up actions. • Indication of the person(s) authorizing release of product. • Nature of the product non-conformities and any subsequent actions taken, including concessions obtained. • Results of corrective action as well as preventive action. The ISO 9000 series of standards was revised last in 2000. ISO 9001:2000 has significantly reduced the documentation requirements and is much less rigid than the 1994 version of the standard. It allows an organization more flexibility in the way it chooses to document its quality management system (QMS). This enables each individual organization to develop the minimum amount of documentation needed in order to demonstrate the effective planning, operation and control of its processes and the implementation and continual improvement of the effectiveness of its QMS. Following are some of the main objectives of an organization’s documentation : Communication of Information", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-10.webp"}, {"n": 11, "text": "81 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality The documentation serves as a tool for information transmission and communication. The type and extent of the documentation will depend on the nature of the organization’s products and processes, the degree of formality of communication systems and the level of communication skills within the organization, and the organizational culture. Evidence of Conformity The presence of documents is a mark of evidence that what was planned has actually been done. Knowledge Sharing Documentation is necessary to publicize and preserve the organization’s experiences. A typical example would be a technical specification, which can be used as a base for design and development of a new product. Simplified Set of Standards One of the most important objectives in the revision of the ISO 9000 series of standards has been to develop a simplified set of standards to achieve the desired results of process activities of the organization. 4.7 IMPLEMENTATION OF ISO 9000 QMS While implementing ISO 9000, the changes in methods, practices, record keeping, etc., will be substantial. The effort and investment involved are generally large, but market conditions may not leave a company much of a choice. Implementation progresses through installing the task force, a review of the existing operations, developing the quality manual, conducting internal audit, rectifying deficiencies, and obtaining the certification through third party assessment. The details of implementation may vary from company to company but the following steps are generally followed : Exposure and Training The first step in ISO implementation is to conduct ISO 9000 and quality management familiarization sessions for everyone in the organization. This should be extended to familiarization with certified vendors. The attractive dissemination of the implementation plans if Quality System should be made known to all employees of the company. An ISO 9000 booklet for internal distribution serves many a purpose. Developing the Company’s Draft Quality Manual In order to properly draft a quality manual, an internal study of all existing quality related systems and existing practices need to be carried out. This includes collection of all charts, records, reports, data sheets, related to the above documented practices. Internal Audit of the Existing Systems/Practices and Incorporating ISO 9000 The auditing process involves the formation of task groups to study the important aspects of the ISO 9000 standards. Checklists are prepared to enable verification of existing practices with ISO 9000 standards as well as removing items or practices not conforming to ISO 9000. A revision of the quality manual and operating manuals is carried out to reflect changes. Audit by the External Certifying Agency After internal audit and necessary rectifications, company should deliver a complete quality manual to its chosen assessing agency. If agency is satisfied with", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-11.webp"}, {"n": 12, "text": "82 Quality Systems the manual, it will respond with a questionnaire. After the questionnaire has been submitted back to the external agency, the inspectors from that agency will schedule a visit to audit the company’s premises and system. The implementation procedure for ISO 9000 depends upon the state of QMS existing in the organization. Basically three types of such organizations exist : Organizations Preparing to Implement a QMS For organizations that are in the process of implementing or have yet to implement a QMS, the ISO 9001:2000 emphasizes a process approach. This includes : • Identifying the processes necessary for the effective implementation of the quality management system. • Understanding the interactions between these processes. • Documenting the processes to the extent necessary to assure their effective operation and control. (It may be appropriate to document the processes using process maps. It is emphasized, however, that documented process maps are not a requirement of ISO 9001:2000.) Organizations Wishing to Adapt an Existing QMS Some organizations might have been certified as ISO 9001 according to 1994 requirements. In order to facilitate transition to ISO 9000:2000, the organization need to incorporate some modifications. These are : • An organization with an existing QMS should not need to rewrite all of its documentation in order to meet the requirements of ISO 9001:2000. This is particularly true if its QMS is based on a process approach. In this case, the existing documentation may be adequate and can be simply referenced in the revised quality manual. • An organization that has not used a process approach in the past will need to pay particular attention to the definition of its processes, their sequence and interaction. Because ISO 9001:2000 is less prescriptive than the 1994 versions of the standard, an organization may be able to carry out some simplification of existing documents, in order to simplify its QMS. Organizations Wishing to Demonstrate Conformity with ISO 9001:2000 For organizations wishing to demonstrate conformity with the requirements of ISO 9001:2000, for the purposes of certification/registration, contractual, or other reasons, it is important to remember the need to provide evidence of the effective implementation of the QMS. In order to claim conformity with ISO 9001:2000, the organization has to be able to provide objective evidence of the effectiveness of its processes and its quality management system. “Objective Evidence” refers to “data supporting the existence or verification of something” which may be obtained through observation, measurement, test, or other means. Where the organization has no specific internal procedure for a particular activity, and this is not required by the standard, it is acceptable for this activity to be conducted using as a basis the relevant clause of ISO 9001:2000. In these situations, both internal and external audits may use the text of ISO 9001:2000 for the purposes of conformity assessment. SAQ 2 (a) List down five major benefits of implementing ISO 9000. (b) What are the different types of documents required for ISO 9000 certification?", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-12.webp"}, {"n": 13, "text": "83 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality (c) Explain the generally required steps for implementing ISO 9000. 4.8 NEED FOR SAFETY AND HEALTH IN INDUSTRY It has been observed that a lot of industrial accidents occur due to a lack of safety awareness. Job-related accidents also occur due to unsafe conditions of operation. Often such negligence results in loss of human life or disable a healthy worker. In fact, statisticians have calculated that person-days lost from job-related disabilities are ten times of loss due to strikes. Apart from negligence on this matter in the industry, the protection of workers from risks related to exposure to chemical, physical and biological agents are not guaranteed. This is commonly observed in industries of developing nations. For example, in small fabrication industries, spray painting workers are not provided with protective equipments such as air masks, gloves and eye-protection glasses. Negligence from the worker is a persistent problem although it is the duty of supervisor in the shop floor to understand that safety is a practice of good management. Excessive working hours in the shop floor, inadequate rest or disruptive working patterns cause adverse effects on the health of the workers. Today, several institutes are creating databases of occupational diseases arising from problems and trends in working life and making them available to public. In absence of a safety effort, all lost production and wages, medical expenses and disability compensation are directly subtracted from profits. In 1970, the Occupational Safety and Health Act was passed by United States Congress to set mandatory occupational safety and health standards applicable to industries. Under the Act, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) was set up within the Department of Labor to : • encourage employers and employees to reduce workplace hazards and to implement new or improve existing safety and health programs, • provide for research in occupational safety and health to develop innovative ways of dealing with occupational safety and health problems, • establish “separate but dependent responsibilities and rights” for employers and employees for the achievement of better safety and health conditions, • maintain a reporting and record keeping system to monitor job-related injuries and illnesses, • establish training programs to increase the number and competence of occupational safety and health personnel, • develop mandatory job safety and health standards and enforce them effectively, and • provide for the development, analysis, evaluation and approval of state occupational safety and health programs. OSHA safety standards are designed to reduce on-the-job injuries; health standards to limit workers’ risk of developing occupational disease. Most OSHA standards cover hazards which exist in a wide variety of industries. These are compiled as the OSHA General Industry standards, OSHA construction standards, OSHA agriculture standards, and OSHA maritime sectors standards. In general the OSHA standards fall into the following four categories : Specification Standards", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-13.webp"}, {"n": 14, "text": "84 Quality Systems Specific proportions, locations and warning symbols for signs to be displayed. Performance Standards The minimum and maximum limits of achievements are specified. Particular Standards (Vertical) The standards relating to particular industry for individual operations. General Standards (Horizontal) Standards related to workplace specifying environmental control, walking surface, exits, illumination etc. 4.8.1 Benefits arising from OSHA The gains resulting from good safety and health efforts help form good community relations, lower employee absenteeism, better employee morale, and steadier work. Employers can expect OSHA to : • help them identify and control workplace hazards, and • focus agency inspections on the most significant hazards in dangerous workplaces. The nuclear industry is one such area where hazards from radiation exposure are a threat to every worker. Employees can expect OSHA to : • promote employee participation in the development and implementation of worksite safety and health programs and in OSHA interventions such as workplace inspections, • respond promptly to employee’s request for correction of serious hazards at workplaces, and • advise employees of their safety and health rights and responsibilities, and complete investigations within 90 days for those employees who believe they have been discriminated for exercising their rights to requests or participation in inspections. The general public can expect OSHA to : • make regulations and interpretations user-friendly, • work in partnership with business and labor to develop new standards that make real sense to real people, • expand training opportunities for private sector employees, and • provide better access to OSHA information through publications and online materials. To prevent accidents and occupational ill-health, rules have to be enforced, safe and hygienic work conditions should be developed and workers should be trained to do the job safely. Any safety programme will be ineffective without first creating a proper safety philosophy, teaching safety principles, and eliminating misconceptions about the causes of accidents. In the next section, the tools devised by OSHA to assess an effective safety and health program shall be discussed. 4.9 SAFETY APPROACHES AND SAFETY MANAGEMENT It is generally considered that safety awareness can be developed by having meetings, posters, safety booklets, special classroom sessions, etc. Though theoretically it seems", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-14.webp"}, {"n": 15, "text": "85 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality correct that these training techniques should make employees judge what is an unsafe practice and what is not, experimental study shows that this mass educational methods fail to apply to the actual work situations. The existing rules of safety practices in most industries do not cover individual risks. In general, the following points are essential to evaluate the effectiveness of safety practices : • The Perception of Employees of the Rules : The employee must appreciate the new safety rules and must not think that the rules were imposed from above. • Keeping Pace : Safety rules should keep pace with changes in technology, work schedules and emergency situations. • Safety rules should not cause unnecessary inconvenience to the employees. At least, the worker must not be worried of meeting safety measures in lieu of the actual assigned work. • Safety rules should be objective in nature, i.e. the rules should not be broad or very general having no real meaning to employees. 4.9.1 Methods and Tools for Assessing Effectiveness Safety and Health Program OSHA states three basic methods for assessing safety and health program effectiveness : Checking Documentation of Activity The checking of documented manuals can also be used to determine the quality of certain activities, such as self-inspections. These inspection records can reveal the evaluator whether serious hazards are being found, or whether the same hazards are being found repeatedly. If certain hazards recur repeatedly after being corrected, someone is not taking responsibility for keeping those hazards under control. Either the responsibility is not clear, or those who are responsible are not being held accountable. Interviewing Employees at all Levels for Knowledge, Awareness, and Perceptions Talking to randomly selected employees at all levels will provide a good indication of the quality of employee training and of employee perceptions of the program. If safety and health training is effective, employees will be able to relate about the hazards they work with and how they protect themselves and others by keeping those hazards controlled. Every employee should also be able to say precisely what he is expected to do as part of the program. Reviewing Site Conditions and Root Causes of Hazards Looking at site conditions and practices is a well established technique for assessing the effectiveness of safety and health programs. Examining the conditions of the workplace can reveal existing hazards. It can also provide information about the breakdown of the management systems meant to prevent or control these hazards. Another way to obtain information about safety and health program management is through root analysis of observed hazards. This approach to hazards is much like the most sophisticated accident investigation techniques, in which many contributing factors are located and corrected or controlled. The tools used by OSHA for assessments of safety and health programs are : (i) Management leadership and employee involvement. (ii) Worksite analysis. (iii) Hazard prevention and control.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-15.webp"}, {"n": 16, "text": "86 Quality Systems (iv) Safety and health training. In the following sub-sections, we shall see how the method of documentation, interviews and site condition reviews are utilized with the help of these tools. Assessing the Key Components of Leadership, Participation, and Accountability Worksite Policy on Safe and Healthful Working Conditions The review of documented policies can help the investigator from OSHA to separate the priority of worker safety and health over organizational values such as production. Interview with employees can reveal if the written policy is visible in work, and whether they are treated as a resource more valuable than production quantities. Previous records on hazards reveal if the employees did not understand the importance of safety precautions. Visible Top Management Leadership Documents cover all written programs which involve top-level management in safety and health activities. For example, top management can receive and approve on inspection reports either after each inspection or in a quarterly summary. These reports can then be posted for employees to see. Top management can provide time once in a week or a month for employees to come in to discuss safety and health concerns. Such a document is a valuable resource to prove the management initiatives on safety. Interview with employees can give further proof of the managers’ involvement with safety, such as wearing personal protective equipment when in shop floor. Employee Participation The presence of employee inspection reports, minutes of joint employee-management or written guarantees of employee protection from harassment resulting from safety and health program involvement are a proof of employee participation in decisions affecting their safety and health. If employees are not comfortable during an interview regarding their participation, it is a clear indication that employees do not count safety measures as an achievement. Accountability of Managers, Supervisors, and Hourly Employees Documented evidence of employees being held accountable for safety and health responsibilities, including safe work practices is a substantial proof that managers and supervisors include importance relating to safety and health protection. If the employee responds that managers do not follow rules the complaints against supervisors regarding safety precautions are justified. The cause of the hazards might be because managers are not being held accountable for the safety of employees. Assessing the Key Components of Worksite Analysis Comprehensive Surveys, Change Analysis, Routine Hazard Analysis Checking documents provide comprehensive analysis of all potential hazards on work site, new facility, equipment, material, or process and the means for eliminating or controlling such hazards. In case of nuclear power, documents that discuss the potential hazards such as nuclear leak must exist. Reporting of Hazards by Employees Employees must know whom to contact and what to do if they see something they believe to be hazardous for themselves or for their coworkers. Such information can be collected through interviews.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-16.webp"}, {"n": 17, "text": "87 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality Assessing the Key Components of Hazard Prevention and Control Documents related to inspection are a proof that vigilant action is taken to prevent avoidable hazards. Such documents also contain information of the corrective action taken. If the contents also state that by inspection the management discovered a potential hazard in an unlikely area, it is a further proof of good safety practice. This provides the inspectors to evaluate the appropriateness of corrective actions. Assessing the Key Components of Safety and Health Training An interview on general safety with the employees is a quick indicator of the safety practices in the organization. Regular training has to be provided to workers on health and safety. From time to time, their awareness about health and safety measures has to be assessed. 4.10 ISO 14000 The ISO 14000 is a set of standards concerning the ways in which an organization’s activities affect the environment throughout the life of its products. These activities range from production to ultimate disposal of the product. It includes effects on the environment such as pollution, waste generation, noise, depletion of natural resources and energy use. At present, many countries and regional groupings are generating their own requirements for environmental issues, and these vary between the groups. The existence of the standards will ensure that there are no conflicts between regional interpretations of good environmental practices. The ISO 14000 specifies that an Environmental Policy, fully supported by senior management must exist. The ISO 14000 standards are designed to cover environmental management systems, environmental auditing, environmental performance evaluation, environmental labeling and life-cycle assessment. It requires that the environmental policies of the company should be brought to the notice of not only staff but also to the public. The key aspects of ISO 14000 are that it is voluntary and flexible. It pushes continual improvement, encourages cost saving by integrating environmental requirements into the overall company systems (design, manufacturing, etc.) and can provide a substantial market advantage. A primary component of the ISO 14000 standard, as mentioned earlier, is the “Environmental Policy” which must be defined by an organization’s top management. These environmental policies must include a commitment to both compliance with environmental laws and company policies, continual improvement and prevention of pollution. A system is then created (or if already existing, documented) which ensures that the environmental policy is carried out by the organization. This involves planning, implementation and operations, checking and corrective action, and management review. SAQ 3 (a) Explain how OSHA safety standards are beneficial to the employer, employee and community. (b) Write a short note on ISO14000.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-17.webp"}, {"n": 18, "text": "88 Quality Systems 4.11 SUMMARY In this unit, the ISO 9000 family of standards is introduced as the world-wide accepted quality standard. The various requirements of implementing a quality management system are discussed in detail. The practice of documentation is very important in establishing ISO standards. Health and safety are integral part of total quality management. Organizations like OSHA are in the forefront of guiding the management as well as worker community for occupational safety and health practices. The recent years have also seen a growing concern for environment issues and the impact of industries through their products directly or indirectly. The ISO 14000 was formulated to bring a uniform conduct in good environmental practices. 4.12 KEY WORDS Document : In the terminology of ISO 9000, document refers to information and its supporting medium in the form of paper, magnetic tape, computer disc, photograph, etc. Generic : The term generic with respect to a quality standard means that the same standards can be applied to any organization, whatever its product, including service in any sector of activity. ISO : ISO is the word that represents the International Organization for Standardization, which frames various sets of quality policies that are to be followed by an organization. ISO 9000 : ISO 9000 is concerned with “quality management”. This means what the organization does to enhance customer satisfaction. ISO 14000 : ISO 14000 is concerned with “environmental management”. This means what the organization does to minimize harmful effects on the environment caused by its activities. OSHA : Occupational Safety and Health Administration is an active body in USA, authorized to assure safe and healthful working conditions for every worker in the industry. Management System Standards : A management system standard provides the organization with a model to follow in setting up and operating the management system. Quality Management : It is that aspect of the company’s overall management function that determines and implements the company’s quality policy. Quality Management System : The quality management system standards of the revised ISO 9000:2000 series contains the principles that can be used by senior management as a framework to guide their organizations towards improved performance.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-18.webp"}, {"n": 19, "text": "89 Role of ISO and OSHA in Quality Record : A record is any document required by International Standard that states results achieved for providing evidence of activities performed. 4.13 ANSWERS TO SAQs Please refer the preceding text for Answers to SAQs. FURTHER READING Crosby, Phillip B., 2001, Quality Without Tears, New York, McGraw Hill Book Company. Henley, Esnest J. and Hiromitsu Kumamoto, 1999, Reliability Engineering and Risk Assessment, Englewood Cliff, N. J., Prentice Hall, Inc. Jwcan Jospeh, M (ed.), 2000, Quality Control Handbook, 3rd, New York, McGraw Hill Book Company. L. Grant Eugene, S. Leavenworth Richard, 1990, Statistical Quality Control, McGraw Hill Book Company. Oakland S. John, 2000, Total Quality Management, Butterwort-Heinemann. Ishikawa, K. 1995, What is Total Quality Control?, The Japanese Way, Prentice-Hall, Englewood Cliffs, N. J. (USA). Ryan, Thomas P., 1989, Statistical Methods for Quality Improvement, New York, Wiley. Banks, Jerry, 1989, Principle of Quality Control, New York, Wiley. Bounds, Greg, Lyle Yorks, Mel Adams and Gipsie Ranney, 1990, Beyond Total Quality Management : Towards the Engineering Paradigm, New York : McGraw Hill Book Company. Denton D., Keity, 1989, Quality Service, Houston, Gulf Publishing Company. Juran, Joseph M., 1988m Juran on Planning for Quality, New York, Free Press.", "image": "assets/pages/sdl4-19.webp"}]}]};